// generated from questions.json — do not edit by hand
const QUESTION_BANK = [{"id": "1.1-L1-01", "topic": "1.1", "topicName": "What Claude Code is", "difficulty": 1, "question": "At the most basic level, what is Claude Code?", "options": ["An IDE plugin that adds AI autocomplete inside editors like VS Code", "A web chatbot tuned specifically for programming questions", "A terminal-based agentic tool that lets a Claude model read files, run commands, and edit code in your project", "A local runtime that downloads Claude's model weights and runs them on your machine"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Claude Code is a CLI agent — Anthropic's own docs define it as \"the agentic harness around Claude.\" The model never runs on your machine: whether you connect through the Anthropic API or AWS Bedrock, the weights stay server-side, and what's installed locally is the harness that assembles requests, executes tools (reading files, running shell commands), and loops until the task is done. That rules out the \"local runtime\" option. It isn't autocomplete either — IDE integrations exist, but they're front-ends to the same terminal agent, not inline suggestion engines. And unlike a web chatbot, its defining feature is agency over your actual files and shell, not a chat window that happens to know code."}, {"id": "1.1-L1-02", "topic": "1.1", "topicName": "What Claude Code is", "difficulty": 1, "question": "Claude Code is often described as \"agentic.\" What does that specifically mean?", "options": ["It works in a loop — deciding on actions, using tools, observing results, and continuing until the task is done", "It acts fully autonomously from the moment you press enter, executing every file edit and shell command without ever pausing for user approval", "It maintains a persistent server-side memory of you and your projects, carrying context forward automatically across all sessions and machines", "It coordinates several different models simultaneously to answer faster"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "\"Agentic\" means model-in-a-loop: the model proposes an action (a tool call), the harness executes it, the result goes back in, and the cycle repeats until the model decides it's finished. That's categorically different from a chatbot's one-question-one-answer exchange. It does not mean unsupervised — a permission system gates risky actions, and you can interrupt at any time. Nor does it mean memory: the API is stateless, so anything the agent \"remembers\" was either re-sent by the harness in the next request or re-read from disk files like CLAUDE.md. And it's one model driving one loop, not a committee — subagents exist, but each is simply another instance of the same loop."}, {"id": "1.1-L2-01", "topic": "1.1", "topicName": "What Claude Code is", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You ask the exact same Claude model the same coding question in claude.ai chat and in Claude Code. What is fundamentally different between the two?", "options": ["Claude Code uses a special code-tuned variant of the model", "The harness — Claude Code wraps the same model with tools, local file access, and an agent loop", "Claude Code gets a much larger context window for the same model", "claude.ai reasons more deeply because it isn't constrained by a terminal"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The same model weights answer in both places — there is no secret \"code model\" behind the CLI. What differs is everything around the model: Claude Code surrounds it with tool definitions, your project files, a permission system, and a loop that executes actions locally. A useful mental model: the model is a pure function from a message array to output tokens, and all the interesting engineering — files, tools, memory, your terminal — lives on the harness side. The context window is a property of the model and API, not of the surface you use, so that distractor fails. This framing matters on Bedrock too: switching providers changes the door you call the model through, never the harness or the weights."}, {"id": "1.2-L1-01", "topic": "1.2", "topicName": "The four layers", "difficulty": 1, "question": "In the four-layer picture — CLI, harness, API, model — which layer actually reads your files and runs your shell commands?", "options": ["The model, which has direct sandboxed access to your filesystem", "The API layer, which proxies filesystem calls back to your machine", "The CLI, which gathers all file contents before anything is sent", "The harness, which executes tool calls locally and sends the results back to the model"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The model can only emit structured text — a tool_use block saying, in effect, \"please run this command.\" It has no hands. The harness on your machine reads that block, performs the action locally, and appends the output as a tool_result in the next request. The API layer is pure transport: it carries message arrays back and forth and never touches your disk — which is also why this works identically on AWS Bedrock and the Anthropic API. The CLI distractor fails because nothing is gathered up front; files enter the conversation only when the model asks for them mid-loop. Keeping execution in the harness is the whole trick that turns a stateless text-completion function into something that appears to work in your repo."}, {"id": "1.2-L1-02", "topic": "1.2", "topicName": "The four layers", "difficulty": 1, "question": "You type a prompt into Claude Code and press enter. Which sequence correctly describes the path it takes?", "options": ["The CLI captures your input → the harness assembles a full request around it → the API transports that request → the model generates tokens", "The CLI sends your text straight to the model → the model asks the harness for any context it needs → the API logs the exchange", "The harness forwards your text to the CLI → the API assembles the request → the model generates a reply", "The API captures your input → the harness drafts a reply → the model verifies and returns it"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Each layer has one job. The CLI is the terminal surface that captures input and renders output. The harness does the heavy lifting: it wraps your sentence in a complete request — system prompt, tool definitions, CLAUDE.md, environment info, and the entire prior transcript, with your new message last. The API (Anthropic direct, or Bedrock as a different door to the same model) transports that array. The model turns it into tokens. The key correction to the second option: the model never \"asks for context\" up front — context is pushed by the harness in the request, not pulled by the model. Because the API is stateless, nothing can be implied or referenced; everything the model knows must be literally present in that assembled request."}, {"id": "1.2-L2-01", "topic": "1.2", "topicName": "The four layers", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Your team switches Claude Code from the Anthropic API to AWS Bedrock. Which of the four layers actually changes?", "options": ["The harness and the API layer — Bedrock replaces the local agent loop with AWS-side orchestration running against its own regional endpoints", "The model layer — Bedrock serves a retrained AWS variant of Claude", "Only the API layer — auth, endpoint, and model IDs change; the CLI, harness, and model weights stay the same", "All four layers — Bedrock ships its own CLI build, request assembler, endpoints, and AWS-tuned weights, which is why sessions behave noticeably differently"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Bedrock is a different door to the same model. The weights AWS serves are the same Claude weights Anthropic serves — not a retrained variant. The CLI and harness are the identical binary on your machine, running the identical loop, prompt assembly, and tool execution. What changes is confined to the API layer: authentication comes from AWS credentials and IAM (enabled via CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK) instead of /login or an Anthropic API key, the endpoint is regional AWS infrastructure, and models are addressed by Bedrock model IDs rather than Anthropic model names. Practical deltas that do exist — feature lag, quotas, billing through AWS — are all properties of that one layer, not evidence that the machinery above or below it changed."}, {"id": "1.2-L2-02", "topic": "1.2", "topicName": "The four layers", "difficulty": 2, "question": "The contents of your project's CLAUDE.md are in the model's context every single turn. Which layer put them there, and how?", "options": ["The model reads it with a file tool at the start of each session", "The harness injects it into the request it assembles on every call", "The API server loads it from your repository once you authenticate", "The CLI uploads it once at startup and the server pins it to your session"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Prompt assembly is a harness job. On every model call, the harness rebuilds the request from scratch — system prompt, tool definitions, CLAUDE.md, environment info, then the transcript — and ships the whole thing. The \"uploaded once and pinned\" option is the instructive wrong answer: it would require server-side session state, and the API is stateless — nothing persists between calls, so there is nowhere to pin anything. That's exactly why CLAUDE.md must be re-sent every time, and why the harness places it in the stable early portion of the request, where prompt caching absorbs the repeated cost. The API never touches your disk (true on Bedrock and Anthropic alike), and the model doesn't need a tool call for it — the content arrives pre-injected."}, {"id": "1.3-L2-01", "topic": "1.3", "topicName": "The stateless loop", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A colleague says: \"Claude Code still knows what I told it twenty minutes ago, so the server must be keeping our session history somewhere.\" What's the correction?", "options": ["No correction needed — the server holds an open session keyed by a conversation ID", "Correct in effect — the prompt cache retains the conversation between calls", "Wrong — the model's weights are temporarily fine-tuned on your session as it runs", "Wrong — the harness re-sends the entire conversation with every call; the API stores no session state"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "This is the keystone fact of the whole system: the Claude API is stateless. There is no server-side session, no conversation ID, no memory of you. Each request is a complete, self-contained message array, and the model reads it as if for the first time — because it is the first time. When the session \"remembers,\" nothing was remembered; the harness re-sent all of it. The cache option is the seductive distractor: prompt caching does exist, but it's a cost-and-latency optimization layered over a resend that still happens — it is not storage standing in for memory. Weights never change during inference. Every downstream topic — compaction, caching economics, why long sessions get expensive — hangs off this one fact."}, {"id": "1.3-L2-02", "topic": "1.3", "topicName": "The stateless loop", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Between two consecutive model calls in your Claude Code session, what conversational state does the API keep on the server?", "options": ["None — each request must carry the complete conversation; there is no session or conversation ID", "The full history, stored under your session ID until the session ends", "A rolling summary of the conversation, refreshed after each call", "The system prompt and tool definitions, so only the messages need re-sending"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Nothing conversational survives between calls. Every request stands alone: system prompt, tool definitions, and the full message history all travel again, every time. The only server-side artifact is the short-lived prompt cache — stored attention state over a request prefix — and that is a compute shortcut, not memory: if it expires, nothing is lost, because the harness was going to resend everything anyway. The last option is a common half-belief worth killing: even the system prompt and tool schemas are re-sent on every call, which is precisely why their stability matters so much for caching. This holds identically on AWS Bedrock and the Anthropic API — statelessness is a property of the protocol, not of the provider."}, {"id": "1.3-L3-01", "topic": "1.3", "topicName": "The stateless loop", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Mid-session, a model call reports cache_read_input_tokens: 120,000. A colleague concludes those tokens \"weren't sent to the API this time.\" What actually happened?", "options": ["The colleague is right — cache reads count tokens the server restored from its stored copy of the conversation, so they never crossed the wire on this call", "The tokens were served from a local prefix cache the harness keeps under ~/.claude, so only the uncached tail was actually uploaded on that call", "They were sent in full; they matched a server-side cached prefix, so they were not recomputed and billed at roughly 0.1× the input rate", "Cache reads count output tokens the model reused from one of its previous replies"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A cache read does not mean the tokens weren't sent — this is the most common misreading of the usage numbers. The full prefix crosses the wire on every single call; the server matches it against a cached prefix (stored attention state) and skips recomputing it, billing at about a tenth of the normal input price. You save money and latency, never bandwidth. The cache lives server-side and has nothing to do with ~/.claude, which holds transcripts, not model state. This is the correct way to reconcile caching with the stateless loop: the resend still happens in full — caching is what makes the resend affordable, not what makes it unnecessary. In real sessions, cache reads routinely cover over 90% of all input tokens."}, {"id": "1.3-L3-02", "topic": "1.3", "topicName": "The stateless loop", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague claims: \"Long sessions cost more, but only linearly — each turn just adds its own tokens to the bill.\" What's wrong with that?", "options": ["Nothing — pricing is per token, so total cost is linear in what you type", "Cost is actually near-flat after the first few turns, because prompt caching serves re-sent prefix tokens at no charge once the cache is warm", "Cost tracks output tokens, which dominate the bill at five times the input rate, and output stays roughly constant per turn", "Every call re-sends the whole conversation so far, so session cost grows roughly quadratically with length"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Turn n doesn't pay for turn n — it pays for turns 1 through n, again. Each call costs roughly the current conversation size, so a session of n turns costs on the order of n². Real transcript accounting makes this vivid: one observed session sent 29.4 million input tokens against 151 thousand output — a 195:1 ratio — because one growing conversation was re-sent 161 times. Caching blunts the price (cached prefix tokens bill at ~0.1×) but does not zero it, so the \"free\" distractor fails; and input, not output, dominates the bill, so the output distractor fails too. This quadratic growth is the real reason compaction must exist. On Bedrock the arithmetic is identical — the same resend, billed through AWS."}, {"id": "1.3-L3-03", "topic": "1.3", "topicName": "The stateless loop", "difficulty": 3, "question": "In your session's token accounting, call 7's total input was 52,410 tokens, and call 8 reports cache_read_input_tokens: 52,408. What does that near-match demonstrate?", "options": ["A known rounding artifact in the tokenizer's byte-pair accounting, which consistently undercounts multi-call sessions by two tokens", "The resend: each call re-ships the previous call's entire payload as its now-cached prefix, then appends new content", "The server silently truncated two trailing tokens from the cached prefix to keep call 8's request inside the context window", "Two tokens of the system prompt are randomized on every call by design"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "This correspondence — cache_read on call n almost exactly equals total input on call n−1 — is the statelessness \"tell,\" and it's visible in your own transcripts under ~/.claude. It means everything shipped in the previous call was shipped again, matched the server-side cache as a prefix, and had the new tokens (a tool result, your next message) appended after it. The off-by-a-couple isn't noise or a bug; it's the boundary between the cached prefix and the fresh tail. This is the fastest way to show a skeptical colleague, rather than tell them, that the harness re-sends the whole conversation every call: the API's own accounting exposes the resend, token by token, call after call."}, {"id": "1.3-L3-04", "topic": "1.3", "topicName": "The stateless loop", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague says: \"Compaction dropped some early details from our context, but the model saw them earlier in the session, so it can still recall them if it really needs to.\" What's wrong with that?", "options": ["Each call is a first reading — anything not in the current request simply doesn't exist for the model, and there is nothing server-side to recall from", "Nothing — the server-side KV cache retains the attention state of earlier turns even after compaction, so the model can still attend to them whenever a later question requires it", "It's mostly right — recall of compacted-away content still works, but it becomes slower and less reliable the further back the dropped details sit", "It's only wrong on Bedrock; the Anthropic API keeps a session backup for recovery"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The model forgets nothing and remembers nothing: it knows exactly what is in the current request — no more, no less. \"Saw it earlier\" is meaningless to a stateless API, because each call is read start to finish as if for the first time. If compaction's summary omitted a detail, the only ways back are re-reading the file, consulting the transcript on disk, or you restating it. The KV-cache distractor is the subtle one: the cache stores attention state for a prefix that is still being re-sent — once compaction rewrites the conversation, the old prefix is no longer sent, and the cache is irrelevant to recall. And no provider differs here: statelessness is a property of the protocol on Bedrock and Anthropic alike."}, {"id": "1.4-L3-01", "topic": "1.4", "topicName": "One turn, end to end", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Within a single turn, the agent loop runs model call after model call with no human in it. What actually terminates the loop and returns control to you?", "options": ["The harness reaching its maximum tool-call count for the turn", "You pressing enter to acknowledge the last tool result", "The model returning a reply that contains no tool_use blocks", "The API closing the stream after a fixed time budget per turn"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The loop's exit condition belongs to the model: as long as each reply contains a tool_use block, the harness executes the tool, appends the result, and re-sends the whole (now longer) array. Only when a reply arrives with no tool request does the turn end and the prompt return to you. That's why one typed sentence can trigger dozens of model calls — 161 in one observed session — each one a full resend of everything so far. Caps like maxTurns and budget limits do exist, but they are emergency brakes, not the normal exit; the tool-count distractor mistakes the backstop for the mechanism. The user never mediates mid-loop either — the whole point of an agentic turn is that tool round-trips need no acknowledgment."}, {"id": "1.4-L3-02", "topic": "1.4", "topicName": "One turn, end to end", "difficulty": 3, "question": "The harness runs a tool the model requested and sends the output back. What role does that tool_result carry in the message array, and why does it matter?", "options": ["role \"assistant\" — so the model recognizes its own tool output; it matters mainly for billing", "role \"user\" — tool output and human speech share one input channel, which is why prompt injection via tool results is a structural risk, not a bug", "role \"system\" — the result is appended to the system prompt for stability", "role \"tool\" — the Messages API gives tool output its own dedicated role, keeping it in a separate channel the model can always distinguish from human input"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The tool_result block goes back into the conversation as a user message, keyed to the tool_use id. The model asks for a file; the answer arrives wearing your name. There is no separate \"tool\" role in the Messages API — from inside the message array, file contents, command output, and genuine human instructions all flow through the same input channel. That's the structural reason prompt injection through tool results is a real security category rather than an implementation bug: text a webpage or file happens to contain sits in the same channel as your instructions, and nothing at the protocol level distinguishes them. Appending to the system prompt would also wreck the cached stable prefix — results belong at the volatile end of the array."}, {"id": "1.4-L3-03", "topic": "1.4", "topicName": "One turn, end to end", "difficulty": 3, "question": "In the assembled request, the harness orders content roughly as: tool definitions → system prompt → CLAUDE.md and environment info → prior transcript → your newest message. Why is that ordering load-bearing rather than cosmetic?", "options": ["Attention weighs earlier tokens more heavily than later ones, so standing instructions must lead the request or the model will privilege the recent transcript over them", "The Messages API validates block ordering and rejects any request where transcript messages precede the system prompt or the tool definitions", "It keeps transcripts readable for humans debugging a session", "Prompt caching matches prefixes — stable content first means the bulk of every resend hits cache; volatile content early would invalidate it on every call"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "This is the stability gradient: most stable first, most volatile last. Prompt caching matches from the very start of the request forward, so anything that changes early invalidates the cache for everything after it. Put a timestamp in the system prompt or reorder the tool list per call, and every request bills at full input price instead of the ~0.1× cache-read tier — a tenfold difference on the bulk of your tokens. Since the stateless loop re-sends the entire conversation on every call, and cache reads typically cover over 90% of input, this ordering is what keeps the resend economical. Attention doesn't privilege early tokens the way the first distractor implies, and the API enforces no such ordering — the harness chooses it because the economics demand it."}, {"id": "1.4-L3-04", "topic": "1.4", "topicName": "One turn, end to end", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You run Claude Code against AWS Bedrock. Tracing one turn end to end — prompt assembly, API call, response blocks, tool execution, resend — where does Bedrock actually differ from Anthropic direct?", "options": ["Only at the API call: endpoint, AWS auth, and model IDs differ; assembly, the loop, and tool execution are harness-local and identical", "Tool execution moves into an AWS-managed sandbox instead of your machine", "Bedrock wraps response blocks in an AWS-specific envelope schema, so the harness swaps in a different parsing path and loop logic per provider", "Bedrock keeps conversation state server-side in your AWS account, so after the first call each resend only needs to carry the new messages and tool results"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Walk the turn and almost every stage is on your machine: the harness assembles the request, executes tool_use blocks locally, appends tool_result messages, and re-sends the growing array. None of that knows or cares which provider is on the other end. The only stage that changes is the API call itself — AWS credentials and IAM instead of an Anthropic key, a regional AWS endpoint, and Bedrock model IDs — while the payload remains the same Anthropic-shaped message array with the same content blocks (thinking, text, tool_use) coming back. The last distractor is the one to stamp out: no provider keeps conversational state between calls. Statelessness, and therefore the full resend, is a property of the protocol — Bedrock is a different door to the same model."}, {"id": "1.5-L1-01", "topic": "1.5", "topicName": "Usage patterns", "difficulty": 1, "question": "When is Claude Code the better choice over claude.ai chat for a task?", "options": ["Whenever you need the smartest model — claude.ai serves smaller distilled variants, while the CLI is the only surface with access to the full-size weights", "When the work lives in your repository — reading and editing files, running tests and commands — because the harness can act locally", "Only when you're offline, since Claude Code doesn't need the internet", "When you need the model to automatically remember all your previous conversations, since Claude Code stores session memory server-side while chat starts fresh"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The deciding factor is agency, not intelligence. The same Claude models are available through both surfaces, so \"smarter model\" is not the differentiator. Claude Code earns its place when the task involves your actual codebase: the harness reads files, edits them, runs tests, and iterates on real command output — things a browser chat can only do through copy-paste. It is not an offline tool: the model runs remotely whether you reach it via the Anthropic API or AWS Bedrock; only tool execution is local. And neither surface gives the model true memory — the API is stateless in both cases; claude.ai's apparent continuity and Claude Code's session history are both harness-side conveniences layered over the same resend mechanics."}, {"id": "1.5-L2-01", "topic": "1.5", "topicName": "Usage patterns", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What does running `claude -p \"summarize the failing tests\"` do?", "options": ["Opens plan mode with that prompt preloaded for review", "Starts an interactive session pinned to a saved project profile, with the quoted prompt loaded as the session's standing instruction", "Runs headless: executes the prompt non-interactively, prints the result to stdout, and exits — the pattern used in CI and scripts", "Queues the prompt to a detached background session that keeps running after you close the terminal, which you can reattach later with --resume"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The -p (print) flag is headless mode: no interactive UI, one prompt in, output to stdout, then exit — which makes Claude Code scriptable and pipeable, and is the standard way to run it in CI. Crucially, it is not a dumber mode: the same agent loop runs underneath, including tool use, subject to your permission configuration — the difference is the absence of a human at the terminal, which is why permission setup matters more in CI. Plan mode is a separate interactive mode, and resuming past sessions is what --resume and --continue are for, so those distractors name real features in the wrong place. For a Bedrock shop, headless runs just need AWS credentials and the Bedrock env configuration present in the CI environment — there's no /login step."}, {"id": "1.5-L2-02", "topic": "1.5", "topicName": "Usage patterns", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You're about to ask for a risky multi-file refactor, so you switch to plan mode first. What does plan mode change about the workflow?", "options": ["It routes exploration to a cheaper model and edits to a stronger one", "It disables every tool, including reads, so the model reasons purely from the conversation and any files you paste in yourself", "It executes the refactor on an auto-created shadow branch, then presents the diff and asks whether to merge the changes back into your branch", "The model can still research — read files, search the codebase — but must present a plan for your approval before making any edits"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Plan mode is a permission posture, not a different engine: the loop runs exactly as always, but the harness holds back mutating tools (edits, arbitrary commands) while leaving read-only research available. The model explores your codebase, then presents a plan you explicitly approve before any change lands. That approval gate is the point — for large or risky changes, you review intent before execution, instead of reviewing diffs after. The \"disables all tools\" distractor overshoots: a plan built without reading the code would be worthless, so read tools stay on. Model selection is untouched, and no branches are created — version-control isolation is something you or your workflow add separately. Mechanically it's the same stateless loop with a harness-side gate on which tool calls get executed."}, {"id": "1.1-L1-03", "topic": "1.1", "topicName": "What Claude Code is", "difficulty": 1, "question": "Anthropic defines Claude Code as \"the agentic harness around Claude.\" In that division of labor, what is the model's job?", "options": ["Executing the tool calls it decides on, while the harness supervises them for safety", "Reading a sequence of messages and producing tokens — nothing else", "Managing permissions and deciding which of its actions need your approval", "Storing what happened in the session so the next request can build on it"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The model does exactly one thing: it reads a message array and generates tokens. It cannot touch a file, run a command, enforce a rule, or remember anything on its own. Everything else — executing tools, checking permissions, reading CLAUDE.md, saving transcripts, assembling each request — belongs to the harness on your machine. That split is the key that unlocks every deeper topic: when Claude Code \"takes an action,\" the harness executed a structured request the model wrote; when it \"remembers,\" the harness re-sent history to a stateless model. The execution and storage options fail because they assign harness jobs to the model, and the permission option fails the same way — the model can be told about rules, but only the harness enforces them. This holds identically on Bedrock and the Anthropic API: the provider changes the door, never the division of labor."}, {"id": "1.1-L1-04", "topic": "1.1", "topicName": "What Claude Code is", "difficulty": 1, "question": "Besides the terminal CLI, Claude Code ships a VS Code extension, a desktop app, and a web surface. How do these relate to each other?", "options": ["They are separate products, each with its own configuration files and behavior", "The IDE extension is an autocomplete engine, while the CLI is the full agent", "The desktop app runs the model on your machine, while the CLI calls it over the network", "They are all front-ends to the same agent engine, sharing the same CLAUDE.md and settings"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The product is the agent engine; terminal, IDE, desktop, and browser are windows onto it. The same CLAUDE.md, settings, and configuration apply everywhere, because underneath every surface the identical harness assembles requests, executes tools, and runs the loop. That's why the \"separate products\" option fails — learning one surface transfers directly to the others. The autocomplete option repeats the most common confusion: inline assistants predict the next few lines in the file you have open, while Claude Code works across the whole project — coordinated multi-file edits, running builds, tracing bugs through modules — regardless of which front-end you drive it from. And no surface runs the model locally: the weights always stay on the provider's hardware, whether you connect through the Anthropic API or AWS Bedrock. Only tool execution is local."}, {"id": "1.1-L2-02", "topic": "1.1", "topicName": "What Claude Code is", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Mid-task, Claude Code runs your test suite and several tests fail. What happens next?", "options": ["The failure output goes back into the loop as a tool result, and the model uses it to decide its next action", "The turn ends with an error, and you must describe the failure in a fresh prompt", "The harness automatically retries the failing command up to three times with backoff, and only involves the model if the failures persist", "The model rolls back its edits to the last passing state, since a failed verification automatically cancels the remainder of the current plan"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A failed test is not an error condition for the agent — it is more information fed back into the loop. Anthropic describes the agentic cycle as gather context, take action, verify results: the harness runs the command, appends its output (including failures) as a tool result, and the model reads that result and decides what to do next — usually editing again and re-running. This mid-task course-correction is precisely what \"agentic\" buys you over a chatbot, where you would have to carry the failure back by hand. The retry option misplaces the intelligence: the harness executes mechanically and doesn't judge outcomes; deciding how to respond to a failure is the model's job on the next call. And nothing auto-cancels or rolls back — the loop simply continues until the model stops requesting actions."}, {"id": "1.2-L1-03", "topic": "1.2", "topicName": "The four layers", "difficulty": 1, "question": "Slash commands like /clear and /model are processed by which part of the system?", "options": ["They are sent to the model as specially delimited chat messages that the system prompt teaches it to parse as commands", "The API layer interprets them and adjusts your server-side session", "They are handled locally by the CLI and harness — they never travel to the model as a prompt", "The model reads them like any message, but they are excluded from billing"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A slash command is an instruction to the harness, not a message to Claude. The CLI captures it, the harness acts on it locally, and the model never sees a prompt — which is why commands like /clear take effect instantly with no round-trip and no tokens spent. Some commands do cause the harness to make model calls of its own (/compact, for instance, triggers a summarization call), but even then the command itself was harness-directed; the model only ever receives whatever request the harness subsequently assembles. The API option fails at a deeper level: there is no server-side session to adjust — the API is stateless, so anything a command changes can only change what the harness sends in future requests. Knowing that the CLI talks to the harness, not the model, is the first step in the \"which layer owns this behavior?\" diagnostic habit."}, {"id": "1.2-L1-04", "topic": "1.2", "topicName": "The four layers", "difficulty": 1, "question": "Of the four layers — CLI, harness, API, model — which run on your machine and which on the provider's side?", "options": ["The CLI and harness are local; the API and model live on the provider's side", "Only the CLI is local; the harness runs on Anthropic's servers alongside the API", "Everything except the model runs locally, including the API layer", "All four run locally in the terminal; only the web surface uses remote layers"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The stack splits exactly in the middle. The CLI (your terminal surface) and the harness (request assembly, tool execution, permissions, local files) live entirely on your machine. The API (a stateless transport) and the model (the weights) live on the provider's hardware. That boundary between harness and API is the most important line in the whole system: everything above it is identical no matter how you connect, and everything below it is where providers differ — which is why switching from the Anthropic API to AWS Bedrock changes auth, endpoints, and model IDs but leaves the loop, prompt assembly, and tool execution untouched. The \"harness on Anthropic's servers\" option would make Claude Code impossible: the harness must be local to read your files and run your shell. And the model never runs locally, on any surface."}, {"id": "1.2-L2-03", "topic": "1.2", "topicName": "The four layers", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Every morning around 9am, your Bedrock-connected Claude Code sessions start hitting throttling errors. Which layer should you investigate?", "options": ["The harness — its request assembly is probably too aggressive during peak hours", "The CLI — terminal rendering backpressure can delay requests until they pile up", "The model — Claude itself slows down and rejects work under heavy shared load", "The API layer — throttling comes from provider-side quotas, here AWS Bedrock's"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Rate limits, quotas, and throttling are properties of the API layer — the one layer that changes when you switch providers. On Bedrock that means AWS quotas and on-demand throughput limits, investigated with AWS tooling (and possibly solved with quota increases or provisioned throughput); on the Anthropic API it would be Anthropic's rate tiers. The 9am pattern is the tell: shared regional capacity and org-level quotas are contended when everyone starts work. The harness assembles the same requests at 9am as at midnight — it has no peak-hours behavior — and the CLI is purely presentational, spending no tokens and issuing no calls. The model option inverts the layering: the weights don't refuse work; the provider's front door does. The diagnostic habit worth keeping: \"throttling errors\" → API layer, \"ignored my CLAUDE.md\" → harness, \"shortcut doesn't work\" → CLI."}, {"id": "1.2-L2-04", "topic": "1.2", "topicName": "The four layers", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You run /clear to start fresh. What travels to the API as a result of that command?", "options": ["A request telling the server to delete your stored session history", "Nothing — /clear is a local harness action; the next model call simply carries an empty history", "A short system message instructing the model to disregard the prior conversation", "A cache-invalidation call that evicts your old prefix server-side, so the stale conversation stops billing at the cached read rate"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "There is nothing on the server to clear. The API is stateless — it never stored your conversation in the first place; the only reason the model knew your history was that the harness re-sent it on every call. So /clear works entirely locally: the harness stops including the old transcript, and the next request is assembled around an empty message history. The \"delete my history\" option fails because it presumes server-side session storage that doesn't exist — on Bedrock or Anthropic alike. The \"disregard\" message fails for the same reason and would be absurdly fragile besides. The cache option gets the mechanics backwards: the prompt cache is a short-TTL compute shortcut that simply expires when its prefix stops being sent — there's no invalidation call, and you were never billed for content you didn't send. Statelessness makes clearing free and instant."}, {"id": "1.3-L2-03", "topic": "1.3", "topicName": "The stateless loop", "difficulty": 2, "question": "During a single turn in which the model uses several tools, how many times does the harness send the full conversation to the API?", "options": ["Once — tool results are streamed into the same open request as they arrive", "Once per user prompt, plus one final call to summarize the tool activity", "Once per model call — every tool round-trip re-sends the entire, now-longer array", "Twice — once when the turn starts and once after all the tools complete"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The resend happens per model call, not per prompt. Each time the model requests a tool, the harness executes it, appends the result, and makes a fresh API call carrying everything so far — system prompt, tool definitions, CLAUDE.md, the whole transcript, plus the new tool result. A turn with a dozen tool uses is a dozen-plus complete resends, each one longer than the last; one observed session reached 161 model calls. The streaming option is the tempting one: the response does stream, but the request is fixed once sent — there is no open channel to feed results into, because each call is a self-contained request to a stateless API. That per-call resend is why a single press of enter can move millions of input tokens, and why prompt caching exists to make the repetition affordable."}, {"id": "1.3-L2-04", "topic": "1.3", "topicName": "The stateless loop", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You closed Claude Code yesterday. Today you run claude --continue and it picks up the conversation exactly where you left off. Where did that continuity come from?", "options": ["The transcript saved on your disk under ~/.claude — the harness reloads it and re-sends it with the next call", "The server-side prompt cache, which pinned your conversation's attention state overnight until the harness reclaimed it at startup", "Your session ID, which the harness presents to the API so the server can restore the stored conversation on its side before the first new call", "The model, which retains recent conversations per account for a limited time"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Continuity across sittings is a purely local trick. Every session is logged to a transcript file under ~/.claude/projects/, and --continue tells the harness to reload the most recent one and resume re-sending it — the stateless API sees just another complete message array and can't distinguish a resumed session from an unbroken one. The prompt-cache option fails on two counts: the cache's TTL is minutes to an hour, not overnight, and it stores attention state over a prefix that must still be sent — it is a compute shortcut, never retrievable storage. The session-ID option describes an architecture the Claude API simply doesn't have: no conversation ID, no server-side session, on Bedrock and Anthropic alike. And model weights never hold your conversations. Everything that persists, persists on your disk."}, {"id": "1.3-L3-05", "topic": "1.3", "topicName": "The stateless loop", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Mid-session, a call's usage object reads input_tokens: 2, cache_creation_input_tokens: 932, cache_read_input_tokens: 280,772. A colleague concludes: \"we only sent 2 tokens that call.\" What is the right reading?", "options": ["The colleague is right — mid-session the harness sends only the new tokens, and the server reconstructs the rest from its cache", "The request was 932 tokens — input_tokens counts your typed text, while cache_creation counts what was actually transmitted", "The request was 280,772 tokens — the other two fields are billing adjustments, not transmitted content", "The request totaled about 281,700 tokens — total input is the sum of all three fields; input_tokens counts only what matched no cache"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Total input for a call is input_tokens + cache_creation_input_tokens + cache_read_input_tokens — three price tiers over one transmitted request, not three kinds of sending. Here: 2 tokens matched no cache (billed 1×), 932 were newly written to the cache (1.25×), and 280,772 hit the cached prefix (roughly 0.1×). All ~281,700 crossed the wire. The first option is the delta-send misconception dressed in cache vocabulary: a stateless API has no stored conversation to reconstruct from — the cache holds attention state for a prefix that must still arrive in full to be matched. The middle options each promote one field to \"the real size,\" but no single field is: the tiny input_tokens figure is itself the signature of the resend working well — mid-session, almost everything you send has been sent before."}, {"id": "1.3-L3-06", "topic": "1.3", "topicName": "The stateless loop", "difficulty": 3, "question": "In your transcript's usage accounting, what does cache_creation_input_tokens represent, and how is it billed?", "options": ["Tokens served from an already-cached prefix this call, billed at roughly a tenth of the base input rate", "Tokens written to the cache this call — the newly stable part of the prefix — billed at 1.25× the base input rate", "Tokens the server stored as session state so future requests can omit them, billed at the standard input rate", "Tokens the model generated to summarize the prefix for storage, billed at the output rate like thinking"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "cache_creation_input_tokens counts what was newly written to the server-side cache on this call — typically last call's fresh tail (your message, the latest tool result) that has now become part of the stable prefix. Writing carries a premium: 1.25× base input for the 5-minute TTL. The bet pays off because those same tokens return as cache reads at ~0.1× on the calls that follow — and in a tool-using turn there are many. The first option describes the adjacent field, cache_read_input_tokens. The third smuggles in the critical error: nothing is stored \"so future requests can omit them\" — the API stays stateless, the harness re-sends the full prefix every call, and the cache only spares recomputation. The fourth invents a mechanism: caching stores attention state directly; no summarization happens, and no output tokens are involved."}, {"id": "1.3-L3-07", "topic": "1.3", "topicName": "The stateless loop", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague argues: \"Our sessions run 96% cache reads at a tenth of list price — at that rate the resend is essentially free, so compaction is pointless.\" What's wrong with that?", "options": ["Cache reads apply only to the system prompt and tool schemas, so the growing transcript still bills at the full input rate", "Compaction exists purely for reasoning quality in long contexts — cost was never part of its rationale in the first place", "A tenth of a quadratically growing bill still grows quadratically, and the context window is a hard token limit no discount changes", "Prompt caching resets whenever a tool result enters the conversation, so real sessions can't actually sustain those read rates"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Caching changes the coefficient, not the curve. Every call still re-sends the whole conversation, so per-call cost still scales with conversation length and session cost still grows roughly quadratically — 0.1× of n² is still n². Worse, price is only half the story: the context window is a fixed token budget, and a growing resend will hit it no matter what anything costs. Compaction addresses both, which is why it must exist even in a well-cached session. The first option is a near-miss: the transcript prefix caches just as well as the system prompt — that's exactly how sessions reach 96% read rates. The last option inverts the mechanics: tool results append to the volatile tail and don't disturb the cached prefix at all. And while long-context quality is a real concern, claiming cost \"was never part of it\" severs the load-bearing link."}, {"id": "1.3-L3-08", "topic": "1.3", "topicName": "The stateless loop", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You step away for twenty minutes mid-session, past the 5-minute cache TTL. When you send your next message, what has actually been lost?", "options": ["Only compute savings — the next call pays cache-write on the prefix again, but no conversation content is lost, since the harness resends everything regardless", "The conversation tail past the last cache breakpoint is dropped — the harness must rebuild that portion from the on-disk transcript and replay it before the session can continue", "The server-side copy of your context — the harness quietly opens a new conversation and re-primes it with a generated summary", "Nothing at all, including cost — expired entries are rebuilt free of charge the next time a matching prefix arrives"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Cache expiry costs money and latency, never memory. The cache stores attention state computed over a prefix — a shortcut for work the server would otherwise redo — and the harness was going to resend that entire prefix anyway, because every call must carry the whole conversation. After expiry, the next call simply finds no match: the prefix is recomputed and re-cached, billed at the 1.25× cache-write rate instead of the 0.1× read rate, and the call runs slower. One expensive call, then normal economics resume. The second and third options both imagine conversational state living server-side — a rebuild-from-disk step or a re-primed session — but there is no server-side conversation to lose; statelessness means expiry can't touch content. The fourth gets the direction right but the price wrong: rebuilding the cache is precisely the part that isn't free."}, {"id": "1.4-L3-05", "topic": "1.4", "topicName": "One turn, end to end", "difficulty": 3, "question": "The model emits a tool_use block requesting a destructive shell command your permission rules don't allow. Why can the permission system guarantee the command never runs, rather than merely discourage it?", "options": ["The API screens every outgoing tool_use block against the permission rules the harness uploads alongside each request, rejecting disallowed ones before they reach your machine", "A tool_use block is a request in structured text — inert until the harness, which enforces your rules locally, chooses to execute it", "The model consults the permission rules included in its system prompt and withholds blocks that would violate them", "Disallowed tools are stripped from the tool schema before every request, so the model is structurally incapable of producing a tool_use block that names them"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Permissions get their leverage from where they sit on the turn's path: after the reply, before execution. A tool_use block is not an action — it's a structured description of a desired action, and nothing happens until the harness, on your machine, decides to run it. That's an enforcement point the model can't route around, which is the difference between a guarantee and a request. The system-prompt option describes \"asking nicely\": instructions influence what the model emits but bind nothing — a confused or injected model could still emit the block, and only harness-side checking makes that harmless. The API option attaches a real idea to the wrong layer: the transport carries message arrays and enforces nothing about your local rules, on Bedrock or Anthropic alike. Schema-stripping is a real hardening technique, but it isn't what permission rules do — the model can still name a gated tool; the harness just refuses or prompts you before executing."}, {"id": "1.4-L3-06", "topic": "1.4", "topicName": "One turn, end to end", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Early in a turn, a search tool returns a 40,000-token result. What is the cost consequence for the rest of the session?", "options": ["It bills once as output tokens, since a tool produced it — subsequent calls reference it for free", "It bills once as input on the next call, then never again — after the cache write, later calls reference the stored copy without retransmitting or rebilling it", "It affects only the current turn — the harness resets the conversation it sends at each turn boundary", "It is appended as a tool_result and re-sent on every later call, billing each time — cheaper once cached, but never free"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Tool output enters the conversation as a tool_result message, and the conversation is what every subsequent call re-sends — so those 40,000 tokens ride along on every model call for the rest of the session, until compaction or /clear removes them. They raise the permanent floor of every later request. The caching option is the precise near-miss: after one cache write (at 1.25×), the block does get cheap — but cache reads bill at ~0.1×, not zero, and 40k tokens at 0.1× on every one of possibly hundreds of calls is real money, plus permanent context-window budget. \"Bills once as output\" gets the direction wrong: output tokens are what the model generates; tool results are input the harness sends. And turn boundaries reset nothing — the array only ever grows. This is why tools that return huge dumps are a cost smell."}, {"id": "1.4-L3-07", "topic": "1.4", "topicName": "One turn, end to end", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A model reply arrives containing a thinking block, a text block, and a tool_use block. How is the thinking block billed?", "options": ["At the input rate, since its purpose is to feed the model's own next step rather than the user", "At the cache-read rate, once the same reasoning prefix has repeated across a few calls", "As ordinary output tokens — reasoning is generated content, priced the same as the prose and the tool call", "It isn't billed — thinking is a harness-side annotation attached to the reply, not model generation"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Everything the model emits — thinking, text, tool_use — is generated token by token in the decode phase, and all of it bills as output. Output is the expensive direction (roughly 5× the base input rate), and on hard problems thinking can dominate a reply's token count, so it materially shows up in your bill. The input-rate option inverts the wire's direction: input is what the harness sends up, and who ultimately \"consumes\" a block doesn't change which side generated it. The cache-read option borrows a real rate from the wrong side of the transaction: prompt caching discounts re-sent request prefixes, and generation is never a cache hit. The harness-annotation option misassigns the layer — the harness assembles requests and executes tools, but every content block in a reply came out of the model. Same accounting through Bedrock: the usage fields are the same Anthropic-shaped response."}, {"id": "1.4-L3-08", "topic": "1.4", "topicName": "One turn, end to end", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Watching the UI mid-turn, it looks like the model \"pauses to read a file\" partway through its answer. At the API level, what actually happened?", "options": ["The model's reply ended with a tool_use block; the harness ran the read locally and started an entirely new API call with the longer array", "The response stream stayed open while the harness piped the file's contents into the model's in-progress generation", "The model fetched the file itself through a scoped filesystem channel the harness grants for the duration of the turn", "The API suspended decoding at the tool_use block, requested the file from the harness over the open connection, and resumed the same response where it had stopped"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "There is no pause — there is a seam between two complete API calls. A model call is one self-contained request and one response: once the reply (ending in a tool_use block) finishes, that call is over. The harness executes the read locally, appends a tool_result as a user message, and issues a brand-new request carrying the entire conversation plus the new result. The model that answers has, as always, never seen any of it before. The other options all invent some form of mid-generation input: streaming is output-only (the request is fixed once sent), the model has no filesystem channel of any kind — it can only emit structured text asking — and the API cannot suspend and resume because it holds no state to resume from. The apparent \"pause\" is the fundamental unit of the agent loop: reply, execute, resend."}, {"id": "1.5-L1-02", "topic": "1.5", "topicName": "Usage patterns", "difficulty": 1, "question": "You run `claude` with no flags inside a project directory. What do you get?", "options": ["A one-shot run: it analyzes the project, prints a report, and exits", "A first-run setup wizard that scans and indexes your entire repository into a local embeddings store before anything else can happen", "An interactive session in your terminal, where the agent works, asks permission when needed, and you steer", "A background daemon that watches your files for changes and proactively suggests edits in the terminal as you work"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Plain `claude` opens the interactive REPL — the default way to drive the tool. You describe what you want, the agent works in its loop, permission prompts appear when it wants to do something your rules gate, and you can steer throughout: Escape interrupts the current action, and typing a correction mid-turn queues it for the model. Nothing is indexed up front — files enter the conversation only when the agent reads them mid-loop, which is part of why startup is instant. The one-shot behavior belongs to the -p (print) flag, which runs headless and exits — the CI pattern, not the default. And there's no watcher daemon; Claude Code acts when you ask, inside a session. Interactive mode is the right choice whenever the task is exploratory or you expect to course-correct."}, {"id": "1.5-L2-03", "topic": "1.5", "topicName": "Usage patterns", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A teammate pastes a screenshot of a stack trace into a Claude Code session and also mentions a URL with the error's docs. What can Claude Code do with these two inputs?", "options": ["It can read the image directly and can fetch the URL's content when needed — both become part of the conversation context", "It can fetch the URL but images are silently discarded, since the API is text-only end to end", "It can read the image, but URLs are never followed — the harness has no network access of its own", "Neither works until you enable multimodal mode in settings.json; by default the harness strips both images and URLs from the prompt before sending"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Claude Code sessions are not text-only. Claude models are multimodal on the input side: an image pasted into the terminal is encoded as an image content block in the messages array and the model genuinely reads it — stack-trace screenshots, UI mockups, and diagrams are all legitimate inputs. URLs work differently: the model cannot reach the network itself, but the harness provides fetch tools, so Claude requests the page through a tool call and the result returns as a tool_result block in the next request. That distinction is the load-bearing part: images travel inside the request; web content arrives via the tool round-trip, subject to permissions. There is no 'multimodal mode' toggle, and nothing is silently discarded. This all works identically on Bedrock and the Anthropic API — multimodality is a property of the model weights, not the door you call them through."}, {"id": "1.5-L2-04", "topic": "1.5", "topicName": "Usage patterns", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Your nightly CI job runs `claude -p \"fix the lint errors and commit\"`, but the run stalls as soon as the agent tries to edit a file. What is the likely cause?", "options": ["The -p flag runs in read-only mode by design — headless invocations get search and read tools only, and can never modify files or run commands", "The edit needs a permission decision and no human is present — headless runs need permissions granted in advance via settings or flags", "CI runners must complete an interactive /login and device-code handshake before any mutating tools unlock, even with API keys configured", "The API rejects tool use on non-interactive requests as a safety measure"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Headless mode runs the full agent loop, tools included — -p removes the human, not the capabilities. That's exactly the problem: when a tool call hits a permission gate, interactive sessions show you a prompt, but in CI there is nobody to answer it, so the run blocks or the action is refused. The fix is deciding permissions ahead of time — allow rules in settings files or flags granting what the job legitimately needs. The /login option is doubly wrong for a Bedrock shop: headless auth comes from environment variables and IAM roles (CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK plus AWS credentials), with no interactive login step at all — which is precisely why the Bedrock setup suits CI runners. And the API neither knows nor cares whether a human is at the terminal: permissions are enforced by the harness on your side of the wire, never by the provider."}, {"id": "2.1-L1-01", "topic": "2.1", "topicName": "Slash commands", "difficulty": 1, "question": "In the middle of a Claude Code session you run `/clear`. What does it do?", "options": ["Deletes the project's CLAUDE.md file so Claude forgets the project instructions", "Redraws the terminal screen but keeps the whole conversation intact", "Wipes the current conversation context and starts you fresh in the same directory", "Logs you out so you have to re-authenticate before the next prompt"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "`/clear` resets the conversation: the next turn is assembled with none of the previous back-and-forth, as if you had just launched a new session in the same project. This matters because of the stateless loop — every turn resends the entire conversation to the API, so a long history means every new turn costs more and eats more of the context window. `/clear` is the cheapest way to drop that baggage when you switch tasks. It does not touch files on disk (CLAUDE.md survives, and the old transcript is still saved and resumable), which is why the CLAUDE.md option is wrong. Redrawing the screen is a different action (Ctrl+L), and authentication is unrelated — especially on Bedrock, where auth comes from AWS credentials, not a session login."}, {"id": "2.1-L1-02", "topic": "2.1", "topicName": "Slash commands", "difficulty": 1, "question": "You want to switch to a different Claude model for the rest of your current session. Which slash command do you use?", "options": ["/model", "/config", "/switch", "/init"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "`/model` changes which model the harness will call for subsequent turns of the current session. Because the loop is stateless — the harness resends the whole conversation on every turn — switching models mid-session simply means the next resend goes to a different model; nothing that already happened is re-run. `/config` opens general settings and can set a default, but `/model` is the direct in-session switch. `/switch` is not a real command, and `/init` does something entirely different (it generates a CLAUDE.md for the project). One Bedrock-flavored note: when running through Bedrock, the models you can pick must be ones enabled in your AWS account and region, and model identifiers there are Bedrock model IDs or inference profiles rather than plain Anthropic API names."}, {"id": "2.1-L2-01", "topic": "2.1", "topicName": "Slash commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A teammate who runs Claude Code against AWS Bedrock asks why they've never needed `/login`. What's the accurate answer?", "options": ["Bedrock users authenticate through /login too, but the OAuth token it stores stays valid for twelve months, so they only did it once and forgot", "With CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK set, auth comes from the standard AWS credential chain, so there's no Anthropic account login step", "Claude Code disables all authentication when talking to Bedrock because the traffic stays inside your VPC and never crosses the public internet", "/login is only required for the -p headless mode, which they apparently never use"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "`/login` exists to connect Claude Code to an Anthropic account — either a Claude subscription via OAuth or a Console API key. When `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK` is set, requests go to AWS instead, and they're signed with whatever the standard AWS credential chain provides: environment variables, an AWS profile, an IAM role, SSO, etc. There is simply no Anthropic-side account in the picture, so the login step doesn't apply. The 'only once' option is wrong because Bedrock users never run it at all. The VPC option confuses network topology with authentication — Bedrock requests are still authenticated, just with AWS SigV4 credentials rather than an Anthropic token. And `/login` has nothing to do with `-p`; headless and interactive modes use the same auth resolution."}, {"id": "2.1-L2-02", "topic": "2.1", "topicName": "Slash commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What is the key difference between `/compact` and `/clear`?", "options": ["/compact deletes old transcript JSONL files from ~/.claude/ to reclaim disk space, while /clear only hides them from the current session view", "/clear summarizes the conversation into a shorter form so context survives; /compact throws the whole conversation away and starts from zero", "/compact is automatic-only and cannot be invoked manually, unlike /clear", "/compact replaces the conversation with a summary so work can continue with context; /clear drops the conversation entirely"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Both commands attack the same problem — the resend grows every turn, so a long conversation gets expensive and eventually hits the context window. `/compact` asks the model to summarize the conversation so far, then continues with that summary (plus a recent tail) in place of the full history: you keep working memory of the task at a fraction of the tokens. `/clear` is the blunt version: it discards the conversation entirely, keeping nothing. Note that compaction itself costs a full read of the conversation, since the model must ingest everything to summarize it. The second option has the two commands reversed. Neither touches transcript files on disk — the session record survives both. And compaction runs automatically near the context limit, but `/compact` exists precisely so you can trigger it manually at a moment you choose."}, {"id": "2.2-L1-01", "topic": "2.2", "topicName": "Modes", "difficulty": 1, "question": "What is plan mode in Claude Code for?", "options": ["Having Claude research the task and propose an approach before it is allowed to change anything", "Scheduling a session to run later at a planned time", "Making Claude respond faster by planning several turns in advance", "Splitting the work across multiple parallel subagents, each assigned one step of a pre-drafted plan before execution begins"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Plan mode restricts Claude to read-only exploration: it can search, read files, and reason, but it can't edit files or run state-changing commands. Instead it produces a plan for you to review; only after you approve does it proceed to execute. The mechanism is a permission mode — the harness withholds approval for mutating tools, which is why this is enforced reliably rather than being a polite request in the prompt. It's the right mode when the change is risky or ambiguous and you want to sanity-check the approach before any file is touched. The distractors misread the word 'plan': there's no scheduling feature by that name, no multi-turn lookahead for speed, and while multi-agent workflows exist, plan mode itself is about gating execution behind your approval, not about parallelism."}, {"id": "2.2-L1-02", "topic": "2.2", "topicName": "Modes", "difficulty": 1, "question": "You switch Claude Code into auto-accept (acceptEdits) mode. What changes compared to the default mode?", "options": ["Claude stops asking for your confirmation on anything at all, including arbitrary shell commands and file deletions", "Claude automatically accepts and submits your typed prompts without you having to press Enter to confirm each one", "File edits are applied without a permission prompt each time, instead of asking you to approve each one", "Claude switches to a faster model to keep up with the accepted edits"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "In the default mode, the harness pauses before each file edit (and other gated actions) and asks you to approve. acceptEdits mode pre-approves file edits specifically, so Claude can write and modify files without stopping — useful when you trust the direction and don't want to click through dozens of small changes. Crucially, it is not a blanket 'yes to everything': commands and other tools that would normally prompt can still prompt. That's the difference from bypassPermissions, which the first option is describing. The permission check happens in the harness, on your machine, before the tool runs — the model itself never executes anything; it only emits tool-use requests that the harness decides to honor. Mode changes never affect which model is used or how your input is submitted."}, {"id": "2.2-L2-01", "topic": "2.2", "topicName": "Modes", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Which set correctly names Claude Code's core permission modes?", "options": ["readonly, readwrite, admin, root", "default, acceptEdits, plan, bypassPermissions", "safe, balanced, fast, unrestricted", "manual, assisted, supervised, autonomous"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The core permission modes are `default` (prompt for approval on gated actions), `acceptEdits` (file edits pre-approved), `plan` (read-only research, propose before acting), and `bypassPermissions` (no prompts at all — for isolated or throwaway environments only). You cycle through available modes with Shift+Tab in the interactive UI, and can start a session in a given mode with the `--permission-mode` flag or set it in settings.json. The mode names matter beyond trivia because they appear verbatim in configuration. The other option sets are invented vocabularies: they sound like plausible product tiers but match nothing in the harness. Note what a permission mode actually is: a policy applied by the harness on your machine when the model requests a tool — it changes nothing about the API request itself, so it works identically on Bedrock and on the Anthropic API."}, {"id": "2.2-L2-02", "topic": "2.2", "topicName": "Modes", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What distinguishes bypassPermissions mode from acceptEdits mode?", "options": ["bypassPermissions can only be set per-project in .claude/settings.json, while acceptEdits can only be enabled per-user in ~/.claude/settings.json", "acceptEdits auto-approves file edits and shell commands alike, while bypassPermissions covers only file edits but also suppresses the confirmation dialog", "They behave identically; bypassPermissions is just the legacy name for acceptEdits", "acceptEdits auto-approves file edits only, while bypassPermissions skips permission prompting entirely, including for shell commands"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "acceptEdits removes the prompt for one category — file edits — while everything else still goes through the normal permission flow. bypassPermissions removes the prompting layer altogether: any tool the model requests, including arbitrary Bash commands, runs without asking. That's why it's paired with the deliberately scary `--dangerously-skip-permissions` flag and is meant for sandboxed or disposable environments (like CI containers), not for a session on your workstation with real credentials lying around. The second option inverts the two modes. They are certainly not aliases — the whole point of having both is the difference in blast radius. And either mode can be set for a session regardless of where settings live; the modes themselves aren't tied to user-vs-project scoping. Remember the model only ever emits tool-use requests; permission modes decide how much of that the harness honors without you in the loop."}, {"id": "2.3-L1-01", "topic": "2.3", "topicName": "Keyboard shortcuts", "difficulty": 1, "question": "Claude is midway through a long response and heading in the wrong direction. Which key stops it so you can redirect?", "options": ["Esc", "Ctrl+Z", "Ctrl+D", "F5"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Esc interrupts Claude mid-turn: the current response or tool call stops, but the work completed so far in the session is kept, and you can immediately type a correction. This is the everyday steering tool — interrupting early is far cheaper than letting a wrong approach run to completion, because every subsequent turn resends the whole (now longer) conversation. The others do different things: Ctrl+Z suspends the entire Claude Code process to your shell (Unix job control — you'd run `fg` to come back), Ctrl+D exits the session, and F5 isn't bound. Worth knowing the neighbors too: Ctrl+C clears the input or exits, and double-Esc on an empty prompt opens the rewind menu to restore an earlier point in the session rather than just stopping the current one."}, {"id": "2.3-L1-02", "topic": "2.3", "topicName": "Keyboard shortcuts", "difficulty": 1, "question": "Which keyboard shortcut cycles between permission modes (default → acceptEdits → plan …) during an interactive session?", "options": ["Ctrl+P", "Tab", "Shift+Tab", "Ctrl+M"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Shift+Tab cycles through the permission modes available in your session — default (shown as 'Manual' in the mode indicator), acceptEdits, plan, and any extra modes you've enabled. It's the fastest way to hop into plan mode before a risky request or into auto-accept for a long mechanical refactor, without touching configuration files. The distractors are all real-feeling but wrong: plain Tab is used for completions and accepting suggestions, Ctrl+P moves through command history (like the Up arrow), and Ctrl+M isn't a mode toggle. A useful mental separation: shortcuts like this only change harness-side behavior — what the harness will auto-approve — and have zero effect on the API request itself, so the model doesn't 'know' which mode you're in except through what the harness tells it (for example, the constraints of plan mode)."}, {"id": "2.3-L2-01", "topic": "2.3", "topicName": "Keyboard shortcuts", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You type `!git status` at the Claude Code prompt. What happens?", "options": ["Claude is asked to run `git status` on your behalf and must first request permission for the Bash tool as with any command", "The input is rejected because `!` is reserved for history expansion", "Claude Code opens a persistent interactive subshell that replaces the session prompt until you type exit to return", "The command runs directly in your shell, and its output is added to the session so Claude can see it"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The `!` prefix enters shell mode: the command executes immediately on your machine without going through the model at all, and both the command and its output are appended to the session context. That last part is the trick worth knowing — because the harness resends the whole conversation each turn, the output becomes visible to Claude on your next prompt, so `!` is a fast way to feed it fresh ground truth (test results, git state) without asking it to run the command itself. The first option describes the normal, non-prefixed path where the model chooses to call Bash and the permission system gets involved; `!` skips both the model and the prompt since you ran it yourself. History expansion with `!` is disabled by default, and there's no session-replacing subshell — each `!` line is one command."}, {"id": "2.3-L2-02", "topic": "2.3", "topicName": "Keyboard shortcuts", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You want to write a multi-line prompt in Claude Code, and it must work in any terminal without extra configuration. Which input inserts a newline instead of submitting?", "options": ["Shift+Enter, which is guaranteed native in every terminal", "Typing a backslash and then pressing Enter", "Ctrl+Enter, the universal newline binding", "Pressing Enter twice quickly"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The backslash-then-Enter 'quick escape' works in all terminals with no setup (Ctrl+J is the other universally available option). The reason terminal support even matters: many terminals send Shift+Enter as a plain Enter, so Claude Code can't distinguish them without help. Shift+Enter does work natively in a good list of modern terminals (iTerm2, Ghostty, Kitty, Windows Terminal, and others), but in environments like the VS Code integrated terminal you first run `/terminal-setup` to install the binding — so 'guaranteed everywhere' is exactly what it isn't. Option+Enter is another route on macOS but requires configuring Option as Meta. Ctrl+Enter isn't the binding, and double-Enter would just submit your first line. This is worth knowing cold, because a submitted half-prompt still becomes a full turn — resent conversation, real tokens, real cost."}, {"id": "2.4-L2-01", "topic": "2.4", "topicName": "Useful built-in skills & commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You run `/init` in a repository that Claude Code has never seen. What does it do?", "options": ["Explores the codebase and generates a CLAUDE.md file documenting the project for future sessions", "Runs `git init`, writes a language-appropriate .gitignore, and creates an initial commit on your behalf", "Creates the ~/.claude directory scaffold, including a default global settings.json and an empty skills folder", "Registers the project with your Anthropic account so usage is billed to it"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "`/init` has Claude explore the repository — build commands, structure, conventions — and write what it learns into a CLAUDE.md at the project root. The payoff comes from how CLAUDE.md is used afterward: the harness injects it into the prompt of every future session in that project, so it's the durable memory layer in an otherwise stateless system. Nothing lives server-side between sessions; a file the harness re-reads and resends is how 'the project's knowledge' persists. It is unrelated to `git init` — Claude Code doesn't initialize repositories as part of `/init` — and `~/.claude/` global scaffolding is created by the CLI itself, not this command. The billing option doesn't correspond to anything real; and for a Bedrock user there's no Anthropic account registration in the picture at all — usage lands on the AWS bill."}, {"id": "2.4-L2-02", "topic": "2.4", "topicName": "Useful built-in skills & commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What does the built-in `/review` command do?", "options": ["Replays the current session's transcript so you can review what Claude did", "Shows a diff of every file Claude has modified since the session started", "Has Claude review a pull request and give feedback on the changes", "Audits your settings.json and permission rules for risky configuration"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "`/review` points Claude at a pull request: it fetches the PR's changes (typically via the `gh` CLI), reads the diff in context, and produces review feedback — the kind of task that plays to an agent's strength of reading many files to judge one change. It's a packaged workflow: you could prompt the same thing by hand, but the command bundles the steps. The distractors each map to a different real surface, which is what makes them tempting: reviewing what Claude did in-session is the transcript view (Ctrl+O), file changes are visible through git itself since Claude edits your working tree directly, and configuration checks are territory for `/doctor` and `/permissions`, not `/review`. Provider-wise this is fully neutral — the review runs through whatever model endpoint you've configured, Bedrock or Anthropic, since the command is just harness-side orchestration."}, {"id": "2.4-L2-03", "topic": "2.4", "topicName": "Useful built-in skills & commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What is the key operational difference between a skill and a slash command in Claude Code?", "options": ["Skills run locally inside the harness process, while slash commands are executed server-side by the API before the response streams back", "Slash commands are built-in and cannot be user-defined, whereas skills are the only supported way for users to add custom behavior", "Skills can call tools but slash commands cannot", "A slash command is invoked explicitly by the user; a skill can be triggered by the model when its description matches the task"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The distinction is who pulls the trigger. A slash command fires only when you type it — deterministic, user-driven. A skill advertises itself via a short description that sits in the model's context; when the model judges that the current task matches, it loads the skill's full instructions and follows them. That's why writing a good skill description matters so much: it's the trigger condition. The mechanism also explains the cost profile — descriptions are always carried in the resent context, bodies only on activation. The first option misplaces the machinery: both are harness-side; the API server executes nothing (a useful invariant on Bedrock and Anthropic alike). The second is false because custom slash commands are fully supported alongside skills. And both paths can lead to tool use — a command's prompt or a skill's instructions can each have Claude invoke tools."}, {"id": "2.4-L2-04", "topic": "2.4", "topicName": "Useful built-in skills & commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You want Claude to permanently remember a project convention ('we use tabs, never spaces') across all future sessions. What's the supported way?", "options": ["Tell Claude once in a session; the model updates its weights with your preference", "Add it to the project's CLAUDE.md, for example via the /memory command", "Set it as an environment variable so the API includes it in every request", "Enable the conversation-sync option so future sessions download past chats"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Persistent memory in Claude Code is a file. CLAUDE.md (project-level, or user-level in ~/.claude/ for personal preferences) is read by the harness and injected into the prompt at the start of every session — that's the entire mechanism. `/memory` opens these files for editing so you don't have to hunt for them. The first option is the classic misconception this topic exists to kill: model weights are frozen; nothing you say in a session trains the model, and the API keeps no state between requests — every turn is a full resend of what the harness chose to include. Environment variables configure the harness (model choice, Bedrock routing) but their values aren't injected as instructions into prompts. And there is no conversation-sync feature; past transcripts exist locally and can be resumed, but new sessions don't automatically inherit old conversations — CLAUDE.md is the designed channel for durable knowledge."}, {"id": "2.5-L2-01", "topic": "2.5", "topicName": "Invocation & flags", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What does running `claude -p \"summarize the failing tests\"` do differently from just running `claude`?", "options": ["It runs headless: processes the single prompt, prints the result to stdout, and exits instead of opening the interactive UI", "It runs the prompt in plan mode, printing a proposed step-by-step plan to stdout for your approval before anything executes", "It runs the prompt with elevated permissions, skipping all confirmation prompts", "It pipes the prompt into the most recently used session for this directory and reattaches your terminal to that conversation"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "`-p` (`--print`) is headless mode: Claude Code takes the prompt from the command line, runs the full agentic loop — including tool calls — non-interactively, writes the final result to stdout, and exits. That makes it composable in shells and CI: pipe input in, capture output, chain with `--output-format json` for machine-readable results. The full harness machinery still applies (same context assembly, same permission rules — which is why CI usage often pairs it with explicit `--allowedTools`). The 'p' tempts people toward 'plan', but plan mode is a permission mode (`--permission-mode plan`), not this flag. It doesn't elevate permissions — that misconception would make `-p` dangerous, and it's the separate, deliberately verbose `--dangerously-skip-permissions` flag. And it starts a fresh conversation unless you explicitly combine it with `--continue` or `--resume`."}, {"id": "2.5-L2-02", "topic": "2.5", "topicName": "Invocation & flags", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What is the difference between `claude --continue` and `claude --resume`?", "options": ["--continue reopens a session that crashed or was killed mid-turn, recovering its state from disk; --resume reopens one you exited cleanly with Ctrl+D", "--continue keeps the conversation history but resets permission decisions to your settings defaults; --resume preserves both the conversation and the grants", "--continue loads the most recent conversation in the current directory; --resume lets you pick a specific past session by ID, name, or from a picker", "They are aliases; --resume is simply the long form of --continue"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "`--continue` (`-c`) is the zero-decision path: it reopens the most recent conversation for the directory you're in. `--resume` (`-r`) is the targeted path: give it a session ID or name, or run it bare to get an interactive picker of past sessions. Both work because every session's conversation is persisted locally as a transcript (JSONL under ~/.claude/) — resuming means the harness reloads that record and resends it as the conversation history on your next turn. Nothing was ever stored server-side, so 'resuming' is purely a local-file operation; this is the stateless loop again, and it behaves identically on Bedrock and the Anthropic API. They aren't aliases, the clean-vs-crashed distinction doesn't exist (either can reopen either), and neither flag touches permission state — permissions re-resolve from your settings whenever a session starts."}, {"id": "2.5-L2-03", "topic": "2.5", "topicName": "Invocation & flags", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What does setting the environment variable CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1 actually change?", "options": ["It downloads a distilled copy of the Claude model weights into your AWS account so the CLI can run inference locally through SageMaker", "The harness routes its model API calls to Amazon Bedrock, authenticated via your AWS credentials, instead of the Anthropic API", "It switches the CLI binary to a special AWS build with different commands", "It enables AWS-specific tools inside the session, such as an S3 browser and a CloudWatch log reader for debugging deployed services"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The flag changes one thing: the destination and signing of API requests. With it set, the same harness sends its (Anthropic-shaped) requests to the Bedrock endpoint in your configured AWS region, signed with credentials from the standard AWS chain — env vars, a profile, an IAM role. Everything above that layer is untouched: same CLI, same slash commands, same tools, same permission system, and the same Claude model weights answering — Bedrock is a different door to the same model, hosted by AWS. That's why the weights-download option is wrong twice over: no weights ever leave the provider, on either path. There's no separate AWS build of the CLI, and no bonus AWS tools appear in the session. Practical corollaries: `/login` becomes irrelevant, model selection uses Bedrock model IDs or inference profiles, and cost shows up on the AWS bill rather than an Anthropic console."}, {"id": "2.5-L2-04", "topic": "2.5", "topicName": "Invocation & flags", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You're scripting Claude Code in CI with `claude -p` and want machine-readable output. Which values does `--output-format` accept?", "options": ["yaml, json, and xml", "plain, rich, and raw", "markdown, html, and json", "text, json, and stream-json"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "`--output-format` in print mode accepts `text` (the default final answer as plain text), `json` (a single structured result including metadata like cost and session ID — the one you usually want in CI, parsed with something like jq), and `stream-json` (newline-delimited JSON events emitted as the run progresses, useful for observing the loop live: each tool call and message appears as its own event). The stream variant is a window into the agentic loop itself — you can watch the turn-by-turn tool round-trips that are normally rendered as UI. The other option sets are plausible-sounding format families that the flag doesn't offer; markdown is how Claude tends to write text, not an output-format value. This is provider-neutral: output formatting happens entirely in the harness after responses come back, so it works identically whether the endpoint behind it is Bedrock or the Anthropic API."}, {"id": "2.1-L1-03", "topic": "2.1", "topicName": "Slash commands", "difficulty": 1, "question": "You can't remember what commands are available in your Claude Code session. Which slash command lists them?", "options": ["/commands", "/help", "/list", "/menu"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "`/help` lists the available commands. There's also a shortcut into the same surface: simply typing `/` opens a menu of everything invocable — built-in commands, bundled and user-authored skills, and commands contributed by plugins or MCP servers — so in practice you often discover commands just by starting to type one. The other three options are invented names that sound plausible but don't exist. Worth noting where this all happens: anything starting with `/` is intercepted by the harness before prompt assembly, and a purely informational command like `/help` executes fixed local logic in the CLI process — no API call is made, no tokens are spent, and it behaves identically whether your session is pointed at Bedrock or the Anthropic API."}, {"id": "2.1-L1-04", "topic": "2.1", "topicName": "Slash commands", "difficulty": 1, "question": "You run Claude Code against AWS Bedrock and want a quick sanity check: which provider, region, and model did this session actually resolve? Which command shows that?", "options": ["/config", "/context", "/login", "/status"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "`/status` reports the session's vitals: version, working directory, active model, and — the part that matters most on Bedrock — which provider and region the session resolved, and where the region setting came from. Since Bedrock configuration is spread across environment variables, AWS profiles, and settings files, `/status` is the fastest way to confirm what actually won. The distractors each do something real but different: `/config` opens the settings interface for changing configuration rather than summarizing what resolved, `/context` visualizes how the context window is being spent per category, and `/login` manages sign-in to an Anthropic account — the one command a Bedrock user never needs, because auth there comes from the AWS credential chain, not a harness-held login."}, {"id": "2.1-L2-03", "topic": "2.1", "topicName": "Slash commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Your session feels heavy and you suspect something is eating the context window. Which command shows a breakdown of how the window is being spent — system prompt, tools, skills, messages?", "options": ["/context", "/usage", "/status", "/doctor"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "`/context` visualizes context-window consumption per category — system prompt, tool schemas, skill listings, conversation messages — which is exactly the view you need when a session feels heavy, because it shows what the harness is actually assembling and resending every turn. The tempting confusion is `/usage` (alias `/cost`): that reports token consumption over time, i.e. what you've spent, not how the window is currently allocated — a cumulative meter versus a snapshot of the payload. `/status` covers session vitals like model, provider, and region but not a per-category window breakdown, and `/doctor` is a setup health check (it does estimate what your skill listing costs in context, but it isn't the context visualizer). Remember why the breakdown matters: everything in that chart rides along in every subsequent request."}, {"id": "2.1-L2-04", "topic": "2.1", "topicName": "Slash commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Claude went down a bad path several turns ago, making edits along the way. What does `/rewind` do?", "options": ["Reverts the single most recent file edit but keeps the conversation as-is", "Replays the conversation from the beginning, re-executing each tool call", "Restores both your code and the conversation to an earlier checkpoint you select", "Deletes your last message from the transcript while leaving all file changes in place"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "`/rewind` (also reachable via double-`Esc` on an empty prompt) rolls the session back to a checkpoint — and it restores both halves of the state: the conversation the harness will resend next turn, and the code changes made since that point. That pairing is the whole value: if it only trimmed the message array, your working tree would still contain the bad edits, and if it only reverted files, Claude's context would still 'believe' the edits exist and act on that. The one-edit-undo and delete-last-message options describe narrower operations than what rewind does, and nothing replays or re-executes tool calls — in a stateless loop there is no server-side session to fast-forward; the harness simply reconstructs an earlier local state and resumes resending from there."}, {"id": "2.2-L1-03", "topic": "2.2", "topicName": "Modes", "difficulty": 1, "question": "In the default permission mode (shown as 'Manual'), what happens when Claude wants to read a file versus edit a file?", "options": ["Reads run without asking; edits and non-read-only commands prompt for your approval", "Both reads and edits prompt for approval every single time, since even a file read could expose sensitive data", "Both reads and edits run without asking; only shell commands and network requests trigger a prompt", "Nothing runs at all until you approve a written plan"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Default mode draws the line at mutation: read-only actions (reading files, searching) run freely, while file edits and non-read-only commands pause for a permission prompt. That's the sensible baseline — reads can't damage anything, so prompting for them would only add friction, while every state-changing action gets a human check. The prompt-for-everything option overstates it, and the reads-and-edits-free option describes something closer to acceptEdits, where edits are pre-approved. The plan-first option describes plan mode, a different mode entirely. The underlying mechanism is worth internalizing early: the model only ever emits tool-use requests as structured text; the harness on your machine decides whether to execute each one, and the permission mode is simply the policy applied at that decision point."}, {"id": "2.2-L1-04", "topic": "2.2", "topicName": "Modes", "difficulty": 1, "question": "Mid-session, how can you tell which permission mode is currently active?", "options": ["Run /permissions, which displays the active mode at the top", "You can't check mid-session; the mode is only shown when the session starts", "The status bar shows an indicator like '⏸ plan mode on' or '⏵⏵ accept edits on'", "Ask Claude which mode it's in and it will report the current setting"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The status bar always displays the active mode — indicators like '⏸ plan mode on' or '⏵⏵ accept edits on' — so a glance tells you how much the harness will auto-approve, and `Shift+Tab` cycles to a different mode from there. The startup-only option is wrong precisely because modes change mid-session all the time; a persistent indicator exists for that reason. `/permissions` manages allow/deny rules, a layer that composes with modes rather than displaying them. Asking Claude is the instructive wrong answer: the permission mode is harness-side policy applied when tool calls are executed, and (plan mode partly excepted) it isn't sent to the model as information — so Claude has no reliable knowledge of which mode you're in and could only guess."}, {"id": "2.2-L2-03", "topic": "2.2", "topicName": "Modes", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Your organization's settings include a deny rule for `Bash(rm -rf *)`. You start a session with `--dangerously-skip-permissions` (bypassPermissions mode). Claude attempts exactly that command. What happens?", "options": ["The command runs — bypassPermissions overrides all configured rules", "A permission prompt appears, since deny rules downgrade bypass mode to default", "The command runs but is logged for later review by the organization", "The command is blocked — deny rules apply in every mode, including bypassPermissions"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Deny rules (and explicit ask rules) are enforced in every permission mode, including bypassPermissions. The mental model: the mode sets the default answer to 'may I?', while rules are specific exceptions that always win. That's what lets an organization hard-block dangerous commands regardless of how permissive an individual session is — the guarantee would be worthless if a flag could switch it off. The 'overrides all rules' option misreads what bypass skips: it removes the prompting layer (everything not otherwise denied runs without asking), not the rule layer. No prompt appears either — bypass mode doesn't prompt, and a deny rule blocks outright rather than asking. Note that the inverse asymmetry also holds: allow rules are meaningless in bypass, since everything is already approved; only deny retains force everywhere. All of this is harness-side and provider-neutral."}, {"id": "2.2-L2-04", "topic": "2.2", "topicName": "Modes", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You want every Claude Code session in a shared repository to start in plan mode, for all teammates who clone it. What's the supported way?", "options": ["Add a line to the project's CLAUDE.md instructing Claude to always plan before acting", "Set permissions.defaultMode to \"plan\" in the project's .claude/settings.json", "Export CLAUDE_CODE_DEFAULT_MODE=plan in the repository's setup script", "Have each teammate alias claude to 'claude --permission-mode plan' in their shell"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "`permissions.defaultMode` in a settings file sets the starting mode, and putting it in the project's checked-in `.claude/settings.json` makes it apply to everyone who works in that repository — which is exactly the scoping the scenario asks for (your personal `~/.claude/settings.json` would cover only you). The CLAUDE.md option confuses instructions with enforcement: text in context is a request the model may follow, whereas a permission mode is harness code that reliably gates execution — this distinction is why modes exist at all. There is no `CLAUDE_CODE_DEFAULT_MODE` environment variable. The shell alias would genuinely work per person, but it's manual per-machine setup that lives outside the repo — the settings file is the designed, shareable mechanism, and `--permission-mode` remains the per-invocation override on top of it."}, {"id": "2.3-L1-03", "topic": "2.3", "topicName": "Keyboard shortcuts", "difficulty": 1, "question": "You type `@` at the start of your input while writing a prompt in Claude Code. What does it do?", "options": ["Mentions another running agent by name so it joins the current session as a collaborator", "Marks the message as high priority for the model", "Opens file path autocomplete so you can drop a file reference into your prompt", "Sends the prompt straight to the model API endpoint, skipping the harness's prompt assembly step"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "`@` triggers file path autocomplete: you pick a path and a reference to that file lands in your prompt, which is the quickest way to point Claude at a specific file without typing the full path or asking it to search. It belongs to a small family of prefix characters that change what the input line means: `/` invokes a command or skill, `!` runs a shell command directly, and `?` toggles the shortcut help panel. There's no agent-mention or priority-flag feature behind `@`, and nothing can skip the harness — every prompt goes through prompt assembly before it becomes an API request, because the harness is the only thing that talks to the API. These prefixes are pure harness features; the model only ever sees their results in the assembled context."}, {"id": "2.3-L1-04", "topic": "2.3", "topicName": "Keyboard shortcuts", "difficulty": 1, "question": "You submitted a long prompt with a typo and want to recall it, fix it, and resubmit. Which key brings back your previous prompt?", "options": ["Up arrow", "Ctrl+Z", "Ctrl+O", "Page Up"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The `Up` arrow steps back through your prompt history — press it once to recall the last prompt, edit, and resubmit. History is stored per working directory, so the prompts you recall belong to the project you're in. One subtlety once your drafts get long: in a multi-row input the arrows first move the cursor within the text, and only step into history once the cursor is at the top or bottom row. For finding an old prompt by content rather than by stepping, `Ctrl+R` opens reverse search. The distractors do other things: `Ctrl+Z` suspends the whole Claude Code process to your shell (Unix job control — `fg` brings it back), `Ctrl+O` toggles the transcript viewer, and `Page Up` scrolls output rather than recalling input."}, {"id": "2.3-L2-03", "topic": "2.3", "topicName": "Keyboard shortcuts", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Claude has started a long test suite through its Bash tool, and you want it to continue working on other things while the tests run. Which shortcut does that?", "options": ["Esc — the command keeps running while Claude moves on", "Ctrl+B — sends the running command to the background", "Ctrl+Z — backgrounds the command inside the session", "Ctrl+D — detaches the command from the session"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "`Ctrl+B` backgrounds the running Bash command: it keeps executing while Claude proceeds with other work, and the results become available to the session when it finishes — you can see background shells listed under `/tasks`. One practical wrinkle: tmux users press `Ctrl+B` twice, because it's the tmux prefix key and the first press is swallowed by tmux. The distractors misapply neighboring keys: `Esc` interrupts — it stops the current response or tool call rather than letting it continue in parallel; `Ctrl+Z` is shell job control that suspends the entire Claude Code process, not one command inside it; and `Ctrl+D` exits the session. Backgrounding is pure harness-side process management on your machine — nothing about the API request changes, so it works identically on Bedrock and the Anthropic API."}, {"id": "2.3-L2-04", "topic": "2.3", "topicName": "Keyboard shortcuts", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You want to re-find a long prompt you wrote earlier in this project without arrow-keying through everything. What does Ctrl+R do in Claude Code?", "options": ["Re-runs your most recent prompt exactly as submitted, reusing the same permission approvals", "Redraws the terminal screen after visual corruption", "Rewinds the session, restoring conversation and file state to the checkpoint you select", "Opens reverse history search: type to filter, Ctrl+R again cycles older matches"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "`Ctrl+R` opens reverse history search over your prompt history, shell-style: type a fragment to filter, press `Ctrl+R` again to cycle to older matches, then `Tab` or `Esc` to accept the match into the input for editing — or `Enter` to accept and submit immediately. Since prompt history is stored per working directory, the search covers your past prompts in this project. The distractors borrow from neighboring conventions: some shells use `Ctrl+R` differently, but here it doesn't re-run anything — accepted history lands in the input unless you submit it; screen redraw is `Ctrl+L` territory in terminals, not this binding; and rewinding to a checkpoint is `/rewind` or double-`Esc` on an empty prompt — a session-state operation, not an input-history one. Like all shortcuts, this is harness-side and provider-neutral."}, {"id": "2.4-L2-05", "topic": "2.4", "topicName": "Useful built-in skills & commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Both `/clear` and `/code-review` live behind the `/` prefix, yet `/code-review` consumes tokens and `/clear` costs nothing. Why?", "options": ["/clear is a built-in command running deterministic harness code locally; /code-review is a bundled skill that injects a prompt and has the model do the work", "Both are bundled skills, but /clear's prompt is short enough to fall under the harness's free threshold of roughly a thousand tokens per invocation", "/code-review is routed to a dedicated review endpoint with separate per-review pricing", "/clear does call the model once to confirm the reset, but with an empty conversation, and a fully cached empty request is free on both Bedrock and the Anthropic API"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The `/` menu mixes two different species. Built-in commands like `/clear` and `/config` execute fixed logic inside the CLI process — same input, same behavior, and typically no API call at all, hence no tokens. Bundled skills like `/code-review`, `/debug`, and `/loop` are prompt-based: invoking one injects prepared instructions into the conversation and the model orchestrates the work with its tools — real agentic workload, with the model's variability and cost, billed to your AWS account when you're on Bedrock. A skill's body also stays in context for the rest of the session, a recurring cost in every resend. Quick tell: needs judgment → skill; manipulates session state → command. There's no free-tier threshold, no separate review endpoint (all requests go to your configured model endpoint), and `/clear` makes no model call — caching never enters into it."}, {"id": "2.4-L2-06", "topic": "2.4", "topicName": "Useful built-in skills & commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You've made local changes and want Claude to review your uncommitted working diff for bugs before you commit. Which built-in do you invoke?", "options": ["/review", "/code-review", "/security-review", "/verify"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "`/code-review` is the bundled skill that reviews your local working diff — the pre-commit checkpoint for bugs and cleanup opportunities. The review family is easy to conflate because the names overlap but the targets differ: `/review` points at a GitHub pull request, not your local changes; `/security-review` examines the pending changes on the current branch but specifically for security vulnerabilities, a narrower lens than a general bug review; and `/verify` is a separate check, not the diff-review skill. In current versions, `/code-review` and `/verify` run only when explicitly invoked, so these token-hungry passes never fire on their own. And because these are skills rather than commands, a review is a genuine model workload — reading the diff, chasing context across files — billed like any other agentic work through whichever provider your session resolved."}, {"id": "2.4-L2-07", "topic": "2.4", "topicName": "Useful built-in skills & commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Your team wants `/code-review` to run its own review checklist instead of the stock behavior, for everyone working in the repository. How do you do it?", "options": ["Edit the bundled skill's file inside the Claude Code installation directory", "You can't shadow bundled skill names; publish the checklist under a distinct name like /team-review instead", "Create a skill named code-review in the project's .claude/skills/ — same-named skills replace bundled ones", "Set skillOverrides in the project's settings.json to point the bundled code-review skill at your own checklist file"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Bundled skills sit at the lowest layer of name resolution: a skill with the same name at the enterprise, personal (`~/.claude/skills/`), or project (`.claude/skills/`) level replaces the bundled one. Dropping a `code-review` skill into the project's `.claude/skills/` and committing it means everyone's `/code-review` now runs the team checklist — a clean way to standardize review criteria in a repo. Editing files inside the installation is the wrong layer (changes would be fragile and lost on update; the override mechanism exists so you never need to). Bundled names absolutely can be shadowed, so a new name isn't required. The `skillOverrides` distractor is the near-miss: it's a real setting, but it hides a skill or collapses it to name-only in the model's skill listing to save context — it doesn't substitute your implementation. Note the contrast: built-in commands (like `/clear`) aren't override-able files at all; they're part of the CLI."}, {"id": "2.4-L2-08", "topic": "2.4", "topicName": "Useful built-in skills & commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What does the `/doctor` command do?", "options": ["Tests connectivity to your configured model endpoint and reports the remaining Bedrock invocation quota for your region", "Scans your codebase for bugs and proposes fixes", "Repairs corrupted transcript JSONL files under ~/.claude/ so old sessions can be resumed after a crash", "Checks installation health and settings problems, including an estimate of what your skill listing costs in context"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "`/doctor` is the setup checkup: it verifies installation health, flags problems in your settings, and estimates how much of your context window the skill listing consumes — a useful nudge, since every skill description rides along in each resent request whether or not the skill ever fires. It's notable as the one built-in that survives even when `disableBundledSkills` turns the rest of the bundled surface off. The distractors assign it neighboring jobs it doesn't have: provider-side concerns like Bedrock quotas live in AWS tooling, not the harness (`/status` covers which provider and region resolved, but not quota); reviewing code for bugs is the `/code-review` skill's territory; and transcript files don't have a repair command — they're plain JSONL on disk. Think of `/doctor` as diagnostics for the harness itself, not for your code or your provider."}, {"id": "2.5-L2-05", "topic": "2.5", "topicName": "Invocation & flags", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What's the difference between `--append-system-prompt` and `--system-prompt`?", "options": ["They're aliases; --system-prompt is the newer preferred spelling", "--system-prompt injects your text into the first API request only and drops it afterward, while --append-system-prompt re-injects the text on every turn of the session", "--append-system-prompt appends your text to the project's CLAUDE.md on disk so it persists across sessions, while --system-prompt injects text per request without touching files", "--append-system-prompt adds your text after the default system prompt; --system-prompt replaces the entire thing, dropping Claude Code's own operating instructions"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The two flags differ in blast radius. `--append-system-prompt` adds your text to the end of the default system prompt, keeping the harness's tool instructions and operating guidance intact — the right choice for adding policy or persona on top of normal behavior. `--system-prompt` replaces the whole thing: powerful, and usually wrong for everyday use, because you lose the instructions that make Claude Code behave like Claude Code. They're not aliases, and neither is turn-scoped — the system prompt is part of the stable prefix the harness assembles into every request, so both flags affect every turn of the session. Neither touches CLAUDE.md, which is a separate context-file mechanism. One caching corollary: since you're editing the stable prefix, first use means a fresh cache write; identical on Bedrock and the Anthropic API."}, {"id": "2.5-L2-06", "topic": "2.5", "topicName": "Invocation & flags", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Your team runs Claude Code on Bedrock and wants `--model sonnet` (the alias) to resolve to one specific inference profile for everyone, so the alias never silently drifts to a model your account can't invoke. Which environment variable pins it?", "options": ["ANTHROPIC_MODEL", "AWS_BEDROCK_MODEL_ID", "ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL", "CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "`ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL` (with sibling `_OPUS_` and `_HAIKU_` variants) pins what the corresponding alias resolves to — set it to a Bedrock inference profile ID or ARN and `--model sonnet` means that exact model for everyone, which is precisely the team-rollout concern in the question. The near-miss is `ANTHROPIC_MODEL`: it sets the primary model directly, but it doesn't define the alias mapping — someone typing `--model sonnet` would still get the default resolution. `AWS_BEDROCK_MODEL_ID` isn't a Claude Code variable at all, and `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK` only routes the API layer to Bedrock without saying anything about which model. Practical tip: these don't have to be shell exports — the `env` block of a settings file (like a project's `.claude/settings.json`) sets them per project, and `/status` confirms what resolved."}, {"id": "2.5-L2-07", "topic": "2.5", "topicName": "Invocation & flags", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You resume a past session with `claude --resume <id> --fork-session`. What does adding `--fork-session` change?", "options": ["It resumes the session inside a separate git worktree so file changes stay isolated from your main checkout", "It continues the conversation under a new session ID, leaving the original transcript untouched", "It spawns a subagent that works on the old session while you start a fresh one", "It copies the transcript to a shared team location so a teammate can resume the session from their machine"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "`--fork-session` resumes the conversation under a new session ID: your continued work is recorded as a fresh transcript, and the original session's record is left exactly as it was — so you can branch an exploration without contaminating the point you might want to return to. Why this is cheap is the keystone in action: a 'session' is only a local transcript file plus whatever the harness resends as history; forking is essentially copying a file and continuing from it — nothing server-side exists to duplicate, on Bedrock or the Anthropic API alike. The distractors graft on unrelated machinery: worktree isolation is a git-level technique used in multi-agent workflows, not this flag; no subagent is involved — you get one ordinary session; and transcripts live locally under `~/.claude/projects/`, with no shared-location copy feature behind this flag."}, {"id": "2.5-L2-08", "topic": "2.5", "topicName": "Invocation & flags", "difficulty": 2, "question": "When Claude Code runs against Bedrock, how does it decide which AWS region to send requests to?", "options": ["AWS_REGION first, then AWS_DEFAULT_REGION, then the active AWS profile's configured region", "It defaults to us-east-1, Bedrock's home region, unless you pass a --region flag at startup", "The region is taken from the model ID string in your settings.json", "Bedrock routes each request automatically to the geographically nearest region with spare capacity"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Region resolution follows the standard AWS chain: `AWS_REGION` wins if set, then `AWS_DEFAULT_REGION`, then the region configured on the active AWS profile. This mirrors how credentials resolve — Claude Code leans on the AWS SDK's conventions rather than inventing its own — and `/status` shows both the resolved region and where the setting came from, which is the first thing to check when requests land somewhere unexpected. There's no `--region` flag or hard-coded us-east-1 default in the harness. The model-ID distractor has a grain of truth worth untangling: cross-region inference profile IDs carry a geographic prefix (like `us.`) that governs where inference may run, but that's not how the harness picks the endpoint region for your requests. And Bedrock doesn't auto-route to 'nearest capacity' — your requests go to the region your configuration resolves, and regional model availability is exactly why that choice matters."}, {"id": "3.1-L2-01", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 2, "question": "The harness assembles a fresh API request every model call. In the canonical assembly order, which piece lands LAST?", "options": ["The tool definitions, so the model reads them with the freshest context", "The system prompt, because instructions should be closest to the answer", "The current turn's newest content — your latest message or tool result", "CLAUDE.md, since project memory changes most often"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Assembly follows a stability gradient: tools param first, then the system param (with CLAUDE.md and env info at or near it), then the messages array, ending with the newest, most volatile content — your latest input or the latest tool_result. The order exists for prompt caching: the cache matches a prefix from the start of the request, so putting the most stable material first makes the longest possible head a cache hit on every call. The distractors invert this — tool definitions are the MOST stable layer and go first, and CLAUDE.md is re-injected in the stable head each request, not appended at the tail. Remember the keystone: the API is stateless, so this entire stack is rebuilt and re-sent every single call, and only the ordering keeps that resend affordable."}, {"id": "3.1-L2-02", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Where does the content of CLAUDE.md actually end up when the harness builds a request?", "options": ["In the stable head of the request, at or near the system layer, re-injected on every call", "In the messages array as your first user message, sent once at session start", "It stays on disk — the model is given the file path and reads it on demand", "In the tools parameter, as a schema the model can query"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "CLAUDE.md is request-shaping memory: the harness injects its content into the stable, cacheable head of the request (at or near the system parameter) on every single call. That is why it survives compaction — it was never part of the compacted history; it is re-added fresh each assembly. The 'sent once at session start' distractor is the tempting one, but it contradicts the stateless loop: nothing is 'sent once' to a server that stores nothing — anything the model must keep seeing must be re-sent every call. And the model never reads files on its own; it can only emit tool_use requests that the harness executes. This works identically on Bedrock and the Anthropic API — assembly is entirely harness-side."}, {"id": "3.1-L3-01", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Why is the tools → system → messages ordering of a request load-bearing rather than an arbitrary API convention?", "options": ["The model attends to the request front-to-back, so tool definitions must precede any message that invokes them or the call is rejected as malformed", "Prompt caching matches a prefix, so most-stable-first maximizes the cached head; anything volatile placed early would invalidate everything after it", "The API rejects requests where the system prompt follows the messages array", "It minimizes bandwidth: the server acknowledges the cached prefix by hash, so the harness re-transmits only the volatile tail on each subsequent call"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Prompt caching operates on prefixes: the server matches the request from its first byte up to a breakpoint and skips recomputing whatever matches. Ordering by stability — tool definitions (most stable), then system prompt and memory, then the volatile conversation tail — means the long stable head hits cache every call, billing around 0.1× instead of 1×. Put anything churning early (a timestamp in the system prompt, a reordering tool list) and every token after it re-bills. The bandwidth distractor is a classic misreading: cached tokens are still transmitted in full on every call; the saving is server-side recomputation, hence cost and latency — never bandwidth. In a stateless loop where the whole conversation is re-sent per call, the stability gradient IS the cache strategy, and the cache is what keeps the quadratic resend economically alive."}, {"id": "3.1-L3-02", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague proposes adding the current timestamp to the top of the system prompt so the model always knows the time. What's the hidden cost?", "options": ["None — the system prompt is free because it is not counted as input tokens", "The model will get confused by seeing different times across turns", "Only a few extra tokens per call — the timestamp itself is tiny, and because the system prompt sits behind a fixed cache breakpoint, the rest of the prefix keeps billing at the ~0.1× cache-read rate", "Every call invalidates the cached prefix from that point on, so the system prompt, and everything ordered after it, re-bills at cache-write rates each turn"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Caching matches a prefix, and a per-call timestamp near the top of the request means the prefix diverges within the first few tokens of every call. Everything after the divergence point — the rest of the system prompt, CLAUDE.md, the entire conversation — stops hitting cache and must be re-processed, with the newly 'stable' portion written back at 1.25× base input price. The 'few extra tokens' answer is the trap: it prices the timestamp itself but misses that cache invalidation is positional, not proportional — a 10-token edit placed early can re-bill hundreds of thousands of tokens behind it. This is exactly why the harness keeps the head still and pushes volatile facts (like dates) into late-positioned injected reminders instead. Same math whether you're billed by Anthropic or through AWS Bedrock."}, {"id": "3.1-L3-03", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A skill-injection log shows matchedSkills=[A, B, C] but injectedSkills=[A]. What does this reveal about how the harness assembles context?", "options": ["Assembly is curation: matching produces candidates, then a selector with a token budget decides what actually enters the request — and drops the rest on purpose", "A bug — every skill whose description matches the prompt must be injected", "B and C were already in the server-side prompt cache from an earlier call, so the selector skipped re-injecting them — the model reads them out of the cached prefix without their tokens being re-sent", "B and C were injected into a subagent's context instead of the main session"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The gap between matched and injected is the whole lesson: assembly is not concatenation of everything relevant, it is selection against a budget. matchedSkills is the candidate set (descriptions that matched your prompt); injectedSkills is what actually entered context; sibling fields like droppedByCap and droppedByBudget name the machinery that cut the rest before the request was built. The cache distractor fails on mechanism — the server-side cache stores computed attention state over whatever prefix was sent; it cannot substitute for content that was never placed in the request, because a stateless model only sees what this call literally contains. The naive model — 'the harness dumps system + tools + memory + history' — is wrong precisely here: there is a curation step, and it can and does leave things out."}, {"id": "3.1-L3-04", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague says: \"The session's .jsonl transcript is exactly the message array that gets sent to the model each turn.\" What's the correction?", "options": ["Correct — the harness streams the transcript file directly to the API", "Wrong in the other direction — the transcript is strictly smaller: it keeps only the user and assistant messages, while every assembled request layers the system prompt, CLAUDE.md, tool schemas, and injected reminders on top of that same conversation", "Wrong — the transcript is a superset with many record types (mode changes, permission switches, file-history snapshots); the harness assembles the message array from a subset of it and adds injected content that was never in it", "Correct, except tool results are stripped before sending"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The transcript is a durable tape that interleaves the conversation with UI and harness state — observed record types include mode, permission-mode, queue-operation, ai-title, attachment, and file-history-snapshot alongside user and assistant records. Only a subset of that becomes the message array. Meanwhile the harness also injects content that never existed in the transcript as authored messages: system-reminder blocks marked isMeta:true, the harness talking to the model over the user channel. So transcript and request are different objects related by an assembly step, in both directions — the transcript holds more record types, and the request holds injected material the transcript's conversation never contained. This distinction is what makes 'resume' a reconstruction rather than a replay, and it is why you cannot audit what the model saw by reading the JSONL alone."}, {"id": "3.1-L4-01", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 4, "question": "In the Claude Messages API request that the harness builds, what is the system prompt, structurally?", "options": ["The first message in the messages array, carrying role \"system\", which the API requires to appear before any user or assistant turn", "A separate top-level parameter of the request, alongside tools and messages — not a message at all", "A special content block type inside the first user message", "A header on the HTTP request, outside the JSON body"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Claude's API takes the system prompt as a dedicated top-level system parameter, distinct from the messages array — unlike some other providers' APIs where 'system' is a message role. The messages array carries only alternating user and assistant roles. This structural fact matters for cache reasoning: the request prefix runs tools param → system param → messages, so the system prompt sits in the stable head, ahead of all conversation content, and editing it invalidates the cached prefix for everything after it. The role-\"system\"-message distractor is the habit imported from other ecosystems; Claude's protocol has exactly two message roles, which is also why tool results must ride inside user messages rather than a third role. Bedrock wraps the same Anthropic-shaped payload, so this anatomy is identical through either door."}, {"id": "3.1-L4-02", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Mid-session, a new tool definition gets added to your request (say, a deferred MCP tool is loaded). Beyond the tokens of the definition itself, what is the subtler cost?", "options": ["The model must re-evaluate all prior tool calls against the new schema", "Nothing subtler — tool definitions sit outside the cacheable span, which starts at the system parameter, so adding one changes only its own token count and leaves the cached prefix untouched", "The harness must restart the session to register the new tool", "The tools param sits earliest in the prefix, so changing it invalidates the cached prefix behind it — the next call re-processes and re-writes what used to be a cheap cache read"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Tool definitions occupy the earliest, most stable slot in the request precisely because they change least. When they do change mid-session, the divergence point moves to almost the very start of the request: the previously cached prefix — system prompt, memory, the whole accumulated conversation — no longer matches, so the next call pays full input rates on it and cache-write (1.25×) to establish the new prefix. On a long session that can mean re-billing hundreds of thousands of tokens for one added tool. That is the real logic behind deferring MCP tool schemas until needed: a busy, dynamic context isn't just bigger, it is less cacheable. The 'excluded from caching' distractor is backwards — tools are the most cached part of all; that's exactly why perturbing them is expensive."}, {"id": "3.2-L2-01", "topic": "3.2", "topicName": "The tool protocol", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What actually IS a tool call, at the protocol level?", "options": ["A tool_use content block — an id, a tool name, and a JSON input — emitted as structured text, after which the model's turn ends", "A remote procedure call the model makes over an open socket to the harness", "A server-side invocation: the API pauses the generation, executes the named tool against its declared schema, and splices the output into the reply before the turn finishes", "A callback function the harness registers with the model at session start"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A tool call is just structured text. The model emits a tool_use content block carrying an id, a name, and a JSON input, and then stops — emitting tool_use is a stop condition for that generation. There is no socket, no callback, no server-side execution: the model cannot do anything, it can only ask. The harness reads the block, executes the tool locally on your machine, and sends the result back as a tool_result block inside a brand-new request. Two text blocks, two separate stateless API calls, correlated by one shared id. Every RPC-flavored distractor imports statefulness the protocol doesn't have; the loop only continues because the harness chooses to send a longer request carrying the answer."}, {"id": "3.2-L2-02", "topic": "3.2", "topicName": "The tool protocol", "difficulty": 2, "question": "When Claude Code 'reads a file' during a session, which component actually touches your disk?", "options": ["The model, which is granted scoped filesystem access for the session", "The API server, which mounts your project directory during the call", "The harness — the local CLI process executes the Read and sends the contents back in the next request", "Nothing touches disk — the file was uploaded into context when the session started"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Only the harness — the Claude Code process on your machine — ever touches your files. The model runs on Anthropic's (or AWS's, if you're on Bedrock) servers and has no filesystem, no network access to you, and no execution ability; it emits a tool_use block asking for the read and halts. The harness performs the read locally and ships the bytes back as a tool_result in the next request. The 'uploaded at session start' distractor fails on the stateless keystone: there is no session store to upload into — the only things the model ever sees are the literal contents of each individual request. This division is also the privacy boundary: your repo stays local, and only what the harness explicitly places into a request ever leaves the machine."}, {"id": "3.2-L3-01", "topic": "3.2", "topicName": "The tool protocol", "difficulty": 3, "question": "In Claude's protocol, what role carries a tool_result back to the model?", "options": ["A dedicated \"tool\" role, distinct from user and assistant", "The \"assistant\" role, since the harness acts on the model's behalf", "The \"system\" role, because tool output is infrastructure, not conversation", "The \"user\" role — tool_result blocks ride inside an ordinary user message"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Claude's API has no tool role — only user and assistant alternate in the messages array (system is a separate top-level parameter, not a role). The tool_result block is placed inside a normal user message, keyed to its request by tool_use_id. Empirically, every tool result in a transcript arrives with role user, without exception. People assume a dedicated role because some other providers' APIs have one; Claude's does not, and the consequence is load-bearing: the output of a Bash command and a sentence you typed arrive on the same channel, wearing the same name. That single-channel design is precisely what makes prompt injection through tool results a structural category rather than an implementation bug — the model has no protocol-level way to know which user bytes a human authored."}, {"id": "3.2-L3-02", "topic": "3.2", "topicName": "The tool protocol", "difficulty": 3, "question": "The model emits three tool_use blocks in a single reply (parallel tool calls). When the results come back, what tells it which result answers which request?", "options": ["The order of the results, which mirrors the order of the requests", "The tool_use_id — each tool_result echoes back the id of the exact request it answers", "The tool name on each result block", "A sequence number the API server assigns to each round-trip"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The id is the entire mechanism. The model mints an id on each tool_use block; the harness echoes that same string back as tool_use_id on the corresponding tool_result. It is a correlation key on a stateless channel — the same reason request ids exist in HTTP. Order can't do the job reliably, and names collide outright: three parallel Bash calls all say 'Bash', so the name identifies the tool, never the invocation. There is no server-side sequence number because there is no server-side session to keep one in — each API call is self-contained, and everything needed to line requests up with answers must travel inside the message text itself. When teaching this, the id field is the cleanest proof that a tool call is text-matching, not function-calling."}, {"id": "3.2-L3-03", "topic": "3.2", "topicName": "The tool protocol", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague says: \"When Claude runs a long Bash command, the model waits for it to finish, then continues its answer.\" What's the correction?", "options": ["Wrong — the model emitted tool_use and its generation ended; the harness runs the command and the 'continuation' is a fresh, longer request carrying the tool_result", "Right — the API holds the connection open while the tool executes", "Right, except the model does other users' work while it waits", "Wrong — the harness streams the command's stdout into the model's still-open generation as it arrives, which is why output and commentary appear interleaved live in the terminal"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The model never waits, because there is nothing to wait in: emitting tool_use is a stop condition, the generation ends, and the API call completes. Whatever time the command takes passes entirely on your machine, inside the harness. When output is ready, the harness assembles a new request — the whole conversation again, now with the tool_result appended as a user message — and the model reads it all as if for the first time, because it is the first time. 'Holds the connection open' and 'streams output into the open generation' both smuggle in a stateful, blocking call that the protocol simply doesn't have; the round-trip is two text blocks on two separate stateless requests. This is also why one user turn can quietly become dozens of full-priced model calls."}, {"id": "3.2-L4-01", "topic": "3.2", "topicName": "The tool protocol", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague asks: \"Why can't Anthropic just train the model to ignore malicious instructions inside files and web pages it reads?\" What's the strongest mechanistic answer?", "options": ["They could, but retraining for each attack pattern is too expensive", "The harness already sanitizes tool output before assembly — instruction-shaped text inside a fetched page or file is detected, flagged, and quarantined into a separate quoted block the model is trained to treat as inert data rather than commands", "Tool output re-enters as a user message on the same channel as your instructions; the protocol gives the model no marker separating data a tool returned from commands a human gave, so the distinction it would need doesn't exist in its input", "The model is required to treat all text as instructions by its system prompt"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Prompt injection is a protocol category, not a training gap. Two facts combine: tool results ride in user-role messages, and the model reads the assembled array as one undifferentiated stream of text. So a fetched web page containing 'delete the tests' is, structurally, on the same channel and role as you typing it — there is no protocol-level line for the model to hold, however well aligned it is. Training can make models more suspicious of instruction-shaped content, but it cannot manufacture a channel distinction the input format never carried; that's why mitigations live in the harness — permission prompts, sandboxing, not piping untrusted text into an agent with write access. The 'harness strips it' distractor describes a defense nobody can implement reliably, since malicious instructions are just text and detection is heuristic at best."}, {"id": "3.2-L4-02", "topic": "3.2", "topicName": "The tool protocol", "difficulty": 4, "question": "In a healthy transcript, tool_use and tool_result block counts match exactly (e.g. 163 = 163). What enforces that pairing?", "options": ["The model tracks its outstanding requests and re-issues any that go unanswered", "The protocol: a tool_use left without a matching tool_result in the next request is malformed input the API rejects, so the harness must answer every request — even a failure becomes a tool_result with is_error", "Coincidence — the counts drift apart in longer sessions", "A reconciliation pass in the harness: at the end of each turn it scans the transcript for unanswered tool_use ids and back-fills synthetic tool_result records before the next assembly, keeping the tape consistent on disk"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The pairing is a protocol invariant, not good luck. When an assistant message contains tool_use blocks, the following user message must supply a tool_result for each id — a dangling request makes the conversation malformed, and the API errors on it. So the harness cannot simply skip a tool that failed, was denied by permissions, or was interrupted: it must still send back a tool_result, using is_error: true or explanatory content to say what happened. That is why the counts match to the block across thousands of calls. The 'model re-issues requests' distractor fails on statelessness — the model has no memory of outstanding requests between calls; it only ever sees what the current message array contains. Understanding this invariant explains oddities like why a rejected permission prompt still produces a visible result message in the conversation."}, {"id": "3.3-L1-01", "topic": "3.3", "topicName": "The special local files", "difficulty": 1, "question": "What is the CLAUDE.md file for?", "options": ["It is Claude Code's changelog — a file the installer refreshes on every update, describing what changed in each release of the CLI you have installed", "It stores your API credentials so the CLI can authenticate", "It is a log Claude writes to summarize what it did in each session", "It holds project instructions and context — memory that Claude Code loads into the model's context so it follows your conventions"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "CLAUDE.md is the memory file: you (or /init) write project conventions, build commands, architecture notes, and rules into it, and the harness injects its content into the model's context on every request. It can exist at several scopes — a user-level file in ~/.claude/ for personal preferences, a project-level file in the repo, even per-directory files — and the scopes stack together rather than replacing each other. It is not written by Claude as a log, and it never holds credentials (auth lives elsewhere — and if you run through AWS Bedrock, auth comes from your AWS credentials, not any Claude Code file). The mental model to start with: the model itself remembers nothing between calls, so anything you want it to always know must live in a file the harness re-sends every time. CLAUDE.md is that file."}, {"id": "3.3-L2-01", "topic": "3.3", "topicName": "The special local files", "difficulty": 2, "question": "For a scalar setting like model, which correctly orders the settings.json layers from highest precedence to lowest?", "options": ["Managed/enterprise > CLI args > local project > project > user (global)", "User (global) > project > local project > CLI args > managed/enterprise", "CLI args > managed/enterprise > user (global) > project > local project", "Project > local project > user (global) > managed/enterprise > CLI args"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The chain runs Managed/enterprise > CLI arguments > local project (.claude/settings.local.json) > project (.claude/settings.json) > user (~/.claude/settings.json), with the highest layer winning for overlapping keys and lower values discarded. The logic is governance-outward: an organization's managed policy must beat anything an individual sets, and an explicit CLI flag beats files because it's the most deliberate per-invocation signal; the user-global file is the broadest default, so it yields to anything more specific. The user-first distractor is the intuitive trap ('my global config is mine, surely it wins') — precedence runs the other way. And remember this override rule is specific to settings.json scalars: CLAUDE.md scopes stack additively and permission rules resolve deny-wins, so 'who wins' has a different answer per surface."}, {"id": "3.3-L2-02", "topic": "3.3", "topicName": "The special local files", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You have a user-level CLAUDE.md in ~/.claude/ and a project CLAUDE.md in your repo, with different content. What does the model see?", "options": ["Only the project file — as with settings.json scalars, the more specific scope wins and the global file is discarded for this repo", "Both — CLAUDE.md scopes are additive, so global and project content concatenate into context together", "Only the user file — it loads first and locks out later ones", "Whichever file was modified most recently"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "CLAUDE.md resolves by additive merge: user-level, project-level, and any subdirectory files all stack into the request at once. Asking 'which one wins' is a category error imported from settings.json, which does use highest-layer-wins override for scalars — but memory files don't compete, they concatenate. That's coherent with what they're for: your personal preferences and the project's conventions are usually complementary instructions, not alternatives, so the model should see both. The practical consequences: contradictions between the files land in context together (the model has to arbitrate in prose, so keep scopes consistent), and every scope you add is tokens on every single request, because the whole stack rides in the stable head of each resend. Three surfaces, three resolution rules — override for settings scalars, additive for memory and skills, deny-wins for permissions."}, {"id": "3.3-L2-03", "topic": "3.3", "topicName": "The special local files", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Where does Claude Code store the durable record of your sessions?", "options": ["On Anthropic's servers, keyed by your account, synced to disk on demand", "In a SQLite database at ~/.claude/sessions.db", "As JSONL transcripts under ~/.claude/projects/<project-slug>/<sessionId>.jsonl", "In the project repo, under .claude/history/"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Every session is appended to a plaintext JSONL file under ~/.claude/projects/, in a directory slug derived from the project path, one file per session id. Each line is a JSON record — user and assistant messages with the API's own usage accounting, but also harness state like mode changes and file-history snapshots. This is the durable tier of state, and it lives only on your disk: none of it is uploaded as a side effect of a turn, and the server holds nothing between calls except the ephemeral prompt cache (true on Bedrock and Anthropic alike — statelessness is the protocol, not the provider). The server-side distractor is the folk model the stateless loop kills: if history lived on the server, the harness wouldn't need to re-send the conversation every call. Two cautions worth knowing: the files are unencrypted plaintext, and their schema is undocumented and version-unstable."}, {"id": "3.3-L3-01", "topic": "3.3", "topicName": "The special local files", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Which of these is NOT stored anywhere under ~/.claude/?", "options": ["Session transcripts, including per-call token accounting", "File-history snapshots that back the rewind/undo feature", "Installed plugins with their skills and commands", "The prompt cache that made most of your input tokens bill at ~0.1×"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The prompt (KV) cache is the one ephemeral, server-side tier in the whole state model: it lives on Anthropic's or AWS's infrastructure, holds computed attention state rather than your text, is scoped per model, and is evicted on a TTL (5 minutes by default, 1 hour where configured). You cannot point at it on disk, and nothing in ~/.claude/ is it. Everything else listed is genuinely durable and local: transcripts under projects/ (with the usage object on every assistant record), file-history/ backing rewind, plugins/ holding installed code and skills. Conflating the cache with local storage is the same error as reading cache_read_input_tokens as 'tokens not sent' — the full prefix is transmitted every call; the cache only spares the server recomputation. Durable state is the harness's, on your disk; the server keeps only a cost-optimizing cache; the model keeps nothing."}, {"id": "3.3-L3-02", "topic": "3.3", "topicName": "The special local files", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague says: \"claude --resume restores the session exactly as it was — the next request picks up byte-identical to where we stopped.\" What's the correction?", "options": ["Resume is a reconstruction, not a restore: the harness replays the transcript to rebuild the conversation but re-assembles the request NOW — current date, current tool list, fresh injected reminders — so the next request is not byte-identical to a live continuation", "Correct — the transcript records every assembled request beside the reply it produced, precisely so --resume can re-send the most recent request byte-for-byte and re-establish the exact cached prefix, tool list, and pricing the session was using before it stopped", "Wrong, because resume only restores the last 10 messages", "Correct, as long as you resume on the same machine — transcripts and file-history snapshots are all local, so nothing can differ unless you switch hosts"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "What persists is the transcript — the durable record. What the model sees is always a reconstruction built fresh at assembly time. On --resume, the harness reads the JSONL to recover the user/assistant history, but everything else is resolved against the present: today's date in env info, whatever tools and MCP servers are configured now, newly injected system-reminders, the current system prompt version. The transcript couldn't be replayed byte-for-byte even in principle — it never stored the assembled request (no system param, no tools param), only the tape the request gets built from. One practical corollary: after a long idle, the reconstructed prefix no longer matches any server-side cache entry (TTL-evicted anyway), so the first resumed call takes a full-price cache-write on the whole history. 'The session is saved' is true; 'the context is restored' is not — it's rebuilt."}, {"id": "3.3-L3-03", "topic": "3.3", "topicName": "The special local files", "difficulty": 3, "question": "From a data-privacy standpoint, which statement about Claude Code's on-disk state is accurate?", "options": ["Transcripts are encrypted at rest with a key derived from your login, so the on-disk files are unreadable until the harness decrypts them for --resume; only the assembled request ever leaves the machine in the clear", "Everything under ~/.claude/ is plaintext — transcripts include anything you pasted into a session — and none of it is uploaded as a side effect of a turn; only the assembled request crosses the wire", "The ~/.claude/ directory is synced to Anthropic's servers for backup", "Only token counts are written to disk; message content is kept in memory"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Both halves matter. Locally: transcripts, settings, and adjacent state files are unencrypted plaintext, so any secret you pasted into a session is sitting readable on disk — and ~/.claude.json (beside the directory) holds OAuth tokens in the clear too, if you authenticate that way (Bedrock users authenticate via AWS credentials instead). Outbound: the boundary is clean — the only thing that ever leaves the machine is the reconstructed request (system + tools + messages) sent to the API endpoint, whether that endpoint is Anthropic or Bedrock in your AWS account. Nothing in ~/.claude/ is synced or uploaded as a side effect; between calls the server retains only the ephemeral prompt cache, not your conversation. The teachable summary: history is local and plaintext; transmission is one assembled array per call; server retention is a TTL'd cost optimization."}, {"id": "3.4-L2-01", "topic": "3.4", "topicName": "Configuration surfaces", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A colleague put their code-style rules ('always use type hints, never abbreviate names') into settings.json, and Claude keeps ignoring them. Why?", "options": ["settings.json only takes effect after restarting the session", "The rules need to be under a special \"rules\" key to be recognized", "settings.json is harness-shaping config — it never crosses the wire, so the model cannot see rules placed there; instructions the model must act on belong in CLAUDE.md, which lands in the request", "The project-level settings file is overriding their user-level rules — the project layer outranks the user layer in the precedence chain, so for overlapping keys the personal rules are discarded before the request is built"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "This is the landing axis doing its job. Every config surface either shapes the request (its content lands in the payload the model reads) or shapes the harness (it governs the loop and never leaves the machine). settings.json is squarely harness-side: model selection, permissions, hooks, UI — none of it is transmitted as content, so no amount of rewording rules inside it will help; the model cannot obey what it never receives. Rules the model must act on are request-shaping by definition and belong in CLAUDE.md, which the harness injects into the system layer on every call. The restart and precedence distractors assume the setting is reaching the model and merely resolving wrong — but the failure is categorical, not a tie-break. The two-bin test settles every case like this: if the model must see it, it's request-shaping; if it governs the loop, it's harness-shaping."}, {"id": "3.4-L3-01", "topic": "3.4", "topicName": "Configuration surfaces", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague asks three questions: \"Which CLAUDE.md wins, global or project? Which permission wins if user-level allows Bash but project-level denies it? Which model setting wins?\" What's the right framing?", "options": ["One rule covers all three: the most specific scope always wins", "One rule covers all three: every surface resolves through the settings precedence chain (managed > CLI > local project > project > user), and the highest layer that defines a value wins while lower layers are discarded", "It's configurable — settings.json has a key that picks the resolution strategy", "Three different rules: CLAUDE.md files don't compete, they stack additively; permissions are deny-wins, so the deny prevails; model follows the settings precedence chain, highest layer wins"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Resolution is not one rule — it's three, keyed by surface. settings.json scalars like model resolve by override: managed > CLI > local project > project > user, higher layer wins, lower discarded. CLAUDE.md (like skills and MCP servers) resolves additively: all scopes concatenate into context simultaneously, so 'which wins' is a category error — both are present. Permissions resolve safety-first: a deny at any layer beats an allow anywhere, so the colleague's Bash is denied regardless of the user-level allow. The single-rule distractors are exactly the bug source this axis exists to kill — people import the override model onto memory files ('my project file should replace the global one') or onto permissions ('my global allow should win') and then misdiagnose the behavior. When a config question confuses you, first ask which resolution family the surface belongs to."}, {"id": "3.4-L3-02", "topic": "3.4", "topicName": "Configuration surfaces", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You flip a session from the Anthropic API to AWS Bedrock by setting the CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK environment variable (plus AWS credentials). On the two-axis config grid, what kind of change is this, and what does the model see of it?", "options": ["Harness-landing: it redirects which door the request goes through; the assembled payload shape and content are unchanged, and the model sees nothing of it", "Request-landing: a Bedrock preamble is added to the system prompt so the model knows its provider", "Both: the tool list is swapped for Bedrock-specific tool definitions", "Request-landing: the harness re-encodes the messages array into Bedrock's Converse format, so the model reads a differently structured conversation even though the underlying content is unchanged"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Provider selection is pure harness configuration: it changes the endpoint, auth mechanism (AWS credentials/IAM instead of an Anthropic key or OAuth), and model identifiers used on the wire — but it lands nowhere in the content the model reads. The same assembly runs, the same tools/system/messages structure ships (Bedrock wraps the same Anthropic-shaped payload), and the same weights answer; Bedrock is a different door to the same model, not a different model. Nothing tells the model which door was used, and no prompt wording can query or change it — the classic property of harness-landing config. Note the Bedrock delta on the resolution axis, though: this surface is env-var heavy (CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK, AWS region and credential variables) rather than settings-file driven, so 'where is this configured?' has a different answer than for most other settings, even though 'what does the model see?' is still: nothing."}, {"id": "3.4-L4-01", "topic": "3.4", "topicName": "Configuration surfaces", "difficulty": 4, "question": "The request-vs-harness landing binary is clean for most surfaces. Which setting most legitimately resists it, and why?", "options": ["permissions — because the model can be told about them in CLAUDE.md", "enabledPlugins — it is harness-side configuration that never ships as content itself, yet it indirectly reshapes the request by adding skills, commands, and tool definitions that DO land in the payload", "model — because the chosen model name appears in the response metadata", "cleanupPeriodDays — nominally harness-side retention, yet by trimming old transcripts it changes what resume can reconstruct, reshaping the message array of any resumed session just as surely as a plugin's skills do"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "enabledPlugins sits in both bins at once: the key itself is harness config the model never sees, but toggling it changes what exists to be assembled — plugin skills add always-present descriptions, plugin MCP servers add tool definitions, plugin commands add invocable content. Its landing is indirect: it shapes the request by changing the inputs to assembly, not by being content. The distractors fail individually: describing permissions in CLAUDE.md doesn't make the permission system request-landing — the enforcement still happens in the harness, invisible to the model (and telling the model about rules it can't verify changes nothing mechanically). Response metadata isn't the request payload. And cleanupPeriodDays governs disk retention of old transcripts — it never alters what a live session resends, because assembly reads the current session's tape, not your archive. The refined test: does the setting change the bytes of any request, directly or by changing assembly's inputs?"}, {"id": "3.1-L2-03", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 2, "question": "How often does the harness run the full prompt assembly — the tools + system + messages build?", "options": ["Once per session, at startup; later turns append deltas to the same request", "Once per user prompt; the tool round-trips inside that prompt reuse the assembled request", "On every model call — including each tool round-trip, so one user prompt can trigger dozens of assemblies", "Only when something changed — a file was read, CLAUDE.md was edited, or a tool was added"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Assembly is per model call, not per session or per user prompt. A single prompt from you can spawn dozens of tool-use round-trips, and each one rebuilds the whole request — tools param, system param, the entire messages array plus the newest tool_result — and sends all of it again. That is forced by the stateless keystone: the API stores nothing between calls, so there is no standing request to append deltas to, and 'reuse' has nothing server-side to reuse. The 'only when something changed' option misses that something always changes — the messages array grows every round-trip, which is exactly why the volatile tail sits last in the stability gradient. Identical on Bedrock and the Anthropic API: assembly is entirely harness-side, and only the door the request goes through differs."}, {"id": "3.1-L2-04", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Which of these is NOT a real destination in the API request the harness builds?", "options": ["The tools parameter, carrying tool definitions and schemas", "The system parameter, carrying the system prompt", "The messages array, carrying the conversation and tool results", "The memory parameter, carrying CLAUDE.md and other context files"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "There are exactly three destinations: the tools parameter (definitions and input schemas), the system parameter (the system prompt, a separate top-level field rather than a message), and the messages array (the whole prior conversation, tool results, injected reminders, and your newest input). There is no dedicated memory or context parameter — CLAUDE.md is injected by the harness at or near the system layer, riding inside a destination that already exists. That placement is deliberate: memory is stable content, so it belongs in the cacheable head, re-sent on every call. The distractor is tempting because memory feels architecturally special, but from the API's point of view it is just more system-layer text; the API has no idea files like CLAUDE.md exist. Bedrock accepts the same Anthropic-shaped payload, so the three-destination anatomy is identical through either door."}, {"id": "3.1-L3-05", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague says: \"Every user-role message in the assembled request is either something the human typed or a tool_result.\" What's the correction?", "options": ["Correct — the protocol permits only those two sources in the user role, which is why there are exactly two message roles", "Wrong — harness-authored notes go into the system parameter, never into the messages array", "Correct on the Anthropic API, but Bedrock additionally injects AWS-authored control messages into the user channel", "Wrong — the harness also injects its own content into the user channel: system-reminder blocks, marked as meta, that no human or tool produced"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The user channel has three authors, not two: the human, the tools, and the harness itself. Each assembly, the harness wraps system-reminder blocks around and between your messages — flagged isMeta:true in the record — carrying harness state the model needs this turn. This is the harness talking to the model over the user role, injecting content that was never authored on the transcript tape. The 'only two sources' distractor confuses roles with sources: the protocol constrains roles to user and assistant, but says nothing about who writes the bytes inside them. The system-parameter distractor is the adjacent mechanism — the system param exists, but per-turn volatile reminders deliberately ride late in the messages array, keeping the stable head still for caching. And the Bedrock option invents a provider delta: assembly happens entirely in the harness before either endpoint sees the request, so the channels are identical."}, {"id": "3.1-L3-06", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Two sessions send roughly the same input tokens per call. Session A's context is static; Session B injects different content near the head of the request each turn. Why does B cost meaningfully more?", "options": ["Because B's injected content is billed at output-token rates, which run several times the input price", "Same size is not same cacheability: B's churn moves the cache boundary earlier each call, so tokens that billed at ~0.1× cache-read in A re-bill at full input and 1.25× cache-write rates in B", "It doesn't — input cost depends only on how many tokens are sent, so equal-size requests cost the same", "Because each head change forces the server to persist a second full copy of the cached conversation state, and that duplicated cache storage is metered and billed to the session until the older copy's TTL expires"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Prompt caching matches a prefix from the first byte forward, so what you pay depends not just on how many tokens you send but on how many still match the cached head. A static context hits cache on nearly everything — in real sessions cache reads cover over 90% of input tokens at roughly 0.1×. Content that churns near the head moves the divergence point earlier every call, converting a long cheap read into full-price reprocessing plus a 1.25× write to establish the new prefix. That is the second-order cost of dynamic context: it is not just bigger, it is less cacheable, which is why curation that keeps the head still is worth more than its raw token savings. The 'tokens sent = cost' option is the naive pricing model this mechanism breaks; the output-rate and duplicate-storage options attach real billing concepts to the wrong tier and layer. The math is the same via Bedrock or Anthropic."}, {"id": "3.1-L3-07", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 3, "question": "After a compaction, Claude still follows every convention in your CLAUDE.md but has forgotten decisions you agreed on in the chat. Why the asymmetry?", "options": ["CLAUDE.md was never part of the compacted history — the harness re-reads it from disk and re-injects it into the stable head at every assembly, while the chat decisions lived only in the messages that got summarized", "The compaction prompt is instructed to preserve rule-like statements and discard decision-like ones", "CLAUDE.md content is held in the server-side prompt cache under its own breakpoint, so it survives the eviction of the compacted messages and is spliced back into context on each call even though the harness no longer sends it", "The model internalized the rules because they repeated across many turns, but the decisions appeared only once"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Compaction rewrites the messages array — the conversation gets summarized, and detail is genuinely lost. But CLAUDE.md never lived in the messages array: it is request-shaping memory that the harness re-injects fresh from disk into the system layer on every single assembly. Compaction cannot lose what assembly re-adds each call. Your in-chat decisions, by contrast, existed only as conversation content, so they went through the lossy summary. The compaction-prompt distractor points at an adjacent mechanism — the summarizer does prioritize, but that is not why memory survives perfectly. The cache option misunderstands what the cache is: it stores computed attention state over whatever was sent, and cannot supply content missing from a request. The internalization option is the folk memory model the stateless loop kills — the model retains nothing between calls. Practical corollary: durable rules belong in CLAUDE.md, not in chat."}, {"id": "3.1-L3-08", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague sees that one prompt produced 40 model calls, each re-sending the entire conversation, and concludes the turn cost roughly 40× the price of one full-context call. What's the correction?", "options": ["Nothing — cost is per-call price times call count, so 40 calls cost 40× one call", "Calls after the first send only the delta since the last call — the harness references the cached prefix by its breakpoint id, and the server reassembles the full conversation without re-transmission, so marginal calls are nearly free", "Each call does re-send everything, but the unchanged prefix bills at ~0.1× as cache reads — which in practice cover on the order of 90% of input tokens — so a marginal call costs a fraction of a cold one", "The harness batches the 40 round-trips into a single billed request at the end of the turn"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Both extremes are wrong. The resend is real: every one of the 40 calls carries the whole conversation, and the messages array grows each round-trip — that is the stateless loop. But the stability gradient exists precisely so that the long unchanged head of each call matches the cached prefix and bills at roughly 0.1× instead of 1×; observed sessions show cache reads absorbing over 90% of all input tokens. So the turn costs meaningfully more than one call, yet far less than 40 cold ones. The delta option is the opposite folk model — nothing is 'sent as a delta'; the full prefix crosses the wire every call and only recomputation is skipped. Batching doesn't exist: each round-trip is a complete, separately billed API call. Caching is what makes the resend affordable, not what makes it unnecessary — and the same arithmetic applies on Bedrock."}, {"id": "3.1-L4-03", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 4, "question": "You want to verify from your own machine exactly where CLAUDE.md lands in the outgoing request — inside the system parameter, or as an early injected message. Why can't reading the session JSONL settle it?", "options": ["The transcript stores each request minus only the tool schemas — the assembled system parameter is written into the assistant record, so you can settle the system-vs-messages question from disk but learn nothing about the tools param", "The transcript is the durable tape, not the outgoing request: it never records the assembled system or tools parameters, so the landing spot is inferred — settling it requires capturing the request on the wire", "The harness encrypts request-side fields in the transcript, and only the provider can decode them", "You can — each assistant record embeds the full request that produced it, precisely so sessions can be replayed byte-for-byte on resume"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The transcript and the request are different objects related by an assembly step, and the transcript sits on the input side: it records the conversation tape plus harness state, never the assembled artifact. The system and tools parameters are built in memory each call and discarded — so message-side facts (roles, block types, injected meta records) are provable from disk, while the exact landing of CLAUDE.md and env info is inference; proving it needs network interception. Each distractor breaks on one load-bearing detail. 'Request minus tools' grants the transcript a stored system param it doesn't have. Encryption is backwards — everything under ~/.claude/ is plaintext; the fields aren't hidden, they're absent. The replay option is how you might design resume, but resume is a reconstruction: the harness rebuilds state from the tape and assembles a fresh request with today's date and current tool list — byte-identical replay was never possible because the bytes were never kept."}, {"id": "3.1-L4-04", "topic": "3.1", "topicName": "Prompt assembly", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Deep into a 150k-token session, compare two mid-session changes: adding one MCP tool definition versus editing one line of CLAUDE.md. A colleague says the tool change is far more expensive because tools sit earliest in the prefix. What's the expert-level correction?", "options": ["The colleague is right, and the gap is large: an earlier divergence point re-bills a proportionally larger span on every subsequent call", "It's backwards — the CLAUDE.md edit costs more, because memory is re-injected on every call while a tool definition is transmitted only once", "In a long session they cost nearly the same: both divergence points sit ahead of the giant messages array, which dominates the re-bill; the positional difference is only worth the few thousand system-layer tokens between the two layers", "Neither costs much: the harness plants cache breakpoints inside the messages array, and matching resumes at the first breakpoint after a divergence, so only the thin span between the head edit and that breakpoint re-bills while the conversation bulk stays cached"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Cache invalidation is positional — everything after the divergence point re-bills — so the question is how much sits behind each edit. The tools param does sit before the system layer, but both sit before the messages array, and at 150k tokens the conversation dwarfs the few thousand tokens of system prompt and memory between the two layers. Both edits therefore trigger a nearly identical re-bill: full input on the mismatched span plus 1.25× cache-write to establish the new prefix. Each trap fails on one detail: option one has the right direction but wildly wrong magnitude — 'earlier' buys only the thin system-layer slice; option two cites a real mechanism (memory re-injection) but wrongly concludes re-sent means re-billed — re-injected identical content still cache-hits, and every layer is re-transmitted every call anyway; option four misunderstands breakpoints — a breakpoint cannot rescue content downstream of a prefix mismatch, because matching runs from the first byte. Same economics through Bedrock."}, {"id": "3.2-L2-03", "topic": "3.2", "topicName": "The tool protocol", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Claude asks to run a Bash command and you answer the permission prompt with 'deny'. What does the model receive?", "options": ["Nothing — the request simply times out and the model gives up on it", "The tool_use is removed from the conversation, so the model never knows it asked", "The API returns an authorization error to the model and the session ends", "A tool_result reporting the denial — the model continues the conversation knowing its request was refused"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Denial is still an answer. The protocol requires every tool_use to be matched by a tool_result in the next request — a dangling request is malformed input the API rejects — so the harness cannot silently drop the call. Instead it sends back a tool_result whose content says the permission was denied (failures similarly come back flagged is_error), and the loop continues: the model reads the refusal and can try another approach or ask you what to do. That is why a denied prompt still produces a visible result message. The deletion option would violate the pairing invariant, and the timeout option imagines the model waiting on something — it never waits; its turn ended when it emitted tool_use, and whatever happens next arrives as text in a fresh request. Permissions are enforced entirely in the harness, identically whether your requests go to Bedrock or the Anthropic API."}, {"id": "3.2-L2-04", "topic": "3.2", "topicName": "The tool protocol", "difficulty": 2, "question": "From the model's perspective, how does calling an MCP tool differ from calling a built-in tool like Read?", "options": ["MCP tools appear as a distinct mcp_use block type, so the model routes them differently", "MCP tools execute on the provider's servers, which host the MCP runtime alongside model inference, while built-in tools like Read execute on your machine — so an MCP round-trip never touches the client side of the loop at all", "It doesn't differ: every tool is an entry in the tools parameter, and every call is the same tool_use/tool_result round-trip — who defines the schema and who executes are client-side details the model never sees", "The model must first open a connection to the MCP server before it can emit calls to its tools"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The protocol is deliberately uniform. Whether a tool is built into the harness or served by an external MCP process, it reaches the model the same way — as a name, description, and input schema in the tools parameter — and gets called the same way: a tool_use block out, a tool_result block back. The real differences live entirely on the client side: for built-ins the harness defines the schema and executes; for MCP tools an external server defines the schema and executes, with the harness relaying. There is no mcp_use block type, and the model opens connections to nothing — it cannot reach anything; it can only emit structured text and stop. The server-side execution option fails on the constant that covers every tool: execution is always client-side, done by the harness or a process the harness talks to, never by the model or the API server — true on Bedrock and Anthropic alike."}, {"id": "3.2-L3-04", "topic": "3.2", "topicName": "The tool protocol", "difficulty": 3, "question": "The model calls a tool provided by an MCP server. What is the actual execution chain?", "options": ["The model connects to the MCP server directly over the transport declared in the tool's schema — stdio for local servers, HTTP for remote ones — and streams the result straight into its own generation", "The API server forwards the call to the MCP server and inlines the result before finishing the model's reply", "The harness executes the tool itself, using code the MCP server uploaded to it at startup", "The model emits an ordinary tool_use; the harness relays it to the MCP server process, which executes and returns the result; the harness ships it back as a tool_result in the next request"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "MCP adds a third executor, but the round-trip shape never changes. At startup the harness discovers the server's tool schemas and forwards them into the tools parameter. When the model emits a tool_use naming one, the harness — not the model, not the API — relays the call to the server process, waits for its result, and delivers it as a tool_result in a fresh, longer request. The direct-connection option forgets what the model is: it has no network reach and no ability to act; schemas describe inputs, not transports it could use. The API-forwarding option smuggles in server-side execution — the reply had already ended when tool_use was emitted; nothing gets 'inlined' into a finished generation. The uploaded-code option inverts the division of labor: the server shares its schema at discovery, but execution stays in the server's own process. What crosses each boundary is only structured text."}, {"id": "3.2-L3-05", "topic": "3.2", "topicName": "The tool protocol", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Within a single user turn, the harness loops: send request, get reply, run tools, send again. What normally ends that loop and returns control to you?", "options": ["A model reply containing no tool_use blocks — the model stops asking, the harness has nothing to execute, and the turn ends", "The harness's iteration limit — the loop always runs until a maxTurns-style cap is reached, then returns whatever the model produced on the final call", "The user approving the final response — each loop exit requires a human confirmation", "The API signals completion server-side once it detects the task is finished"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The loop's exit condition is the model's own behavior: as long as replies contain tool_use blocks, the harness executes them and re-sends the growing conversation; the moment a reply arrives with no tool request, there is nothing left to run and control returns to you. Observed sessions run to dozens or even 160+ calls in a single turn this way — with no human in the loop at all, which kills the approval option (permission prompts gate individual dangerous tools, not loop continuation). The iteration-cap option mistakes the emergency brakes for the engine: limits like max-turn or budget caps exist, but they are backstops for runaways, not the normal exit. The server-side option fails on statelessness — the API sees isolated, self-contained requests and completes each one; it has no concept of a task spanning calls, so there is nothing for it to detect. The loop lives entirely in the harness."}, {"id": "3.2-L3-06", "topic": "3.2", "topicName": "The tool protocol", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Given that prompt injection is structural — untrusted tool output shares the user channel with your instructions — where do the real defenses actually sit?", "options": ["In the API, which screens tool_result content for instruction-like text before the model reads it", "In the harness: permission gates, sandboxing, and limiting what untrusted output can trigger — consequences are stopped where actions execute", "In model training: newer models enforce a strict instruction hierarchy ranking system over user over tool-result content, which closes the injection category outright", "In MCP servers, which are required by the protocol to sanitize content before returning it"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "If the channel cannot distinguish authors, the defense must sit where actions have consequences — and that is the harness. It decides which tools run without asking, sandboxes what execution can touch, and puts a human prompt in front of dangerous operations, so even a fully 'injected' model request gets stopped before it acts. The API-screening option describes a filter nobody can build reliably: malicious instructions are just text, detection is heuristic, and the API's job is to pass the request through faithfully — it screens nothing. The training option is the tempting near-miss: alignment genuinely makes models more suspicious of instruction-shaped content, but it hardens a judgment call rather than closing the category — no training manufactures a channel distinction the input format never carried. And MCP imposes no sanitization duty on servers; their output re-enters the same user channel as everything else, which is exactly the problem."}, {"id": "3.2-L4-03", "topic": "3.2", "topicName": "The tool protocol", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague says: \"Read and Bash are built into the Claude API — the harness just switches them on per request.\" What's the correction?", "options": ["Right — the API ships built-in coding tools, and the harness selects which toolset each request may use", "Right in effect — Claude was trained on these exact schemas, so the definitions are baked into the weights and the harness sends them only as a formatting hint that could be omitted without changing behavior", "Wrong — the tool schemas live in CLAUDE.md, which the harness injects at the system layer of every request", "Wrong — the harness defines those schemas and sends them in the tools parameter on every call; the API keeps no tool registry, and the model knows only the tools present in the current request"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Read, Bash, Edit and friends are harness constructs: the CLI defines their schemas, ships them in the tools parameter of every single request, and executes them locally when asked. The API stores nothing between calls — there is no registry to switch anything on in — and the model's knowledge of available tools is exactly the tools parameter of the request in front of it. Each trap fails on a specific detail. The 'built-in tools' option is true in a different configuration: the Anthropic API does offer some server-executed tools, but Claude Code's file and shell tools are not those, and on Bedrock you're even more clearly outside that model — AWS runs inference, not your shell. The training option confuses capability with definition: Claude is trained for tool use in general, but without the schema in the request the tool does not exist for this call. And CLAUDE.md is memory in the system layer — tool schemas have their own dedicated parameter."}, {"id": "3.2-L4-04", "topic": "3.2", "topicName": "The tool protocol", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A deferred MCP tool's name has been mentioned in context, but its schema has not been loaded into the request. A colleague claims the model can just call it by name. What actually governs whether it's callable?", "options": ["They're right — the API resolves tool names against the full registry the harness uploaded at session start", "They're right — emitting tool_use with the name is enough: the harness intercepts the unknown name, fetches the schema from the MCP server on demand, validates the input retroactively, and executes in the same round-trip with no extra model call", "Tools exist per-request: only entries in this call's tools parameter are callable, so the model must first trigger the schema load, which places the definition into subsequent requests — and moves the cache boundary once when it does", "They're wrong, but for a different reason — deferred tools are callable only from subagents, which load their own tool sets"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A tool is not a capability the session has; it is an entry in the tools parameter of one specific request. If the schema isn't in this call, the tool does not exist for this call — a name floating in context is just text. Deferral exploits exactly that: rarely-used MCP schemas are withheld to keep every request smaller and its head more stable, and a search step loads them into the requests that follow, paying one cache invalidation at the earliest prefix position. The registry option fails on statelessness — nothing was 'uploaded at session start' because the API retains nothing between calls. The fetch-on-demand option breaks the round-trip's arrow of time: the schema's job is to shape generation of the call's input, so it must precede emission — a call minted without it is malformed, not lazily repairable. The subagent option attaches a real mechanism (subagents do assemble their own tool sets) to the wrong layer. Identical through Bedrock."}, {"id": "3.3-L1-02", "topic": "3.3", "topicName": "The special local files", "difficulty": 1, "question": "You close your terminal, come back tomorrow, and Claude Code can pick up yesterday's session. What makes that possible?", "options": ["It isn't possible — when the terminal closes, the conversation is gone for good", "The model remembers you and your project between sessions", "Your conversations are synced to a cloud account tied to your login, and the CLI downloads yesterday's chat again when you return to the project", "The harness saved the whole session to a transcript file on your own disk, and rebuilds the conversation from it"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Everything durable in Claude Code lives on your disk. As you work, the harness appends every exchange to a transcript file under ~/.claude/projects/, and commands like --resume and --continue read that file back to rebuild the conversation. The model itself remembers nothing — it is stateless, seeing only what each individual request contains — so option two is the folk model to unlearn first. Nor is there a cloud copy: nothing under ~/.claude/ is uploaded as a side effect of your sessions; the only thing that ever leaves your machine is each assembled request, sent to the API (for Bedrock users, into their own AWS account) and not retained as conversation between calls. So 'the session is saved' is true, but be precise about where and by whom: saved locally, by the harness — the same component that does everything else with state in this system."}, {"id": "3.3-L2-04", "topic": "3.3", "topicName": "The special local files", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You want settings that apply only to you, in one specific project, without ever being pushed to your teammates. Which file is designed for that?", "options": ["The project's .claude/settings.json — project settings apply per-checkout, so each developer's clone keeps its own values without affecting teammates", "Your global ~/.claude/settings.json — personal settings always live at user level", ".claude/settings.local.json in the project — the personal project layer, meant to be gitignored, and it outranks the shared project file", "A private CLAUDE.md section — memory files are the per-user configuration surface"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The settings chain has two project-level files with opposite audiences: .claude/settings.json is the shared file, committed so the whole team inherits it, while .claude/settings.local.json is yours alone — conventionally gitignored, and sitting one rung higher in precedence (managed > CLI args > local project > project > user), so your personal values override the team's where they overlap. The first distractor gets the audience backwards: the plain project file is exactly the one that does get pushed. The user-global file is genuinely personal but applies to every project, so it can't express 'only this repo' — and being the lowest precedence layer, it loses to any project file. The CLAUDE.md option is a category error on the landing axis: memory is request-shaping content the model reads, not harness configuration — settings placed there configure nothing, they just cost tokens on every call."}, {"id": "3.3-L2-05", "topic": "3.3", "topicName": "The special local files", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What's the difference between claude --continue and claude --resume?", "options": ["--continue reopens the most recent session for the current project; --resume lets you pick which past session to reopen", "--continue keeps the API connection alive between invocations; --resume re-establishes one that closed", "--resume reopens the most recent session; --continue lets you choose from a list", "They are aliases — both reopen the last session"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Both flags do the same kind of work — read a saved transcript from ~/.claude/projects/ and rebuild the conversation — and differ only in which transcript: --continue grabs the most recent session for the project, --resume offers a choice. The 'keeps the connection alive' option is worth dwelling on, because it imports statefulness that doesn't exist anywhere in this system: there is no persistent API connection to keep alive, no server-side session to reconnect to. Every call is self-contained, so 'resuming' can only ever mean the harness re-reading its own local record and reassembling a fresh request from it — with today's date, the current tool list, and fresh reminders, which is why resume is a reconstruction rather than a restore. That holds identically on Bedrock: your AWS endpoint holds no session either. The swapped option is there to check you have the two flags the right way around."}, {"id": "3.3-L2-06", "topic": "3.3", "topicName": "The special local files", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You run Claude Code against Bedrock, but a teammate logs in to the Anthropic API directly. Where did their OAuth credentials end up on disk?", "options": ["In ~/.claude.json — a file beside the ~/.claude directory, stored in plaintext; your own Bedrock setup keeps nothing there because auth comes from AWS credentials", "In ~/.claude/settings.json, under a dedicated auth key", "In the operating system keychain — the CLI hands tokens to macOS Keychain or the Linux secret service, so they are encrypted at rest and never written to a plaintext file", "In .claude/settings.local.json inside each project they've logged in from"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Anthropic-direct login writes OAuth tokens into ~/.claude.json — note the location: a JSON file sitting beside the ~/.claude/ directory, not inside it, holding global harness state along with the tokens. And it is plaintext, like everything else in Claude Code's on-disk state — worth knowing before you back up or share a home directory. The keychain option is what a security-minded user assumes, which is exactly why the real answer is worth teaching. The settings files hold configuration — model, permissions, hooks — not credentials, and nothing auth-related is scattered per-project. The Bedrock half matters for your own setup: with CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK set there is no /login and no token file — the harness signs requests with whatever your AWS credential chain provides (profiles, SSO, IAM roles), so credential storage and rotation follow your AWS tooling, not any Claude Code file."}, {"id": "3.3-L3-04", "topic": "3.3", "topicName": "The special local files", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Reading a transcript's usage objects, you notice cache_read_input_tokens on each call almost exactly equals the previous call's total input, off by a token or two. What are you looking at?", "options": ["An accounting artifact — those tokens were billed on the previous call and shouldn't be counted again", "The resend made visible: each call re-transmits the entire previous request, which now matches the cached prefix — this correspondence is the statelessness signature you can read off your own disk", "Evidence the server keeps the conversation between calls: cache_read counts the stored tokens the harness did not need to re-send, which is why only the new tail crosses the wire on each round-trip of the loop", "Cache pre-warming: the server speculatively re-runs the previous request so the next one starts hot"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "This near-equality is the cleanest empirical proof of the stateless loop. Call n re-sends everything call n−1 contained — that content is now a stable prefix, so it registers as cache reads — plus the few new tokens (the model's last reply, the newest tool_result) that show up as fresh input and cache writes. Total input on one call becoming cache_read on the next, off by a token or two, is the resend caught in the API's own accounting. The 'server stores the conversation' option reads the evidence exactly backwards: cache_read means those tokens were sent and matched, not that they didn't need sending — the full prefix crosses the wire every call, and the cache (attention state, TTL-evicted) only spares recomputation. Nothing is double-billed: reads bill at ~0.1×, a discount, not an error. And no speculative re-running exists. The same tell appears in transcripts whether you run through Bedrock or Anthropic."}, {"id": "3.3-L3-05", "topic": "3.3", "topicName": "The special local files", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Analyzing a session's JSONL, you find records flagged isSidechain: true interleaved with the rest. What are they, and what's the trap if you ignore the flag?", "options": ["Subagent conversations: they're stored inline in the parent session's file but never entered the parent's assembled context — count them and you inflate what the main model actually saw", "Parallel branches created by the rewind feature: each rewind forks the tape, and the abandoned branch's records stay inline, flagged, so they re-enter context whenever you rewind past their fork point again", "Abandoned streaming drafts — the same message written multiple times, which you deduplicate by message id", "MCP server logs mirrored into the transcript for debugging, excluded from token accounting"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Subagents run their own separate loop with their own context, but their records land inline in the parent session's transcript, flagged isSidechain: true. The flag marks the boundary between the tapes: sidechain messages were sent in the subagent's requests, never assembled into the parent's message array — only the subagent's final report crosses back, as a tool result. Ignore the flag and any analysis of 'what the main model saw' or what the main context cost is inflated by entire private conversations. That's the transcript-is-not-the-message-array lesson with a twist: here the tape holds a superset spanning two different loops. The streaming-drafts option names a real but different gotcha — streaming does write several assistant records per message, deduplicated by message id, unrelated to this flag. The rewind option points at file-history snapshots, a separate record family, and past turns never 're-enter' context anyway — every request is rebuilt fresh."}, {"id": "3.3-L3-06", "topic": "3.3", "topicName": "The special local files", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague says: \"cleanupPeriodDays defaults to 30, so nothing older than a month can exist under ~/.claude/.\" What's the accurate picture?", "options": ["They're right — the harness sweeps every store under ~/.claude/ against that setting at startup", "They're wrong about scope — the cleanup applies only to files above a size threshold, so small old files persist", "The setting nominally trims old chat transcripts, but retention is uneven and undocumented across the other stores — adjacent logs and state can persist far longer, so if you need to know what's still on disk, look; purging is a separate, explicit act", "They're right for Anthropic-direct sessions, but Bedrock sessions are exempt from the sweep — with CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK set, the harness retains every transcript indefinitely, because AWS-side compliance expects an auditable local record of each request sent"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "cleanupPeriodDays (default 30) governs the trimming of old chat transcripts — but ~/.claude/ is a sprawl of stores beyond transcripts: plugin logs, file-history snapshots, shell snapshots, todos, and more, and retention across them is uneven and undocumented. In practice you can find files months older than the window — a plugin telemetry log, say — because they were never in the setting's scope. The durable lesson: retention is per-store and unverifiable from the setting alone, so audit the disk rather than trust the number, and treat purging (deleting under ~/.claude/projects/, or the explicit purge command) as an action you take, not something that happens. The size-threshold option invents a mechanism. The Bedrock option is a category error worth naming: all of this state is harness-side, on your machine — the provider door your requests go through has nothing to do with what your own disk retains."}, {"id": "3.4-L2-02", "topic": "3.4", "topicName": "Configuration surfaces", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Your team wants every developer's Claude Code to hit Bedrock with the same region and model, with no per-machine setup drift. What's the standard way to do it?", "options": ["Commit a .claude/settings.json to the repo with the Bedrock variables (CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK, region, model) in its env block, so every session resolves them through the normal precedence chain", "Add the variables to the project CLAUDE.md so the model reads and applies them", "Have each developer export the variables in their own shell profile", "Commit a .claude/settings.local.json with the variables — the local layer outranks the shared project file in precedence, so values committed there win on every clone regardless of personal overrides"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Bedrock configuration is env-var heavy — the provider switch itself is CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK, with region and model identifiers riding alongside — and settings files can pin env vars via their env block. Committing them in the shared .claude/settings.json makes the wiring part of the repo: every clone gets identical provider config, resolved through the same precedence chain as everything else. Shell profiles genuinely work, but they're per-machine hand-setup — exactly the drift you're trying to eliminate. The CLAUDE.md option fails on the landing axis: provider selection is harness-shaping — it steers which door the request leaves through, and the model neither sees nor can act on it; memory-file text about endpoints configures nothing. And settings.local.json is the personal, conventionally gitignored layer — committing it inverts its purpose; the shared project file is the team surface. Auth itself still comes from each developer's AWS credential chain — the committed file carries wiring, not secrets."}, {"id": "3.4-L3-03", "topic": "3.4", "topicName": "Configuration surfaces", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Which of these active extensions adds ZERO tokens to your requests?", "options": ["A PreToolUse hook that lints every command before the harness runs it", "An installed skill you never happen to trigger", "A connected MCP server whose tools you never call", "An output-style customization you set once and forgot"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Sort each surface by where it lands. Hooks are pure harness-side: they run as callbacks in the loop's own process — even one that fires on every single tool call executes entirely outside the request, costing zero context tokens. The model never knows the hook exists. Everything else on the list is request-shaping and bills whether used or not: a skill's name and description sit in context on every call even if its body never loads; an MCP server's tool schemas land in the tools parameter — and even deferred MCP tools leave a small stub presence in context, so idle is not free; an output-style customization is a system-layer append, re-sent every call. The MCP option is the near-miss to respect: 'never called' feels like 'never paid for', but cost follows presence in the payload, not usage. The two-bin test settles it — if the model must see it, it costs tokens; if the harness runs it, it doesn't."}, {"id": "3.4-L3-04", "topic": "3.4", "topicName": "Configuration surfaces", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Mid-session, you edit CLAUDE.md to add a new convention. When and how does the running session's model see the change?", "options": ["It doesn't until you restart — memory files are read once at session start", "On the next model call: assembly re-reads the file and injects the new content into the stable head — at the price of a moved cache boundary re-billing the prefix behind the system layer once", "Immediately — the harness watches memory files for changes and pushes the diff to the server mid-turn, which patches the cached copy of the system layer in place without waiting for the next assembly", "Only after /compact — compaction is what rebuilds the context from source files"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Settings and memory files are durable config that shape the reconstructed request — they are not themselves 'in' the session. Since the request is rebuilt from scratch on every model call, an edited CLAUDE.md simply gets picked up at the next assembly: no restart, no push, no special command. The cost rides along automatically: the system-layer content changed, so the cached prefix diverges there, and the next call re-bills everything behind that point (in a long session, effectively the whole conversation) at full input plus 1.25× cache-write — once, until the new head is established. The push option fails twice: nothing watches your files into the server, and the server-side cache stores computed attention state that cannot be 'patched' — a changed prefix is simply a different prefix. The /compact option misattributes the rebuild: compaction rewrites the messages array; the head containing CLAUDE.md is re-injected every call regardless. Same behavior through Bedrock — assembly is harness-side."}, {"id": "3.4-L4-02", "topic": "3.4", "topicName": "Configuration surfaces", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Hooks are the canonical harness-landing surface: they run in the loop's process and cost zero context. Which hook usage most legitimately breaks that classification, and why?", "options": ["A PreToolUse hook that blocks a command — the block changes what the model observes next, making the hook request-landing", "A hook whose output the harness injects into the prompt (a user-prompt-submit hook adding text to your message) — harness-executed, but its output becomes request content: it costs tokens, can move the cache boundary, and is model-visible", "A Stop hook that writes a log file — logs are durable state, and durable state shapes the reconstructed request", "A hook that invokes claude -p inside itself — the subprocess inherits the parent's session id and appends its exchanges to the same transcript tape, so on the next assembly those records enter the parent's message array and land in a request after all"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The clean statement 'hooks are harness-landing' holds until a hook's output is injected into the prompt: then a harness mechanism is manufacturing request content — text the model reads, tokens billed every affected call, and churn that can move the cache boundary if it varies turn to turn. Like enabledPlugins, it straddles the binary: the configuration never ships, but its product does. The traps each misapply a real fact. The blocking hook's visible effect is an ordinary tool_result reporting denial — the protocol's normal output; the hook contributed no content, and by that logic every permission rule would be request-landing. The logging hook misuses the data-model tiers: assembly reconstructs from the session tape and config surfaces, not from arbitrary files a hook wrote. The claude -p hook does generate API calls, but they are separate requests in a separate loop; nothing lands in this session's payload. The refined test stands: does the mechanism change the bytes of this session's requests?"}, {"id": "3.5-L1-01", "topic": "3.5", "topicName": "Permissions", "difficulty": 1, "question": "You ask Claude Code to run a shell command and the session pauses, showing you the exact command and asking whether to allow it. What is happening?", "options": ["The model is unsure of itself and is asking for reassurance before continuing", "The API provider is requesting authorization to bill the command execution", "The harness intercepted the tool call before executing it and is asking you for permission", "The operating system's own security layer blocked the process from starting"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "This is a permission prompt, and it lives entirely in the harness. In the stateless loop, the model only ever emits a structured tool_use request — it cannot execute anything itself. The harness receives that request and, before running it, checks its permission rules; if no rule allows or denies the call, it pauses and asks you. The model isn't 'unsure' — it has already committed to the call and is simply waiting for a tool_result. The provider is irrelevant here: this gate sits below the API line, so it works identically whether your requests go to AWS Bedrock or the Anthropic API. And it isn't OS security — it's Claude Code's own policy layer, configurable through allow/deny rules in settings.json."}, {"id": "3.5-L1-02", "topic": "3.5", "topicName": "Permissions", "difficulty": 1, "question": "What do the allow and deny rules in Claude Code's settings control?", "options": ["Whether the harness will execute a tool call the model requests, without asking you each time", "Which topics the model is willing to discuss during the session", "Which local files the harness bundles into the request payload it uploads to the API provider on each turn of the loop", "How many tokens the model may spend on each response"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Permission rules are the harness's execution policy. When the model emits a tool_use block — run this Bash command, edit this file — the harness consults its rules: an allow rule lets the call run silently, a deny rule blocks it outright, and anything unmatched triggers a prompt. The rules never shape what the model says or thinks, because they are never sent to the model at all — they govern the loop, not the conversation. They also have nothing to do with uploads (no local files leave the machine except what's assembled into the request) or token budgets. Think of permissions as a gate bolted onto the tool-execution step of the loop, which is why they behave identically on Bedrock and on the Anthropic API."}, {"id": "3.5-L2-01", "topic": "3.5", "topicName": "Permissions", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Your team wants a shared set of permission rules for a repository — every teammate should get them automatically — but you also want a few personal rules that stay off git. Where do these two sets belong?", "options": ["Both in ~/.claude/settings.json, with a per-user section for the personal rules", "Shared rules in the project's .claude/settings.json (committed); personal rules in .claude/settings.local.json (not committed)", "Shared rules in CLAUDE.md; personal rules in ~/.claude/settings.json", "Both in the project's .claude/settings.local.json, committed to git — the local tier overrides every other settings file, so rules placed there are guaranteed to apply"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "settings.json comes in tiers precisely for this split: the project file (.claude/settings.json) is meant to be committed and shared, while the local project file (.claude/settings.local.json) is personal and typically gitignored. The user-level file (~/.claude/settings.json) applies across all your projects, so it's the wrong home for repo-specific team policy. CLAUDE.md is the wrong surface entirely — it's request-shaping content the model reads as instructions, not machine-enforced configuration; a 'rule' there is advisory prose, not a gate. Note the resolution nuance: for most settings the local tier takes precedence over project, but permissions specifically combine deny-wins across all tiers rather than one file simply overriding another."}, {"id": "3.5-L2-02", "topic": "3.5", "topicName": "Permissions", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A project's settings contain the permission rule Bash(npm run test:*). What does this rule do?", "options": ["It denies the model access to any npm command that touches test files — rules written with a trailing * are treated as deny patterns unless explicitly allowlisted", "It defines an npm script alias the model can invoke as a custom tool", "It caps how long any npm test process may run before the harness kills it", "It matches Bash tool calls whose command starts with 'npm run test', so the listed action applies to those without prompting"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Permission rules use a Tool(specifier) pattern: the tool name plus a matcher for its input, where a trailing * makes it a prefix match. Placed in an allow list, this rule lets any command beginning with 'npm run test' run without a permission prompt; placed in deny, it blocks those same commands at any layer. The rule itself is neutral — the list it sits in decides allow versus deny, which is why option A is wrong as stated. It defines no alias and no timeout; it's purely a matcher the harness evaluates when the model emits a Bash tool_use. This matching happens entirely harness-side, so the model never knows the rule exists — it just observes that some commands execute immediately while others come back rejected or wait on the user."}, {"id": "3.5-L3-01", "topic": "3.5", "topicName": "Permissions", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A Bash pattern is allowed in your user-level ~/.claude/settings.json but denied in the project's .claude/settings.json. A colleague predicts the user layer wins because 'it's your machine, your global settings.' What actually happens, and why?", "options": ["The call is denied — permissions resolve deny-wins: a deny at any layer beats an allow anywhere, unlike ordinary settings precedence", "The colleague is right — user settings sit above project settings in the precedence chain, so the allow wins", "The harness detects the conflict and falls back to prompting you on every matching call", "The most recently modified of the two files wins, since the harness re-reads them in timestamp order"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The colleague is importing the wrong resolution rule. settings.json scalars (model, env, output style) do resolve by a precedence chain — and in that chain project actually outranks user anyway. But permissions don't use precedence at all: rules from every layer combine, and a deny anywhere defeats an allow anywhere. This is deliberate, safety-first design — no lower-priority file should be able to quietly re-enable something a stricter scope forbade, and an enterprise or project deny can't be undone from a personal file. The three configuration surfaces carry three different resolution rules: override for settings scalars, additive merge for CLAUDE.md and skills, deny-wins for permissions. Mixing them up — asking 'which layer wins?' about a surface that doesn't compete by layers — is exactly the category error this grid exists to prevent."}, {"id": "3.5-L3-02", "topic": "3.5", "topicName": "Permissions", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague says: 'I wrote NEVER run destructive git commands in our CLAUDE.md, so that's just as safe as a deny rule in settings.json.' What's wrong with that claim?", "options": ["Nothing — CLAUDE.md is re-injected every request, so the instruction is always in force", "CLAUDE.md is resolved only once at session initialization, like the tool inventory and MCP connections, so the instruction silently goes stale mid-session the moment anyone edits the file on disk", "A CLAUDE.md line is advisory text the model can misweigh or deprioritize; a deny rule is enforced by the harness before any tool call executes, regardless of what the model decides", "CLAUDE.md instructions apply only to file-editing tools, never to Bash commands"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The two surfaces land in different places, and landing determines what enforces them. CLAUDE.md is request-shaping: it becomes tokens the model reads, and compliance depends on the model weighing that instruction correctly against everything else in a large context — usually it will, but it's probabilistic, not guaranteed. A deny rule is harness-shaping: it never reaches the model at all, and the harness mechanically blocks any matching tool_use before execution. The model literally cannot talk its way past it. So for anything genuinely destructive, the deny rule (or a PreToolUse hook) is the correct mechanism, with CLAUDE.md as complementary guidance so the model doesn't keep attempting blocked calls. Option A describes CLAUDE.md's injection correctly but draws the wrong conclusion — always-present advice still isn't enforcement."}, {"id": "3.5-L3-03", "topic": "3.5", "topicName": "Permissions", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague claims: 'The model reads our permission rules from settings.json at the start of the session, which is how it knows not to attempt forbidden commands.' What's the correction?", "options": ["Mostly right, but the rules arrive via the system prompt the harness assembles at session start — the model can't read disk, so the harness serializes settings.json into that block for it", "Wrong — permission rules are never transmitted to the model; the harness applies them to tool calls, and a blocked call comes back to the model as a rejection in the tool_result", "Right for Anthropic API sessions, but on Bedrock the rules are enforced by IAM instead", "Wrong — the model discovers the rules empirically by parsing the harness's error messages at session start"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Permissions are the textbook harness-shaping surface: they govern the loop and never cross the wire. The model doesn't know a deny rule exists — it emits a tool_use like any other, the harness matches it against the rules, and if it's blocked, the model receives a rejection message as the tool_result on the next iteration of the loop. That feedback is how the model 'learns' to try another approach, one rejection at a time, not by reading policy up front. This is why you can't prompt the model into respecting (or circumventing) a permission rule: no wording can make it obey — or evade — configuration it never receives. The Bedrock distractor is a category confusion: IAM governs your AWS credentials' access to the Bedrock API itself; Claude Code's tool permissions are the same local harness mechanism on every provider."}, {"id": "3.6-L2-01", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You run /compact in a long Claude Code session. What does it actually do?", "options": ["Compresses the transcript file on disk so the project folder takes less space", "Truncates the assembled context to the most recent exchanges and discards everything older — the same reset as /clear, but keeping the last handful of turns in place", "Switches the session to a smaller, cheaper model to reduce spend from that point on", "Has the conversation's older history summarized, so future requests carry the summary plus recent exchanges instead of every old turn"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Compaction folds history: older turns are replaced, in the assembled context, by a model-written summary that keeps key decisions, while your most recent exchanges survive verbatim. It is deliberately lossy — early verbose detail like long tool outputs is collapsed or dropped — because its job is to bound how much every future turn must resend. It is not a disk operation: the durable .jsonl transcript is append-only and untouched (the summary and a compact_boundary marker are appended to it, nothing is erased). And it's not blunt truncation — a summary preserves the thread of what was decided, which is the whole reason compaction is preferable to simply cutting the conversation off. Model choice is a separate lever entirely; compaction changes what is sent, not which model receives it."}, {"id": "3.6-L2-02", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You've finished a task and are about to start something completely unrelated in the same Claude Code window. Why is /clear usually the better move than /compact here?", "options": ["/clear folds the history into a summary just as /compact does, but keeps a longer recent tail, so it preserves more of the old conversation and is the safer of the two resets", "/compact permanently deletes your transcript from disk while /clear keeps it", "There's no practical difference — both reset the context window the same way", "/clear drops the accumulated context entirely and costs no model call; /compact pays a model pass to summarize history you no longer need"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The two commands answer different questions. /compact is for when you must continue the same thread but the window is filling: it spends a model call reading the whole conversation to distill a summary, because continuity is worth paying for. /clear is for when continuity is worthless — a new, unrelated task: it simply empties the working context, instantly and for free, so your next turn starts from a lean prompt again (CLAUDE.md and other always-injected layers still load as ever). Compacting before an unrelated task buys you a summary you'll never use and pays full price for it. Neither command touches the durable transcript on disk — both operate on what gets assembled into future requests, not on the ledger. Starting a brand-new session is the third option, giving a fresh context plus a fresh session record."}, {"id": "3.6-L2-03", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You're starting a session that will likely run long enough to auto-compact, and one instruction ('all commit messages in English, imperative mood') must hold for the entire session. Where should it go?", "options": ["In your very first prompt, stated emphatically so the summary will keep it", "In CLAUDE.md, because it's re-injected into every request and therefore survives every compaction", "Repeated in every third message or so, to refresh the model's memory", "In settings.json, so the harness enforces it independent of context"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Compaction is documented to be lossy about early conversation content: 'specific instructions from early in the conversation may not be preserved.' Your opening prompt lives in the foldable tier — after enough turns it's exactly what gets summarized away, however emphatic it was. CLAUDE.md lives in the always-present tier: the harness re-injects it into every single request, so it exists after every fold by construction rather than by luck. That's the rule worth internalizing — durable instructions go in the durable layer, never the foldable one. Manually repeating yourself sort of works but wastes tokens and attention. settings.json fails for a different reason: it's harness-shaping configuration the model never sees, so a style rule there influences nothing. You can even add a 'when summarizing, preserve…' note to CLAUDE.md — the compactor reads it too."}, {"id": "3.6-L3-01", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 3, "question": "At the mechanism level, why must something like compaction exist in Claude Code at all?", "options": ["Because transcripts would otherwise grow past filesystem limits on long projects", "Because API providers enforce a hard cap on the number of messages per request — past a few hundred turns both Bedrock and the Anthropic API reject the payload, so the harness must fold older turns together to stay under it", "Because every turn resends the entire accumulated conversation into a finite context window — without a bound, cost grows roughly quadratically and the session eventually hits the window and cannot continue", "Because the model's attention decays with distance — past a few dozen turns the earliest messages contribute effectively nothing, so the harness must periodically re-encode them into a form attention can still reach"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "This follows directly from the stateless loop. Nothing persists server-side between turns, so the harness rebuilds and resends the whole conversation every turn. Two consequences compound: total session cost scales roughly with n squared in conversation length (each of n turns pays for everything before it), and the assembled context is hard-capped by the model's window. Unbounded accumulation therefore doesn't just get expensive — it hits the wall, and the session literally cannot proceed. Compaction is the one mechanism that bounds the growth, by folding old turns into a summary. Note the division of labor with caching: caching makes the resend cheap (reads at a fraction of base input price), but only compaction makes it bounded — a cached 190k-token prefix still occupies 190k tokens of window. You need both, on Bedrock exactly as on the Anthropic API."}, {"id": "3.6-L3-02", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Compaction saves money over the rest of a session, but it isn't free at the moment it runs. What is its immediate cost?", "options": ["A second, parallel session is spawned to hold the summary, doubling ambient cost until it exits", "The harness re-reads and re-uploads every file the session touched so the summarizer can check its summary against ground truth — on a large project that re-read is itself a substantial one-time input cost", "The durable transcript must be rewritten from scratch, which blocks the session while it runs", "The compactor must read essentially the whole conversation to write the summary — a full-context model call — and the rewritten prefix abandons the existing cache, forcing a fresh cache write"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Two costs, both real. First, summarization is itself a model call whose input is the very conversation you're trying to shrink — you pay a full read of the accumulated context one more time to produce the summary. Second, the fold rewrites the front of your conversation: old turns become a summary, so the previously cached prefix no longer matches and is abandoned; the post-fold context must be written to cache fresh. That's a deliberate one-time cache miss, paid once so that every future turn stops carrying the folded bulk. The trade is favorable exactly when the folded history was large. The wrong options misplace the work: the durable transcript is append-only (summary and boundary are appended, nothing rewritten), no files are re-uploaded, and no second session exists."}, {"id": "3.6-L3-03", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague says: 'Compaction deletes my old messages from the transcript on disk — that's how it frees up space.' What's the correction?", "options": ["Wrong on both counts: compaction manages the context window, not disk, and the durable .jsonl transcript is append-only — the fold appends a summary and a compact_boundary record; nothing is erased from disk", "Right about the deletion, but the freed resource is API quota rather than disk space", "Wrong — compaction doesn't touch the transcript because the summary is held only in the harness's in-memory working state; it vanishes when the process exits, which is why a resumed session always rebuilds the full pre-fold history", "Mostly right, except the deleted messages are first archived to a backup directory"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "This misconception collapses two distinct tiers of state. The durable tier — the .jsonl on your disk — is an append-only ledger; compaction adds records to it (the summary, plus a system message with subtype compact_boundary marking the fold) and removes none. What compaction changes is the reconstructed tier: the context the harness assembles into future requests, where the summary now stands in for the folded turns. So after compaction your ledger is intact and your working set is smaller — the opposite of a disk cleanup. Option C fails the other way: the summary is durable too, precisely so a resumed session reconstructs the post-fold state. Understanding compaction as a reconstruction-tier operation is what makes its other properties (transcript safety, the cache reset, resumability) fall out naturally."}, {"id": "3.6-L3-04", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Immediately after an auto-compaction, the next API call's usage shows a large cache write and near-zero cache read. Why?", "options": ["Auto-compaction always switches the session to a model that has no cache history yet", "Folding old turns into a summary rewrote the conversation's prefix, so the old cached prefix no longer matches — the post-fold context must be written to cache fresh", "The compaction call consumed the session's cache-read allowance for the current five-minute TTL window, so the next request bills as writes until the allowance resets at the next window boundary", "Compaction disables caching for one turn so the summary can't be poisoned by stale entries"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Prompt caching matches byte-for-byte from the start of the request. Compaction changes early tokens by construction — many old turns become one summary — so everything cached beyond that point is invalidated. The next request finds no matching prefix, reads almost nothing from cache, and writes the new post-fold prefix fresh: a large cache-creation figure and a tiny cache-read figure, exactly as observed. This is not a malfunction; it's the priced-in half of the compaction trade. You accept one full-price re-write so that every subsequent turn resends a bounded context instead of the ever-growing original. The distractors invent mechanisms that don't exist: there's no cache 'allowance,' no anti-poisoning bypass, and no model switch — the same model continues, on Bedrock or Anthropic alike, with a rewritten and much shorter prefix."}, {"id": "3.6-L4-01", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 4, "question": "You want to archive the complete, unfolded conversation state every time compaction is about to run — whether triggered automatically or by /compact. Which mechanism is designed for exactly this?", "options": ["A Stop hook, which fires when the session's context is about to be modified", "Polling the transcript for compact_boundary records and copying the file when one appears", "The PreCompact hook, which fires before the fold and receives a trigger field of 'manual' or 'auto'", "Setting cleanupPeriodDays to a large value so folded content is retained longer"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "PreCompact is the named seam for this: a hook the harness fires before it folds the conversation, with a trigger field telling you whether a user's /compact ('manual') or the near-limit automation ('auto') initiated it — your documented chance to snapshot or export state before older detail is summarized away. The distractors each fail on timing or category. A Stop hook fires when the model finishes a turn, not around compaction. Watching for compact_boundary records is reactive: by the time the boundary is on disk the fold has happened (and remember the transcript itself was never in danger — it's append-only; what you'd want to capture is any surrounding state before the working set shrinks). cleanupPeriodDays is retention configuration for old transcript files and has nothing to do with compaction. Hooks run harness-side at zero context cost, so this archive step is invisible to the model."}, {"id": "3.6-L4-02", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague argues: 'With prompt caching, the resend is nearly free — cache reads cost a fraction of base input. So compaction is pointless; we should just disable auto-compact.' What's wrong with this?", "options": ["Caching solves cost but not the bound: the context window is a hard cap that cached tokens still fill, so an uncompacted session eventually cannot continue at all — and cache reads are discounted, not free, on a resend that keeps growing", "Nothing is wrong — sessions with caching enabled genuinely never need compaction", "Caching and compaction are mutually exclusive; enabling one disables the other, so the choice is forced", "The argument holds on the Anthropic API, but on Bedrock prompt caching must be provisioned per model and region and is off by default — an unprovisioned Bedrock session pays full input price on every resend, so compaction remains the only cost lever there"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The colleague has half the picture. Caching attacks price: a stable prefix re-read from cache costs a small fraction of base input, which makes the every-turn resend affordable. But it does nothing to the window: a cached token still occupies its slot in the model's finite context, so an ever-growing conversation marches toward the ceiling regardless of how cheaply it gets there — and at the wall, the session simply cannot take another turn. Compaction attacks size: it bounds what's resent. The lesson's formulation is exact: caching makes the resend cheap; compaction makes it bounded; you need both. There's also a residual cost point — discounted reads on an unbounded, growing prefix still accumulate, and output pricing never had a cache discount. The Bedrock distractor is false: prompt caching is supported for Claude models on Bedrock, and compaction is harness logic that's provider-independent anyway."}, {"id": "3.7-L3-01", "topic": "3.7", "topicName": "Mid-session changes", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Mid-session, you edit the project's CLAUDE.md to add a new coding rule. When does the model see the change, and why?", "options": ["Never in this session — context files are resolved once at session initialization alongside the tool inventory and MCP connections, so edits surface only in a new or restarted session", "On the next turn — the harness rebuilds the request every turn and re-injects CLAUDE.md fresh, so the new content rides along on the next assembly", "Immediately, because the harness pushes a live update into the model's server-side session state", "Only after you run /compact, which is what re-reads context files"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "This is the stateless loop paying off as a prediction. There is no server-side session to push updates into — every turn, the harness reassembles the entire request from its durable sources, and CLAUDE.md is one of the layers it re-injects each time. Edit the file, and the very next assembly picks up the new text; no restart, no /compact, no special reload step. That per-turn re-injection is the same property that makes CLAUDE.md survive compaction: it isn't part of the foldable history at all, it's re-read from disk into every request. The 'frozen at session start' intuition belongs to a different class of configuration — things resolved per session rather than per turn — and applying it to CLAUDE.md gets this exactly backwards."}, {"id": "3.7-L3-02", "topic": "3.7", "topicName": "Mid-session changes", "difficulty": 3, "question": "What is a system-reminder in a Claude Code session?", "options": ["A scheduled notification the CLI renders in the terminal status line — harness-side UI that costs no tokens because it never enters the message stream the model receives", "A server-side flag the API provider attaches to responses when usage limits approach", "An entry the model writes to its own long-term memory to consult in later sessions", "A block of context the harness injects into the message stream — not typed by you — used to surface state changes and guidance to the model mid-conversation"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "system-reminders are one of the harness's channels for talking to the model inside the conversation. When something the model should know about changes or needs flagging — an edited context file, mode information, housekeeping guidance — the harness injects a system-reminder block into the message stream during assembly. Two properties matter mechanically. First, they're synthesized at assembly time rather than stored history: the durable transcript and the message array the model sees are different objects, and injected reminders are part of the difference. Second, they land in the request, so they cost tokens like any other content — this is request-shaping injection, not harness-side bookkeeping. They're not UI notifications, not provider metadata (the harness works the same against Bedrock), and the model has no writable long-term memory — that folk model dies at the stateless loop."}, {"id": "3.7-L3-03", "topic": "3.7", "topicName": "Mid-session changes", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Mid-session you make two changes: (a) you edit CLAUDE.md, and (b) you add a new MCP server to your configuration. Which change takes effect on the very next turn of the running session?", "options": ["Both — all configuration re-resolves on every turn", "The MCP server — tool schemas are re-fetched from every connected server at each request assembly so new tools appear immediately, while context files like CLAUDE.md are snapshotted into the session once at startup and never re-read", "The CLAUDE.md edit — context files are re-read into every request, whereas MCP servers are processes connected at session scope, so a new server generally isn't live until a new or restarted session picks it up", "Neither — both kinds of change require ending the session"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The useful split is per-turn versus per-session resolution. Content layers like CLAUDE.md are re-injected on every request assembly, so file edits surface on the next turn automatically. MCP servers are different in kind: they're external processes the harness launches or connects to, with handshakes and tool discovery — session-scoped setup, not per-turn file reads. Editing the config on disk doesn't reach into a running session and spawn the connection; the new server's tools become available when a session establishes it (a fresh session, or an explicit reconnect where the CLI offers one). The mnemonic: things assembled into the request re-resolve per turn; things that are infrastructure for the loop — connected servers, launched processes — resolve per session. When in doubt about whether a change 'took,' ask which side of that line it lives on."}, {"id": "3.7-L4-01", "topic": "3.7", "topicName": "Mid-session changes", "difficulty": 4, "question": "You edit CLAUDE.md mid-session in a long conversation. Beyond the model seeing the new rule next turn, what side effect should you expect on that next API call's cost profile?", "options": ["A cache miss from the CLAUDE.md layer onward: it sits early in the assembled request, so changing it invalidates the cached prefix behind it and forces a re-write of everything after that point", "No cost change — context files are exempt from caching, so editing them is always free", "A cheaper call, because the harness diffs the file and sends only the changed lines", "A one-turn fallback to an uncached request — the harness detects the edit, bypasses the cache for that single call, and the previously cached prefix resumes matching on the following turn once the content is stable again"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Prompt assembly follows a stability gradient — stable layers first, volatile content last — precisely because caching matches prefixes byte-for-byte from the start. CLAUDE.md is injected into the early, stable region of the request. Edit it and every cached token from that layer onward stops matching: the next call reads little from cache and re-writes the tail of the prefix fresh, which on a long session can be a substantial one-time cost. It's structurally the same event as compaction's cache reset — an early-region rewrite abandons the prefix — just triggered by you. There is no diffing; the stateless loop resends full content every turn, and caching is the only discount. The 'old cache resumes' option misunderstands matching: the old entries are simply never matched again, because the prefix has permanently changed. Batch your CLAUDE.md edits accordingly on long sessions."}, {"id": "3.7-L4-02", "topic": "3.7", "topicName": "Mid-session changes", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague says: 'When I --resume a session from yesterday, Claude Code restores it exactly — the next request is byte-identical to what it would have been had I never left.' What's the correction?", "options": ["Right in general — the harness persists the exact assembled request alongside the transcript precisely so resume can replay it — and wrong only if the session auto-compacted before you left, since the boundary record then forces a fresh rebuild of the request", "Wrong — resume is a reconstruction, not a restore: the harness replays the durable transcript but assembles the next request fresh, with the current date, current tool list, and freshly injected system-reminders, so it is not byte-identical", "Wrong — resume sends only a summary of the old session, never its actual messages", "Right — byte-identity is guaranteed by design so that the prompt cache always hits on resume"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "What persists is the durable transcript; what the model receives is always a fresh reconstruction. On --resume, the harness replays the .jsonl to rebuild in-memory conversation state, then assembles the next request under current conditions: today's date, the tool schemas as currently configured, the current system prompt, newly injected system-reminders. The conversation content carries over; the packaging is rebuilt now — so byte-identity fails at exactly the volatile spots. This has a practical corollary the last option gets backwards: far from guaranteeing cache hits, a long-idle resume tends to miss — the server-side cache was TTL-evicted hours ago, and the reconstructed prefix may no longer match what was cached anyway. Option C overcorrects: resume replays actual history, not a summary — that's compaction's trick, not resume's. Same behavior on Bedrock; reconstruction is harness logic."}, {"id": "3.8-L3-01", "topic": "3.8", "topicName": "Skill injection mechanics", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You have a dozen skills installed. In a turn where none of them is used, what part of the skill system is nonetheless present in the request?", "options": ["Nothing — skills contribute zero tokens until one is invoked", "Each skill's full body, pre-loaded so the model can act instantly when needed", "Each skill's name and description — the bodies stay out of context until a skill is actually triggered", "A single index token per skill, resolved server-side into the description on demand"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Skills use progressive disclosure, and the split is the whole design: the name-plus-description of every available skill is injected into every request, while the body (the SKILL.md instructions and any assets) loads only when the skill is invoked. The reason the descriptions must always be present is selection: the model can only choose to use a skill it can see, and at decision time the description is all it has. The reason bodies stay out is cost: loading every skill's full instructions ambiently would bloat every single request for capabilities mostly unused that turn. So the steady-state price of an installed skill is its description, paid on every call; the body's price is paid only on trigger. Options A and B each collapse one half of this two-tier scheme, and option D invents a server-side indirection the protocol doesn't have — the request payload is literal content."}, {"id": "3.8-L3-02", "topic": "3.8", "topicName": "Skill injection mechanics", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague installs thirty skills, uses about two of them, and argues: 'Unused skills are free — the body only loads when triggered.' What's the flaw?", "options": ["Every one of the thirty descriptions is injected into every request, so unused skills impose a small ambient token cost on every single API call — and skill-set churn perturbs the cached region they occupy", "Skills are genuinely free only at the project scope, where the harness lazy-loads them per trigger; user-scope skills under ~/.claude/skills preload their full bodies at session start so they can fire without a disk read", "The flaw is the opposite — even triggered skills cost nothing because skill content is exempt from token billing", "There is no flaw; descriptions are stored harness-side and never transmitted"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The colleague is right about bodies and wrong about the ambient layer. Progressive disclosure means the expensive part (the body) is deferred — but the descriptions of all thirty skills ride along in every request so the model can choose among them. Thirty descriptions is a modest but real tax on every call, forever, multiplied by every turn of every session. And because that description block is part of the request's stable region, installing or removing skills changes those bytes and disturbs the cached prefix — a churn cost on top of the ambient one. The right mental model: an installed skill's rent is its description; its usage fee is its body. Prune skills you don't use, exactly as you'd prune unused MCP servers whose tool schemas also bill ambiently. Nothing here is harness-side storage — skills are request-shaping by definition, since the model must see them to pick them."}, {"id": "3.8-L3-03", "topic": "3.8", "topicName": "Skill injection mechanics", "difficulty": 3, "question": "In ambient token cost — what you pay on requests where the feature isn't used — how do skills and custom slash commands differ?", "options": ["They're identical — both keep a description resident in context at all times", "Commands are more expensive ambiently because their full file stays loaded; skills load nothing until invoked", "Skills cost ambient tokens only in sessions where they have fired at least once — the description loads on first use; commands bill a small fixed header on every request so the parser can match /name against them", "A command costs zero ambient tokens (its content enters the messages only when you type /name); a skill's description is present in every request so the model can trigger it itself"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The ambient-cost difference falls straight out of who does the triggering. A custom command is user-triggered: the harness needs nothing in context ahead of time, because you typing /name is the signal — only then is the command's content injected into the messages. Zero ambient tokens. A skill is model-triggered: for the model to decide 'this task matches that skill,' the skill's name and description must already be in the request — so every installed skill bills its description on every call, used or not. That's the price of autonomy: you pay a standing description tax to let the model self-select, versus paying nothing and doing the selection yourself. Practical corollary: workflows you always invoke explicitly are cheaper as commands; workflows the model should recognize and reach for on its own justify a skill's ambient cost."}, {"id": "3.8-L4-01", "topic": "3.8", "topicName": "Skill injection mechanics", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Two skills have identical, well-written bodies. One triggers reliably at the right moments; the other almost never fires. At the mechanism level, what most likely explains the difference?", "options": ["The reliable skill was installed first, and earlier-loaded skills get scheduling priority in the harness", "The quality of the frontmatter description — at decision time the model sees only names and descriptions, so a vague description gives it nothing to match the task against, no matter how good the unseen body is", "The failing skill's body exceeds the assembler's per-skill size budget, so the harness silently drops it from the ambient layer at assembly time — the model never sees the skill exists, which is why it never fires no matter how well the description is written", "The reliable skill registered a keyboard shortcut, which is the primary trigger path for skills"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Progressive disclosure has a sharp corollary: the body is invisible until after the trigger decision is made. When the model surveys what it could do with your request, its entire knowledge of each skill is the name and description sitting in context. A description that states concretely when to use the skill — trigger phrases, task shapes, 'use when the user asks X' — gives the model a pattern to match; a vague one ('helps with documents') matches nothing, so the brilliant body never loads. This is why description-writing is the highest-leverage part of skill authoring, and why skill tooling focuses on optimizing descriptions for triggering accuracy. The distractors invent mechanisms outside the design: there's no install-order priority or shortcut path for model-triggered skills, and a body's size is irrelevant to triggering precisely because the body isn't in context yet."}, {"id": "3.8-L4-02", "topic": "3.8", "topicName": "Skill injection mechanics", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague reasons: 'Skill bodies load on trigger, so every time a skill fires it must rewrite the front of the prompt — that's a cache reset per invocation, just like compaction.' What's the correction?", "options": ["Correct — the body is spliced in ahead of the conversation history so the model reads instructions before context, and that splice rewrites the request from the descriptions block onward, abandoning the cached prefix on every single invocation — which is why heavyweight skills are expensive", "Backwards — bodies live in the stable head and descriptions arrive per-invocation in the tail", "Half wrong: the body is injected into the message flow at invoke time — appended content in the volatile region, like any new turn — whereas it's changing the installed skill set (the always-present descriptions) that perturbs the stable, cached region", "Wrong only because skill bodies are transmitted out-of-band and never enter the context at all"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The colleague has the two tiers of the skill system mapped to the wrong ends of the request. The assembly follows a stability gradient: stable, always-present material (system prompt, tool schemas, skill descriptions, CLAUDE.md) sits at the front where caching can hold it; per-turn material lands toward the volatile end. A skill body arrives when the skill is invoked — it's injected into the ongoing message flow at that point, which is append-like: the existing cached prefix ahead of it still matches, exactly as when any tool result or user message is added. No compaction-style prefix rewrite occurs. What does disturb the cached head is churn in the always-present layer — installing, removing, or editing skills changes the description block that's part of every request's front matter. So: invoking a skill is cheap cache-wise but pays the body's tokens from then on in that conversation; changing your skill set is the cache event."}, {"id": "3.5-L1-03", "topic": "3.5", "topicName": "Permissions", "difficulty": 1, "question": "You want Claude Code to explore an unfamiliar repository with zero risk of it changing files or running destructive commands. Which permission mode is designed for exactly this?", "options": ["Accept-edits mode, which shows every proposed edit for review without ever applying it", "Plan mode, which restricts Claude to read-only exploration — it can search and read, but not mutate anything", "Bypass-permissions mode, which routes every action through an extra safety check first", "Default mode after running /clear, so there is no accumulated context to act on"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Plan mode is the harness's read-only dial: Claude can roam the codebase — searching, reading, building an understanding — but the harness withholds the mutating tools, so nothing can be edited or destructively executed. That's what makes it the safe way to let the agent explore unfamiliar code. The other modes point the opposite direction: accept-edits auto-approves file edits (it reduces prompting, it doesn't prevent changes), and bypass-permissions skips prompting entirely — it's the least protective mode, meant for disposable environments like containers, not for caution. /clear is a context operation, not a permission one: it empties the conversation but changes nothing about what tools may run. Like all permission machinery, modes live in the harness on your machine, so they behave identically whether you're on Bedrock or the Anthropic API."}, {"id": "3.5-L1-04", "topic": "3.5", "topicName": "Permissions", "difficulty": 1, "question": "At a permission prompt, you pick the option to allow this command 'always' so you're never asked about it again. What did that choice actually do?", "options": ["Set a temporary flag that lasts until you close the terminal window", "Taught the model your preference, so it remembers it in future sessions", "Turned off permission prompts entirely for this project going forward", "Wrote a durable allow rule into a settings file, so future matching calls are pre-approved"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Answering 'always' is rule-writing: the harness appends an allow rule for that command pattern to a settings file, and from then on the rule matches before any prompt is needed. That's why the choice survives restarting the terminal — it's on disk, not in session memory. It is not model memory: the model is stateless and never receives permission rules at all, so nothing was 'taught.' And it is far narrower than disabling prompts — only calls matching that specific rule are pre-approved; anything else still triggers the gate as usual. This is the intended workflow: if you find yourself approving the same command every day, promote the decision into a durable rule instead of re-answering the prompt. The finer point of exactly which settings file receives the rule comes later — the beginner takeaway is: 'always' means a rule was written."}, {"id": "3.5-L2-03", "topic": "3.5", "topicName": "Permissions", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You script claude -p \"...\" in a CI pipeline, and the run stops partway when the model proposes a command not covered by any rule. Why, and what's the fix?", "options": ["Headless runs have no human to answer permission prompts, so unmatched actions can't be interactively approved — configure the allowlist or a non-prompting permission mode up front", "The -p flag disables all tools by default, so any tool call fails until you re-enable them with a flag", "CI machines lack the per-user approval cache under ~/.claude where your interactive 'always allow' answers are stored, so you must run one interactive session on the runner first to warm it before headless runs will succeed", "Bedrock-backed sessions require IAM approval for each new command pattern, so the pattern must be added to the role policy"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The permission gate works identically in headless mode — what's missing is the fallback. Interactively, an unmatched tool call pauses the loop and asks you; with claude -p there is nobody to ask, so the run stops at the gate. Non-interactive runs therefore need their permission decisions made in advance: allow rules covering everything the task legitimately needs, or a permission mode that doesn't prompt (bypass-permissions being acceptable precisely in isolated CI environments, where the blast radius is bounded). The -p flag changes input/output behavior, not tool availability. Past approvals do persist — but as rules in settings files, which you can ship to CI; there's no separate 'approval cache' to warm up. And the IAM option is a category confusion: IAM governs whether your AWS credentials may call the Bedrock API at all, while tool permissions are local harness policy, identical on every provider."}, {"id": "3.5-L2-04", "topic": "3.5", "topicName": "Permissions", "difficulty": 2, "question": "File-edit approvals are drowning you in prompts, but you still want to personally approve every shell command. Which setup fits?", "options": ["Bypass-permissions mode — it approves edits automatically while still prompting for Bash", "Plan mode — it lets file edits through silently while keeping commands gated", "Accept-edits mode — it auto-approves file edits while other actions, like commands, still go through the normal gate", "Default mode plus an allow rule for Bash(*) — that removes the noisiest prompts"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Accept-edits mode exists for exactly this asymmetry: file edits are the routine bulk of permission prompts, so the mode auto-approves them while leaving everything else — notably shell commands — under the normal ask behavior. The distractors each break one half of the requirement. Bypass-permissions doesn't discriminate: it skips prompting for everything, commands included, which is why it belongs only in isolated, disposable environments. Plan mode points the opposite way — it's the read-only mode, so edits aren't silently allowed, they're not allowed at all. And allowing Bash(*) is precisely backwards: it silences command prompts (the ones you wanted to keep) while doing nothing about edit prompts. The underlying model: modes shift where the prompting threshold sits without touching the rule machinery — accept-edits moves edits below the threshold and leaves commands above it."}, {"id": "3.5-L3-04", "topic": "3.5", "topicName": "Permissions", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague says: 'Sandboxing is just another name for the allow/deny rules — one mechanism, two doc pages.' What's the correction?", "options": ["Close — sandboxing is the deny list specifically, while 'permissions' properly refers to the allow and ask lists", "They're different layers answering different questions: rules are harness policy on whether a tool call may run at all; the sandbox is OS-level containment on what a running command can touch — and they compose", "Backwards — once sandboxing is enabled it replaces the rules entirely, because the operating system takes over policy from the harness", "They're the same gate under two names: 'permissions' is the label for the interactive prompting surface, while 'sandboxing' is what the identical rule engine is called when it runs non-interactively — in CI pipelines or against Bedrock"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Two layers, two questions. Permission rules sit at the harness's gate and decide 'may this tool call execute at all?' — policy, evaluated before anything runs. Sandboxing assumes the call runs and uses operating-system isolation primitives to bound what it can do: restricting filesystem writes outside the workspace and limiting network access. Because the layers are independent, they compose into defense in depth — a command the rules permit can still be unable to write outside the project, because the OS stops it underneath. That composition has a practical payoff: when the harness knows a command is confined, it can afford to prompt less, since even a bad command has a bounded blast radius — sandboxing reduces prompt fatigue rather than adding friction. Neither layer replaces the other, and neither is provider-specific: both run locally, identical on Bedrock and the Anthropic API."}, {"id": "3.5-L3-05", "topic": "3.5", "topicName": "Permissions", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You're in accept-edits mode, yet one particular file edit still got blocked. A colleague concludes the mode is broken. What's the more likely explanation?", "options": ["A deny rule matched the action — modes only shift where the ask threshold sits; explicit rules still exist underneath, and a deny is enforced in every mode", "Accept-edits mode only covers files that existed when the session started; edits that create new files always go through the full gate", "The mode silently reset — modes are per-turn state, and a system-reminder switched the session back to default between your turns", "The file's content sat in the cached region of the prompt, and cached content can't be modified until the cache entry expires"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Modes and rules are different instruments on the same gate. A mode is a session-level dial over the ask behavior — accept-edits moves routine edits below the prompting threshold. Rules are durable configuration underneath, and they resolve deny-wins: a deny from any settings tier blocks the matching call outright, in any mode. So a blocked edit under accept-edits usually means a deny rule (perhaps from the project or a managed tier) matched that path — the mode was never in a position to override it, because modes adjust asking, not deny enforcement. The distractors misapply real mechanisms: there's no existing-files-only carve-out; system-reminders inform the model about state, they don't flip permission modes between turns; and the prompt cache lives server-side as attention state over request bytes — it has no connection to whether a local file may be edited."}, {"id": "3.5-L3-06", "topic": "3.5", "topicName": "Permissions", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague answers a permission prompt with 'always allow' and claims: 'That just set an in-memory flag in the harness — nothing on disk changed.' What actually happened, and where?", "options": ["A rule was appended to the project's shared .claude/settings.json — the tier permission prompts always write to — so the whole team inherits the approval on their next git pull, which is why teams review that file in PRs", "The colleague is right — 'always' persists only for the running session and is forgotten when it ends", "A durable allow rule was appended to a settings file — typically the project's .claude/settings.local.json, which stays out of version control — making the approval personal and permanent", "A rule was appended to ~/.claude/settings.json, making the approval global across every project on the machine"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "'Always' is rule-writing, and where the rule lands is the load-bearing detail. The harness appends it to a settings file — typically the project's settings.local.json, the tier designed to be personal and gitignored. That placement threads two needles: the approval is durable (unlike a session-scoped answer, which is what the prompt's 'for this session' option gives you), yet it's scoped to you and this project. The near-miss tiers are wrong in instructive ways: writing to the shared project settings.json would silently push your personal approval onto every teammate through version control, and writing to the user-level file would apply it to every project on your machine — both broader than what you consented to at the prompt. Because the rule is just a settings entry, you can inspect it, edit it, or deliberately promote it to a shared tier later — the prompt simply automated the first write."}, {"id": "3.6-L2-04", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You run /compact focus on the database schema decisions — with that trailing text after the command. What does it do?", "options": ["Filters the transcript on disk so only the database-related messages are retained", "Restricts the fold to messages mentioning the database, leaving all other turns untouched in context", "Sets a persistent session topic that every future auto-compaction will also follow", "Runs a normal compaction, but passes your text as guidance so the summary emphasizes and preserves that content"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "/compact accepts focus instructions: the fold proceeds as usual — older history is summarized, recent exchanges kept — but your trailing text steers the summarizer toward what to preserve, so the schema decisions survive in the summary rather than being averaged away with everything else. The distractors misplace the effect. It's not a disk operation: the durable transcript is append-only and compaction only ever appends to it (summary plus boundary marker). It's not selective folding: the fold still covers the older history as a whole; your text shapes the summary's emphasis, not which messages get folded. And it's one-shot guidance for this fold, not persistent state — if you want standing guidance that every compaction honors, the documented lever is a 'when summarizing, preserve…' note in CLAUDE.md, which the compactor reads each time it runs."}, {"id": "3.6-L2-05", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 2, "question": "In a typical coding session, which contributor tends to fill the context window fastest — and therefore brings auto-compaction closer?", "options": ["Your own prompts, since user messages are kept at higher fidelity than other content", "Tool outputs — file reads, search results, command output — which the loop appends in full and then resends every turn", "The system prompt, which the harness grows a little on every turn to track session state", "The model's replies, and extended thinking especially — output tokens count double against the window, so verbose reasoning fills it fastest"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Context grows with everything the loop appends — prompts, replies, tool inputs — but the big one is tool outputs. A single file read can add thousands of tokens; a broad search or verbose test run, more; and because the stateless loop resends the whole conversation each turn, every one of those tokens is carried forward on every subsequent call. That's why exploration-heavy sessions hit auto-compaction far sooner than chat-heavy ones. The distractors miss the accounting: user prompts are usually tiny by comparison and nothing is stored at 'higher fidelity'; the system prompt is a stable, re-injected layer, not an accumulating one; and everything in the assembled request counts against the window, not just replies. The practical lever follows directly: push heavy exploration into subagents, whose tool outputs stay in a separate loop and return only a summary — keeping the parent's window lean and the fold further away. And no token counts double against the window — thinking or not, each token occupies exactly one slot."}, {"id": "3.6-L2-06", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What actually causes auto-compaction to trigger in a session?", "options": ["The assembled context approaching the model's window limit", "The session passing a fixed number of conversation turns", "The transcript file on disk exceeding a size threshold", "A timer — sessions compact automatically after a period of inactivity"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Auto-compaction is window-pressure driven: as the context that would be assembled into the next request nears the model's finite window, the harness folds older history into a summary so the session can continue. (The exact trigger threshold is deliberately undocumented — what's documented is the mechanism, not the number.) The wrong options each measure the wrong thing. Turn count is uncorrelated with size: ten turns of file reads can outweigh a hundred turns of chat. Disk size is the wrong tier entirely — compaction manages the reconstructed context sent in requests, while the durable transcript is append-only and never the constraint. And there's no idle timer; an untouched session consumes no window. Since the window is a property of the model and compaction is harness logic, the behavior is identical against Bedrock and the Anthropic API — same weights, same window, same fold."}, {"id": "3.6-L3-05", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 3, "question": "To check whether compaction has ever run, a colleague greps their transcript .jsonl files for 'compact_boundary', gets several hits, and declares proof. What's the flaw?", "options": ["There is none — that string only ever appears inside genuine boundary records, so any hit is proof", "The flaw is the tool: transcript files are stored in a binary encoding, so grep cannot see real records at all", "Grep matches substrings, not records: a conversation that merely discussed compaction contains the word in message text — you must parse structurally and check for a system record with subtype compact_boundary", "The flaw is the location: boundary markers live in a separate per-session index file the harness keeps alongside the transcript, so any hit inside the .jsonl itself can only be quoted message text rather than a real boundary record"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A real fold leaves a structural artifact: a record with type \"system\" and subtype \"compact_boundary\" appended to the transcript. But grep finds substrings anywhere — and a session where you talked about compaction (reading docs, writing a quiz about it) contains the literal string inside ordinary message text, with no boundary record behind it. The two are indistinguishable to grep and trivially distinguishable to a parser that checks the record's type and subtype fields. So hits alone prove only mention, not event. The other options fail on facts: transcripts are plain JSONL, perfectly greppable — that's exactly why the false positive is easy to hit; and the boundary marker does live in the transcript stream, not a side index. The transferable habit: when a grep looks like evidence about structured logs, confirm it's a record, not a mention, before building a conclusion on it."}, {"id": "3.6-L3-06", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You keep a running task list inside the conversation and want it to survive every auto-compaction. Which lever is actually documented to influence what the fold preserves?", "options": ["A PreCompact hook that receives the summarizer's draft on stdin and can rewrite it before the fold is appended — its trigger field says whether the fold was manual or auto", "Pinning the task-list messages with a /pin command, which the compactor must carry verbatim", "Setting a compaction ratio in settings.json so that less of the history is dropped per fold", "Adding a 'when compacting, always preserve the current task list' note to CLAUDE.md — the compactor reads your memory files and follows that guidance"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The compactor reads the conversation including your CLAUDE.md when producing its summary, and a free-form 'when summarizing, preserve…' section is the documented way to steer every fold — it matches on intent, so the task list gets carried into each summary. For a one-off fold you can also pass focus text to /compact directly. The distractors are near-misses worth separating. PreCompact is real, but it's an observation seam: it fires before the fold with a trigger field of manual or auto, giving you a chance to archive state — it isn't an editor for the summarizer's output. There is no /pin command, and no documented ratio setting — the fold's thresholds are explicitly undocumented. The stronger move for anything that must always hold: put it in CLAUDE.md as content, since that layer is re-injected into every request and survives every fold by construction, guidance or no guidance."}, {"id": "3.6-L3-07", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A session auto-compacted yesterday; today you --resume it. What conversation state does the model see on your first new turn?", "options": ["The full pre-fold history — resume replays the append-only transcript record by record, and since compaction never erases anything from disk, every original message is still there to be rebuilt into context", "The post-fold working set — the summary plus the recent tail — because the fold's summary and boundary marker are durable records, and reconstruction honors them instead of replaying the folded turns", "Only the summary — resume drops the recent tail as well, to be conservative about the window", "A freshly re-summarized version — resume always re-runs compaction over the whole transcript before continuing"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Keep the two tiers separate. The durable transcript really does still contain every pre-fold message — compaction is append-only on disk — and that's precisely the expert trap in the first option: true premise, wrong conclusion. Resume is a reconstruction, not a raw replay: the harness rebuilds the working state the session left behind, and because the fold appended its summary and a compact_boundary marker as durable records, reconstruction assembles the post-fold state — summary standing in for the folded turns, recent tail intact. The boundary marker is what tells the assembler where the fold sits. This is exactly why the summary is persisted at all: so a resumed session continues from where compaction left it rather than resurrecting the oversized pre-fold context. No new summarization pass runs on resume, and the tail isn't dropped — resume neither redoes nor deepens the fold; it honors it."}, {"id": "3.6-L3-08", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Why is letting auto-compact fire in the middle of a complex task considered the worst timing, compared with running /compact yourself at a natural boundary?", "options": ["Mid-task, the details you still need are exactly the 'older history' at risk of being folded, and you forgo the control a manual compact gives you — timing and focus instructions; at a boundary, what's summarized away is finished work", "Auto-compact uses a smaller, cheaper summarizer model than /compact does, so its summaries are systematically lower quality", "Auto-compact fires from inside the loop between tool calls, where hooks cannot run — so it skips PreCompact entirely and a mid-task fold can never be archived first, whereas manual /compact runs from the prompt line where hooks are live and always fire", "Mid-task folds risk corrupting the transcript's record ordering on disk, which manual compaction is designed to avoid"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The fold mechanism is identical either way — same summarizer, same lossiness. What differs is what the loss lands on. Compaction is documented to be lossy about earlier conversation content, and mid-task, 'earlier content' includes constraints, decisions, and partial results your current work still depends on; a fold at that moment can quietly remove exactly what you need next. At a natural boundary, the foldable material is completed work whose summary suffices. Manual /compact adds two controls auto-compact can't: you pick the moment, and you can pass focus instructions to steer what the summary keeps. The distractors invent differences that don't exist: no separate cheaper summarizer model is documented, PreCompact fires for both paths — its trigger field distinguishing 'manual' from 'auto' exists precisely because both invoke it — and the transcript is append-only, never at risk of corruption from a fold. Compacting deliberately is context hygiene; compacting by accident is context roulette."}, {"id": "3.6-L4-03", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague adopts a habit of running /compact every ten turns: 'a small context means cheap turns, so compacting constantly minimizes total cost.' What's wrong with the math?", "options": ["Nothing — on the 1-hour cache TTL, prefix rewrites are free, so frequent folds cost only the summary's output tokens", "Compaction's price is per-session, not per-fold: after the first expensive fold, later folds reuse the same summary at no extra model cost", "Each fold pays a full-price read of the whole conversation plus a fresh cache write of the rewritten prefix, and abandons cheap ~0.1x cache reads — frequent small folds repeat those costs for little size relief, and every pass loses detail", "It's correct against the Anthropic API but backwards on Bedrock, where cache writes are the discounted operation and reads carry the premium — AWS prices for storage rather than retrieval, so frequent small folds actually get cheaper there, not costlier"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Compaction has a real per-fold price, and it's the same price whether the fold was needed or not: the summarization pass must read essentially the entire conversation at full input rates (little of it is cacheable), and the rewritten prefix abandons the existing cache, so the next request pays a cache write at a premium (1.25x base input at the 5-minute TTL) instead of ~0.1x reads. That trade is favorable when the folded bulk is large and many turns remain; folding every ten turns pays the fixed costs repeatedly for marginal size relief — and stacks lossiness, since each summary is a summary of a summary. The traps: 1-hour-TTL writes are more expensive than 5-minute writes (2x base), not free; there's no per-session amortization — every fold is its own model call; and Bedrock doesn't invert cache economics — writes carry the premium and reads the discount on both providers."}, {"id": "3.6-L4-04", "topic": "3.6", "topicName": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "difficulty": 4, "question": "The compaction pass must re-read the entire conversation to write its summary — yet your session's long prefix is already sitting in the prompt cache. Why is that pass billed as an expensive full read rather than cheap cache reads?", "options": ["Because the harness applies the fold first and summarizes second: by the time the summarizer reads the conversation, the prefix has already been rewritten around the compact_boundary marker, so nothing left in the cache matches the bytes it is handed", "Because prompt caches are scoped to a single conversation thread, and the compactor runs as a separate thread under its own cache namespace", "Because the harness deliberately bypasses the cache for summarization, to guarantee the summary isn't produced from stale cached content", "Because caching matches byte-identical prefixes from position zero, and the summarization request is assembled differently from the session's ordinary requests — little of it lines up with the cached prefix, so the read bills at full input price"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Cache hits require the new request to reproduce the cached bytes exactly, from the very start. The session's cache entries correspond to the session's own assembly — its system prompt, tool schemas, then history. The summarization call is a differently shaped request: the same history wrapped in different instructions and structure, so the byte-for-byte prefix match fails early and the bulk of the read bills at full input price. That's why the fold's first cost is one full pass over everything being folded. The first option is an order trap: the summary is produced before the fold exists — the prefix rewrite is a consequence of the summarization, not something that precedes its read. The others invent mechanisms: caches aren't namespaced per conversation thread (any request reproducing the prefix can hit), and there's no staleness bypass — the cache stores attention state over exact bytes, so 'stale content' isn't a failure mode that needs dodging."}, {"id": "3.7-L3-04", "topic": "3.7", "topicName": "Mid-session changes", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Mid-session, you run /model and switch to a different Claude model. What happens?", "options": ["The session restarts with fresh context, because a conversation is bound to the model that started it", "The very next API call goes to the new model carrying the entire existing conversation — model choice is just a field the harness sets per request, and the resend delivers full history to whichever model receives it", "The switch is queued until the next compaction, when the summary can be handed to the new model cleanly", "New turns go to the new model, but tool_results answering calls the old model emitted still route back to it — the loop keeps each tool_use/tool_result pair on the model that opened it, for consistency of that exchange, before switching over fully"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "This is the stateless loop paying off as a prediction. There is no server-side conversation bound to any model — every turn, the harness reassembles the full request from scratch and stamps a model identifier on it. /model changes that per-request field, so the next call simply targets the new model, which reads the entire resent history exactly as any model does on any turn: for the first time. Nothing needs restarting, queuing, or migrating, and there's no split routing — the loop has one outbound call per iteration, and every part of it goes to the currently selected model. One cost caveat worth knowing: prompt caches are model-specific, so the first call after a switch typically misses the cache and pays a fresh write of the prefix on the new model. On Bedrock the mechanics are identical — you're just switching which model ID the harness puts on the request."}, {"id": "3.7-L3-05", "topic": "3.7", "topicName": "Mid-session changes", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Mid-session, you add a new deny rule to your settings. When does it start biting, and what does the change cost in tokens?", "options": ["It gates the very next matching tool call, and costs zero tokens — permission rules are consulted at each gate check and never enter the request payload", "It takes effect at the next session, and costs zero tokens — settings files are snapshotted once when the session starts", "It gates the next tool call, at the cost of a small system-reminder injection so the model knows to stop proposing the blocked command", "It takes effect immediately, at the cost of a one-time cache rewrite, since permission rules hash into the request's stable prefix"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Permissions sit on the per-turn side of the cadence line: the harness consults the rules at each gate check, when a tool_use actually arrives, so a rule added mid-session governs the very next matching call — no restart needed. And because permissions are harness-shaping configuration, they never cross the wire: zero tokens, zero cache effect, invisible to the model. The near-misses each borrow a real mechanism from an adjacent surface. Session-start snapshotting is true of the tool inventory and MCP connections, not of permission rules. System-reminders are a real channel, but enforcement doesn't depend on one — the harness blocks the call regardless, and the model discovers the boundary through the rejection that comes back as its tool_result. And nothing about rules is 'hashed into the prefix' — the request payload doesn't contain them, which is precisely why editing them is the free kind of mid-session change."}, {"id": "3.7-L3-06", "topic": "3.7", "topicName": "Mid-session changes", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Mid-session, you drop a new skill directory into ~/.claude/skills. Can the model trigger that skill in this same session?", "options": ["Yes, from the next turn — the assembler re-scans the skill directories at every request assembly and re-collects names and descriptions, the same way CLAUDE.md is re-read from disk and re-injected each turn", "Yes, but only after the next compaction, which rebuilds the request's stable head with the new description included", "Generally not — the skill and plugin inventory is established at session initialization, like the tool set, so a new skill becomes available in a new or restarted session", "No, and not next session either, until the skill is also registered under enabledPlugins in settings.json"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Mid-session-change questions always reduce to cadence: is the surface re-resolved per turn, or fixed at session start? Content layers like CLAUDE.md are re-read into every request — that's the per-turn side. But the loaded skill/plugin inventory is infrastructure for the loop, resolved when the session initializes alongside the tool schemas and MCP connections; the assembler doesn't re-scan skill directories every turn. So the new skill's description won't appear in this session's ambient layer, and the model can't trigger what it can't see — a new or restarted session picks it up. The first option is the classic near-miss: applying CLAUDE.md's per-turn cadence to a session-scoped surface. Compaction is irrelevant — it folds history, it doesn't re-run configuration resolution. And enabledPlugins governs plugins, not ordinary skill directories; no such registration gate applies here."}, {"id": "3.7-L4-03", "topic": "3.7", "topicName": "Mid-session changes", "difficulty": 4, "question": "System-reminders are consistently injected near the tail of the message array rather than folded into the system prompt at the head. Why is that placement load-bearing?", "options": ["Because models weight recent tokens most heavily, and reminders must outrank the system prompt to be reliably obeyed", "Because the Messages API enforces strict user/assistant role alternation and rejects system-role content mid-array, so harness-authored state updates are only legal when embedded in the latest user-role message — which is necessarily at the tail", "Because tail tokens are billed at cache-read rates while head tokens bill at full input price, making the tail the economical place to inject", "Because caching matches from the start of the request: appending at the tail leaves the cached head byte-stable, while mutating the system layer would invalidate the prefix and force an expensive rewrite every time state changed"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "System-reminders exist to solve a genuine tension: the model is stateless, so fresh, volatile state must be delivered inside the request — but prompt caching only pays off if the request's head stays byte-identical turn over turn. Injecting near the tail resolves both: the reminder reaches the model, while everything ahead of it still matches the cached prefix, exactly like appending any new user message or tool result. Delivering the same information by editing the system layer would rewrite the stable head and abandon the cache on every state change — precisely the expensive class of mid-session edit. The traps: recency effects in attention are real but are not the documented design driver here — placement is a cache decision, not a psychology one. No role-alternation rule forces this; the harness shapes the message array freely. And the third option inverts the economics — the cached head is where the cheap ~0.1x reads happen; fresh tail tokens bill as new input."}, {"id": "3.7-L4-04", "topic": "3.7", "topicName": "Mid-session changes", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague declares: 'Any mid-session configuration change invalidates the prompt cache, so freeze all config the moment a long session starts.' What's the accurate cost picture?", "options": ["Close — every surface except permissions invalidates the cache, because rules are the one piece of config the payload never carries, while all other edits rewrite the prefix", "Wrong twice over: harness-only changes (rules, hooks, modes) never touch tokens or cache at all; tail-delivered updates cost their own tokens but preserve the cached head; only rewrites of the stable head — the CLAUDE.md layer, the tool set — abandon the prefix", "Inverted — mid-session edits are free across the board, because the cache keys only on the system prompt, and none of these surfaces ever modify what that parameter contains", "The invalidation claim holds, but the remedy is wrong: the cache re-forms on the very next turn at the discounted ~0.1x read rate rather than the write premium, so even constant config churn has its total cost bounded at one cheap re-read per edit — freezing config buys nothing measurable"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Rank every mid-session edit by where it lands. Class one, harness-only: permission rules, hooks, mode switches — never transmitted, so zero tokens and zero cache effect; change these freely. Class two, tail-delivered: system-reminders, new messages, tool results — they cost their own tokens but append after the cached head, leaving the prefix intact. Class three, stable-head rewrites: editing content in the CLAUDE.md/system layer or changing the tool set perturbs the prefix, and the next request pays a fresh cache write (1.25x base at the 5-minute TTL) for everything after the edit point instead of ~0.1x reads. So 'freeze everything' overcorrects — only class three is priced, and even that is a one-time miss, often worth it when deliberate. The first option flips the exception into the rule; the third pretends class three away; the fourth gets the re-forming right but the arithmetic wrong — the recovery write is the premium operation, and repeated churn pays it repeatedly."}, {"id": "3.8-L3-04", "topic": "3.8", "topicName": "Skill injection mechanics", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A skill triggers at turn 10 of a session, injecting its roughly 3,000-token body. What happens to that body over turns 11, 12, 13, and beyond?", "options": ["It is now part of the conversation history, so the stateless loop resends it on every subsequent turn — billed (at cache-read rates once stable) until a compaction folds it or /clear drops it", "The harness prunes it from the assembly once the skill's task completes — bodies are tagged as ephemeral content at injection, so the next rebuild drops them and the context returns to its pre-trigger size", "It is resent only on turns where the model actively uses the skill; idle turns skip it to save tokens", "It migrates into the stable head after one turn, joining the descriptions so that future triggers of the same skill are free"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Skill bodies are loaded on demand, but they don't unload on their own. Once injected into the message flow, the body is conversation history like anything else — and the stateless loop resends the whole history every turn. So a 3,000-token body invoked at turn 10 is carried on turns 11, 12, 13... typically at cache-read rates once the prefix over it stabilizes, until a compaction folds it into a summary or /clear empties the context. The distractors each require machinery that doesn't exist. The harness has no notion of a skill's task 'completing,' and history isn't editable retroactively — content woven in stays in. Nor is assembly selective per turn: the request carries the accumulated conversation, not a per-turn curation of which past pieces seem relevant. And nothing promotes into the stable head — the ambient layer holds descriptions only; bodies always live in the foldable flow. Invoke heavyweight skills with this tail cost in mind."}, {"id": "3.8-L3-05", "topic": "3.8", "topicName": "Skill injection mechanics", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Skills can trigger two ways: the model decides to use one, or you type its slash name. How do the two paths differ mechanically?", "options": ["User-triggered skills run inside a subagent, while model-triggered ones run inline in the main loop", "Model-triggered skills load only the body, while user-triggered ones also inject the description alongside it for grounding", "Only the decision differs: model-triggering relies on the ambient description matching the task, while typing the name forces the load directly — after the trigger, both converge on the same mechanics of injecting the body and proceeding with it in context", "User-triggered skills bypass the model's decision at the mechanical level too: the harness parses the slash name, executes the skill's instructions itself as a script, and injects only the finished output into the conversation as a tool_result for the model to build on"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Two doors into one room. On the model-triggered path, the model surveys the ambient name-and-description layer, judges that the task matches a skill, and invokes it; on the user-triggered path, typing the slash name is the signal, and no description matching is involved at all — which is why an explicit invocation works even when a description is too vague for the model to ever pick the skill itself. From the trigger onward the paths are identical: the harness loads the body into the message flow, and the model proceeds with those instructions in context. The distractors invent asymmetries: whether a skill delegates to a subagent is a property of that skill's design, not of which door was used; descriptions are already ambient in every request regardless; and the harness never executes skill instructions itself — a skill body is content for the model to follow, not code for the harness to run."}, {"id": "3.8-L3-06", "topic": "3.8", "topicName": "Skill injection mechanics", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Skills' progressive disclosure — descriptions always in context, bodies loaded only on trigger — has a close structural analog elsewhere in Claude Code. Which is it?", "options": ["Hooks, which keep lightweight event names ambient in context and load their scripts only when an event fires", "MCP deferred tool loading, where tool names ride along ambiently and full schemas are fetched only when needed — the same trade of a small permanent index deferring a large cost until proven necessary", "Compaction, which keeps a summary ambient and reloads the full folded history whenever the model asks for it", "Subagents, which keep a lightweight agent roster ambient in the parent's context and page each agent's full transcript into the parent's window on demand — the same trade of a small resident index against a large deferred payload"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Both mechanisms resolve the same impossible trade-off with the same shape. The model can only use what it can discover, so some index must always be present — but shipping every full definition ambiently would devour the window. Skills split description (always) from body (on trigger); deferred MCP loading splits tool names (always) from full schemas (fetched on demand). Small permanent tax, large deferred cost — paid only when a capability is actually exercised. The distractors fail the pattern on one side or the other. Hooks have no ambient half at all: they're harness-side callbacks costing zero context, with nothing for the model to discover. Compaction is one-way: folded history is not reloadable on request — the summary replaces it. And subagent transcripts never page into the parent; isolation is the entire point, with only the final result returning by value. The recurring design lesson: context is the scarce resource, so indexes stay resident and payloads stay lazy."}, {"id": "3.8-L4-03", "topic": "3.8", "topicName": "Skill injection mechanics", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A skill wraps a heavy forty-step procedure that generates large intermediate outputs. Its author designs it to delegate the work to a subagent rather than run inline. At the mechanism level, what does that buy?", "options": ["The subagent inherits the parent's cached prefix — the harness assembles it with the same system prompt and tool schemas so the existing cache entries carry over — and every step of the procedure therefore bills at ~0.1x cache-read rates instead of as fresh input", "The body never needs to be written at all — the subagent can derive the full procedure from the skill's ambient description alone", "It makes the procedure durable: a subagent's context survives /clear and compaction, unlike skill bodies injected inline", "The procedure — its instructions, tool calls, and verbose outputs — runs in a separate loop with its own context, and only the final result returns to the parent as a single tool_result; the parent's conversation never carries the bulk"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "This combines skill injection with context isolation. Run inline, the forty steps and their outputs would all enter the parent's history — resent every subsequent turn and dragging the session toward compaction. Delegated, the subagent runs a separate loop with its own system prompt, context window, and transcript; it sees none of the parent's history, does the heavy work there, and hands back only its final text as one tool_result. The parent's context grows by a summary, not a procedure. The traps each break a real boundary. A subagent doesn't inherit the parent's cached prefix — it's a differently assembled request, so the parent's cache entries don't apply to it. The body is still essential: it's what instructs the delegation and the work; descriptions are routing signals, far too thin to derive a procedure from. And durability is backwards — a subagent's context is discarded when it finishes; nothing about it outlives the parent's resets."}, {"id": "3.8-L4-04", "topic": "3.8", "topicName": "Skill injection mechanics", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague asserts: 'Once a skill has fired, its instructions govern the rest of the session — they're in context for good.' What's the correction?", "options": ["The body lives in the foldable conversation history: a compaction can summarize it away and /clear drops it outright — only the ambient description is guaranteed present afterward, and recovering the full instructions means re-triggering the skill", "The opposite problem is real — the harness ages injected bodies out of the assembly after a fixed turn budget to bound their tail cost, so a skill must be re-triggered even in short sessions that never compact or clear, which is why long procedures re-fire midway", "Right about persistence but wrong about scope: a fired skill's body is promoted into the stable head, where it persists across sessions until the skill is uninstalled", "Right for user-triggered invocations, but model-triggered bodies are released as soon as the model emits its next end-of-turn"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The skill system's two tiers have two lifecycles, and the colleague has granted the body the description's durability. Descriptions live in the always-present ambient layer — re-injected into every request, surviving every fold, like CLAUDE.md. The body, once injected, is ordinary conversation history: the same foldable tier as your early prompts, subject to the documented lossiness of compaction and to /clear. After a fold, the summary may retain a trace of what the skill said, but the operative instructions can be gone — and if the session still needs them, the fix is to re-trigger the skill, which reloads the body fresh. The traps invent lifecycles that don't exist: no turn-count expiry, no promotion into the stable head (bodies never join the prefix — that's precisely why invoking a skill doesn't reset the cache), and no trigger-path dependence — how a body arrived has no bearing on how long it survives. Rules that must always hold belong in CLAUDE.md, not in a fired skill's body."}, {"id": "4.1-L2-01", "topic": "4.1", "topicName": "Hooks", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You want your linter to run after every file edit Claude Code makes, with zero chance of it ever being skipped. What is the most reliable mechanism?", "options": ["Add \"always run the linter after editing files\" to the project CLAUDE.md", "Remind Claude at the start of every session to lint after each edit", "Configure a PostToolUse hook matching Edit|Write in settings.json that runs the linter", "Create a skill whose frontmatter description instructs Claude to invoke it automatically after every Edit or Write tool call"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A hook is a shell command the harness itself executes when a named loop event fires — here, PostToolUse after an Edit or Write. Because it runs in the harness process, not in the model, it is deterministic: it fires every time, regardless of what the model is paying attention to. CLAUDE.md instructions and session reminders are 'asking nicely' — they land in the prompt and the model usually complies, but compliance is probabilistic and degrades as context fills. A skill is also model-triggered: the model must choose to invoke it. This is the core hooks-vs-instructions distinction: if a behavior must be guaranteed, put it in the harness (a hook); if it only needs to usually happen, prompt-level instructions are fine and cheaper to maintain."}, {"id": "4.1-L2-02", "topic": "4.1", "topicName": "Hooks", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Where does a Claude Code hook actually execute when its event fires?", "options": ["In the harness process on your machine, as a shell command it spawns", "Inside the model, as special high-priority instructions", "On the provider's servers (Anthropic or AWS Bedrock), alongside the model", "In a separate subagent loop with its own context window"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Hooks are harness-side machinery. When a lifecycle event fires (PreToolUse, PostToolUse, UserPromptSubmit, Stop, SessionStart…), the harness runs your configured command locally, feeding it JSON about the event on stdin. No tokens are sent to the model for this, and nothing about hooks depends on the provider — they behave identically on Bedrock and the Anthropic API, because they never leave your machine. The 'inside the model' option is the classic confusion between the two halves of the system: the model only ever sees text the harness chooses to send; it has no notion of hooks. And a hook is not a subagent — it is a plain process, not another agent loop. This locality is exactly why hooks are deterministic where prompt instructions are not."}, {"id": "4.1-L3-01", "topic": "4.1", "topicName": "Hooks", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A PreToolUse hook script inspects a proposed Bash command and exits with code 2. What happens next?", "options": ["The tool call runs anyway; the exit code is only recorded in the debug log", "The tool call is blocked and the hook's stderr is fed back to the model as the error, so it can adjust its approach", "The whole session terminates immediately with an error", "The harness retries the hook up to three times with exponential backoff, then runs the tool once the hook finally exits 0"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Exit code 2 is the documented blocking signal. For PreToolUse it short-circuits the loop: the tool never executes, and whatever the hook wrote to stderr is returned to the model in place of a tool result. On the next request the model sees that rejection text and typically tries a different approach — the hook has steered the loop without consuming any context of its own until that moment. Exit 0 means success (stdout parsed for optional JSON decisions), and other non-zero codes are non-blocking errors surfaced to the user, not the model. Note the asymmetry with PostToolUse: exiting 2 there still shows stderr to the model, but cannot un-run a tool that already executed. Blocking is only possible at 'pre' seams."}, {"id": "4.1-L3-02", "topic": "4.1", "topicName": "Hooks", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague claims: \"Our settings.json has a dozen hooks now — that's a dozen scripts' worth of extra tokens bloating every request.\" What's wrong with that?", "options": ["Nothing — the harness embeds every configured hook's full script in the system prompt each turn so the model knows which commands fire at each lifecycle event", "The claim is wrong because hooks execute on the provider's servers alongside the model, adding server-side load and latency but never consuming request tokens", "The claim is wrong because hooks consume output tokens, which are cheaper than input tokens", "Hooks are harness-side callbacks that cost zero context by default; only output a hook explicitly injects (e.g. UserPromptSubmit stdout) ever lands in the request"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Hook configuration and hook scripts never enter the prompt. The harness reads settings.json itself, fires the matching commands at loop events, and the model is unaware any of it exists. That is what makes hooks the cheap intervention point: you can observe or block every tool call without paying a single token. The only way a hook touches the request is when it deliberately produces content the harness injects — for example, stdout from a UserPromptSubmit hook, or stderr from a blocking exit-2 that becomes the tool's rejection message. Compare that with skills, whose descriptions do sit in context every turn. The 'server-side' distractor confuses hooks with server tools; hooks run locally, identically under Bedrock or the Anthropic API."}, {"id": "4.1-L4-01", "topic": "4.1", "topicName": "Hooks", "difficulty": 4, "question": "For most hook events, stdout from a successful (exit 0) hook goes to logs, not to the model. Which event is the notable exception, whose stdout is added to the model's context — making it a per-turn context-injection mechanism?", "options": ["UserPromptSubmit", "PostToolUse", "Notification", "PreCompact"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "On exit 0, stdout from a UserPromptSubmit hook (and similarly SessionStart) is appended to the context the model receives, whereas for events like PostToolUse, Notification, or PreCompact, stdout stays in the debug log. That makes UserPromptSubmit the sanctioned way to inject fresh, computed context every turn — current branch, ticket state, environment facts — without asking the model to fetch it. It also resolves a neat category question: hooks execute harness-side, yet this one produces request-shaping content. Hooks are the bridge that turns loop events into payload. Mechanically it fits the stateless loop: the model only knows what's in the assembled request, so anything you want it to reliably 'know' each turn must be injected at assembly — and this event fires exactly there, before the prompt is processed."}, {"id": "4.2-L2-01", "topic": "4.2", "topicName": "Skills", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What determines whether Claude Code triggers a skill on its own in the middle of a task?", "options": ["The harness scans each skill's body for keywords matching the user's request and injects the best-scoring body automatically", "The skill's frontmatter description, which is always present in context — the model matches the task against it and chooses to invoke", "The skill's file name must appear verbatim in the user's message", "The harness runs a vector-similarity search over every installed skill's body each turn and auto-loads any skill scoring above a relevance threshold"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Skill triggering is a model decision, not a harness lookup. Every installed skill contributes its name and description to the assembled prompt on every turn; the body stays on disk. When the model judges that the current task matches a description, it invokes the skill, and only then does the harness load the body into context. This is why the description is the entire trigger surface: the model cannot be persuaded by brilliant instructions it hasn't loaded yet. There is no keyword scan, filename match, or embedding search in the harness — the 'retrieval' is just the model reading descriptions like any other prompt text. Consequences follow directly: vague descriptions cause missed or spurious triggering, and descriptions (unlike hook scripts) do cost a small amount of context per turn."}, {"id": "4.2-L2-02", "topic": "4.2", "topicName": "Skills", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What is the key difference between a skill and a custom slash command in Claude Code?", "options": ["Skills execute as shell scripts in the harness process at lifecycle events, while commands are expanded by the model itself from its list of registered names", "Commands can take arguments, while skills cannot receive any input", "Skills are built into the product, while only commands can be user-defined", "A command runs only when the user explicitly invokes it, while a skill can also be chosen by the model because its description sits in context"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Both are ultimately instructions injected into the conversation — neither 'executes' anywhere except as text the model acts on. The real difference is who pulls the trigger. A slash command is user-initiated: you type /name, the harness expands the file into a prompt, done. A skill adds a second trigger path: its description is always in context, so the model can decide mid-task that the skill applies and invoke it without you asking. Skills are also structurally richer — a directory with SKILL.md frontmatter plus optional supporting files, loaded progressively — where a command is typically a single markdown file. The argument distractor is wrong in both directions: commands take $ARGUMENTS, and skills can receive arguments too. Both can be user-defined at user or project scope."}, {"id": "4.2-L3-01", "topic": "4.2", "topicName": "Skills", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You have 40 skills installed. On a turn where none of them is used, what do they cost in context?", "options": ["Nothing — skills are loaded only when triggered, so unused skills are free", "Their full bodies, since the model must read a skill to decide whether to use it", "Only their names and descriptions, which are part of the assembled prompt every turn; bodies load on invocation", "A one-time ingestion cost at session start, after which the prompt cache serves them for free without occupying any context-window space"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "This is progressive disclosure, the skill system's cost model. Descriptions are the always-paid part: they must be in every request so the model can choose among skills, and 40 of them is a real, recurring slice of your context window. Bodies are the pay-on-use part, loaded only when a skill is invoked. So 'unused skills are free' is wrong (descriptions aren't free), and 'full bodies every turn' is wrong (that's exactly what the design avoids). The caching option is half-true and that's the trap: because descriptions sit in the stable prefix, they're usually cache reads (~0.1× input price on both Anthropic and Bedrock), which makes them cheap in dollars — but cache reads still occupy context-window space and still get reprocessed into attention state. Cheap is not free, and window budget is separate from price."}, {"id": "4.2-L3-02", "topic": "4.2", "topicName": "Skills", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Your team's skill contains excellent step-by-step instructions, but Claude Code almost never triggers it automatically. What is the most likely effective fix?", "options": ["Rewrite the frontmatter description to explicitly name the tasks and phrasings it should trigger on — the description is all the model sees before invoking", "Move the most important instructions to the top of the skill body", "Convert the skill into a PreToolUse hook so the harness fires its instructions deterministically whenever a matching tool call occurs, instead of relying on the model to trigger it", "Switch to a larger model, which reads skill bodies more thoroughly"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Triggering happens before the body is ever loaded. On each turn the model sees only names and descriptions; if the description doesn't clearly announce 'use me when the user asks for X, Y, or Z,' the model has no basis to invoke it, no matter how good the body is. So triggering problems are description problems: name the concrete task types, include the phrases users actually type, state when NOT to use it. Reordering the body changes nothing pre-invocation — the body is invisible until the skill fires. A hook is the wrong seam: hooks fire deterministically on loop events, not on semantic task matches, and can't 'be' a workflow guide. And no model reads bodies before triggering, so model choice doesn't address the mechanism. Description-writing is effectively prompt engineering for a one-paragraph router."}, {"id": "4.3-L2-01", "topic": "4.3", "topicName": "Custom commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You create a file at .claude/commands/deploy.md inside a project. What does this give you?", "options": ["A hook that fires whenever a deployment-related tool call runs", "A skill that Claude will trigger automatically when deployment comes up", "A /deploy slash command whose markdown body is used as the prompt when invoked, available to anyone working in the repo", "A background subagent that the harness spawns at session start to watch the repository for deployment-related changes and act on them"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Files in a project's .claude/commands/ directory become project-scoped slash commands, named after the file: deploy.md yields /deploy. Because the directory lives in the repo, the command travels with version control — every teammate who clones the project gets it, which is the main reason to prefer project scope over the personal ~/.claude/commands/ equivalent. Mechanically, invoking the command just expands the markdown into the prompt for that turn (optionally with frontmatter like allowed-tools or model); it is request-shaping text, nothing more. It is not a hook — nothing fires automatically on events — and not a skill: the model won't spontaneously invoke /deploy because command bodies aren't advertised in context the way skill descriptions are. The user types it, or it doesn't run."}, {"id": "4.3-L2-02", "topic": "4.3", "topicName": "Custom commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You want a custom command invoked as \"/fix-issue 417\" to receive the issue number. How do arguments reach the command?", "options": ["They can't — custom commands are fixed prompts with no parameterization", "You put $ARGUMENTS (or positional $1, $2…) placeholders in the command file; the harness substitutes them into the prompt before sending", "You declare an args: list in the command's frontmatter; the harness parses flags and named parameters against it and rejects invocations that don't match", "The model infers the arguments from recent conversation history"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Command expansion is plain text substitution done by the harness at assembly time. $ARGUMENTS receives everything typed after the command name; $1, $2 and so on capture individual positional arguments when you want them separated. The substituted markdown then becomes the turn's prompt — by the time the request is sent, the model just sees ordinary text with '417' already inline. That's the right mental model: a command is a prompt template, not a program, so 'argument passing' is string interpolation, not flag parsing. There is no automatic frontmatter-driven parser (frontmatter fields like description, allowed-tools, and argument-hint configure the command, they don't parse input). And relying on the model to infer values from history is exactly the nondeterminism templates exist to remove."}, {"id": "4.3-L2-03", "topic": "4.3", "topicName": "Custom commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "When is a custom slash command a better choice than a skill?", "options": ["When the model should notice mid-task that the behavior applies and invoke it on its own, based on a description advertised in every request", "When the workflow needs bundled helper scripts and reference files loaded progressively", "When the behavior must deterministically block certain tool calls", "When you want a short, reusable prompt that you fire explicitly at a moment you choose, with no need for the model to decide anything"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Choose by trigger and by structure. A command is the right tool when the human knows the moment: /review-pr, /deploy-checklist, /fix-issue 417. It costs nothing in context until typed, needs no trigger-worthy description, and its behavior is fully predictable — type it, the template expands. A skill earns its keep when the model should recognize the situation itself (its description is advertised every turn precisely for that) or when the workflow is big enough to want progressive disclosure and bundled resources — which is option B's scenario, a skill strength, not a command strength. Option C describes hooks: only harness-side code can deterministically block tool calls; neither commands nor skills can guarantee anything, since both just add instructions. The one-line heuristic: user-initiated and simple → command; model-initiated or resource-heavy → skill; must-always-happen → hook."}, {"id": "4.4-L2-01", "topic": "4.4", "topicName": "MCP", "difficulty": 2, "question": "The model emits a tool_use for mcp__github__create_pr. Where does that tool actually execute?", "options": ["In the harness itself, like Bash or Read — all tools run locally", "In an external MCP server process (local or remote) that the harness routes the call to — a third executor alongside built-in and server-side tools", "On the model provider's servers, executed in-request the way server-side tools like web_search are, with the result spliced into the response before it returns", "Inside the model, which simulates the tool from its training data"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "There are three executors behind one protocol. Built-in tools (Bash, Read, Edit) run in the harness on your machine. Server-side tools (like web_search) run on the provider's infrastructure inside the request. MCP adds the third: an external server — a local process or a remote endpoint — that the harness forwards the tool_use to, then wraps the reply as a tool_result. Crucially, the model can't tell them apart and doesn't need to: it emits a structured tool_use block and later receives a tool_result block; the routing is entirely the harness's job. The mcp__server__tool naming is the harness's routing key, not something the model executes. This is why prompt-injection risk extends to MCP results — whatever the server returns is fed straight back into the model's next context, same as any tool output."}, {"id": "4.4-L2-02", "topic": "4.4", "topicName": "MCP", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You want everyone on your team to get the same MCP server configuration when they clone the repo. Which scope do you use, and where does it live?", "options": ["Project scope — a .mcp.json file at the repo root, checked into version control; teammates approve it on first use", "Local scope — .claude/settings.local.json, which git automatically syncs", "User scope — ~/.claude.json, which applies across all your projects and syncs to teammates through your organization's managed settings", "Enterprise scope — a shared file in the system-wide Claude directory"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "MCP servers have three scopes, and the question 'who should get this?' picks between them. Project scope writes to .mcp.json in the repo root — the only scope designed for sharing, since the file rides along in version control; Claude Code asks each user to approve the servers on first use as a safety gate against a cloned repo silently wiring up executors. Local scope (the default for claude mcp add) is you-only and this-project-only, and — a common trip-up — it's stored in ~/.claude.json keyed by project path, not in settings.local.json. User scope is also in ~/.claude.json but loads across all your projects; it's personal, never shared. So the distractors fail on the sharing axis: local and user scopes never leave your machine, and settings.local.json is a settings file, not where MCP local scope lives."}, {"id": "4.4-L3-01", "topic": "4.4", "topicName": "MCP", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You're wiring up two MCP servers: a hosted SaaS endpoint and a small script on your own machine. Which transport fits each?", "options": ["Both should use stdio; the harness tunnels it over the network for the remote one", "The remote server uses stdio tunneled over SSH by the harness; the local one runs an HTTP listener on localhost that the harness polls for results", "The remote server uses HTTP (SSE is deprecated); the local one uses stdio — the harness spawns the process and speaks over stdin/stdout", "The remote server uses gRPC; the local one uses a Unix socket"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Transport follows where the server lives. A stdio server is a child process the harness launches itself, exchanging JSON-RPC over stdin/stdout — ideal for local scripts and tools needing direct system access, and it dies with the session. A remote service can't be spawned, so you connect over the network: HTTP is the recommended transport (claude mcp add --transport http <name> <url>), with OAuth via /mcp or bearer headers for auth; SSE exists but is deprecated in favor of HTTP. The harness never 'tunnels stdio over the network,' and gRPC isn't part of MCP. One provider note for Bedrock users: transports are purely between your harness and the MCP server — your model traffic going to Bedrock changes nothing about how MCP servers connect."}, {"id": "4.4-L3-02", "topic": "4.4", "topicName": "MCP", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague on your Bedrock-based team says: \"We can't use MCP servers — MCP requires the Anthropic API and we route everything through AWS.\" What's the correct response?", "options": ["They're right — MCP tool_use blocks are resolved on Anthropic's infrastructure before the response returns, and Bedrock traffic never reaches Anthropic's servers, so the calls have nowhere to execute", "They're right in practice — Bedrock strips tool_use blocks from responses, so MCP tools never fire", "They're half right — stdio servers work anywhere since the harness spawns them locally, but remote HTTP servers authenticate through Anthropic's OAuth endpoints, which Bedrock-only environments can't reach", "They're wrong — MCP is harness-side and provider-agnostic; the model just emits tool_use, and the harness routes it to the server. Only if the MCP server itself calls an LLM does it need its own, separate credentials"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "MCP lives entirely in the harness layer, below the provider question. Whether the completion comes from Bedrock or the Anthropic API, it arrives as the same Anthropic-shaped response with tool_use blocks; the harness sees mcp__server__tool, forwards the call to the configured server, and feeds the result back. Nothing in that path touches Anthropic's API, and Bedrock passes tool_use through untouched — tool use is core to the protocol on every provider. The one real caveat is the last clause: an MCP server is an independent program, and if it internally calls a model (say, a summarization server hitting the Anthropic API), it uses its own credentials and provider choice — your CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK setting doesn't flow into it. Governance teams should vet that separately; the harness-to-server plumbing itself is provider-neutral."}, {"id": "4.4-L4-01", "topic": "4.4", "topicName": "MCP", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Mid-session, you add an MCP server that contributes 30 tool schemas. Beyond the extra tokens those schemas occupy, what is the caching consequence on your next request?", "options": ["The tools parameter changes at the top of the request, so the cached prefix no longer matches — everything from that point on is re-written to cache at cache-write rates instead of read at ~0.1×", "None — tool definitions sit outside the cacheable region of the request", "Only the 30 new schemas are written at cache-write rates; every existing block keeps its own independent cache entry, so the rest of the conversation is still served as ~0.1× reads on the next request", "The cache TTL resets, but all existing content is still served as cache reads"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Prompt caching is prefix matching: the provider reuses stored attention state only for a byte-identical leading span of the request. Tool definitions serialize near the very front — ahead of system prompt, context files, and history — precisely because they're normally the most stable content (the stability gradient). Add a server mid-session and that front section changes, so the prefix diverges almost immediately: the next turn re-ingests and re-caches nearly the whole conversation at cache-write pricing (e.g. 1.25× base input for 5-minute TTL) instead of ~0.1× reads. There's no per-block independence — a miss at position N invalidates everything after N. This applies on Bedrock exactly as on the Anthropic API. Practical rule: connect the MCP servers you need before long sessions; toggling them mid-flight buys one expensive turn each time."}, {"id": "4.4-L4-02", "topic": "4.4", "topicName": "MCP", "difficulty": 4, "question": "With many MCP servers configured, Claude Code may list some tools as \"deferred,\" loadable via a tool-search mechanism. What problem does deferred tool loading solve, and how?", "options": ["It defers execution of MCP calls to the end of the turn so they can be batched into one request", "Hundreds of full tool schemas would bloat the tools parameter of every request; deferring sends only names up front, and the model pulls in full schemas on demand via a search tool", "It downloads tool implementations from the MCP server only when first called, saving disk space", "It hands deferred tools off to the provider's servers, where they execute inside the request itself, so the harness avoids a local round-trip for every MCP call and the loop stays fast"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Every request resends the full tools parameter, and MCP servers can contribute hundreds of verbose JSON schemas — paid on every single turn of a stateless loop, whether or not any of those tools are used. Deferred loading is progressive disclosure applied to tools, the same trick skills use for bodies: keep a lightweight name-level index always present, and let the model call a search tool (ToolSearch) to fetch full schemas only when it actually needs one. Once fetched, the schema is in context and the tool is callable normally. The gains compound: a smaller request per turn, and a more stable prefix for caching, since the always-present portion changes less. The distractors misplace the mechanism — nothing about execution timing, disk storage, or provider-side execution changes; deferral is purely about what schema text enters the assembled request, and when."}, {"id": "4.5-L2-01", "topic": "4.5", "topicName": "Subagents", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Claude Code spawns a subagent to investigate part of your task. What does that subagent see of your conversation so far?", "options": ["The full parent conversation history, shared in real time", "A summary of the parent session that the harness compacts automatically at spawn time, so key decisions and file context carry over", "Nothing but the task prompt it was handed — it runs a separate loop with its own context window and system prompt", "The last few parent messages, for continuity"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A subagent is not a thread within your conversation; it is a whole second agent loop. It gets its own system prompt, its own empty context window, and its own transcript, and the only bridge from the parent is the task prompt written into the Agent tool call. Nothing else crosses — not your history, not CLAUDE.md discussion, not files the parent already read. This isolation is the feature, not a limitation: the subagent can burn through dozens of file reads without any of it landing in the parent's context, and the parent receives only the final report. The practical corollary trips people up constantly: the delegating prompt must be self-contained. Any constraint, file path, or decision the subagent needs must be restated in that prompt, because 'as we discussed above' refers to a conversation the subagent has never seen."}, {"id": "4.5-L2-02", "topic": "4.5", "topicName": "Subagents", "difficulty": 2, "question": "How do you define a custom subagent type (say, a \"security-reviewer\") that's available in every session of a project?", "options": ["Add an \"agents\" array to the project's settings.json, with each entry declaring the agent's name, tool allowlist, and system prompt inline for the harness to register at startup", "Add a \"## security-reviewer\" section to CLAUDE.md describing the role", "Configure an MCP server that registers itself as an agent", "Create a markdown file in .claude/agents/ with frontmatter (name, description, optionally tools and model) whose body becomes the agent's system prompt"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Custom subagents are file-defined: a markdown file per agent in .claude/agents/ (project scope, shared via the repo) or ~/.claude/agents/ (personal, all projects). Frontmatter carries the identity — name, a description that tells the main agent when to delegate to it, an optional tools allowlist, and optionally a model tier — and the markdown body becomes the subagent's own system prompt, replacing what a generic agent would get. The description matters the same way a skill description does: it is what the delegating model reads when choosing an agent type. The distractors put the definition at the wrong seams — settings.json configures the harness but has no agents array for prompts; CLAUDE.md text is advisory memory, creating no invocable type; and MCP servers contribute tools, which are calls into an executor, not new agent loops."}, {"id": "4.5-L3-01", "topic": "4.5", "topicName": "Subagents", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague says: \"I had a subagent read 50 files, so now the main conversation can reference any of those file contents.\" What actually happened?", "options": ["Only the subagent's final text report returned to the parent, as a single tool_result — results pass by value, and anything not in that report is gone from the parent's view", "They're right — subagents write their findings into the parent's context as they work", "The parent holds a reference to the subagent's transcript, and the harness pages the relevant portions back into the parent's context on demand whenever it needs to check a detail", "The file contents transferred, but compressed, so only approximate contents are available"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Subagent results are pass-by-value. The 50 files' contents lived in the subagent's context window, and when its loop finished, exactly one thing crossed back: its final message, wrapped as the tool_result of the Agent call. The parent's context grew by that summary — nothing else. There's no reference to dereference later: the parent's next request to the model contains the report text, not the subagent's transcript, and the stateless model only knows what's in the request. (The subagent's transcript does exist on disk, and the parent can message a still-alive agent to ask follow-ups — but that produces new report text, it doesn't import history.) The practical discipline: tell subagents what to include in their report — paths, line numbers, exact signatures — because whatever the report omits, the parent must rediscover from scratch."}, {"id": "4.5-L3-02", "topic": "4.5", "topicName": "Subagents", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Which of these tasks is the strongest candidate for delegating to a subagent rather than doing inline in the main conversation?", "options": ["Reading a single known file to check one constant's value, so that even that file's contents stay out of the parent's context window", "A sweeping search across many candidate files and naming conventions, where you only need the conclusion", "Making a small edit to the file you were just discussing", "Asking the user a clarifying question about requirements"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The decision rule is: delegate when the work's intermediate output is bulky but its answer is small. A broad search means reading excerpts from dozens of files, most of them dead ends; done inline, every one of those dumps enters the parent's context and gets resent on every subsequent turn for the rest of the session — the stateless loop makes context pollution a recurring tax, not a one-time cost. Done in a subagent, the dead ends stay in the child's disposable context and the parent pays only for the conclusion. The other options invert the tradeoff: a single known-file read is cheaper than the overhead of spawning and briefing an agent; the small edit depends on conversation state a subagent doesn't have; and user interaction belongs to the main session. Isolation is the point — use it where isolation saves the most."}, {"id": "4.5-L4-01", "topic": "4.5", "topicName": "Subagents", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague reasons: \"Subagents are nearly free — whatever they do, only a short summary comes back to us, so we're billed for a summary's worth of tokens.\" What's wrong with that?", "options": ["Nothing — providers bill subagent traffic at reduced background rates", "It's wrong only for output tokens; subagent input tokens genuinely aren't billed", "Subagents are actually more expensive per token: each spawn starts a fresh conversation with no cached prefix to reuse, so none of their traffic ever gets the ~0.1× cache-read discount", "The subagent is its own stateless loop: every one of its turns resends its full growing history at normal rates. What you save is the parent's context growth, not the subagent's tokens"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The summary is what returns; it is not what you pay for. A subagent runs a complete agent loop — assemble, send, tool call, resend — and like any stateless loop, each of its turns retransmits everything before it, so a 30-turn investigation bills for its own superlinear resend curve at full input/output rates, on Bedrock or Anthropic alike. No provider discounts subagent traffic, and subagents do use prompt caching on their own prefixes, so both those distractors fail. What delegation genuinely buys is downstream: the parent's context grows by a summary instead of the full investigation, so every later parent turn resends kilobytes less — for the remaining life of the session. Subagents are a context-hygiene lever that often lowers total session cost, but each spawn is a real, fully-billed conversation. Fan out accordingly."}, {"id": "4.6-L3-01", "topic": "4.6", "topicName": "Agent teams & orchestration", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You fan a review task out to six parallel subagents instead of working through it in one session. What happens to your token consumption?", "options": ["Total tokens stay the same; they're just split six ways", "Total tokens drop: each agent works in a small dedicated context, and the shared task briefing is deduplicated by the prompt cache across all six loops, so aggregate input shrinks", "It multiplies: six separate loops each resend their own growing context concurrently, so aggregate tokens — and especially tokens-per-minute — jump by roughly the fan-out factor", "Only output tokens increase; input tokens are deduplicated across the agents"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Every agent in the fan-out is a full stateless loop: its own context, rebuilt and resent every turn. Six agents means six resend curves running at once, so your burst consumption — tokens per minute — scales with the fan-out even before totals settle. That rate dimension is the one people miss: sequential work spreads the same tokens over time; parallel work concentrates them, which is exactly what rate limits and quotas measure. On totals, 'split six ways' and 'deduplicated inputs' are wrong because contexts are independent — shared background (the codebase framing, the task brief) gets sent and cached separately per agent; caching is per-conversation-prefix, not cross-agent. The one grain of truth in option B: six short contexts can beat one giant session's late-stage resends on total cost. But the concurrency multiplier is the operative fact for anyone running against provider limits."}, {"id": "4.6-L3-02", "topic": "4.6", "topicName": "Agent teams & orchestration", "difficulty": 3, "question": "When running several agents in parallel on the same repository, why give each agent its own git worktree?", "options": ["So each agent gets an isolated working copy and branch state — parallel edits, builds, and test runs don't clobber a shared checkout, and each result can be reviewed or merged independently", "So agents can't read each other's code, which is required for context isolation", "Because each worktree gets its own prompt-cache prefix keyed on the directory path, so agents in separate worktrees get ~0.1× cache reads instead of invalidating each other's cached prefixes", "Because the harness cannot run more than one agent in a single directory"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Subagent context isolation solves the conversation-level problem — histories don't mix — but the filesystem is still shared state. Two agents editing the same checkout will trample each other: conflicting edits to one file, a build reading a tree that's mutating mid-compile, tests failing on a colleague-agent's half-finished change. A git worktree gives each agent a separate directory with its own checked-out branch backed by the same repository, so edits are physically separated and each agent's output lands as a clean branch you can diff, review, or discard on its own. Note what worktrees are not: they're not a secrecy mechanism (context isolation happens at the conversation layer, and agents could read any path anyway), they have nothing to do with the prompt cache (that's keyed on request prefixes, not directories), and the harness happily runs many agents in one directory — that's precisely the hazard."}, {"id": "4.6-L4-01", "topic": "4.6", "topicName": "Agent teams & orchestration", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Your team runs Claude Code against AWS Bedrock. Soon after adopting a parallel multi-agent workflow, sessions start failing with throttling errors. What's the mechanism?", "options": ["Bedrock caps each AWS account at one model invocation at a time; parallel agents must be serialized", "All agents draw on the same account-and-region on-demand quotas (tokens- and requests-per-minute); fan-out multiplies concurrent consumption, so you hit ThrottlingException far sooner than single-session use ever did", "Each parallel subagent assumes its own scoped IAM role for isolation, and AWS STS rate-limits role assumption per account, so the fan-out exhausts the assume-role quota before most model calls fire", "The MCP servers the agents share are hitting Anthropic-side rate limits, and the harness surfaces those upstream 429s as Bedrock ThrottlingExceptions, so the failures look like AWS throttling when they aren't"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Bedrock on-demand capacity is metered per account per region as TPM/RPM quotas, and every agent in your fan-out spends from that one shared pool — the harness may see six independent conversations, but AWS sees one caller. Since each agent is a full loop resending its growing context, six agents multiply burst consumption roughly sixfold, and quota headroom that comfortably absorbed single sessions now produces ThrottlingException storms; the harness retries with backoff, so it often shows up first as agents mysteriously slowing down. There's no one-invocation cap, IAM roles don't multiply per subagent (all calls use the session's credentials), and MCP traffic never touches Bedrock's meters. Remedies map to the mechanism: cap parallelism, request quota increases, use cross-region inference profiles to widen the pool, or buy provisioned throughput. This is the fan-out cost multiplier expressed as a rate problem rather than a dollar problem."}, {"id": "4.7-L3-01", "topic": "4.7", "topicName": "Dynamic workflows", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You're building a nightly pipeline with fixed stages: triage failing tests → attempt fixes → run the suite → open a PR. Which construction fits best, and why?", "options": ["One interactive session driven by a detailed prompt that walks the model through all four stages in order, explicitly instructing it to verify each stage's success before starting the next", "A single subagent given the entire pipeline as its task prompt", "A script that drives each stage as its own headless invocation (claude -p) or subagent — deterministic code owns the sequencing and gating; each stage starts with a clean, focused context", "Four skills, one per stage, and trust the model to trigger them in sequence"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The dividing line is whether the workflow's structure is known in advance. Here it is: four stages, fixed order, a clear gate (don't open a PR unless the suite passed). Structure you already know belongs in code — a script guarantees ordering and can enforce gates by checking real exit codes, where a prompted model merely tends to follow instructions and tends to degrade as one context fills with all four stages' debris. Per-stage invocations also mean each stage gets a small context containing only its own concern, instead of stage four wading through stage one's test dumps. The single-session and single-subagent options put probabilistic sequencing plus accumulated clutter inside one window; the skills option is worst on the load-bearing property, since skill triggering is itself a per-turn model judgment. Save model-driven delegation for work whose path can't be predetermined — that's where its adaptivity pays."}, {"id": "4.7-L4-01", "topic": "4.7", "topicName": "Dynamic workflows", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague argues: \"Scripting workflows over subagents is pointless — one big session with everything in context is strictly better, because the model sees all the information at once.\" What's the strongest counter?", "options": ["Big sessions are impossible anyway, since the harness caps sessions at a fixed number of turns", "They're right on the Anthropic API but wrong on Bedrock, where the served context window is capped well below the model's native size, so a big-session workflow hits the ceiling and force-compacts before finishing", "Subagent-based workflows are strictly cheaper in total tokens — every delegated step returns as one short summary instead of a long resend curve, so decomposition wins on cost no matter how entangled the work is, which settles the question", "\"Seeing everything\" is the liability: every turn resends the whole context at growing cost, instructions compete with clutter for attention, and compaction eventually discards detail — decomposition gives each step a small fresh context and deterministic ordering"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The claim assumes context is free and attention is uniform; neither holds. Mechanically, the stateless loop resends everything each turn, so a monolithic session's cost curve bends superlinearly while a decomposed workflow keeps each step's resend short. Behaviorally, a window stuffed with four subtasks' file dumps dilutes the instructions that matter now — long-context recall is real but not perfect, and it competes with noise. And structurally, the big session can't actually keep everything: hit the window limit and compaction folds history into a lossy summary, discarding exactly the kind of detail the 'sees it all' argument depends on. Decomposition converts sequencing from a probabilistic behavior into code, and passes forward only distilled results. The honest caveat: for genuinely entangled work where every part informs every other, one context can win — but that's a judgment about the task's structure, not a universal rule. The other options lean on invented facts: no turn cap exists, Bedrock serves the same context windows, and decomposition isn't always cheaper."}, {"id": "4.1-L2-03", "topic": "4.1", "topicName": "Hooks", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You want a PreToolUse hook to fire before every tool call coming from your GitHub MCP server — and only those. What do you put in the hook's matcher?", "options": ["\"github\" — the harness expands a bare server name to all of its tools", "\"mcp__github__.*\" — a regex matching the full names of every tool that server contributes", "\"*\", plus a separate \"server\" field in the hook entry that filters by origin", "Nothing — MCP tools are matched by listing the server under a \"servers\" key instead of a matcher"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Tool-event matchers filter on the tool's full name, and MCP tools are named mcp__<server>__<tool> — so a regex like mcp__github__.* catches every tool one server contributes while ignoring the rest. Matchers accept an exact string (Bash), alternatives (Edit|Write), a regex, or * for everything; there is no special MCP-aware syntax beyond the naming convention itself. That's the elegance: because MCP tools enter the loop as ordinary tools with a structured name, every name-based surface — hook matchers, permission rules, subagent tool lists — can target them with plain string patterns. A bare \"github\" would only match a tool literally named github, and there is no \"server\" field or \"servers\" key in hook config; those options invent machinery the naming convention makes unnecessary."}, {"id": "4.1-L2-04", "topic": "4.1", "topicName": "Hooks", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You've written a PostToolUse formatting hook and want every teammate who clones the repository to get it automatically. Where does it go?", "options": ["~/.claude/settings.json — the user-level settings file, which applies across every project on your machine", ".claude/settings.local.json — the project settings file", ".claude/settings.json in the repo, checked into version control", "A \"hooks\" section in the project's CLAUDE.md"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Hooks live in the same tiered settings files as other configuration, and the tier decides who receives them. .claude/settings.json is the project-shared tier: it rides along in version control, so anyone who clones the repo gets the hook. ~/.claude/settings.json is personal and applies to all your projects but never leaves your machine; .claude/settings.local.json is deliberately project-private — the local tier exists precisely for settings you don't want to share. CLAUDE.md is memory, not configuration: text there is injected into the prompt for the model to read, but the harness doesn't parse it for hooks, so a \"hooks section\" would just be asking nicely in a different file. One caveat the sharing tier creates: hooks execute arbitrary commands with your credentials, so treat hooks in a freshly cloned repo like any other code you're about to run — read them before trusting the workspace."}, {"id": "4.1-L3-03", "topic": "4.1", "topicName": "Hooks", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A Stop hook checks whether tests were run this session and exits 2 with \"tests have not been run\" on stderr. What happens when Claude tries to finish its turn?", "options": ["Claude is prevented from stopping: the stderr text is fed back to it and it continues working — in this case, presumably running the tests", "The session terminates and the stderr message is displayed to the user as the failure reason", "Nothing changes — Stop fires after the final message has already been emitted, so exit 2 is purely advisory feedback, exactly like exit 2 on a PostToolUse hook after its tool has run", "The harness re-executes the last tool call so the hook can re-evaluate the session state"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Exit 2 is the blocking signal, but what \"blocking\" means depends on what the event was about to do. Stop fires when Claude is about to finish its turn, so blocking it blocks the stopping itself: the model receives the stderr text and must keep going — which makes a Stop hook a deterministic \"you're not done yet\" gate for things like untested changes. The PostToolUse comparison is the tempting near-miss: there, the tool has already executed, so exit-2 feedback is advisory because the action can't un-happen. Stop is different — the stop hasn't happened yet, so it's still preventable, exactly like PreToolUse cancelling a tool call. Non-2 failure codes are the ones surfaced to the user without blocking, and no hook ever causes the harness to re-run a tool. The general contract: exit 2 blocks whatever hasn't happened yet, and stderr becomes the model's explanation."}, {"id": "4.1-L3-04", "topic": "4.1", "topicName": "Hooks", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A PreToolUse hook exits 0 and prints JSON with hookSpecificOutput.permissionDecision set to \"allow\" whenever the proposed command is \"npm test\". What is the effect?", "options": ["The preference is recorded as advisory input — the user still sees the permission prompt, with the hook's recommendation displayed alongside it, and makes the final call", "None — on exit 0, stdout goes only to the debug log, so the JSON is never read", "The command is added to the permanent allowlist in settings.json for future sessions", "The tool call is auto-approved and runs without a permission prompt — the hook is acting as a policy engine in front of the permission system"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "PreToolUse hooks have the richest JSON interface: on exit 0, the harness parses stdout for structured decisions, and permissionDecision can be \"allow\" (auto-approve, skipping the prompt), \"deny\" (block), or \"ask\" (escalate to the human). \"Allow\" therefore short-circuits the permission prompt entirely — this is what turns hooks from tripwires into policy engines: approve npm test silently, deny writes to production paths, route everything ambiguous to the user. The advisory option describes \"ask\", not \"allow\". The stdout-to-log rule is the near-miss trap: it's true for plain text output on most events, but decision JSON is exactly the sanctioned exception — the harness parses it. And nothing is persisted: the decision applies to this one tool call, made fresh by your script each time, which is the point — a script can express conditional policy (\"allow only in this directory\") that a static settings.json allowlist rule cannot."}, {"id": "4.1-L4-02", "topic": "4.1", "topicName": "Hooks", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Compliance requires that API keys appearing in command output are redacted before the model ever sees them — guaranteed, every time. Which mechanism does this?", "options": ["A PreToolUse hook that rewrites the tool's input to add redaction flags before the command runs", "A PostToolUse hook that, via its JSON output, replaces the tool's result with a redacted version before it enters context", "A PostToolUse hook that exits 2, so its stderr is substituted for the tool's output", "A UserPromptSubmit hook that scans the assembled request just before it is sent and scrubs secrets from any tool output already sitting in the conversation history"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "PostToolUse hooks can do more than observe: through the JSON channel, a hook can replace the tool's output entirely — the harness feeds the model the hook's substituted content instead of the raw result. Since the hook runs in the harness after execution but before the result is folded into the next request, redaction is guaranteed at the only seam that sees real output before the model does. Each trap is a real mechanism at the wrong point. PreToolUse input rewriting exists, but it edits what the tool is asked to do — it can't guarantee anything about what the output will contain. Exit 2 on PostToolUse feeds stderr to the model as advisory feedback; it does not replace the tool result, which still lands in context unredacted. UserPromptSubmit injects content at prompt time — the wrong direction entirely: it shapes what you add, not what tools returned. Only the output-replacement channel intercepts the actual payload."}, {"id": "4.2-L2-03", "topic": "4.2", "topicName": "Skills", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You've built a /deploy skill, but you never want Claude deciding on its own that the code \"looks ready\" and invoking it mid-task. What do you do?", "options": ["Set user-invocable: false in the skill's frontmatter", "Delete the description field so the model has nothing to match against", "Set disable-model-invocation: true in the frontmatter — only you can trigger it, and its description drops out of the always-loaded listing", "Move it to ~/.claude/commands/, since files in the commands directory are user-invocable only and are never advertised to the model as triggers"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "By default a skill is invocable two ways: by you (/deploy) and by the model when the description matches the task. disable-model-invocation: true severs the second path — exactly what you want for side-effect workflows like deploying, committing, or messaging, where timing should be a human decision. It has a pleasant side effect: since the model can no longer invoke it, the description no longer needs to sit in the always-loaded listing, so the skill costs zero context until used. user-invocable: false is the mirror image — it makes the skill model-only and hides it from the / menu, the opposite of what's wanted. Deleting the description is a broken version of the right idea: it degrades the skill (no autocomplete documentation, unclear purpose) rather than using the switch built for this. And moving it to commands/ doesn't change the trigger model reliably — commands and skills are merged machinery; the frontmatter switch is the explicit control."}, {"id": "4.2-L2-04", "topic": "4.2", "topicName": "Skills", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You have a personal note-taking skill you want available in every project you work on, without sharing it with any team. Where does it live?", "options": ["~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md — the personal skills directory", ".claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md, copied into each repository you work in", "An entry in .claude/settings.local.json pointing at the skill file", "A section in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md describing the procedure"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Skills have three homes, and each answers a different \"who gets this?\" Personal skills live in ~/.claude/skills/ and load in every project on your machine — yours everywhere, shared nowhere. Project skills live in .claude/skills/ inside a repo and travel through version control to the whole team; copying a personal skill into every repo would work mechanically but shares it with everyone and leaves you maintaining N copies. Plugin skills arrive namespaced via installed plugins. The other two options put the content at the wrong seams: settings files configure the harness (permissions, hooks, env) and have no skill-registration entry, and ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md — user memory — would inject the procedure into every prompt of every session, paying for it on every turn of the stateless loop. The entire point of a skill is to avoid that: a one-line description per turn, full body only when invoked."}, {"id": "4.2-L3-03", "topic": "4.2", "topicName": "Skills", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague says: \"Skills are fully lazy — once Claude finishes the task that triggered a skill, the body unloads and the context is freed again.\" What's the truth?", "options": ["They're right — the harness drops the body from context as soon as the invoking task completes", "They're right in effect — the harness re-reads the body from disk on each turn where the skill is still relevant and drops it from the request once the model stops referencing it, so nothing persists in the conversation", "They're wrong only for user-invoked skills; model-invoked skill bodies do unload after use", "They're wrong — once invoked, the rendered content enters the conversation as a message and persists for the rest of the session; the lazy part is loading, not lifetime"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Progressive disclosure is one-directional. Before invocation, only the description is in context; at invocation, the harness renders the skill into the conversation as a message — and messages don't un-happen. Because the harness resends the entire conversation every turn (the stateless loop), that body is now part of every subsequent request until the session ends, is cleared, or compaction summarizes it away. Nothing selectively removes it when the task completes — the harness has no notion of \"the task is done now.\" The re-read-from-disk option describes a plausible alternative design that isn't how it works: disk is touched once, at load; afterwards the copy in history is what the model sees. And there's no user-vs-model invocation asymmetry in lifetime — both paths inject the same content the same way. Practical consequence: invoking a huge skill early in a long session is a recurring cost, so prefer skills whose entrypoints stay lean and push detail into supporting files."}, {"id": "4.2-L3-04", "topic": "4.2", "topicName": "Skills", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A skill's frontmatter includes allowed-tools pre-approving a test command. The skill is invoked, works, and you then send your next message. What is the permission state now?", "options": ["The grant persists to session end, just like the skill's injected text does", "The grant has cleared — allowed-tools pre-approves tools only for the turn that invoked the skill; the skill's text persists, its permissions don't", "The grant was persisted to .claude/settings.local.json as an allow rule at invocation time, so it now applies for the rest of this session and to future sessions too", "The grant only ever applied inside a forked subagent context, never to the main conversation"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "allowed-tools is a scoped permission grant: for the turn the skill is invoked, the listed tools run without permission prompts, letting a workflow execute smoothly. But the grant clears on your next message — deliberately, because a permission is a live capability and shouldn't outlive the moment that justified it. This creates the asymmetry the question probes: the skill's rendered text persists in the conversation for the rest of the session (it's a message in history, resent every turn), while the permission attached to it evaporates. Text is inert; permissions are power — they age differently. The settings.json option confuses a transient frontmatter grant with the persistent rules users write by hand or via \"always allow\"; no skill silently edits your settings. And the fork option misattaches a real feature: context: fork controls where a skill runs, not where allowed-tools applies — the grant works for inline skills in the main conversation."}, {"id": "4.3-L2-04", "topic": "4.3", "topicName": "Custom commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A project contains both .claude/commands/review.md and .claude/skills/review/SKILL.md. What happens when someone types /review?", "options": ["The skill runs — on a name clash, a skill beats a same-named command file", "The command runs — legacy commands load first and shadow skills", "Claude Code refuses to start the command and asks the user to resolve the clash", "Both load — the command body is appended to the skill body in one prompt"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Commands and skills have been merged into one machinery: both file shapes create a /review invocation, support the same frontmatter, and expand into the prompt the same way. When both exist under one name, the skill wins — the skill directory is the newer, richer packaging (supporting files, invocation control, tool pre-approval), so it's treated as the authoritative definition, and the legacy command file is simply shadowed. Nothing errors and nothing concatenates: name resolution picks exactly one definition, the same way a project code-review skill replaces the built-in /code-review rather than merging with it. The practical takeaway is the upgrade path: when a flat command file outgrows itself, move review.md to review/SKILL.md — the /review invocation is unchanged, and if you forget to delete the old file, the skill's precedence means the migration still behaves correctly."}, {"id": "4.3-L2-05", "topic": "4.3", "topicName": "Custom commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You want a /standup command available in every project on your machine, without adding anything to any repository. Where do you create it?", "options": ["As .claude/commands/standup.md in each project you use it in", "As an entry in the \"commands\" array of ~/.claude/settings.json", "As a section in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md that describes the standup format", "As ~/.claude/commands/standup.md — the user-level commands directory"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Custom commands have the same two-level scoping as skills: project-level files in .claude/commands/ travel with the repo to the whole team, while user-level files in ~/.claude/commands/ load in every project on your machine and never leave it. Since the requirement is \"everywhere for me, nowhere for the team,\" the user directory is the answer; per-repo copies would work but leak the command to teammates and multiply maintenance. The settings.json option invents a registration mechanism that doesn't exist — commands are discovered from the filesystem, not declared in settings, which configure harness behavior like permissions and hooks. The CLAUDE.md option confuses memory with templates: text there is injected into every prompt whether or not you're doing standup, costing tokens on every turn of the resend loop, and it creates no /standup invocation. A command file costs nothing until the moment you type its name."}, {"id": "4.3-L2-06", "topic": "4.3", "topicName": "Custom commands", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You type \"/fix-issue 417\". By the time the request reaches the model, what does the model actually receive?", "options": ["The command template plus a separate arguments object it must combine", "The command name as a structured invocation block that the model resolves against the list of registered commands the harness sends with every request", "Only the final expanded prompt, with 417 already substituted where $ARGUMENTS was — the model never sees the template", "The path to the command file, which the model reads with a tool call"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Command expansion is finished before the request is assembled. The harness takes the template, substitutes $ARGUMENTS (or $1, $2…) with what you typed, and places the resulting plain text into the turn's message — so the model sees ordinary instructions with \"417\" inline, indistinguishable from text you typed by hand. There is no template-plus-arguments protocol, no command-name token for the model to resolve, and no file for it to fetch; all three distractors imagine the model participating in expansion, when expansion is precisely the step that happens without the model. This is why commands are deterministic in a way instructions aren't: the same invocation always produces the same injected text, because it's string interpolation in the harness, not model interpretation. It also connects to the stateless loop: the model only ever receives assembled text — every extension point, commands included, is ultimately a different way of shaping that text."}, {"id": "4.4-L2-03", "topic": "4.4", "topicName": "MCP", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A query tool on one of your MCP servers can return enormous results. What stops a single call from flooding your context window?", "options": ["The harness detects oversized results and spends one extra model call summarizing them down before folding the summary into the conversation as the tool result", "Claude Code caps MCP tool output — 25,000 tokens by default, with a warning threshold — raisable via MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_TOKENS", "Nothing — MCP results flow into context in full, so you must trust each server's restraint", "The MCP specification requires servers to paginate all responses to a fixed page size"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The harness enforces the limit itself: MCP tool outputs are capped at 25,000 tokens by default, with a warning as outputs approach it (at 10,000), and the ceiling is adjustable through the MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_TOKENS environment variable. The cap matters because of the resend: a tool result doesn't cost its tokens once — it enters the conversation and is retransmitted on every subsequent turn, so one unbounded dump would tax the entire remainder of the session and accelerate compaction. The trust-the-server option describes the world this guard exists to prevent — an external server is exactly the kind of executor whose verbosity you can't control. No summarization model call happens; the harness truncates rather than spending tokens to compress. And the MCP spec doesn't mandate pagination — response discipline varies by server, which is why the protection lives client-side, in the harness, where it protects you regardless of who wrote the server."}, {"id": "4.4-L2-04", "topic": "4.4", "topicName": "MCP", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A remote MCP server you've added requires OAuth. How do you authenticate Claude Code to it?", "options": ["Through the /mcp panel, which walks you through the browser OAuth flow and stores the tokens", "By running /login, which authenticates all connected services at once", "By pasting an access token into the project's CLAUDE.md so the model can present it", "You can't — MCP supports only API keys passed as headers, not OAuth"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "OAuth for remote MCP servers is handled by the /mcp panel: it launches the browser authorization flow, and the resulting tokens are stored for reuse — the harness authenticates to the server on your behalf thereafter. The /login distractor confuses the two auth layers that beginners most often blur: /login authenticates the harness to the model provider (and as a Bedrock user you don't even use it — your model auth comes from AWS credentials), while MCP auth is between the harness and each tool server; the two are independent. Pasting a token into CLAUDE.md is a category error with a security sting: that text goes into the prompt, so the secret would be resent to the model every turn and sit in your transcript — and the harness, not the model, presents MCP credentials anyway. Simpler setups do use API keys via headers or env vars in the server config, but that's an alternative, not the only mechanism."}, {"id": "4.4-L3-03", "topic": "4.4", "topicName": "MCP", "difficulty": 3, "question": "The same MCP server name is configured in local scope, in the project's .mcp.json, and in user scope — with different settings in each. Which definition does Claude Code use?", "options": ["The project one — a shared .mcp.json is authoritative over any personal configuration", "The user one — the widest scope resolves last and overrides narrower ones", "The local one — precedence runs local over project over user, most specific first", "None — Claude Code reports the collision and disables the server until you remove the duplicates"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "MCP scope precedence is local beats project beats user: the most specific, most personal definition wins. The logic is the same as settings-file precedence — the closer a configuration sits to \"this person, this project, right now,\" the more it reflects a deliberate choice, so your local override of a team server (say, pointing it at a staging endpoint) must beat the checked-in .mcp.json, which in turn beats your cross-project default. The project-wins option is the tempting inversion — \"team config is authoritative\" sounds like governance, but it would make personal overrides impossible, and shared config that can't be locally overridden is a trap in any layered system. The user-wins option gets layering backwards: wide scopes are fallbacks, not trumps. And name collisions are resolved silently by precedence, not treated as errors — shadowing is the designed behavior, exactly so a developer can locally rewire a server without editing the shared file."}, {"id": "4.4-L3-04", "topic": "4.4", "topicName": "MCP", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You hand-edit your MCP config JSON to add a remote server, giving it just a \"url\" field. On startup, Claude Code skips the server with an error. Why?", "options": ["Remote servers can't be configured via JSON at all — only through claude mcp add", "The URL is missing the SSE endpoint path that remote servers must expose", "The server hasn't been approved in the project trust prompt yet — hand-added remote servers bypass the claude mcp add approval flow, so the harness skips them until you accept via /mcp", "The entry lacks a transport declaration — without a \"type\", it's parsed as a stdio server, which expects a command, so the url-only entry is invalid"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "This is a documented config gotcha: stdio is the default transport, so a JSON entry without a \"type\" field is interpreted as a stdio server — a child process the harness would spawn from a \"command\". An entry with only a \"url\" therefore parses as a stdio server missing its command, and the harness skips it with an error rather than guessing. The fix is declaring the transport explicitly: \"type\": \"http\" (or the spec's alias \"streamable-http\"). The claude mcp add path avoids the trap because --transport http sets the field for you — which is why hand-editing is where this bites; JSON config is fully supported. The SSE option points at a deprecated transport and invents a path requirement. The approval option describes a real mechanism — project-scoped servers do require your acceptance — but that gate produces an approval prompt, not a parse-level skip, and it applies to .mcp.json entries regardless of transport. The error here is structural, not trust-related."}, {"id": "4.4-L4-03", "topic": "4.4", "topicName": "MCP", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A GitHub MCP server ships bundled inside a plugin named \"devtools\". You want a permission rule allowing only its create_pr tool. What is the tool's full name?", "options": ["mcp__plugin_devtools_github__create_pr", "mcp__github__create_pr", "mcp__devtools__github__create_pr", "plugin:devtools:github:create_pr"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Plugin-bundled MCP servers get an extended naming form: mcp__plugin_<plugin>_<server>__<tool>, so this tool is mcp__plugin_devtools_github__create_pr. The name matters operationally because it's the routing key everywhere names are matched — permission rules, hook matchers, subagent tools lists — and a rule written against the wrong form silently matches nothing, which for an allow rule means unexpected prompts and for a deny rule means a hole in your policy. Each trap is a real convention misapplied. mcp__github__create_pr is exactly right for the same server added directly (not via a plugin) — the era/configuration trap: your rule would break the day the server moves into a plugin. mcp__devtools__github__create_pr misreads the plugin form as just another nesting level, dropping the plugin_ marker that distinguishes the two namespaces. And the colon form borrows plugin skill naming (plugin-name:skill-name) — a genuine Claude Code convention, but for skills, a different extension surface entirely."}, {"id": "4.4-L4-04", "topic": "4.4", "topicName": "MCP", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Which of these statements about MCP transports in Claude Code is accurate?", "options": ["SSE remains the recommended transport for remote servers because it supports streaming responses; plain HTTP is retained only for legacy servers that can't hold connections open", "WebSocket servers are added with claude mcp add --transport ws and support the full OAuth flow", "In JSON config, \"streamable-http\" is accepted as an alias for the http transport, matching the MCP specification's own name for it", "stdio entries must declare \"type\": \"stdio\" explicitly, or the harness skips them with an error"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The MCP specification calls its HTTP transport \"streamable-http\", and Claude Code accepts that string in JSON config as an alias for http — a small compatibility detail that matters when you're adapting a server's documented config verbatim. Each distractor inverts a true fact. The SSE claim reverses the deprecation arrow: HTTP is the recommended remote transport and SSE is the deprecated one — true in an earlier era, which is what makes it an expert trap. The WebSocket claim is wrong on both counts: ws exists for servers that push events unprompted, but it supports only header-based auth (no OAuth flow) and cannot be added via the --transport flag — it's the most constrained transport, not a first-class peer. The stdio claim inverts the real parsing gotcha: stdio is the default when \"type\" is absent; it's remote entries — a url with no type — that get misparsed as stdio and skipped. Explicit typing is required for remote, optional for local."}, {"id": "4.5-L2-03", "topic": "4.5", "topicName": "Subagents", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Claude Code needs to answer a broad question about an unfamiliar codebase without flooding the main conversation. Which built-in subagent fits, and what can it do?", "options": ["There are no built-in subagents — every agent type must be defined in .claude/agents/ first", "Explore — a read-only search agent that sweeps the codebase at a requested thoroughness and returns conclusions, not file dumps", "Plan — which researches the codebase and then executes the implementation plan it writes", "Explore — which sweeps the codebase read-mostly, but can also apply small single-file fixes it finds along the way, since trivial edits are pre-approved for built-in agents"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Explore is the built-in read-only search agent: Claude delegates broad codebase questions to it, specifies how hard to look (quick, medium, or very thorough), and receives conclusions while all the intermediate file reading stays in the child's disposable context. Read-only is a load-bearing property, not a limitation — an agent that cannot edit can be granted wide freedom to roam without permission friction, which is what makes automatic delegation to it safe. That's why the \"can also apply small fixes\" variant is wrong: give Explore write access and every delegation becomes a risk decision. The Plan option breaks on its second half — Plan does read-only research during plan mode, keeping exploration out of the main window precisely while the conversation must stay non-mutating; executing plans is the main loop's job after you approve. And built-ins exist alongside custom definitions (general-purpose rounds out the set, with the full tool kit) — .claude/agents/ is for adding roles, not for bootstrapping the feature."}, {"id": "4.5-L2-04", "topic": "4.5", "topicName": "Subagents", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A subagent working on a delegated task wants to run a command that requires permission. What happens?", "options": ["The parent agent evaluates the request against your session's permission rules and approves or denies it on your behalf, so delegated work never interrupts you", "The subagent inherits auto-accept for everything, since delegation implied trust", "The command is silently denied — subagents can only use pre-approved tools", "The permission prompt surfaces in your main session, naming which subagent is asking — a child can't approve anything on your behalf"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Permission authority never delegates. A subagent runs its own loop with its own tool calls and its own permission checks, but when a check needs a human decision, the prompt routes to the one place a human is: your main session, labeled with which subagent asked. Neither the parent agent nor the child can stand in for you — models don't approve permissions; the permission system and the user do, and that invariant is exactly what keeps delegation from becoming a privilege-escalation path. The parent-approves option imagines the model in the human's chair. The auto-accept option would mean spawning an agent silently widens what can run unattended — the opposite of how trust boundaries should behave. The silent-denial option overcorrects: subagents aren't restricted to pre-approved tools — undecided cases escalate rather than fail. Custom agents can narrow the surface via a tools allowlist in frontmatter, but that's scoping, not a substitute for your approval."}, {"id": "4.5-L3-03", "topic": "4.5", "topicName": "Subagents", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague says: \"Our CLAUDE.md coding rules apply to every delegated search too — all subagents load the project context when they start.\" What's the correction?", "options": ["Not all of them — Explore and Plan skip loading CLAUDE.md to keep research fast and cheap; other subagents do load it", "None needed — the harness injects CLAUDE.md into every agent loop it runs, children included", "The opposite is true — no subagent ever loads CLAUDE.md; project memory belongs exclusively to the main session", "Subagents receive a compacted summary of CLAUDE.md rather than the full file, so the rules apply approximately"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Subagent context assembly is per-agent-type, not uniform. Most subagents load your CLAUDE.md files like a fresh session would — but the built-in Explore and Plan agents deliberately skip them: they exist to answer read-only research questions cheaply, and paying the project-memory overhead on every quick search would defeat their purpose. The practical consequence is exactly the trap the colleague is walking into: conventions written in CLAUDE.md (\"we call it FooService, not FooManager\") do not inform Explore's searching or reporting, so a delegated search that depends on those rules needs them restated in the task prompt. The always-injected option is the natural but wrong generalization — assembly is a per-loop decision by the harness, not a global constant. The never-loaded option overcorrects into a different false rule. And no summarization step exists here: agents that load CLAUDE.md load it in full; compaction is a context-lifecycle mechanism, not a memory-distribution one."}, {"id": "4.5-L3-04", "topic": "4.5", "topicName": "Subagents", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A subagent's final report is good but raises one follow-up question about what it found. What is the cheapest way to get the answer?", "options": ["Spawn a fresh subagent with the original task prompt plus the follow-up question appended", "Have the parent pull the child's saved transcript from disk into its own context and read the answer directly out of the recorded tool results", "Send the follow-up to the same subagent — it can be resumed with its context intact, so it answers from work already done", "Redo the relevant part of the investigation inline in the main conversation"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Subagents can be resumed: a follow-up message continues the same child with its context intact, so the question lands on an agent that already holds the investigation in its window — it answers from memory instead of re-exploring. That's the cheapest path by construction: no re-briefing, no repeated tool calls, and the parent still receives only a short reply. Spawning a fresh agent pays the full exploration again — the new child shares nothing with the old one; a subagent's accumulated context is not transferable, only continuable. Pulling the transcript into the parent is the near-miss: the child's transcript does persist on disk, but importing it wholesale dumps the entire investigation into the parent's context — the exact pollution delegation existed to prevent, now taxed on every subsequent resend. Redoing the work inline is that same mistake with extra steps. The pattern to internalize: pass-by-value for results, resume-in-place for follow-ups."}, {"id": "4.5-L4-02", "topic": "4.5", "topicName": "Subagents", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague writes .claude/agents/security-reviewer.md, assuming the markdown body adds extra instructions on top of the standard subagent system prompt. What actually happens to that body?", "options": ["It's appended after the default system prompt, so the agent gets both the standard behaviors and the additions", "It replaces the default system prompt entirely — the agent's baseline behavior is only what the body says, plus harness-level basics like environment details", "It's injected as the agent's project memory, the way CLAUDE.md content is, on top of an unchanged system prompt", "It isn't sent to the child at all — the body documents the role for the delegating model, which reads it alongside the description when choosing an agent"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The body of a custom agent definition becomes the child's system prompt — replacing, not extending, what a generic agent would get. This is the detail that bites in practice: an author who writes only \"Review code for security issues\" has thrown away the default prompt's operational guidance and gets an oddly bare agent, wondering where the standard behaviors went. If you want them, your body must provide them; a custom definition is a rewrite of the agent's constitution, not an addendum. Each trap relocates the body to an adjacent layer. Appending is the wrong direction — the design intent is full control over the child's identity, which appending can't deliver. The CLAUDE.md option confuses the memory layer (project context in the conversation) with the system-prompt layer (standing instructions). And the delegation-matching option promotes a real mechanism — the description field is what the delegating model reads when choosing — but the body is for the child, never routing metadata."}, {"id": "4.6-L3-03", "topic": "4.6", "topicName": "Agent teams & orchestration", "difficulty": 3, "question": "The lead of an agent team spawns a teammate for a task. What does that teammate actually know when it starts?", "options": ["The lead's full conversation history, streamed into the teammate's window at spawn time so the whole team reasons over one shared context", "A compacted summary of the lead's session, generated at spawn time", "Only its spawn prompt, in a bare context with no project configuration loaded", "Its spawn prompt plus whatever a fresh session loads — CLAUDE.md, MCP servers, skills — but nothing of the lead's conversation"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "A teammate is not a child loop inside the lead's session — it is a separate, full Claude Code session. That cuts both ways. Like any fresh session, it loads the project context: CLAUDE.md files, configured MCP servers, skills. But conversation history is per-session state, and the lead's does not carry over; the only bridge is the spawn prompt, so any decision or constraint from the lead's conversation must be restated there. The full-history option imagines a shared team memory that doesn't exist — coordination happens through the explicit machinery of the shared task list and inbox messages, not ambient context. The compaction option borrows a real mechanism from the wrong lifecycle: compaction manages one session's window, not spawn-time briefings. The bare-context option is the near-miss in the other direction — closer to a lean subagent like Explore, whereas teammates pay full session freight, which is also why team cost scales roughly linearly with team size."}, {"id": "4.6-L3-04", "topic": "4.6", "topicName": "Agent teams & orchestration", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Which of these workloads actually justifies an agent team rather than plain subagents?", "options": ["Debugging by competing hypotheses, where workers should actively try to disprove each other's theories as they investigate", "A broad read-only sweep of the codebase where only the conclusion needs to come back", "A refactor that must proceed through strictly sequential steps, where the shared task list lets each teammate claim the next step and build directly on the last one's output", "A large batch of edits concentrated in one shared file, parallelized for speed"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The decision rule is: do the workers need to talk to each other? Teams add peer-to-peer machinery — inbox messaging between any two agents, a shared task list — and that overhead pays off only when the work benefits from lateral discussion. Competing-hypothesis debugging is the canonical case: its value comes from teammates challenging each other's theories mid-flight, which requires the peer channel subagents simply don't have (a subagent can only report to its caller). The broad sweep is textbook subagent work — one conclusion flowing up needs no peers; a team would spend its tokens on coordination nobody consumes. The sequential refactor is wrong for both constructs: with each step gated on the last, a single session outperforms any fan-out. And the shared-file batch fails at the filesystem layer — nothing merges concurrent edits, so parallel writers to one file overwrite each other regardless of topology. Parallelize only what decomposes; add a team only when the parts must negotiate."}, {"id": "4.6-L4-02", "topic": "4.6", "topicName": "Agent teams & orchestration", "difficulty": 4, "question": "You want a hard \"definition of done\" for an agent team: no task may be marked completed unless the checks actually pass — enforced, not requested. Which mechanism?", "options": ["A Stop hook in each teammate's session that exits 2 whenever checks haven't passed", "A line in the lead's spawn prompt instructing it to verify every task before accepting completion", "A TaskCompleted hook that runs the checks and blocks the task's transition to completed when they fail", "A TeammateIdle hook that rejects task completion whenever a teammate still has failing checks"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Hooks extend into the team layer with events on the coordination machinery itself: TaskCreated and TaskCompleted can block task-state transitions, so a TaskCompleted hook that runs the checks and blocks on failure makes \"done\" a fact the harness verifies, not a claim an agent makes. That's the load-bearing property — deterministic enforcement at the exact transition being guarded. Each trap fails at a specific joint. The Stop hook is real and its exit-2 blocking is real, but it guards the wrong transition: it fires when a session tries to finish a turn, and turn boundaries don't correspond to task completions. The spawn-prompt instruction is asking nicely: probabilistic compliance from the lead, degrading as its context fills — precisely what \"enforced, not requested\" rules out. TeammateIdle is the adjacent-event trap: it fires when a teammate is about to go idle, where exit 2 sends feedback to keep it working; it has no authority over task-state transitions."}, {"id": "4.7-L3-02", "topic": "4.7", "topicName": "Dynamic workflows", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Your pipeline script runs an analysis stage and then an implementation stage as separate headless (claude -p) invocations. How should stage two receive stage one's conclusions?", "options": ["Launch stage two with --resume so it continues stage one's session where it left off", "Have stage one write its findings to a file, and have the script hand that file to stage two as input", "Nothing is needed — invocations spawned by one script share a context automatically", "Rely on provider-side session state, which persists between API calls from the same machine"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Artifacts through files is the pipeline discipline: stage one distills its work into findings.md, the script passes it forward, and stage two starts a fresh context containing only that distilled input. It's the same pass-by-value principle subagents enforce, applied between whole invocations — each stage gets a small window holding exactly what it needs. The --resume option is the seductive near-miss because it genuinely would give stage two the information: but it continues the session, carrying stage one's entire history — every exploration dead-end included — into stage two's context. You'd pay the full resend and forfeit the clean-slate benefit that made the pipeline worth building; resuming continues conversations, it doesn't hand off results. The automatic-sharing option imagines state the harness doesn't keep between invocations. And provider-side session state doesn't exist at all — the API is stateless on Anthropic and Bedrock alike; every request contains everything the model will know. That statelessness is exactly why the file is the right channel."}, {"id": "4.7-L4-02", "topic": "4.7", "topicName": "Dynamic workflows", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague argues: \"Choosing a scripted pipeline means giving up model judgment — once the orchestration is code, every decision has to be pre-coded.\" What's the accurate picture?", "options": ["They're right — headless claude -p invocations run without the Agent tool, so no delegation can happen inside a stage", "They're right unless you build on the Agent SDK, which is the only integration point where scripted and model-driven orchestration can mix", "They're wrong, because the script itself can call the model at each gate to decide whether to proceed — since the script owns control flow, routing gate decisions through a model call keeps the pipeline fully deterministic while adding judgment", "They're wrong — the strongest pattern is a deterministic skeleton with a dynamic interior: the script fixes stages and gates, while within each stage the model retains full agency, including spawning subagents as it sees fit"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Scripted orchestration constrains the structure between stages, not the behavior within them. Each stage is a complete agent loop: inside it, the model plans, uses tools, and delegates to subagents exactly as it would interactively. The hybrid is the recommended architecture because the layers answer different needs — code where you need guarantees (ordering, retries, gates on real exit codes), judgment where you need adaptation. Option A invents a restriction: headless mode changes the interface, not the capability set — delegation works under -p. Option B overstates a real tool into exclusivity: the SDK is one way to build orchestrators, but a shell script driving claude -p mixes the layers just as legitimately. Option C is the subtle one: routing gating decisions through a model call makes the gates probabilistic, surrendering exactly the determinism that justified scripting the skeleton — judgment belongs inside stages; gates should stay code."}, {"id": "5.1-L1-01", "topic": "5.1", "topicName": "Auth methods", "difficulty": 1, "question": "You run Claude Code against AWS Bedrock at work. Where does your authentication actually come from?", "options": ["The `/login` command, which opens a browser OAuth flow against Anthropic", "An `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` environment variable issued by the Anthropic console", "Your AWS credentials (IAM role, profile, or keys) resolved through the standard AWS credential chain", "A per-session Bedrock token you generate manually in the AWS console before each session"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "In Bedrock mode, Claude Code is just another AWS API client: it picks up credentials the same way the AWS CLI does — environment variables, a named profile, an SSO session, or an attached IAM role — and your permission to call Claude is an IAM policy question, not an Anthropic account question. That's why Bedrock users never run `/login`: that command exists for the Anthropic-direct path, where auth is either an API key or an OAuth session tied to a Claude subscription. There is no per-session Bedrock token to mint by hand; credentials come from the chain and are refreshed by it. This is the first concrete Bedrock-vs-Anthropic delta: same harness, same loop, different front door — and the Bedrock door is opened with IAM, not anything Anthropic issues."}, {"id": "5.1-L2-01", "topic": "5.1", "topicName": "Auth methods", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Which environment variable tells Claude Code to send its API traffic to AWS Bedrock instead of the Anthropic API?", "options": ["CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK", "ANTHROPIC_PROVIDER", "CLAUDE_BACKEND", "AWS_BEDROCK_ENABLED"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Setting `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1` flips the provider: the harness stops targeting Anthropic's Messages API endpoint and starts making SigV4-signed calls to Bedrock in your configured AWS region (a sibling variable, `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_VERTEX`, does the same for Google Vertex AI). The other names are invented — there is no `ANTHROPIC_PROVIDER` or `CLAUDE_BACKEND` switch, and `AWS_BEDROCK_ENABLED` is not a real AWS variable. This matters for the Bedrock POV because Bedrock configuration is env-var heavy by design: provider selection, region (`AWS_REGION`), and model choice typically all flow through the environment rather than through `/login` or the Anthropic console. Note what does NOT change when you flip this switch: the harness machinery — prompt assembly, tools, the stateless resend loop — is identical; only the transport, auth, and billing endpoint move."}, {"id": "5.1-L2-02", "topic": "5.1", "topicName": "Auth methods", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A teammate uses Claude Code against the Anthropic API directly, signs in with `/login`, and pays for a Claude Max plan. How is their usage authenticated and billed?", "options": ["With an API key; every token is billed to a pay-as-you-go account", "With an IAM role; usage lands on a corporate cloud bill", "With an OAuth session, but every input and output token is still metered and invoiced per million tokens through the Anthropic console", "With an OAuth session tied to their subscription; usage draws on the plan's included allowance rather than per-token invoices"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "`/login` establishes an OAuth session against the user's Claude account, and when that account has a Pro/Max subscription, Claude Code usage is covered by the plan's included allowance (subject to the plan's usage limits) — no per-token invoice is generated. That's a fundamentally different commercial model from the other two doors: an Anthropic API key means pay-per-token metered billing through the Anthropic console, and Bedrock means per-token charges on the AWS bill. Option C is the tempting near-miss: OAuth is the right mechanism but subscription usage is not invoiced per million tokens. As a Bedrock user you have no equivalent of the subscription path at all — there is no 'Max plan for Bedrock'; every token you send, including the giant resent prefix each turn, is metered AWS spend. Same model, three doors, three billing regimes."}, {"id": "5.1-L3-01", "topic": "5.1", "topicName": "Auth methods", "difficulty": 3, "question": "At the wire level, how does a Claude Code request prove its identity to Bedrock, versus to the Anthropic API?", "options": ["Both paths send a bearer token issued by Anthropic in the Authorization header; Bedrock merely validates that token against your AWS account before serving the call", "Bedrock requests are SigV4-signed with AWS credentials; Anthropic-direct requests carry an `x-api-key` header or an OAuth bearer token", "Bedrock embeds your Anthropic API key inside the AWS request envelope", "Bedrock authenticates via client TLS certificates; Anthropic uses API keys"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The two providers use entirely disjoint auth machinery. Anthropic-direct requests carry credentials in a header: `x-api-key` for key-based access, or an OAuth bearer token for subscription sessions. Bedrock requests carry no Anthropic credential at all — they are AWS API calls, cryptographically signed with SigV4 using your IAM credentials, and Bedrock authorizes them against IAM policies (e.g. whether your role may invoke that model in that region). This is why 'my Anthropic key expired' is never a Bedrock failure mode, and why Bedrock failures instead look like AWS ones: AccessDenied, expired STS sessions, missing model access grants. The distractors all imagine some Anthropic credential surviving inside the AWS path; nothing does. Anthropic supplies the model weights to AWS; AWS supplies the entire front door — auth, authorization, and metering."}, {"id": "5.2-L2-01", "topic": "5.2", "topicName": "Model identity & availability", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Which of these identifiers is shaped like what a Bedrock-based Claude Code setup would actually use to select a model?", "options": ["claude-sonnet-4-20250514", "us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-20250514-v1:0", "bedrock://claude-sonnet-4", "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Bedrock has its own model-ID scheme: a vendor-prefixed ID like `anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-20250514-v1:0`, often further prefixed with a geography (`us.`, `eu.`, `apac.`) when it names a cross-region inference profile rather than a single-region model. Option A is the Anthropic-direct name — what `/model` resolves to when you talk to Anthropic's API — and it will not work as-is against Bedrock. Options C and D are shapes from other ecosystems (a made-up URI scheme and an OpenRouter-style path). The practical consequence for a Bedrock user: model selection usually happens through environment variables carrying these long IDs, and a config snippet copied from an Anthropic-direct colleague will fail until the model name is translated. Same underlying weights, but each provider issues its own naming for the door you enter through."}, {"id": "5.2-L2-02", "topic": "5.2", "topicName": "Model identity & availability", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Anthropic announces a new Claude model this morning. Why might you, on Bedrock, not be able to use it in Claude Code today, while your Anthropic-direct colleague can?", "options": ["Bedrock availability depends on AWS's own rollout — new models often arrive later and initially in only some regions", "Bedrock always receives new models before the Anthropic API as part of the partnership", "The weights are shared, so the model is instantly available everywhere; only a CLI update is needed", "New models require retraining before AWS can host them, which takes months by design"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Model availability is a per-provider, per-region rollout, not a global switch. Anthropic ships new models to its own API first; AWS then makes them available on Bedrock on its own schedule, typically region by region, and your account may additionally need to enable model access before you can invoke it. So 'the model exists' and 'the model is invokable through your door, in your region, by your account' are separate facts. Option C captures the tempting misconception: yes, the weights are the same everywhere they run, but sharing weights with AWS, standing up serving capacity, and enabling the model per region is real work that lags the announcement. The same lag pattern applies to API features (new caching options, beta headers), which is a recurring theme for Bedrock users: expect the feature set to trail the Anthropic API by some amount."}, {"id": "5.2-L3-01", "topic": "5.2", "topicName": "Model identity & availability", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Your Bedrock model ID starts with `us.` — a cross-region inference profile. What does that prefix actually buy you?", "options": ["It pins every request to the single US region named in your AWS_REGION variable, guaranteeing data residency for compliance-sensitive workloads", "It selects a cheaper US-only pricing tier for the same model", "It enables the model to be fine-tuned on your account's data within US regions", "It lets Bedrock route your requests across several regions in that geography, drawing on pooled capacity instead of one region's quota"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "An inference profile is a routing construct: instead of invoking the model in exactly one region, requests sent to a `us.` (or `eu.`/`apac.`) profile can be served from any participating region in that geography. The point is capacity and resilience — you draw on a pooled set of serving capacity rather than a single region's on-demand pool, which matters when one region is saturated. Note it does the opposite of option A: it spreads requests across regions within the geography rather than pinning them to one, which is exactly why compliance-sensitive teams must check whether cross-region routing is acceptable before using profiles. For newer Claude models on Bedrock, the inference profile is often the only offered way to invoke them. Anthropic-direct users never see this concept — region selection and capacity pooling are entirely a Bedrock-layer concern, above the shared model weights."}, {"id": "5.2-L3-02", "topic": "5.2", "topicName": "Model identity & availability", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague claims: \"Claude on Bedrock answers differently than on the Anthropic API because AWS runs a modified copy of the model.\" What's wrong with that?", "options": ["Nothing — AWS fine-tunes each Claude model for enterprise workloads before hosting it", "It's wrong only about the direction: Anthropic runs the modified copy, Bedrock the original", "The weights are the same model; real differences come from provider-level factors — which model versions and API features are available, defaults, and sampling nondeterminism — not altered weights", "It's wrong because Bedrock doesn't host the weights at all: InvokeModel is a thin proxy that forwards each request over a private link to Anthropic's own servers, which is why responses match byte-for-byte across providers"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Bedrock is a different door to the same model: Anthropic supplies the weights, AWS supplies the serving front-end, auth, and billing. AWS does not retrain or fine-tune Claude. So where do real observed differences come from? Provider-level deltas: Bedrock may offer an older model lineup or lag on API features, defaults can differ, and — most often overlooked — sampling is nondeterministic, so two runs of the same prompt differ even on the same provider, which people misattribute to the platform. Option D overcorrects: Bedrock genuinely hosts and serves the model inside AWS infrastructure (that's its enterprise selling point — traffic stays in your cloud perimeter), it is not a proxy to Anthropic. The durable rule: harness behavior and model behavior are provider-invariant; availability, features, quotas, and billing are where the providers actually diverge."}, {"id": "5.3-L2-01", "topic": "5.3", "topicName": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "difficulty": 2, "question": "When a Claude Code request gets a large prompt-cache hit, what exactly is being saved?", "options": ["Bandwidth and latency — the matched prefix is never retransmitted; the harness ships only the delta since the last call, which is exactly why time-to-first-token drops", "Prefill compute — the server skips recomputing attention state for the matched prefix; the tokens still cross the wire, but you pay ~0.1× and get lower latency", "Output cost — the model's reply is retrieved from cache instead of regenerated", "Context space — cached tokens no longer count against the context window"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A cache read is a discount on recomputation, not on transmission. Every call still ships the entire message array — the full resend — to the server. What the server skips is prefill: for the matched prefix it reloads previously computed attention state instead of recomputing it, which is why cached input bills at ~0.1× of base input price and why time-to-first-token drops. Option A is the most common folk model and it's backwards: you save cost and latency, never bandwidth. Option C confuses prompt caching with response caching — the reply is always freshly generated. Option D is wrong because cached tokens are fully part of the prompt and count against the window like any others. This is the mechanism that makes the stateless loop survivable: the resend grows every turn, but almost all of it lands in the 0.1× tier."}, {"id": "5.3-L2-02", "topic": "5.3", "topicName": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Relative to base input price, what does writing tokens into the prompt cache at the default 5-minute TTL cost?", "options": ["1.25× base input price", "2× base input price", "0.1× base input price", "0.5× base input price"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The stable, documented ratios are: uncached input 1×, cache write at the default 5-minute TTL 1.25×, cache write at the 1-hour TTL 2×, and cache read 0.1×. So a write is a small premium paid once to store the prefix's computed state, and every subsequent read of that prefix costs a tenth of base price — the economics pay for themselves after roughly one or two reads, and a coding session reads each written chunk back dozens or hundreds of times. Option B (2×) is the tempting near-miss: that's the 1-hour TTL write price, the more expensive option a harness buys when it expects gaps between turns longer than five minutes. Option C is the read price, not the write price. These ratios hold across providers that support caching, applied to whatever the base token price is on that provider."}, {"id": "5.3-L3-01", "topic": "5.3", "topicName": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Mid-session you enable a new MCP server. On the very next model call, cache reads collapse to near zero and cache writes spike to the size of the whole conversation. Why?", "options": ["The MCP server's tools ran immediately on connection, and their results arrived as a conversation-sized burst of new output tokens on that first call", "MCP servers disable prompt caching while they are connected", "Tool definitions sit near the front of the request; adding one changed the stable head, so the prefix no longer matches and everything after the change must re-prefill and re-write", "The cache entry idled past its 5-minute TTL while the MCP server was starting up, so the entry was evicted and the next call had to re-prefill and re-write the whole conversation from scratch"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Prompt caching is exact-prefix matching from the very start of the request, in the canonical `tools → system → messages` order. Tool definitions live at the head. Enabling an MCP server inserts new tool schemas into that head, so the first bytes of the request differ from what was cached — the prefix match fails at the earliest change point, and every token after it (potentially hundreds of thousands) must be re-prefilled at full price and re-written at the write premium. That is the real cost of context churn: not the new tool tokens themselves, but the huge cached prefix they dump. Option A confuses input-side cache accounting with output tokens; option B invents a rule (MCP tools cache fine once stable); option D describes TTL expiry, a different miss cause with the same signature. Practical rule, either provider: keep the head still — stability is worth more than size."}, {"id": "5.3-L3-02", "topic": "5.3", "topicName": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Why would an agent harness deliberately pay the 2× write price for the 1-hour cache TTL instead of the cheaper 1.25× five-minute default?", "options": ["The 1-hour tier gives faster cache reads on every subsequent call", "The 1-hour tier is mandatory once the cached prefix exceeds the per-model size threshold — roughly 1,024–4,096 tokens depending on model — so agent-sized prompts have no real choice", "The 5-minute tier only caches the system prompt, not the message history", "Coding sessions have think-time gaps longer than five minutes; a 5-minute entry would expire during a pause and force a catastrophic full re-prefill of the whole prefix"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The TTL choice is an insurance calculation. With a 5-minute TTL, any pause longer than five minutes — reading the diff, a coffee break, a meeting — silently evicts the entry, and the next call pays full prefill on the entire conversation plus a fresh write. With hundreds of thousands of cached tokens at stake, one such miss dwarfs the write premium many times over. Paying 2× instead of 1.25× on each (small) incremental write buys the prefix an hour of survival, and since each written chunk is then read back at 0.1× dozens or hundreds of times, the premium is rounding error against the read savings. Options A–C invent tier differences that don't exist: reads cost and perform the same either way, and both TTLs cache the same content. One provider caveat from the Bedrock seat: longer-TTL support is a serving-stack feature and has historically landed on the Anthropic API before Bedrock — check what your provider actually offers."}, {"id": "5.3-L3-03", "topic": "5.3", "topicName": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Which of the following does NOT invalidate an existing cached prefix?", "options": ["Switching to a different model mid-session", "Appending a new user message at the end of the messages array", "Compaction rewriting the earlier conversation history", "A newly added tool definition changing the front of the request"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Caching is exact-prefix matching: everything up to the last cache breakpoint must be byte-identical to what was stored. Appending new content at the end is precisely what the scheme is designed for — the old prefix still matches, the new suffix prefills at normal price, and the freshly settled part gets written for future reads. That's the normal shape of every turn. The other three all perturb tokens inside the existing prefix: a model switch invalidates everything because the cache is per-model (a different model's attention state is simply different numbers — nothing transfers); compaction rewrites earlier history, so tokens the cached state depended on are gone; and a tool-set change edits the head of the request, invalidating everything behind it. The mental model: the cache dies from edits to the past, never from growth at the live edge. This behavior is identical on Bedrock and Anthropic — it follows from how prefix caching works, not from provider policy."}, {"id": "5.3-L4-01", "topic": "5.3", "topicName": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "What does the server-side prompt cache physically store?", "options": ["Per-layer key/value attention state produced by prefill, keyed by the exact token prefix", "A compressed copy of the prompt text, so it doesn't need to be uploaded again", "The model's decoded output, so identical prompts return the same reply instantly", "A session object mapping your credentials to the running conversation history"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "During prefill, every layer of the model computes key and value vectors for each input token — the attention state. The prompt cache stores exactly that per-layer K/V state, keyed by the exact token sequence that produced it. A cache read reloads those vectors instead of recomputing them, which is valid because same tokens plus same weights provably yield identical state — and it's why one changed token early invalidates everything after it (later vectors were computed attending to the old token), and why the cache is strictly per-model. Option B is the bandwidth folk model: text still crosses the wire every call. Option C describes response caching, which this is not — decode always runs fresh. Option D is the 'server-side session' myth: the API stays fully stateless; the cache is an ephemeral, TTL-evicted compute shortcut, not memory. Evict it and nothing is forgotten — the next call just costs more."}, {"id": "5.3-L4-02", "topic": "5.3", "topicName": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague says: \"Bedrock serves the same Claude weights, so every caching capability — breakpoints, both TTLs, the same betas — is automatically identical there.\" What's wrong with that?", "options": ["Nothing — caching is a property of the weights, so it must be identical", "Bedrock has no prompt caching at all; it's an Anthropic-API exclusive", "Prompt caching lives in the provider's serving stack, not the model; each provider decides which caching features, TTL options, and models it supports, and Bedrock has typically trailed the Anthropic API on newer options", "Caching on Bedrock is handled client-side: the harness stores the computed prefix state locally under ~/.claude and replays it on the next call, so what AWS's serving stack supports — TTLs, breakpoints, betas — is irrelevant to what you can use"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The KV-cache mechanism is universal physics of transformer serving, but the product features around it — `cache_control` breakpoints, which TTLs you may buy, which models support it, whether a capability is GA or behind a beta flag — are implemented in each provider's serving stack and shipped on each provider's schedule. Bedrock does support prompt caching for Claude (so option B overshoots), but the feature set has historically lagged the Anthropic API: new options like the extended 1-hour TTL appeared on Anthropic first, and Bedrock support arrives later and per-model. Option D is exactly backwards — the whole point is that the cache is server-side state; the harness can only request caching, never perform it. For a Bedrock-first user the durable habit is: assume mechanism parity, verify feature parity. Same weights guarantee the same model behavior, never the same API surface."}, {"id": "5.4-L2-01", "topic": "5.4", "topicName": "Rate limiting & quotas", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Your Claude Code session on Bedrock suddenly starts failing mid-task under heavy use. What does hitting Bedrock's throughput limit typically look like?", "options": ["An Anthropic-style rate_limit_error telling you which usage tier to upgrade to", "Requests queue silently on the server until capacity frees up", "Your AWS account is temporarily suspended until the next billing period", "ThrottlingException errors, because you've exceeded the on-demand quota for that model in that region"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Bedrock's on-demand mode enforces service quotas — requests-per-minute and tokens-per-minute style limits, tracked per account, per model, per region. Exceed them and calls fail fast with AWS's ThrottlingException; nothing queues server-side, and the client (Claude Code) is expected to back off and retry. Option A is what the same situation looks like through the other door: the Anthropic API returns a 429 `rate_limit_error` governed by usage tiers on your Anthropic organization. Knowing which vocabulary your provider speaks matters when debugging: 'tier' language means Anthropic, 'quota/throttling' language means AWS. Remedies differ too — on Bedrock you request quota increases through AWS, spread load via cross-region inference profiles, or buy provisioned throughput, rather than climbing Anthropic spend tiers. Agentic workloads hit these limits faster than chat ever would, because every turn resends the whole conversation as input tokens."}, {"id": "5.4-L3-01", "topic": "5.4", "topicName": "Rate limiting & quotas", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Which statement correctly contrasts how the Anthropic API and Bedrock govern how much Claude traffic you can send?", "options": ["Anthropic uses org-level usage tiers whose request/token-per-minute limits grow with your account's history and spend; Bedrock uses per-account, per-region, per-model service quotas, adjustable via AWS quota-increase requests", "Both use the same Anthropic-defined tiers, since the model is the same", "Anthropic has hard fixed limits for everyone; Bedrock is effectively unlimited on demand", "Bedrock quotas bind each IAM principal separately, so spreading traffic across roles multiplies effective throughput, while Anthropic limits attach to each individual API key rather than the organization, so rotating keys resets your throughput"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The two governance models mirror each provider's ecosystem. Anthropic organizes accounts into usage tiers: as your organization spends and builds history, it advances tiers and its RPM/token-per-minute ceilings rise, with custom limits at the top. AWS instead treats Bedrock like any other service: Service Quotas define your on-demand ceilings per model, per region, per account, and you raise them by filing quota-increase requests (or sidestep the shared pool entirely with provisioned throughput or cross-region inference profiles). Option B fails because limits are provider infrastructure, not model property — the same weights sit behind completely different traffic cops. Option C is wrong in both directions: Anthropic tiers grow, and Bedrock on-demand is very much finite, as any parallel-agent workload discovers. Option D misplaces the granularity — Bedrock quotas bind the account+region, not individual IAM users, and Anthropic limits bind the organization, not each key."}, {"id": "5.4-L3-02", "topic": "5.4", "topicName": "Rate limiting & quotas", "difficulty": 3, "question": "What is Bedrock \"provisioned throughput,\" and when would a Claude Code-heavy team reach for it?", "options": ["A free burst allowance AWS grants automatically when on-demand traffic spikes", "Dedicated model capacity you purchase in units for guaranteed throughput — chosen when shared on-demand quotas throttle a steady, high-volume workload", "A client-side setting that raises Claude Code's parallel request count", "A committed-spend discount program that lowers Bedrock's per-token prices once monthly usage passes a threshold, akin to advancing an Anthropic usage tier"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Bedrock offers two throughput models. On-demand is the shared pool: pay per token, but compete with everyone else in the region under your account's quotas, and absorb ThrottlingExceptions when you exceed them. Provisioned throughput is the alternative: you commit to purchasing model units — dedicated serving capacity with a guaranteed token throughput, billed by the hour or via term commitment — so your traffic no longer contends in the shared pool. Teams reach for it when a workload is sustained and throttling-sensitive: fleets of agents, CI pipelines running headless Claude Code, batch processing. The trade is cost shape: you pay for reserved capacity whether or not you saturate it, versus per-token billing that throttles unpredictably. Nothing like this exists on the Anthropic API side, where the lever is tier advancement or negotiated limits. Options A, C, and D describe mechanisms Bedrock doesn't have — capacity there is either shared-and-quota'd or bought outright."}, {"id": "5.4-L4-01", "topic": "5.4", "topicName": "Rate limiting & quotas", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague says: \"Fanning out five parallel subagents is quota-safe on Bedrock — they run under the parent session, so it counts as one request stream.\" What's wrong with that?", "options": ["Nothing — Bedrock meters throughput per streaming connection, and subagents multiplex their calls over the parent's connection, so all five draw from a single request-stream allocation rather than five separate ones against the regional quota", "It's wrong only because Bedrock forbids concurrent connections from one IAM principal", "Each subagent is its own stateless loop making its own API calls, each resending its own full context — five agents multiply token throughput against the same per-region, per-model on-demand quota, inviting throttling", "Subagents don't call the API at all; they reuse the parent's cached responses"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "There is no 'session' at the API layer for quotas to attach to — the API is stateless, and Bedrock meters raw requests and tokens per account, per model, per region. A subagent is not a lightweight thread of the parent: it is a full agent loop with its own context, and every one of its turns resends that entire context as input tokens. Five parallel subagents therefore multiply your tokens-per-minute consumption roughly fivefold (more as their contexts grow), all drawn from the same regional on-demand quota — which is exactly how fan-out workloads walk into ThrottlingException storms that a single interactive session never sees. Option D inverts reality: subagents make real API calls; only their final report returns to the parent. Mitigations live at the AWS layer: quota increases, cross-region inference profiles to pool capacity, provisioned throughput, or simply capping fan-out. On Anthropic direct the same multiplication happens — it just lands on your org's tier limits instead."}, {"id": "5.5-L1-01", "topic": "5.5", "topicName": "Costs", "difficulty": 1, "question": "Per token, how does the price of Claude's generated output compare to the price of the input you send?", "options": ["Input tokens cost more, because prompts are long", "Output tokens cost several times more than input tokens", "They cost the same; a token is a token", "Only output tokens are billed; input is free"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Across the Claude family, output tokens are priced at a multiple of input tokens — roughly five times the input rate. The asymmetry reflects how the model runs: input is ingested in one parallel prefill pass, while output is generated one token at a time in decode, each new token requiring a pass over all accumulated state — output is simply more expensive to produce. This pricing holds through both doors: Bedrock and the Anthropic API keep the same input/output ratio for the same model, applied to their respective price sheets. But don't let the per-token ratio mislead you about session totals: an agentic session resends the entire conversation as input every turn, so input volume vastly exceeds output volume, and most of the bill is input — which is why prompt caching, a discount on input, is the lever that matters most."}, {"id": "5.5-L2-01", "topic": "5.5", "topicName": "Costs", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Where do you actually see what your Bedrock-backed Claude Code usage costs?", "options": ["In your AWS bill — Cost Explorer and billing reports under your AWS account, like any other AWS service", "In the Anthropic console's usage page — Bedrock reports token counts back to the Anthropic organization linked to your AWS account", "Nowhere itemized — it's bundled into a flat Claude subscription fee", "In a monthly statement emailed by Anthropic for Bedrock customers"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "On Bedrock, Anthropic is not your vendor — AWS is. Every token Claude Code sends and receives is metered as Bedrock usage and lands on your AWS bill, inspectable in Cost Explorer, filterable by service and region, taggable, and governable with AWS budgets and alerts like any other cloud spend. Anthropic's console knows nothing about it: your organization there (if you even have one) shows zero usage, because no Anthropic credential was involved. That consolidation is a major reason enterprises choose Bedrock — one bill, one procurement relationship, existing cloud governance. Option C describes the subscription world (Claude Pro/Max via OAuth), which doesn't exist on Bedrock: there is no flat-fee option through the AWS door; it's pay-per-token, always. The practical habit this implies: cost visibility for your Claude Code usage is an AWS-tooling skill, not an Anthropic-console one."}, {"id": "5.5-L3-01", "topic": "5.5", "topicName": "Costs", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Why does the cost of a Claude Code session grow superlinearly with its length, rather than in proportion to the new text each turn adds?", "options": ["Because the provider raises per-token prices as a conversation gets longer", "Because the model thinks harder about later turns, generating more output tokens", "Because tool results are billed at a premium input rate — the same 1.25× multiplier as cache writes — and later turns carry ever more tool output", "Because the API is stateless: every call resends the entire conversation, so turn n pays input costs for turns 1 through n all over again"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The keystone: nothing lives server-side between calls. Each turn, the harness rebuilds the whole message array — system prompt, tools, every prior exchange, every tool result — and ships it again as input. So the input cost of turn n is proportional to the size of the entire conversation so far, and summing that across a session gives roughly quadratic total input. Turn 41 re-pays for reading turns 1–40, which turn 40 already paid to read. Options A–C invent per-token pricing changes that don't exist; the per-token price is flat — it's the token count that compounds. Prompt caching is what makes this survivable, dropping most of the resend into the ~0.1× tier, but 0.1× of a quadratically growing base still grows. This is identical on Bedrock and Anthropic — statelessness is a property of the protocol, not the provider — though on Bedrock the compounding also burns your regional token quota, not just money."}, {"id": "5.5-L3-02", "topic": "5.5", "topicName": "Costs", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You've finished a long refactoring task in a Claude Code session and are about to start a completely unrelated task. Purely as a cost lever, what's the best move?", "options": ["Keep going in the same session — the cache makes the old history nearly free", "Run /compact so the history is summarized before the new task", "Run /clear (or start a new session) so the resend base drops to just the stable prefix", "Switch to a smaller model in the same session to offset the longer context"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Once the old task's history is irrelevant, every remaining turn still pays to resend it — cached or not. `/clear` resets the conversation to the stable prefix, so the quadratic growth restarts from near zero; for an unrelated task you lose nothing, because none of that history would help anyway. Option A underestimates the residue: cache reads are 0.1×, not 0×, the dead weight consumes finite context window, and any cache miss re-bills the whole pile at write prices. Option B is the wrong tool here — compaction costs a full read of the conversation to produce its summary and exists to preserve continuity you don't need; it's the lever for a task that must continue, not one that's over. Option D backfires doubly: a mid-session model switch invalidates the entire cache (it's per-model), forcing a full re-prefill of history you didn't even want. Rule of thumb: compaction preserves, `/clear` resets — match the lever to whether continuity has value."}, {"id": "5.5-L4-01", "topic": "5.5", "topicName": "Costs", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A prefix chunk is written to cache at the 1-hour TTL (2× base input price). Reads cost 0.1×. Compared with never caching it, after how many subsequent reads does the write pay for itself?", "options": ["1 read", "2 reads", "About 8 reads", "About 20 reads"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Do the arithmetic per token of the chunk. Without caching: the first send costs 1×, and each of n later resends costs 1× — total 1 + n. With caching: the first send is written at 2×, and each later resend reads at 0.1× — total 2 + 0.1n. Caching wins when 2 + 0.1n < 1 + n, i.e. 0.9n > 1, so n ≥ 2: by the second read you're ahead, and every read after that banks a further 0.9× saving. That's the entire economic argument for agent harnesses buying even the expensive TTL: in a session with dozens or hundreds of model calls, each settled chunk is reread on every subsequent call, so the write premium is rounding error against cumulative read savings. The same arithmetic at the 5-minute write price (1.25×) breaks even after a single read. The ratios — not the absolute prices — are the durable facts, and they apply on Bedrock and Anthropic alike wherever each tier is supported."}, {"id": "5.5-L4-02", "topic": "5.5", "topicName": "Costs", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague argues: \"With cache reads at 0.1×, long sessions are basically free, so compaction and /clear are pointless cost-wise on Bedrock.\" What's wrong with that?", "options": ["0.1× of a quadratically growing resend still grows; the window is finite so compaction is eventually forced anyway; and one cache miss — TTL expiry, model switch, head churn — re-bills the whole prefix at write prices", "Nothing — with reads at 0.1× and refresh-on-hit keeping entries alive at no extra charge, the cached portion's marginal cost genuinely rounds to zero, leaving only the small uncached remainder and the output tokens to matter on the bill", "It's wrong only because Bedrock bills cache reads at full input price", "It's wrong because cache reads still consume output-token budget"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Three compounding errors. First, 0.1× is a discount, not zero: the resend base grows quadratically across a session, and a tenth of a quadratic is still a quadratic — a bloated context also keeps consuming regional token quota on Bedrock, where throughput is as scarce as dollars. Second, the context window is a hard budget; grow long enough and compaction happens whether you planned it or not, at a worse moment. Third, the discount is fragile: the cached prefix survives only while nothing perturbs it — a pause past the TTL, a model switch, a tool-set change — and any miss converts the entire accumulated prefix into a full re-prefill plus re-write at the write premium. The larger the pile you let accumulate, the bigger that downside. Option C is false (Bedrock uses the same discounted read tier where caching is supported), and option D confuses token classes — cache reads are input-side accounting. Caching makes length survivable, not free; hygiene still pays."}, {"id": "5.6-L3-01", "topic": "5.6", "topicName": "Request/response anatomy", "difficulty": 3, "question": "When Claude Code finishes running a tool, under which role does the tool_result content block go back to the model in the next API call?", "options": ["A dedicated \"tool\" role message", "A \"system\" message, since the harness produced it", "An \"assistant\" message, appended to the model's own turn", "A \"user\" message, keyed to the request by tool_use_id"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The Messages API has no tool role. The model's `tool_use` block arrives in an assistant message; the harness executes the tool locally and appends the `tool_result` block inside a user message, linked back via `tool_use_id`, then resends the whole array. So from the model's perspective there is exactly one inbound channel — the user turn — and file contents, command output, and actual human speech all arrive through it wearing the same name. That is the structural reason prompt injection via tool results is a protocol-level security category rather than a bug: text inside a tool result is indistinguishable in kind from text a user typed. Option A imports another ecosystem's convention (some APIs do define a tool role; Anthropic's does not). This shape is provider-invariant: Bedrock's InvokeModel carries the identical Anthropic message structure, so the tool round-trip looks exactly the same through either door."}, {"id": "5.6-L3-02", "topic": "5.6", "topicName": "Request/response anatomy", "difficulty": 3, "question": "An API response comes back with stop_reason \"tool_use\". What does the Claude Code harness do, and how does that differ from \"end_turn\"?", "options": ["On tool_use it holds the stream open while the server executes the tool remotely, then receives the result pushed down the same connection before decoding resumes", "Both end the turn; tool_use just annotates that tools were used along the way", "On tool_use it asks the user to run the command manually and paste the output", "On tool_use it executes the requested tool locally, appends the result, and immediately calls the API again; only end_turn returns control to the human"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "stop_reason is the hinge of the agent loop. `tool_use` means the model stopped because it emitted a tool request — a structured block with an id, name, and input. The server does nothing further; execution is entirely the harness's job, on your machine. Claude Code runs the tool (subject to permissions), appends the `tool_result` as a user message, and sends the now-longer array straight back — no human involved. Only when a response arrives without a tool request (`end_turn`) does the loop exit and control return to you; one 'turn' can therefore span dozens or hundreds of API calls. Option A is the classic misreading of where execution lives: the server only ever emits text — it has no access to your filesystem or shell. Other stop reasons like `max_tokens` and `stop_sequence` end a single decode for different causes. The loop logic is identical on Bedrock, which returns the same stop_reason values in the same response shape."}, {"id": "5.6-L4-01", "topic": "5.6", "topicName": "Request/response anatomy", "difficulty": 4, "question": "How does the raw request Claude Code sends to Bedrock's InvokeModel differ from the equivalent request to Anthropic's Messages API?", "options": ["Bedrock requires rewriting the conversation into AWS's normalized Converse schema — camelCase fields, toolSpec definitions, cachePoint markers — discarding Anthropic's native content blocks before any Claude model on Bedrock will accept the request", "There is no difference at all — even the URL and headers are identical", "The body is the same Anthropic-shaped payload (messages, content blocks, max_tokens), but the model is named in the request URI rather than a body field, and the body carries an anthropic_version marker; auth and transport are AWS's", "Bedrock only accepts a single flattened prompt string, so the harness concatenates the messages array"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Bedrock's InvokeModel for Claude is a thin AWS envelope around the native Anthropic payload. Inside the body you find the same structure the Messages API takes — the messages array, content blocks, system, tools, max_tokens — with two Bedrock-specific edits: the model isn't a body parameter (it's the modelId in the request path, since InvokeModel is generic across vendors) and the body includes an `anthropic_version` field pinning the payload schema. Everything around the body is AWS: SigV4 auth, AWS endpoints, AWS error shapes, and a streaming variant (InvokeModelWithResponseStream) wrapping the same event stream. Option A describes Bedrock's separate Converse API, which does normalize messages into an AWS-unified format across vendors — but Claude Code uses the native-payload path precisely to keep full access to Anthropic-specific features. This wrapping is why the harness barely changes between providers: the protocol the model speaks — blocks, tool_use, stop reasons — passes through Bedrock untouched."}, {"id": "5.1-L1-02", "topic": "5.1", "topicName": "Auth methods", "difficulty": 1, "question": "Besides the Anthropic API and AWS Bedrock, which other cloud platform is a first-class 'door' Claude Code can be pointed at?", "options": ["Azure OpenAI Service", "Google Vertex AI", "Hugging Face Inference Endpoints", "Oracle Cloud Infrastructure"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Claude Code ships three first-class provider paths: Anthropic direct (API key or subscription OAuth), AWS Bedrock (`CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1` plus AWS credentials), and Google Vertex AI (`CLAUDE_CODE_USE_VERTEX=1` plus Google Cloud credentials) — the Vertex story is structurally the Bedrock story with GCP nouns: cloud-native auth, project and region settings, cloud billing. Azure OpenAI hosts OpenAI models, not Claude, and Hugging Face and OCI are not supported doors. A fourth pattern exists on top: an enterprise gateway, where Claude Code points its base URL at a proxy that injects real credentials server-side. The invariant across every door is the important part: only transport, identity, and billing change — the request payload, the tool protocol, and the stateless resend loop are identical everywhere."}, {"id": "5.1-L2-03", "topic": "5.1", "topicName": "Auth methods", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Mid-session on Bedrock, requests start failing with an expired-token error. What's the right fix?", "options": ["Run /login to re-establish the session with Anthropic", "Set a fresh ANTHROPIC_API_KEY in the environment and retry", "Restart Claude Code — on startup the harness mints a fresh AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK session token automatically", "Refresh your AWS credentials (e.g. `aws sso login`) — optionally automated via the awsAuthRefresh setting"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "On Bedrock your identity is your AWS credentials, and they expire on AWS's schedule — an SSO session or STS token running out mid-session is a normal event. The fix is AWS-side: re-run `aws sso login` (or whatever refreshes your credential source), and Claude Code's `awsAuthRefresh` setting can even run that command for you automatically when credentials lapse. `/login` is the wrong door entirely — it authenticates against a Claude subscription, which plays no part in Bedrock auth. Setting `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is equally irrelevant: no Anthropic credential is ever sent on the Bedrock path. And restarting the session mints nothing — Claude Code doesn't create AWS credentials, it only borrows whatever the standard credential chain resolves, so a restart with the same expired source fails identically."}, {"id": "5.1-L2-04", "topic": "5.1", "topicName": "Auth methods", "difficulty": 2, "question": "When Claude Code targets Bedrock, how does it decide which AWS region to send requests to?", "options": ["AWS_REGION first, then AWS_DEFAULT_REGION, then the region configured on your AWS profile", "It always uses us-east-1 unless a flag overrides it", "It asks Bedrock for the nearest region with capacity and uses that", "The region is encoded in your IAM role and cannot be changed per session"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Region resolution follows the standard AWS convention: `AWS_REGION` wins if set, then `AWS_DEFAULT_REGION`, then the region attached to your active AWS profile. This matters more on Bedrock than it would for most AWS services, because model availability, prompt-caching support, and your on-demand quotas are all per-region facts — pointing at the wrong region can mean the model you pinned simply isn't there. There is no hardcoded default region and no automatic 'nearest capacity' discovery at the client; the closest thing to capacity-aware routing is a cross-region inference profile, and that routing happens server-side within a geography after your request arrives, not in the harness. IAM roles govern what you may invoke, not where requests go. Anthropic-direct users never touch any of this — region is purely a Bedrock-layer concern."}, {"id": "5.1-L3-02", "topic": "5.1", "topicName": "Auth methods", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Your IAM policy grants `bedrock:InvokeModel` on the foundation-model ARN, yet Claude Code still can't run. What's the likely gap?", "options": ["Bedrock requires full `bedrock:*` permissions for interactive clients", "The policy must also reference your Anthropic organization ID, since Bedrock validates each invocation against the Anthropic account that licenses the weights", "The policy is missing `bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream` and doesn't cover the inference-profile ARN the harness actually invokes", "IAM policies can't authorize Bedrock model calls; that requires an SCP"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Two common gaps hide in that policy. First, Claude Code streams responses, so it calls `InvokeModelWithResponseStream`, a separate IAM action from `InvokeModel` — granting one without the other fails. Second, newer Claude models on Bedrock are typically invoked through cross-region inference profiles, which have their own ARNs distinct from the foundation-model ARN; the policy must cover the profile you actually call (plus `ListInferenceProfiles`/`GetInferenceProfile` so the harness can discover and resolve them). Option A overshoots — least-privilege scoping to the invoke and profile-discovery actions is exactly how this should be done, no wildcard needed. Option B imports the wrong provider's nouns: no Anthropic identity exists anywhere on the Bedrock path. Option D inverts the AWS permission model — IAM policies are precisely the surface that authorizes Bedrock calls; SCPs only constrain from above."}, {"id": "5.2-L2-03", "topic": "5.2", "topicName": "Model identity & availability", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Invoking a Claude model on Bedrock by its bare foundation-model ID fails with \"on-demand throughput isn't supported\" for this model. What is Bedrock telling you?", "options": ["Your account's on-demand TPD quota for this model in this region is exhausted; requests resume when the daily bucket resets", "This model is only served through an inference profile — invoke the geography-prefixed profile ID instead", "The model requires a paid support plan before on-demand use", "Streaming is disabled for this model; switch to non-streaming calls"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Many newer Claude models on Bedrock are not offered for direct single-region on-demand invocation at all — they are served exclusively through cross-region inference profiles. The error message is Bedrock's (admittedly cryptic) way of saying 'use the profile ID': prepend the geography prefix, e.g. `us.anthropic.claude-...` instead of `anthropic.claude-...`, or use the profile's ARN. It is not a quota problem — quota exhaustion surfaces as ThrottlingException, a different error with a different remedy — and it has nothing to do with support plans or streaming. The practical habit: on Bedrock, treat the inference-profile ID as the default spelling of a model's name, and use `aws bedrock list-inference-profiles` as ground truth for what your account can invoke. Anthropic-direct users never meet this: there, a model name either exists or it doesn't."}, {"id": "5.2-L2-04", "topic": "5.2", "topicName": "Model identity & availability", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Your team wants every Bedrock-backed Claude Code install to use the exact same pinned model. Where does that pin actually live?", "options": ["In the Anthropic console, as the organization's default model", "In the Bedrock console's per-account default-model setting, which every SigV4-signed invocation in the account inherits", "Each user runs /model once; the choice syncs across the team automatically", "In environment variables like ANTHROPIC_MODEL and the ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_*_MODEL family, set to inference-profile IDs"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Bedrock model selection is configuration-driven: `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` pins the primary model, and `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_OPUS_MODEL` / `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL` / `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL` steer what each alias resolves to — all set to inference-profile IDs or application-inference-profile ARNs, distributed via managed settings or shared environment setup. This matters because Claude Code's built-in alias defaults may not even be enabled in your account, in which case it falls back to another model with only a notice — an unpinned deployment can quietly drift onto different weights and a different per-token price. Option A is the wrong door: the Anthropic console knows nothing about Bedrock usage. Option B doesn't exist — Bedrock has no default-model setting. And `/model` is a per-user, per-install choice; nothing about it syncs. On Bedrock, fleet consistency is an env-var and settings-management problem."}, {"id": "5.2-L3-03", "topic": "5.2", "topicName": "Model identity & availability", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Beyond cross-region profiles, Bedrock also offers *application* inference profiles. What are they for?", "options": ["Account-specific ARNs that wrap a model so usage can be cost-tracked and access-controlled per application or team", "Profiles that pre-route requests to whichever geography is cheapest that hour", "Reserved capacity blocks — model units purchased per application to guarantee its throughput outside the shared on-demand pool", "Per-application fine-tuned variants of the base Claude weights"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "An application inference profile is a governance wrapper, not a routing or capacity construct: you create an ARN in your own account that points at an underlying model (or cross-region profile), then invoke through that ARN. Because the ARN is yours, you can tag it, attach IAM policies to it, and attribute every token of spend to the team or application it represents in Cost Explorer — which is how enterprises answer 'which project is burning our Claude budget?' on a shared account. Option B describes what cross-region profiles do (route within a geography for capacity, not price). Option C is provisioned throughput, a different product with different billing. Option D repeats the fine-tuning myth — AWS never modifies Claude weights. Nothing like this exists on the Anthropic API, where per-team attribution is done with workspaces in the Anthropic console instead."}, {"id": "5.2-L3-04", "topic": "5.2", "topicName": "Model identity & availability", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A fresh AWS account has a perfect IAM policy and the right region, yet every Claude invocation on Bedrock is refused. A colleague insists IAM is all Bedrock checks. What are they missing?", "options": ["New accounts must age 30 days before Bedrock accepts any model traffic", "Bedrock also requires the AWS account to be linked to an Anthropic organization first, so Anthropic can approve the use case before AWS serves its weights", "Anthropic model access must first be enabled for the account in the Bedrock console — a one-time use-case submission that IAM cannot substitute for", "The IAM policy must be attached to the root user, not a role"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Bedrock has an authorization layer IAM doesn't cover: model access. Before any Anthropic model can be invoked, the AWS account must enable access to it in the Bedrock console — a one-time use-case form per account. IAM then governs which principals may invoke which ARNs, but a flawless policy is moot while the account-level grant is missing; the two gates are independent and both must be open (plus the model must exist in your region — the third gate). Option B smuggles the wrong provider in: no Anthropic account or organization is ever involved on the Bedrock path. Option A invents an aging rule — new accounts get reduced quotas, not a traffic ban. Option D is an anti-pattern, not a requirement. Anthropic-direct has no equivalent gate: if a model exists there, your key can call it."}, {"id": "5.3-L2-03", "topic": "5.3", "topicName": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "difficulty": 2, "question": "In a healthy cached session, the usage object shows `input_tokens: 2` on a call that clearly carried a 100k-token conversation. What does `input_tokens` actually count?", "options": ["The tokens of the newest user message only — the single message appended since the last call, with everything older itemized elsewhere", "A sampling artifact — the field is unreliable during cache hits", "Only the uncached remainder after the last cache breakpoint; the rest is itemized under the cache read and write fields", "The total request size divided by the cache discount factor"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The usage object splits input into three exclusive buckets: `cache_read_input_tokens` (the matched prefix, billed ~0.1×), `cache_creation_input_tokens` (the newly written chunk, billed at the write premium), and `input_tokens` — only what's left after the last breakpoint, billed at 1×. In a well-cached session almost the entire conversation lands in the read bucket and the freshly settled part in the write bucket, so the uncached remainder is routinely tiny. Total input is the sum of all three, not the `input_tokens` field alone — misreading that field as 'total input' is the most common way people underestimate what a call actually shipped. Option A is close but wrong in mechanism: the boundary is the breakpoint the harness placed, not the message boundary. Bedrock's Converse API reports the same split with different names (`cacheReadInputTokens`/`cacheWriteInputTokens`); Claude Code's InvokeModel path uses the Anthropic field names."}, {"id": "5.3-L2-04", "topic": "5.3", "topicName": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A cache entry is written with the 5-minute TTL. The session then makes a call every two minutes for an hour. When does the entry expire?", "options": ["It doesn't — the TTL refreshes on every hit, so steady traffic keeps the entry alive indefinitely at no extra charge", "Five minutes after creation, regardless of traffic", "It survives the hour, but each two-minute hit re-bills the whole prefix at the 1.25× write price to fund the extension", "After 12 refreshes, the maximum allowed for the 5-minute tier"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The TTL is an idle timeout, not a lifespan: every cache hit resets the clock, and the refresh itself costs nothing — you pay only the 0.1× read. So a session ticking along faster than the TTL keeps its 5-minute entry alive for hours. That's the key to reasoning about TTL choice: the 5-minute tier is only risky during *gaps* — think time, a meeting, a long build — because the moment no hit lands within the window, the entry is evicted and the next call pays full prefill on everything. The 1-hour tier exists purely as insurance against those gaps, not because 5-minute entries wear out. Option C misprices refreshes (re-billing at write price would destroy the economics), and option D invents a refresh limit. Same refresh-on-hit behavior applies on Bedrock wherever caching is supported."}, {"id": "5.3-L3-04", "topic": "5.3", "topicName": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You place a cache breakpoint after a prefix of about 500 tokens. What happens?", "options": ["The request is rejected with a 400 validation error naming the breakpoint, since prefixes must clear the per-model minimum up front", "The prefix is cached but reads back at a reduced discount", "The prefix is padded up to the minimum and cached normally", "Nothing is cached — prefixes below the per-model minimum (roughly 1,024–4,096 tokens) are silently billed as ordinary input"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Caching has a per-model minimum prefix length — on the order of 1,024 tokens for larger models and up to 4,096 for some — and a breakpoint covering less than that is simply ignored: no error, no write charge, no cache entry, just normal 1× input billing. The silence is the trap: a request can look correctly instrumented while caching never engages, and the only tell is `cache_creation_input_tokens` and `cache_read_input_tokens` sitting at zero in the usage object. In Claude Code this threshold is rarely binding — the system prompt and tool schemas alone clear it — but it bites hand-rolled API integrations with short system prompts. Options A and C imagine the API being louder or more helpful than it is, and option B invents a partial-discount tier that doesn't exist: caching is binary per breakpoint — either the prefix qualifies and caches, or it's ordinary input."}, {"id": "5.3-L3-05", "topic": "5.3", "topicName": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Across a long session, every call's usage shows a small cache write (a few hundred to a few thousand tokens) and a huge cache read. What harness strategy produces that signature?", "options": ["The server speculatively auto-caches whatever content it predicts will recur across calls; the small writes are its incremental guesses being stored", "A rolling breakpoint trailing the settled tail: each turn the newly settled chunk is written once, and everything before it is read back", "The whole prefix is rewritten each call; the columns just split it by TTL tier", "The harness caches only tool schemas, which change slightly every call"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "That small-write/large-read shape, repeated call after call, is the fingerprint of a breakpoint that advances just behind the conversation's live edge. Each turn, the harness marks the newly settled content — the latest exchange and tool results — as cacheable: that chunk is written once at the write premium, and every prior chunk, written on earlier turns, is read back at 0.1×. Write once, read dozens or hundreds of times: that ratio is the entire economic engine of the resend. Option C would show writes the size of the whole conversation every call — the signature of a cache that keeps missing, the opposite of health. Option A imagines speculative server-side caching that doesn't exist: caching is opt-in via explicit markers the client places (on Bedrock there is no automatic caching at all — the harness must mark everything). Option D fails on arithmetic: tool schemas are stable and nowhere near the read volumes observed."}, {"id": "5.3-L3-06", "topic": "5.3", "topicName": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "After moving your Bedrock deployment to a different AWS region, cache read and write counts sit at exactly zero on every call, though nothing else changed. What should you check first?", "options": ["Whether the new region supports prompt caching at all — support is per-region on Bedrock, and an unsupported region silently bills everything as ordinary input", "Whether prompt caching was re-enabled for the account in the new region's Bedrock console — like model access, it's a one-time opt-in that must be granted per region", "Whether the InvokeModel API supports cache markers, since only Converse does", "Whether the cache entries need time to replicate from the old region"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Prompt caching on Bedrock isn't uniformly available: it's supported for specific models in specific regions, and an unsupported combination doesn't error — the cache markers are simply ignored and every token bills at the full 1× rate. Flat-zero cache counters after a region move point straight at the support table, and checking it should precede any debugging of harness configuration. Option B invents a console toggle: model *access* is a per-account console step, but caching has no such switch. Option C inverts the API facts — `cache_control` markers work on the InvokeModel path (which is what Claude Code uses); Converse has its own `cachePoint` equivalent. Option D misunderstands the cache's nature: entries are ephemeral, per-region serving-fleet state, not replicated data — there is nothing to migrate, and a region move always starts cold anyway. Anthropic-direct users never face this: caching support there doesn't vary by geography."}, {"id": "5.3-L4-03", "topic": "5.3", "topicName": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Your team's sessions have long think-time gaps and you want the 1-hour cache TTL. Which statement is correct from the Bedrock seat?", "options": ["The harness already buys the 1-hour TTL on every write through every provider door — observed usage shows essentially all writes on that tier — so there is nothing to configure or opt into through the Bedrock door", "The 1-hour TTL writes at 1.25× and the 5-minute at 2×, so it's also the cheaper option", "On Bedrock the 1-hour TTL exists only for specific models and is an explicit opt-in (e.g. ENABLE_PROMPT_CACHING_1H=1); generally available on the direct API, it can't be assumed through the AWS door", "The provider selects the TTL server-side based on your observed traffic pattern"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "This is a clean provider delta. On the Anthropic API the 1-hour TTL is generally available, and Claude Code leans on it heavily there — observed usage shows essentially all writes buying the 1-hour tier. On Bedrock, the same capability is narrower: it exists only for specific models, is requested via a `ttl` field on the cache marker, and Claude Code gates it behind an explicit opt-in like `ENABLE_PROMPT_CACHING_1H=1`. That makes option A the canonical trap — approximately true through the direct door, false through yours: without the opt-in, your Bedrock writes stay on the 5-minute tier and a long pause can evict the whole prefix. Option B swaps the multipliers (5-minute writes are 1.25×, 1-hour are 2× — the longer hold costs more, not less). Option D puts TTL selection at the wrong layer: it's a client-requested, per-breakpoint choice, never server-inferred."}, {"id": "5.3-L4-04", "topic": "5.3", "topicName": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "The 1-hour TTL (2× writes) is usually presented as obviously worth it for coding sessions. Under which usage pattern does it actually LOSE money versus the 5-minute default (1.25× writes)?", "options": ["Sessions with long breaks between turns, where entries would otherwise expire", "Steady traffic where calls never gap more than five minutes — refresh-on-hit keeps the 5-minute entry alive indefinitely, so the extra 0.75× per written token buys nothing", "Sessions with very large stable prefixes — at 200k tokens the extra 0.75× per written token compounds fastest, overwhelming whatever eviction savings the longer hold provides", "Any session on Bedrock, where 1-hour writes are billed at the 5-minute rate anyway"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The 1-hour TTL is pure insurance against gaps, and insurance against a risk you never face is money burned. Because the TTL refreshes free on every hit, a workload whose calls always land within five minutes of each other — headless CI runs, tight batch loops, back-to-back agent turns — keeps a 5-minute entry alive indefinitely; buying the 1-hour hold adds 0.75× per written token for protection that never pays out. Option A is the inverted trap: long breaks are precisely when the 1-hour tier wins, by dodging a catastrophic full re-prefill. Option C confuses absolute size with the decision variable — a bigger prefix scales the premium and the miss cost together; the gap pattern, not size, decides. Option D invents a Bedrock billing quirk: where Bedrock supports the 1-hour tier at all, it bills the 2× write premium like the direct API — there is no discounted back door."}, {"id": "5.4-L2-02", "topic": "5.4", "topicName": "Rate limiting & quotas", "difficulty": 2, "question": "During a heavy Bedrock session, occasional ThrottlingExceptions appear in the background but the session keeps making progress. What's happening?", "options": ["Bedrock is queueing throttled requests server-side and replaying them in order as quota frees up, which is why progress continues without any client action", "The harness silently switched to a smaller model to duck under the quota", "AWS automatically raised your quota after the first throttle", "The AWS SDK and harness retry throttled calls with exponential backoff; transient throttling is absorbed, and only sustained throttling warrants a quota look"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Throttling is a normal, expected signal in AWS's model: the service fails the call fast, and the client is responsible for backing off and retrying. Claude Code (via the AWS SDK's standard retry behavior) does exactly that, so occasional quota bumps during a burst — a batch of tool calls, a subagent spinning up — resolve themselves after a short delay you may barely notice. The diagnostic rule that follows: intermittent throttling is weather, sustained throttling is climate — the latter means your steady-state demand exceeds the region's quota, and the fix lives in AWS (quota-increase request, cross-region inference profile, provisioned throughput), not in your prompts. Option A inverts the mechanism — nothing queues server-side; the failure is immediate. Option B invents behavior — the harness never swaps models to evade quota. Option C misreads AWS — quota increases only happen through explicit requests, never as a reaction to throttling."}, {"id": "5.4-L3-03", "topic": "5.4", "topicName": "Rate limiting & quotas", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Your Bedrock TPM quota keeps running out even though the AWS bill looks modest and cache reads dominate your token mix. Which token class is actually draining the quota?", "options": ["Output tokens — they burn down TPM at a model-specific multiplier (5× for most Claude models, more for some), so a little generation consumes a lot of quota", "Cache reads — cheap on the bill at 0.1×, but they're the bulk of your tokens, so they're the bulk of what burns down the TPM bucket each minute", "Uncached input — it counts double against TPM to discourage cold prompts", "Cache writes — the 1.25×/2× billing premium applies to quota as well"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Bedrock's quota arithmetic is deliberately not billing arithmetic. Against TPM/TPD quotas: cache reads count zero, uncached input and cache writes count 1:1, and output burns down at a model-specific multiplier — 5× for most Claude models, 10× or more for some newer ones. So a session generating 10k output tokens consumes 50k+ quota tokens while the bill records only the actual 10k at output rates — exactly the 'modest bill, exhausted quota' signature in the question. Option B is the tempting inversion: cache reads dominate the token mix precisely because they're the resend, but they draw no quota at all — one more reason cache stability matters doubly on Bedrock. Options C and D project billing intuitions onto quota: no double-counting exists for uncached input, and the write premium is a price, not a burndown rate. None of this machinery exists on the Anthropic API, which meters ITPM and OTPM separately."}, {"id": "5.4-L3-04", "topic": "5.4", "topicName": "Rate limiting & quotas", "difficulty": 3, "question": "How does a high cache-hit rate change your effective throughput against per-minute token limits?", "options": ["It doesn't — rate limits count tokens as sent, cached or not", "It helps only on Bedrock; Anthropic tiers count every input token equally", "Substantially: cache reads are excluded from the input-side accounting on both providers (Anthropic's ITPM for most models; Bedrock quota burndown), so effective throughput can be several times the printed limit", "It helps only at the billing layer, where reads bill at 0.1×; throughput accounting reuses the same counters on both providers, so every input token draws per-minute quota as sent, cached or not, exactly as it appears in the usage object"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "This is one of the few places the two providers agree in your favor. On the Anthropic API, `cache_read_input_tokens` don't count toward ITPM for most models — only uncached input and cache writes do. On Bedrock, cache reads consume no quota tokens at all. Since a healthy Claude Code session's input is overwhelmingly cache reads (the resend, discounted), your effective sustainable throughput can be several times what the printed per-minute limit suggests — and conversely, one broken cache converts that entire read volume into full-rate quota consumption, so a cache miss is a throttling event, not just a billing event. Option A describes how it would work without cache-aware metering. Options B and D each get one provider or one layer right and generalize wrongly: the exclusion holds on both providers, and throughput accounting is genuinely separate machinery from billing, which always charges reads at 0.1×."}, {"id": "5.4-L4-02", "topic": "5.4", "topicName": "Rate limiting & quotas", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague sets max_tokens to the model's ceiling on every call, arguing: \"It's just a safety cap — it has zero effect unless the model actually generates that much.\" What's wrong with that on Bedrock?", "options": ["Nothing — on both providers max_tokens only matters if generation reaches it", "Bedrock deducts input plus the full max_tokens from quota at request start (settling and refunding afterward), so an inflated cap throttles concurrent requests even when generation never comes close — invisible in the bill", "Bedrock bills the full max_tokens whether generated or not, so the cost impact is direct", "Both providers reserve the full max_tokens at request start — Anthropic against OTPM, Bedrock against TPM — so the cap throttles equally everywhere; Bedrock is merely stricter about refunding the unused part after generation settles"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Bedrock's quota accounting runs in three steps: at request start it deducts total input plus your entire `max_tokens` from the TPM bucket (throttling the request if that overdraws), adjusts during generation, then settles to actual usage — input, cache writes, and output at its burndown multiplier — refunding the unused reservation. The reservation is the trap: ten concurrent calls each reserving a huge cap can exhaust quota upfront and throttle each other, while the bill, which only ever records actual tokens, shows nothing unusual. Option A is the canonical provider-delta trap — it is essentially true on the Anthropic API, whose docs state `max_tokens` does not factor into OTPM, but false through your door. Option C errs the other way: billing is unaffected everywhere; this is purely quota machinery. Option D falsely symmetrizes the providers. On Bedrock, right-sizing `max_tokens` is a concurrency lever, not just tidiness."}, {"id": "5.5-L1-02", "topic": "5.5", "topicName": "Costs", "difficulty": 1, "question": "Ordering the Claude model tiers from cheapest to most expensive per token, which is correct?", "options": ["Opus, then Sonnet, then Haiku", "Sonnet, then Haiku, then Opus", "Opus, then Haiku, then Sonnet", "Haiku, then Sonnet, then Opus"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The family ladder runs Haiku → Sonnet → Opus in capability, and pricing climbs the same ladder: each step up costs several times the tier below it, applied to every token class at once — input, output, cache writes, and cache reads all scale from the model's base rate. That's why model choice is one of the highest-leverage cost decisions: routing routine work to Sonnet or Haiku instead of Opus cuts the whole bill proportionally, with no other change to the session. The ratio structure (output at a multiple of input, cache reads at ~0.1×) stays the same within every tier; only the base rate shifts. This holds through both doors — Bedrock prices each tier from its own published sheet, billed through AWS — and it's why an unpinned Bedrock deployment silently defaulting to a bigger model than intended is a genuine budget risk, not a cosmetic detail."}, {"id": "5.5-L2-02", "topic": "5.5", "topicName": "Costs", "difficulty": 2, "question": "During extended thinking, the model produces reasoning tokens you never see rendered as normal prose. How are they billed?", "options": ["As output tokens, at the full output rate — thinking is decoded generation like any other", "As input tokens, since they feed the model's own next steps", "They aren't billed — only user-visible text is metered", "At the cache-read rate, since they're internal to the provider"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Thinking tokens are real decode: the model generates them one at a time exactly like visible prose, they arrive in the response as `thinking` content blocks, and they land in `output_tokens` — the most expensive token class, at several times the input rate. That makes extended thinking a genuine cost dial, not a free accuracy upgrade: a hard problem can spend more on reasoning you never read than on the answer you do, and the only place you'll see it is the usage object's output count exceeding the visible text. Option C is the intuitive trap — billing follows computation, not visibility. Option B misplaces the direction: these tokens are produced by decode, not sent by you. Option D borrows a discount from the wrong mechanism entirely; the 0.1× tier applies only to cached input prefixes. Identical accounting through both doors — Bedrock meters thinking output the same way, onto the AWS bill."}, {"id": "5.5-L3-03", "topic": "5.5", "topicName": "Costs", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Cost-wise, why is delegating a 50k-token codebase investigation to a subagent cheaper than doing it in the main session?", "options": ["Subagent traffic is billed at the cache-read rate as a delegation discount", "The 50k tokens stay in the subagent's context and die with it; only a short report joins the main conversation, so the investigation isn't resent on every subsequent main-thread turn", "The subagent inherits the parent's cached prefix — same system prompt and tool schemas, so the prefix matches — and its entire exploration therefore reads back at the discounted 0.1× cache-read rate", "Subagent tokens draw on a separate quota pool, so they're effectively free capacity"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The saving is structural, not a rate discount. Whatever enters the main conversation gets resent on every later call for the rest of the session — a 50k-token investigation absorbed at turn 10 of a 60-turn session is re-shipped fifty times, and even at cache-read prices that residue compounds, while also eating finite context window. A subagent runs the same investigation in its own disposable context, pays full price for its own loop once, and returns only a compact report; the parent's resend base grows by the report's size, not the exploration's. Option C is the adjacent-mechanism trap: a subagent's context is a different token prefix, so it builds its own cache — nothing is shared with the parent's entries. Option A invents a rate class. Option D is dangerously backwards on Bedrock: subagent calls draw on the same per-account, per-region quota, which is exactly why fan-out invites throttling."}, {"id": "5.5-L3-04", "topic": "5.5", "topicName": "Costs", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Studying per-call usage data, you notice cache reads on call n roughly equal the total input of call n−1. What is that correspondence showing you?", "options": ["The cache is thrashing — reads should exceed the prior call's input many times over", "The model re-reading its own previous answer before composing the next one", "The resend itself, in the provider's own accounting: everything call n−1 shipped is shipped again by call n and served from cache", "Proof that the conversation is stored server-side between calls and re-read from the provider's own copy each turn, sparing the client from resending it"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Each call's request is the previous call's request plus the newest additions (the latest output and tool results). So when call n arrives, the portion matching call n−1's content — which was written to cache by then — comes back as cache reads, and the read count naturally tracks the prior call's total input. It's the stateless loop made visible in billing data: the provider's own meters confirming that the whole conversation crosses the wire every time. Option D draws the exact wrong conclusion from the right observation — reads measure what you resent and the server *didn't recompute*, not what it remembered; no conversation is stored, only ephemeral K/V state keyed to the prefix you must still supply. Option A inverts health signals: this steady correspondence is what a working cache looks like; thrashing looks like reads collapsing and writes spiking. Option B anthropomorphizes an accounting identity."}, {"id": "5.5-L4-03", "topic": "5.5", "topicName": "Costs", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A session has a 200k-token prefix cached at the 1-hour TTL. One call breaks the cache and re-writes the whole prefix. Roughly how does that call's prefix cost compare to a normal cache-hit call's?", "options": ["About 2× — you pay the write premium instead of the read discount", "About 5× — the miss is billed like output tokens", "About 10× — full base price instead of the read rate", "About 20× — 200k tokens at the 2× write rate versus 200k at the 0.1× read rate"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Run the ratio: a normal call reads the 200k prefix at 0.1×, costing the equivalent of 20k base-rate tokens. The miss call re-prefills and re-writes the same 200k at the 1-hour write rate of 2× — 400k base-rate equivalents. That's 20× the normal prefix cost, concentrated in a single call, and it's why one broken cache late in a long session is a visible spike in any cost dashboard. Option A prices the miss as if only the freshly-added chunk were involved, but a miss re-bills everything behind the break, not just the delta. Option C forgets the miss doesn't just lose the discount — it also pays the write premium to re-store the state. Option B borrows the output multiplier from the wrong token class. On Bedrock the same event lands twice: the re-billed tokens also hit your TPM quota at full weight, where cache reads had been costing zero quota."}, {"id": "5.5-L4-04", "topic": "5.5", "topicName": "Costs", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague says: \"On Bedrock, cache reads are free — they don't even count against our TPM quota.\" What's wrong with that?", "options": ["It conflates two ledgers: cache reads do consume zero quota on Bedrock, but they still bill at ~0.1× of base input on the AWS bill — quota-free is not billing-free", "The quota half is wrong too — cache reads burn down TPM at 5× like output", "Nothing — Bedrock genuinely serves cache reads free on both ledgers; only the direct Anthropic API bills them at its 0.1× rate, one of Bedrock's real pricing advantages", "It's wrong only because Bedrock doesn't support prompt caching in the first place"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The claim is half right, and the half-right part is what makes it dangerous. Bedrock keeps two independent ledgers: the throttling ledger (Service Quotas, burndown rates), where cache reads genuinely count zero, and the billing ledger, where every cache-read token is metered at roughly 0.1× base input and lands on the AWS bill. In a long session, cache reads are the overwhelming majority of all input tokens — 0.1× of a quadratically growing resend is the single largest input line in most sessions' costs, so 'free' is off by the bulk of your input spend. Option B misapplies the output burndown multiplier to the wrong token class. Option C extends the quota fact into the billing ledger, exactly the conflation being tested — no provider serves cache reads gratis. Option D overcorrects into falsehood: Bedrock supports caching for Claude, per model and region. Always name which ledger you're reasoning about."}, {"id": "5.6-L3-03", "topic": "5.6", "topicName": "Request/response anatomy", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A response comes back with stop_reason \"max_tokens\". What does that tell you about the content you received?", "options": ["The model finished its thought but was close enough to the cap to flag it", "Decode was cut off at the requested cap — the output is truncated, possibly mid-sentence or mid-tool-call, and is not a natural ending", "The remainder was generated but held server-side, and will be prepended automatically to the next response once you call again", "The conversation has filled the model's context window and must be compacted"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "`max_tokens` is a guillotine, not a graceful landing: decode was generating token by token, hit the cap you set on the request, and stopped mid-stream. The response can end mid-sentence, mid-code-block, or — worst for a harness — mid-`tool_use` block, leaving a structurally incomplete tool call that can't be executed. That's why the stop reason matters operationally: `end_turn` means the model chose to stop; `max_tokens` means you stopped it, and the caller must decide whether to retry with a higher cap or continue the generation. Option C imagines server-side state that a stateless API doesn't keep — nothing is held for later. Option D confuses the two budgets: `max_tokens` caps one call's *output*; the context window bounds the whole *input plus output*, and exhausting it is a different failure with different remedies. The two-layer stop distinction applies identically through Bedrock, which relays the same stop_reason values."}, {"id": "5.6-L3-04", "topic": "5.6", "topicName": "Request/response anatomy", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Why do responses stream out token by token, while your prompt is never 'streamed in' the same way?", "options": ["Streaming reduces the billed token count by avoiding response buffering", "It lets the server begin executing tool_use blocks the moment they stream out, overlapping tool latency with the rest of the reply's generation — the main latency win of the agentic loop", "Both directions could stream; SSE just happens to be response-only in HTTP", "The two phases are asymmetric: prefill ingests the whole prompt in one parallel pass, but decode produces output sequentially, one token per step — streaming ships each token as it exists"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The streaming envelope mirrors the model's two-phase reality. Prefill consumes the entire input at once in a parallel pass — there's no incremental input to dribble in, which is also why time-to-first-token tracks prompt length (and drops on cache hits). Decode is the opposite: each output token requires its own forward pass over all accumulated state, so tokens come into existence one at a time, and streaming simply delivers each one the moment it exists instead of buffering until the last. Option A is false — billing counts tokens identically streamed or not. Option B misplaces execution: the server never runs tools; a `tool_use` block is parsed and executed by the harness after it arrives. Option C treats an architectural asymmetry as a transport accident. Same rationale on both providers — Bedrock streams the equivalent events through `InvokeModelWithResponseStream`, differing only in envelope framing, not in why streaming exists."}, {"id": "5.6-L4-02", "topic": "5.6", "topicName": "Request/response anatomy", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Claude Code streams fine against the Anthropic API but hangs when the same traffic runs to Bedrock through a corporate gateway. Where does the classic failure sit?", "options": ["Bedrock streams the Converse event schema — camelCase deltas, contentBlockDelta events — so the harness's Anthropic-format parser needs a translation layer the gateway must provide", "Bedrock cannot stream tool_use blocks, so agentic sessions must run non-streaming there", "Bedrock streaming uses AWS binary event-stream framing rather than SSE; a gateway that re-encodes the stream or rewrites its content-type breaks the harness's parsing", "Both providers emit identical SSE, so the gateway is innocent — the region is missing streaming support"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The event *content* is equivalent through both doors — message start, content-block deltas, a stop reason — but the envelope differs: the direct API uses server-sent events, while Bedrock's `InvokeModelWithResponseStream` wraps the same sequence in AWS's binary event-stream framing (content-type `application/vnd.amazon.eventstream`). Corporate gateways tuned for text protocols are the classic saboteur: transparently re-encoding the binary stream, buffering it, or rewriting its content-type produces exactly the 'works direct, hangs through the proxy' signature. Option D is the canonical delta trap — 'it's all SSE' is true only on the Anthropic side, and it misdirects the debugging toward regions. Option A attaches a real mechanism to the wrong path: Converse *is* AWS's normalized schema, but Claude Code uses the InvokeModel passthrough precisely so the native Anthropic events arrive untranslated. Option B invents a limitation — tool_use streams fine on Bedrock. When streaming breaks only on Bedrock, inspect the envelope first."}, {"id": "6.1-L1-01", "topic": "6.1", "topicName": "What a model is", "difficulty": 1, "question": "At the most basic level, what actually is a large language model like Claude?", "options": ["A compressed database of past conversations and documents that it searches at runtime for the closest matching answer", "A live search index over the internet that retrieves and rewrites web pages", "Billions of learned numbers (weights) that a program multiplies against your input to compute the next token", "A large collection of hand-written if/then rules covering grammar and facts"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A model is, physically, a huge array of numbers — its parameters or weights — learned during training and then frozen. Inference is arithmetic: your tokenized input flows through layers of matrix multiplications against those weights, producing a probability distribution over the next token. Nothing is looked up in a conversation database, and nothing is fetched from the web unless a tool does it — the weights themselves are read-only and identical for every user. The rules-engine option describes pre-neural AI; no human writes the model's behavior directly. This is also why the API can be stateless: since the weights never change between your calls, the only thing that makes turn 41 different from turn 1 is the text you resend — the harness must carry all state, because the model carries none."}, {"id": "6.1-L1-02", "topic": "6.1", "topicName": "What a model is", "difficulty": 1, "question": "What is a model's \"context window\"?", "options": ["A hard limit on the total number of tokens the model can process in a single call — input and output together", "The amount of disk space the model needs to be installed", "A rolling buffer of recent conversations the model keeps in short-term memory between sessions until it fills and old ones fade", "The number of files Claude Code is allowed to open at once"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The context window is a hard token budget per call: everything — system prompt, tool schemas, CLAUDE.md, the whole conversation history, and the reply being generated — must fit inside it. It is not disk space (that's set by weights and their precision), and it is not memory across sessions: the model retains nothing between calls, so 'remembering' is really the harness resending the transcript each turn, and even that resend must fit the window. The file-count option confuses the harness's product surface with the model's constraint. The window is why compaction exists in Claude Code: since the resend grows every turn, an unbounded session would eventually exceed the budget, so the harness must summarize or drop history. Thinking of the window as a strict per-call budget — not a memory — is the foundation for all context-management reasoning."}, {"id": "6.1-L2-03", "topic": "6.1", "topicName": "What a model is", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Two models both advertise 70 billion parameters, yet one is a ~40 GB download and the other ~140 GB. What explains the difference?", "options": ["The larger one bundles a compressed copy of its training corpus alongside the weights so facts can be looked up at inference time", "They store each weight at different precision — file size is roughly parameters × bits-per-weight ÷ 8", "The smaller one has had unused parameters deleted after training", "The larger one bundles a bigger context window, which is stored on disk"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Parameter count and file size are two different numbers linked by precision: size (GB) ≈ parameters (billions) × bits-per-weight ÷ 8. A 70B model at 16 bits per weight is ~140 GB; the same 70B quantized to ~4.5 bits is ~40 GB. Same weights, same count — just stored more coarsely. Models never ship with their training data (that's why 'open weights' isn't quite 'open source'), parameters aren't deleted to shrink files (that would be a different model), and the context window is a runtime processing limit, not something stored on disk. The practical takeaway: when you see a model's size, you're seeing params × precision, and capability correlates with parameters and training quality — not with the raw gigabytes of the file."}, {"id": "6.2-L3-01", "topic": "6.2", "topicName": "The read phase (prefill)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "During the prefill phase of a model call, what is actually happening?", "options": ["The model generates a draft answer and then refines it token by token", "The server assembles your system prompt, tools, and history into the message array", "The model starts streaming its first few tokens immediately while the remainder of the prompt loads through the layers in the background", "All input tokens are processed in parallel through every layer, computing attention state (K/V) for each token"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Prefill is the read phase: the entire prompt is pushed through all layers at once, and every layer computes a Key and Value vector per token — the attention state that decode will later read. Because all input tokens are known up front, this work parallelizes beautifully across the GPU, which is why it's compute-bound and fast per token. No output exists yet, so nothing streams and nothing is drafted — generation only starts after prefill completes. And prompt assembly happens on your machine, in the harness: the server receives the message array already built and does token-level work only. Prefill matters to your cost intuition because it is exactly what the stateless resend repeats — turn 41 re-prefills turns 1–40 unless a cached prefix lets the server skip that recomputation."}, {"id": "6.2-L3-02", "topic": "6.2", "topicName": "The read phase (prefill)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Why are input tokens priced several times cheaper than output tokens?", "options": ["Input is ingested in one parallel pass that uses hardware efficiently, while each output token requires its own serial pass through the model", "Providers subsidize input to encourage longer, more detailed prompts", "Input tokens are compressed into a compact internal representation before processing, so each one consumes a fraction of the memory bandwidth an output token needs", "Output tokens include hidden quality checks that run on every generated word"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The price gap mirrors a real hardware asymmetry. All input tokens are known when the request arrives, so prefill processes them simultaneously — one big, parallel, compute-efficient sweep. Output tokens can't be parallelized: token N+1 depends on token N, so decode is a serial loop where each step re-reads the model's weights and all accumulated attention state, leaving expensive hardware mostly waiting on memory. One serial pass per output token versus one shared parallel pass for all input tokens is why Anthropic prices output at roughly five times input. There's no subsidy, no compression discount, and no per-word quality check — the ratio is physics, not marketing. This is also why an agentic session's cost is dominated by the ever-growing resent input, which caching then discounts further, rather than by the comparatively short replies."}, {"id": "6.2-L4-03", "topic": "6.2", "topicName": "The read phase (prefill)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague complains: \"Claude takes ages before the first word appears — must be network latency between us and Bedrock.\" What's the more likely mechanism?", "options": ["The server queues requests alphabetically by account ID, so wait time varies", "The model composes its complete answer internally during a hidden decode pass, then re-reads it for consistency before releasing the first token to the stream", "Time-to-first-token is dominated by prefill: the model must process the entire prompt and build its K/V state before it can generate anything", "Streaming buffers the first 100 tokens for safety scanning before sending"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Not a single token can be generated until the whole prompt has been prefilled — every layer must compute attention state for every input token first, because the first output token attends over all of them. So time-to-first-token grows with prompt length, and in a long Claude Code session where the resend carries tens of thousands of tokens, prefill — not the network round-trip — dominates the wait. This is also why prompt caching visibly improves TTFT: a cache hit lets the server load the stored K/V state for the unchanged prefix and prefill only the new suffix. The model does not compose a full answer before streaming — decode emits tokens as they're sampled — and there's no alphabetical queue or fixed 100-token safety buffer. Same story on Bedrock or Anthropic direct: same weights, same two-phase serving."}, {"id": "6.2-L4-04", "topic": "6.2", "topicName": "The read phase (prefill)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "What does prefill produce that the decode phase then consumes — and that prompt caching stores server-side?", "options": ["A compressed plain-text summary of the prompt", "Per-layer Key/Value attention state for every input token", "A ranked list of the most relevant sentences in the prompt", "An updated copy of the model weights specialized to this conversation"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Prefill's output is the K/V cache: at every layer, a Key and Value vector for each input token. Decode consumes it directly — each new token attends over that stored state instead of reprocessing the raw text. This is exactly what prompt caching persists between API calls: not your text, not a summary, but the numeric attention state, keyed by the exact token prefix. The reuse is valid because same tokens plus same frozen weights provably produce identical vectors — which is also why one changed token early in the prefix invalidates everything after it, since later K/V was computed attending to the old token. The weights themselves never change per conversation (they're loaded once, read-only, shared across all users), and nothing summarizes or ranks your prompt — attention weighting happens fresh at each decode step."}, {"id": "6.3-L3-01", "topic": "6.3", "topicName": "The write phase (decode)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Why does Claude's reply stream out word by word instead of appearing all at once?", "options": ["The API deliberately throttles delivery so the response is easier to read", "The network fragments the finished response into small packets", "Streaming is a UX affordance — the full response is computed during prefill, and the API reveals it gradually at a fixed, readable pace", "Generation is inherently serial: each token requires a full forward pass that depends on all previously generated tokens"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Streaming reflects how decode genuinely works, not a presentation choice. The model produces exactly one token per forward pass, and each pass conditions on everything generated so far — token 200 literally cannot be computed until token 199 exists. So the text you see appearing is being created at that rate; there is no finished answer hidden behind a reveal animation. That's the opposite of prefill, where all input tokens are known up front and processed in parallel. The serial dependency is also why long replies take proportionally long, why output tokens are the expensive class, and why max_tokens caps matter: every additional output token is another full pass reading weights and KV state from memory. Packet fragmentation and deliberate throttling are red herrings — the bottleneck is in the model's generation loop, not the transport."}, {"id": "6.3-L3-02", "topic": "6.3", "topicName": "The write phase (decode)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Anthropic prices output tokens at roughly 5× input tokens. What about the decode phase justifies that ratio?", "options": ["Each output token needs its own serial pass through the model, holding serving capacity far longer per token than parallel input ingestion does", "Output tokens are longer strings than input tokens, so they carry more data", "The model must re-run prefill across the entire prompt for every output token it generates, so each new word re-bills the whole input at the full rate", "Output is billed higher to offset the free tier's costs"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Decode's cost driver is time-on-hardware per token. Prefill amortizes: thousands of input tokens share one parallel sweep. Decode cannot: each output token is a separate forward pass that must stream the model's weights and the request's accumulated K/V state through the GPU, and passes can't overlap because each depends on the last. A request generating 1,000 tokens occupies its slot in the serving batch for 1,000 sequential steps. That capacity-per-token asymmetry — not string length, which is meaningless since tokens are just vocabulary IDs — is what the ~5× ratio prices in. Prefill is not re-run per output token; the K/V cache exists precisely so the prompt is processed once and then only read. For session economics this cuts the other way, though: replies are short compared to the ballooning resent input, so total spend is usually input-dominated despite the higher output rate."}, {"id": "6.3-L4-03", "topic": "6.3", "topicName": "The write phase (decode)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "During decode, which hardware resource is typically the bottleneck on the serving side?", "options": ["Raw GPU compute — each decode step performs dense matrix multiplications that saturate the floating-point units", "Disk I/O reading the model file for each token", "Memory bandwidth — each token's pass must read the weights and the request's K/V state from GPU memory", "Network bandwidth between the user and the datacenter"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Decode does relatively little arithmetic per step but must move enormous amounts of data: every forward pass reads the (active) model weights plus all of the request's stored K/V attention state out of GPU memory to produce one token. The GPU's compute units spend most of the step waiting on those reads, so tokens-per-second is set by memory bandwidth, not FLOPs — the inverse of prefill, which saturates compute by batching all input tokens into parallel work. Weights are loaded from disk once at server start and stay resident in GPU RAM, shared read-only across all users' requests, so disk is irrelevant per token; and network transfer of a few bytes of text is trivial next to gigabytes of internal memory traffic. This bottleneck explains two things you can reason with: why serving providers love quantization (fewer bits = fewer bytes to move = faster decode) and why continuous batching exists (fill the waiting compute with other users' requests)."}, {"id": "6.3-L4-04", "topic": "6.3", "topicName": "The write phase (decode)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague says: \"Once Claude starts streaming, the size of the prompt no longer matters — we already paid for it in prefill.\" What's wrong with that?", "options": ["Nothing — after prefill completes, decode speed is independent of prompt length", "Every decode step still attends over the K/V state of the entire context, so a bigger prompt means more memory traffic per generated token and slower streaming", "The prompt is re-tokenized and re-embedded between every output token, so a longer prompt means more preprocessing work before each generated word can be sampled, which is what slows decode", "Streaming speed is fixed by the API tier, so prompt size only affects billing"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Prefill pays the up-front cost, but the prompt keeps taxing decode afterward: generating each token means attending over the K/V state of everything before it — the whole prompt plus all tokens generated so far. Since decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, a 150K-token context makes every single output token drag more K/V data through GPU memory than a 5K-token context would, so streaming genuinely slows as sessions bloat. That's an extra, often-overlooked reason to compact or /clear in Claude Code beyond token pricing: the resend doesn't just cost input tokens, it degrades output speed for the rest of the session. The prompt is not re-tokenized per output token (tokenization happens once), and streaming rate isn't a fixed tier property — it's a function of model size, load, and context length, on Bedrock and Anthropic alike."}, {"id": "6.4-L2-01", "topic": "6.4", "topicName": "Architecture basics", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Models are billed and limited in \"tokens\" rather than words. What is a token?", "options": ["A chunk of text — roughly three-quarters of an English word on average — mapped to an integer ID from the model's fixed vocabulary", "A single character, so token counts equal character counts", "One complete sentence, as split by punctuation", "A metered unit of GPU time — roughly one millisecond of accelerator work — that providers use to normalize billing across different hardware generations"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Before the model sees anything, text is tokenized: split into chunks from a fixed vocabulary and replaced by integer IDs. Common words are often one token; rarer words split into several pieces; in English a token averages out to about three-quarters of a word. Everything downstream is denominated in these units — the context window is a token budget, pricing is per token, and the K/V cache holds state per token. Prompt caching is keyed by the exact token prefix, which is why a one-character edit early in a prompt can invalidate a huge cached span: it changes the token sequence from that point on. Tokens are not characters (counts would be far higher), not sentences (far lower), and not a time unit — though token counts do correlate with the compute your request consumes, which is why they're the billing unit."}, {"id": "6.4-L3-02", "topic": "6.4", "topicName": "Architecture basics", "difficulty": 3, "question": "At the concept level, what does the \"attention\" mechanism in a transformer do?", "options": ["It filters out unsafe or irrelevant text before the model processes it", "It keeps the model focused on the most recent tokens in the window, letting older context gradually fade so processing stays tractable", "It compresses the prompt into a short summary the layers can share", "It lets each token's processing weigh which earlier tokens in the context matter most for predicting what comes next"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Attention is the transformer's core trick: at every layer, each position computes how relevant every earlier token is to it, and blends information from the context weighted by that relevance. When the model generates a closing brace, attention is what lets that step 'look back at' the matching opening brace 3,000 tokens earlier. The Key/Value vectors that prefill computes per token exist for exactly this — they're the lookup material attention reads, and they're what the KV cache and prompt caching store. Attention doesn't privilege the most recent message (it spans the whole window), doesn't summarize (all token state is kept, which is why KV memory grows with context), and isn't a safety filter. It's also the part MoE leaves untouched — which is why MoE routing never disturbs the caching story."}, {"id": "6.4-L4-03", "topic": "6.4", "topicName": "Architecture basics", "difficulty": 4, "question": "What is the repeating structural unit of a transformer language model?", "options": ["One giant attention matrix spanning the whole network at once, followed by a single feed-forward output layer that maps onto the vocabulary", "A block combining an attention sublayer with a feed-forward network (FFN), stacked dozens of times", "Alternating encoder and decoder towers that pass summaries to each other", "A separate specialized module per language and programming language"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A transformer is the same block repeated many times: an attention sublayer (each token gathers information from earlier tokens) followed by a feed-forward network (each token's representation is transformed independently), with the stack often running to dozens of layers in frontier models. Tokens pass through every block in order, and each layer maintains its own K/V state — which is why the KV cache is per-layer, and why its size scales with layers × tokens. Knowing the block structure pays off when reasoning about MoE: mixture-of-experts swaps the single FFN in each block for many parallel expert FFNs plus a router, while leaving attention alone. There is no single network-wide attention matrix, no per-language module (the same weights handle Python and English), and modern LLMs like Claude are decoder-only — the encoder/decoder tower design belongs to earlier translation-era transformers."}, {"id": "6.5-L2-01", "topic": "6.5", "topicName": "Parameters & model size", "difficulty": 2, "question": "When a model is described as \"a 200B model\", what does the number refer to?", "options": ["It can attend over up to 200 billion tokens of combined input and output context in a single call", "It was trained on 200 billion documents", "It has roughly 200 billion learned parameters — the numeric weights that make up the network", "Its download size is 200 billion bytes (200 GB)"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The 'B' number counts parameters: the individual learned weights in the network's layers. It's the closest thing to a headline capacity figure — more parameters mean more room to store patterns and knowledge — but it is a different number from everything else it gets confused with. Context length is a separate spec entirely (a 200B model might have a 200K window — billions vs thousands). Training data is measured in tokens seen, not stored, and isn't shipped with the model. And file size depends on precision: 200B parameters is ~400 GB at 16 bits per weight but ~112 GB at ~4.5 bits, per size ≈ params × bits ÷ 8. Getting these four numbers — parameters, context window, training scale, file size — cleanly separated is the foundation for reasoning about any model card you read."}, {"id": "6.5-L3-02", "topic": "6.5", "topicName": "Parameters & model size", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Your team defaults every Claude Code task to the largest available Claude model \"because bigger is better.\" What's the flaw in that policy?", "options": ["Parameter count trades capability against speed and cost — for many routine tasks a smaller model is faster, far cheaper, and just as correct", "Larger models have smaller context windows, so they see less of your code", "Larger models hallucinate measurably more, because every additional parameter stores extra facts that can be confidently misretrieved under heavy load", "There is no flaw — a larger model is superior on every dimension"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "More parameters buy headroom on hard problems, but every token generated must flow through those parameters — so bigger means slower decode and a higher per-token price, on Bedrock and Anthropic alike. For renaming symbols, writing commit messages, or bulk classification, a top-tier model produces the same output as a mid-tier one while costing several times more and streaming more slowly; the extra capability is simply unused. In agentic workflows the waste multiplies, since the stateless resend means every turn re-bills the whole growing context at the big model's input rate. The right policy is task-based routing — e.g. Haiku-class for volume work, Sonnet-class as the daily driver, Opus-class for genuinely hard reasoning. Context window size is not inversely tied to parameters, and hallucination isn't a function of 'containing more information' — capability generally reduces it."}, {"id": "6.5-L3-03", "topic": "6.5", "topicName": "Parameters & model size", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague argues: \"Model A has twice the parameters of model B, so A will beat B at our coding tasks.\" Why is that reasoning unreliable?", "options": ["Parameter counts are always marketing numbers with no technical meaning", "Coding ability tracks context window size rather than parameter count, since how much of the repository the model can see at once is what determines code quality", "Smaller models are always better at code because they are more focused", "Capability depends heavily on training data, recipe, and architecture — a newer or better-trained smaller model routinely outperforms an older larger one"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Parameters set a capacity ceiling, not a performance guarantee. What fills that capacity — the volume and quality of training data, the post-training for instruction following and tool use, and architectural choices like MoE — often matters more than the raw count. The field demonstrates this constantly: each model generation brings mid-size models that beat the previous generation's flagships, and code-specialized training lifts a model far above generalist peers of equal size. That's why 'choose by benchmark and fit for the task' beats 'choose by B-number.' The extreme counter-positions fail too: parameter counts are technically meaningful (they genuinely correlate with capability within a generation), context window is orthogonal to skill, and smaller isn't inherently better — the claim isn't that size doesn't matter, but that size alone can't be compared across different training lineages and eras."}, {"id": "6.6-L2-01", "topic": "6.6", "topicName": "Quantization", "difficulty": 2, "question": "What does quantizing a model actually do to it?", "options": ["It removes the least-used parameters, making the network smaller", "It stores each weight with fewer bits, rounding its value to a coarser grid — same parameter count, smaller file", "It retrains the model on a smaller dataset to reduce its size", "It compresses the weight file on disk like a zip archive, then decompresses it back to full 16-bit precision in memory at load time"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Quantization keeps every parameter but stores each one more roughly: instead of a 16-bit value like 0.7341828, a 4-bit encoding keeps something closer to 0.73. Multiply that saving across billions of weights and the memory footprint drops 3–4×, per size ≈ params × bits ÷ 8. The classic analogy is JPEG: the picture (parameter count, architecture, knowledge) is unchanged; it's just saved at a lower quality setting, and there's a sweet spot around 4–5 bits where the loss is barely visible. It is not pruning (no parameters are removed), not retraining (weights are converted, not relearned), and not zip-style compression (the quantized form is what actually runs — the whole point is fewer bytes resident in memory and moving through it, which also speeds up bandwidth-bound decode)."}, {"id": "6.6-L2-02", "topic": "6.6", "topicName": "Quantization", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Using the sizing rule of thumb, roughly how much memory do the weights of a 30B-parameter model need when quantized to ~4.5 bits per weight?", "options": ["~17 GB", "~60 GB", "~34 GB", "~4 GB"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The formula is size (GB) ≈ parameters (billions) × bits-per-weight ÷ 8: here 30 × 4.5 ÷ 8 ≈ 17 GB. The ÷ 8 converts bits to bytes. The distractors are the classic mistakes: ~60 GB is what the same model needs unquantized at 16 bits (30 × 16 ÷ 8) — the comparison that shows quantization shrinking a model 3–4×; ~34 GB is forgetting to halve (using ÷4 or 9 bits); ~4 GB is dividing the parameter count by 8 and ignoring bits entirely. Real downloads land ~10–15% above the raw estimate due to bundled overhead, and running a model needs working memory on top of weights — notably the KV cache, which grows with context length. The back-of-envelope skill transfers to any provider-scale reasoning about why frontier models need weights sharded across many GPUs."}, {"id": "6.6-L3-03", "topic": "6.6", "topicName": "Quantization", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A fixed memory budget fits either a 30B model quantized to 4 bits or an 8B model at full 16-bit precision. For a demanding coding task, which is generally the stronger choice, and why?", "options": ["The 8B at full precision — 4-bit rounding error compounds across billions of multiplications, making the 30B unreliable for the exactness code demands", "Neither — quantized models cannot be used for code generation at all", "The 30B at 4 bits — extra parameters matter more than extra precision, so a bigger model at moderate quantization beats a smaller pristine one", "They will perform identically, since memory footprint determines capability"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "This is the core lesson of the quality/memory/speed triangle: when both options fit, spend memory on parameters before precision. Moderate quantization (~4–5 bits) sits at the JPEG sweet spot — the accumulated rounding error is barely measurable — while nearly 4× the parameters is a step-change in knowledge and reasoning capacity. So the 30B at Q4-level precision out-codes the 8B at full precision despite its coarser weights. The tempting distractor overweights rounding error: quality loss becomes real at aggressive levels (2–3 bits can genuinely break a model), but not at the moderate levels serving stacks actually use. Capability is set by parameters and training, not by footprint — two models with equal footprints can differ wildly. The same triangle governs provider decisions: serving at moderate precision cuts memory and speeds bandwidth-bound decode at negligible quality cost."}, {"id": "6.6-L4-04", "topic": "6.6", "topicName": "Quantization", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Beyond shrinking memory footprint, why does quantization also tend to make token generation faster?", "options": ["Quantized weights skip the attention computation for most tokens", "Lower precision lets the sampler take coarser steps through the distribution, so the model can emit several tokens per forward pass instead of one", "Quantized models use fewer layers, shortening each forward pass", "Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, and fewer bits per weight means fewer bytes to read per token — the bottleneck itself shrinks"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Decode speed is governed by how many bytes each token-generation pass must pull through memory: the resident weights plus the K/V state. Quantization attacks that bottleneck directly — weights at 4 bits move 4× less data than at 16 bits, so each serial decode step completes faster even though the arithmetic performed is essentially the same. That's why lower quant is often faster as well as smaller, and why serving providers care: at scale, precision choices translate directly into tokens-per-second and cost-per-token. The distractors invent mechanisms that don't exist: quantization changes how each weight is stored, not the network's structure — every layer still runs, attention still runs for every token, and decode remains strictly one token per pass regardless of precision (sampling picks one token from one distribution; precision doesn't batch it). The trade, as always, is accumulated rounding error if pushed too far."}, {"id": "6.7-L3-01", "topic": "6.7", "topicName": "MoE (mixture of experts)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A mixture-of-experts model is listed as \"120B total parameters, 5B active.\" What do the two numbers govern?", "options": ["Active sets memory requirements, since only the routed experts stay loaded; total sets generation speed through the router's per-token overhead", "Total counts the training corpus; active counts the parameters kept afterward", "Total sets memory requirements and knowledge capacity; active sets per-token compute, and therefore speed and serving cost", "The model dynamically shrinks to 5B parameters when the prompt is simple"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "MoE splits what dense models fuse into one number. Every expert must be resident in memory because any token might be routed to it — so total parameters (120B) set the memory footprint and the model's overall knowledge capacity. But for each token, the router activates only a few experts, so just ~5B parameters actually compute — active parameters set the per-token FLOPs and memory traffic, and therefore tokens-per-second and serving cost. The result is a model that stores like a 120B but runs like a 5B: big-model quality at small-model speed, which is exactly why the architecture is ubiquitous at frontier scale. The first option inverts the mapping — the classic error. The model never 'shrinks': routing happens per token by learned dispatch, not by prompt difficulty, and all experts stay loaded throughout. Remember memory = total, speed = active."}, {"id": "6.7-L4-02", "topic": "6.7", "topicName": "MoE (mixture of experts)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague reasons: \"That frontier model reportedly has a huge total parameter count, so every token must be slow and expensive to serve.\" What does MoE do to that logic?", "options": ["It breaks it — per-token latency and serving cost track active parameters, and an MoE activates only a small fraction of its total per token", "It confirms it — MoE models are strictly slower because the router adds overhead to every token", "It makes cost unpredictable, since a different random subset of experts fires per request", "It only helps prefill, where tokens batch across experts in parallel — during serial decode, every expert must still run for each generated token"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "With MoE, total parameter count stops predicting per-token economics. Each transformer block replaces its single FFN with many expert FFNs plus a tiny router that picks a few (say top-2 of 128) per token — so a model with hundreds of billions of total parameters might compute with only a few percent of them on any given token. Latency and serving cost track that active slice; the total mainly costs memory and sharding complexity (expert parallelism across GPUs). The router itself is negligible overhead compared to the FFN work it saves. Routing is deterministic given the token's internal state, not random per request, so costs stay predictable. And the trick applies in both phases — prefill and decode each route every token through its selected experts. Caveat worth keeping: Anthropic hasn't published Claude's architecture, so treat MoE as industry-standard frontier design, not a confirmed fact about Claude."}, {"id": "6.7-L4-03", "topic": "6.7", "topicName": "MoE (mixture of experts)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A teammate worries: \"If the provider's model is MoE, expert routing must make prompt caching unreliable — different experts, different cache.\" Why is that wrong?", "options": ["Providers disable MoE routing whenever caching is enabled for a request", "MoE replaces the feed-forward network, not attention — the K/V cache lives in attention, and routing is deterministic, so cached prefixes stay exactly reusable", "It's actually correct — MoE models cannot support prompt caching", "The cache stores the raw token IDs of the prefix rather than any computed state, so the server just replays the transcript and architecture never enters the picture"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Two facts dissolve the worry. First, location: prompt caching stores per-layer Key/Value attention state, and MoE modifies a different component — it swaps each block's single FFN for many experts plus a router, leaving the attention sublayer (and thus everything the KV cache holds) untouched. Second, determinism: the router is a learned function of the token's internal state, not a coin flip, so the same token prefix through the same frozen weights takes identical routes and produces identical vectors every time — the exact invariant that makes reusing cached state valid. So MoE changes sharding (expert parallelism), batching (keeping all experts busy), and per-token economics, while the caching story — and the whole stateless-resend picture — survives unchanged. The cache does not store raw text (recomputing state from text is precisely the cost caching avoids), and no provider toggles architecture per request; the weights are fixed at deployment."}, {"id": "6.8-L3-01", "topic": "6.8", "topicName": "Sampling & stop conditions", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You send the exact same prompt to the same Claude model twice and get noticeably different replies. What is the primary mechanism behind this?", "options": ["The model remembers your first request and deliberately varies the second", "The model outputs a probability distribution over its vocabulary for each next token, and sampling at nonzero temperature draws from it randomly", "Bedrock and Anthropic route identical prompts to slightly different model versions", "Nondeterministic floating-point rounding on the GPU perturbs the stored weights slightly between calls, so no two forward passes compute exactly the same numbers"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The model never 'picks a word' — each forward pass ends with a probability distribution over the entire vocabulary, and a separate sampling step draws one token from it. At temperature 0 the sampler always takes the top token, making output essentially deterministic; at higher temperatures the distribution flattens and lower-probability tokens get real chances, so two identical requests diverge as soon as one draw differs — and every subsequent token then conditions on that divergence, compounding it. The memory option is impossible: the API is stateless, and your second call arrives as an anonymous entrant in the serving batch with no link to the first. Weights are frozen and read-only — they don't drift. And a given model ID serves the same weights on both providers. Understanding sampling as a dial, not a defect, is what lets you choose determinism-leaning settings for repeatable tasks and looser ones for creative work."}, {"id": "6.8-L3-02", "topic": "6.8", "topicName": "Sampling & stop conditions", "difficulty": 3, "question": "At the concept level, what does the top_p (nucleus sampling) parameter control?", "options": ["The maximum number of tokens the model may generate before stopping", "How sharply the probability distribution is rescaled toward the single most likely token before the draw, from fully flat to fully peaked", "It truncates the candidate pool to the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability reaches p, before a token is drawn", "The percentage of the prompt the model is required to attend to"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Sampling has two distinct dials that both shape which token gets drawn. Temperature rescales the whole distribution — low values sharpen it toward the top token, high values flatten it. Top_p instead trims the candidate pool first: sort tokens by probability, keep the smallest set whose probabilities sum to p (say 0.9), renormalize, and draw only from that nucleus. The effect is adaptive — when the model is confident, the nucleus might be two tokens; when uncertain, dozens — which cuts off the long tail of nonsense tokens without forcing determinism. The 'sharpness' option describes temperature, the classic conflation. Max-token limits and stop conditions are a separate mechanism entirely: they decide when decoding ends, not which token is chosen at each step. And attention always spans the full context — no parameter rations it by percentage."}, {"id": "6.8-L4-03", "topic": "6.8", "topicName": "Sampling & stop conditions", "difficulty": 4, "question": "In an agentic session, \"the model stopped\" can mean two different things at two different layers. Which pairing is correct?", "options": ["The API gateway ends the decode; the model itself decides when the turn ends", "Both are decided by the harness, which cuts the stream when it has enough text", "A decode ends when the assembled context window fills with generated text; an agentic turn ends only when the user types /clear or closes the session", "A model-level stop (end-of-turn token, stop sequence, or max_tokens) ends one decode; the harness ends the agentic turn when a reply contains no tool_use block"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Keep the two layers separate. Inside one API call, decode halts for a model-level reason, reported as the stop_reason: the model emitted its end-of-turn token (a learned 'I'm done' signal), matched a configured stop sequence, or hit the max_tokens ceiling — that last one meaning truncation, not completion. But in Claude Code, one agentic turn spans many such calls: if the reply carries a tool_use block, the harness executes the tool locally, appends the tool_result, and resends the grown conversation for another full prefill-and-decode. Only a reply with no tool_use ends the loop and returns control to you. The gateway never decides stops, and the harness never cuts a stream short on its own judgment. Conflating the layers causes real confusion — e.g. reading a mid-turn tool_use stop as 'Claude finished' when the turn is only halfway through its loop."}, {"id": "6.9-L1-01", "topic": "6.9", "topicName": "The Claude model family", "difficulty": 1, "question": "Within a Claude model generation, how do the Haiku, Sonnet, and Opus tiers differ?", "options": ["They form a capability ladder: Haiku is the fastest and cheapest, Sonnet the balanced middle, Opus the most capable and most expensive", "They target different content types: Haiku is tuned for short-form writing, Sonnet for prose and code, Opus for long-form creative composition", "They are the same model with different context window sizes", "They differ only in release date — each replaces the previous one"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The tiers are a deliberate size/capability ladder within each generation. Haiku is the small, fast, cheap end — built for high-volume, latency-sensitive work. Opus is the large, most capable end — strongest reasoning, highest per-token price, slower generation. Sonnet sits between as the balanced default, and is what Claude Code typically runs day to day. The poetic names are just branding by length of the literary form (haiku short, opus grand) — they say nothing about content type; all tiers handle code, prose, and analysis. The tiers coexist within a generation rather than replacing each other: a new generation refreshes the whole ladder, and a newer generation's smaller tier often rivals the older generation's larger one. Context window is a per-model spec, not the tier-defining axis. This ladder is the basis for the key cost skill: matching the tier to the task."}, {"id": "6.9-L2-02", "topic": "6.9", "topicName": "The Claude model family", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A CI pipeline calls Claude thousands of times a day to label commit messages with a category — a simple, well-defined task. Which model choice best fits, and why?", "options": ["The largest Opus-class model — production pipelines justify maximum quality, and its accuracy edge compounds over thousands of daily calls", "Alternate between tiers randomly to average the cost out", "A mid Sonnet-class model, because Haiku cannot follow instructions reliably", "A Haiku-class model — the task needs no deep reasoning, and at thousands of calls the speed and per-token price dominate"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Model choice is task-fit economics. Labeling commit messages is narrow, patterned work well within a small model's ability — a Haiku-class tier follows well-specified instructions reliably, and 'small model' does not mean 'unreliable'; it means less headroom for deep multi-step reasoning this task never uses. At thousands of calls daily, tier pricing multiplies directly into the bill, and per-call latency multiplies into pipeline time, so Opus-class pricing (roughly an order of magnitude above Haiku-class) buys nothing but identical labels delivered slower — capability beyond the task's ceiling is pure waste. Random alternation gets you a mid-tier average price with no benefit and inconsistent latency. The durable rule: reach up a tier when the task shows real reasoning depth (architecture, subtle debugging), reach down for volume and simplicity. On Bedrock, high-volume Haiku traffic also consumes less of your account's throughput quota per useful unit of work."}, {"id": "6.9-L3-03", "topic": "6.9", "topicName": "The Claude model family", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague claims: \"Claude on Bedrock is a cut-down variant — the real full-strength models are only on Anthropic's own API.\" What's the accurate picture?", "options": ["Correct — AWS licenses a distilled version of each Claude model", "The weights are the same either way — Bedrock is a different door to the same model, though IDs differ and new releases can arrive there later", "Bedrock hosts the models at a lower serving precision to fit AWS inference hardware, so output quality is measurably worse than on Anthropic's API", "Bedrock models are identical but capped at half the context window"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A given Claude model is the same set of weights whether you reach it through Anthropic's Messages API or through Bedrock — AWS is an alternative serving door with AWS auth (IAM credentials rather than API keys), AWS billing, and AWS quotas wrapped around the same model. What genuinely differs is the surface: Bedrock uses its own model identifiers and inference profiles rather than Anthropic's model names, availability varies by AWS region, and new models or API features (newer caching options, betas) can land on Bedrock after Anthropic direct. So 'which model am I on?' has the same answer either way, while 'what can I configure, and is the newest release available yet?' may not. No distillation, no secret quantization tier, no halved context window — those would defeat Bedrock's enterprise purpose, which is running the genuine frontier models inside AWS governance, compliance, and network boundaries."}, {"id": "6.1-L1-04", "topic": "6.1", "topicName": "What a model is", "difficulty": 1, "question": "You tell Claude an important project detail today. Tomorrow, in a brand-new session, will the model itself know it?", "options": ["Yes — the model's weights adjust slightly with every conversation it has", "Yes, for about a week — recent conversations persist in the model's short-term activation memory before decaying, unless a new session overwrites them first", "No — the weights were frozen at training time, so anything it should 'know' tomorrow must be resent in the prompt, for example via CLAUDE.md", "Only if you end today's session cleanly so the model can save what it learned"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A model's weights are produced once during training and then frozen; inference is read-only arithmetic against them. Nothing you say ever writes into the model — there is no per-user state inside it, no short-term memory that fades, and no save step at session end. That is the physical root of the stateless loop: if Claude appears to 'remember' something across turns, it is because the harness resent it in the assembled prompt, and if it should persist across sessions, you must put it somewhere the harness re-injects each time, like CLAUDE.md. The weight-update option describes training, which happens long before you ever call the model and never during serving. Same story on Bedrock or Anthropic direct: same frozen weights, different door."}, {"id": "6.1-L1-05", "topic": "6.1", "topicName": "What a model is", "difficulty": 1, "question": "Thousands of users are talking to the same Claude model at this moment. How does the serving side hold the model for all of them?", "options": ["One read-only copy of the weights is loaded and shared — every user's request flows through the same numbers", "Each active user gets a private copy of the weights paged into accelerator memory for the duration of their session, then released on disconnect", "Each user's copy is lightly fine-tuned to their style while the session runs", "Busy periods assign users to smaller replica models until the main one frees up"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The weights are loaded into accelerator memory once, read-only, and shared: every request — yours, a stranger's, a CI pipeline's — flows through the same numbers. There is no per-user copy (frontier-scale weights run to hundreds of gigabytes; duplicating them per user would be physically absurd), no per-user fine-tuning during serving (inference never writes to weights), and no silent swap to a smaller model under load — a given model ID serves the same weights, with load handled by batching requests together and running more replicas. This shared, stateless design is why the API can treat your calls as anonymous entrants: nothing about you lives on the server between requests, so the harness must carry all conversational state itself by resending it every turn."}, {"id": "6.1-L2-06", "topic": "6.1", "topicName": "What a model is", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A long Claude Code session keeps growing turn after turn. At the model level, why must the harness eventually compact or clear it?", "options": ["The model gradually gets confused as old topics accumulate in its context, so the harness trims them periodically to keep attention focused on the current task", "The API applies a pricing penalty once a conversation crosses a size threshold", "The transcript file on disk has a maximum size the harness must stay under", "Every turn resends the whole conversation, and the assembled prompt must fit the context window — a hard per-call token budget — or the next call simply cannot be made"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The context window is not a memory that fills gradually across a session — it is a hard budget on each individual call. Because the API is stateless, every turn's request must carry the entire assembled prompt: system prompt, tool schemas, CLAUDE.md, the whole history, plus room for the reply. As the session grows, the resend grows with it, and when it approaches the window the harness has no choice but to summarize and drop history — compaction is an engineering necessity, not a tidiness feature. There is no size-threshold pricing penalty (cost grows because resent tokens grow, which is different), and the on-disk transcript is unbounded — it is the durable record, not what must fit. 'Confusion' framing gets the layer wrong: the constraint is a token budget, enforced by arithmetic, not attention span."}, {"id": "6.2-L3-05", "topic": "6.2", "topicName": "The read phase (prefill)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Pasting a 50,000-token file into a session delays the start of the reply by only a couple of seconds — yet generating 50,000 tokens of output would take many minutes. Why the asymmetry?", "options": ["Input is heavily compressed before processing, while output must be produced at full fidelity", "All input tokens are known up front, so prefill processes them simultaneously in parallel; each output token requires its own serial forward pass, one after another", "The server prioritizes reading over writing to keep its request queue short", "Large pasted files usually hit K/V state already cached from other users who uploaded the same or similar files, so the server skips most of the reading work entirely"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Reading is parallel; writing is serial. During prefill every input token is already known, so at each layer all 50,000 tokens are processed alongside each other in dense matrix operations that saturate the hardware — input is chewed through at rates orders of magnitude faster than decode emits output. Generation cannot be parallelized the same way: token n+1 depends mathematically on token n, so 50,000 output tokens means 50,000 sequential forward passes. No compression is involved, and there is no scheduling preference for reads — the asymmetry is intrinsic to the two phases. The cross-user cache option is a near-miss trap: prompt caching does reuse K/V state, but it is keyed by your exact token prefix within your requests, not by similarity to other users' files. This parallel-read/serial-write split is the root of the input/output price gap too."}, {"id": "6.2-L3-06", "topic": "6.2", "topicName": "The read phase (prefill)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "On a warm prompt-cache hit, what work does the server actually skip for the cached prefix?", "options": ["Recomputing the prefix's per-layer K/V attention state — the tokens still cross the wire, but their prefill compute is replaced by loading stored state", "Transmitting the prefix over the network — the harness detects the cache breakpoint and sends only the new suffix, saving both bandwidth and upload latency", "Both prefill and decode for that portion, which is why cached turns are essentially free", "Tokenizing the prefix — the cache stores the raw text so it needn't be split again"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A cache read saves compute, not bandwidth. The harness still sends the full message array every turn — the stateless resend never shrinks — and the server still receives and tokenizes everything. What it skips is the expensive part: instead of re-running prefill for the prefix, it loads the stored per-layer K/V attention state, valid because the same tokens through the same frozen weights provably produce identical vectors. Only the new suffix prefills for real. That is why cache reads are priced around 0.1× input rather than free: receiving, matching, and loading still cost something, and decode is never cached — every reply is generated fresh. The 'sends only the suffix' option is the classic wrong-layer trap: it would require server-side conversation state, which is exactly what the stateless API doesn't have. The cache stores computed attention state, not raw text."}, {"id": "6.2-L4-07", "topic": "6.2", "topicName": "The read phase (prefill)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague claims: \"Long prompts are slow to ingest because prefill has to stream so much data through memory — it's bandwidth-limited, just like generation.\" What's wrong with that?", "options": ["Nothing — both phases are limited by memory bandwidth, which is why input and output are priced the same", "Prefill is actually network-bound: the dominant cost is uploading the prompt to the server", "Prefill has the opposite profile: with all tokens known up front it runs as dense parallel matrix work that saturates compute — it's decode that's memory-bandwidth-bound", "Prefill is compute-bound only for short prompts — once the growing KV cache spills out of on-chip memory it becomes bandwidth-limited, exactly like generation"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The two phases live in opposite hardware regimes, and this claim attaches decode's bound to prefill. Prefill batches every input token into simultaneous work at each layer — enormous dense matrix multiplications, the exact shape accelerators are built for — so the chips are genuinely busy computing: compute-bound. Decode inverts it: one token's worth of arithmetic per step, but each step must drag the weights and all accumulated K/V state through memory, so it waits on bandwidth. The 'priced the same' option fails on the facts — output runs roughly 5× input precisely because the phases differ. Network upload is real but trivial: megabytes of text against seconds of accelerator work. And there is no fill-threshold regime change — the KV cache grows throughout, but prefill's parallelism, not cache occupancy, is what determines its bound. Wrong bound, wrong phase: the claim inverts the one distinction that drives the economics."}, {"id": "6.2-L4-08", "topic": "6.2", "topicName": "The read phase (prefill)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Prompt caching matches an exact token prefix. When one token early in the prompt changes, why can't the server still reuse the cached K/V for the unchanged text that comes after the edit?", "options": ["It can, and does — the cache is per-token, so only the edited token itself is recomputed", "Because the cache key is a cryptographic hash of the entire prompt, any single-token change forces a full miss — including the unchanged portion before the edit", "Because attention looks forward as well as backward, so the edit also invalidates the state computed before it", "Because each token's K/V was computed attending to everything before it — the state after the edit encodes the old token, so it is no longer the correct state"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "K/V state is contextual, not per-token-independent. When prefill processes token 500, its Key and Value vectors are computed by attending over tokens 1–499; change token 100 and every vector from position 100 onward was derived from a context that no longer exists — reusing it would silently answer against the old prompt. That is why caching is exact-prefix and order-sensitive. The per-token option fails for exactly this reason. The whole-prompt-hash option is a subtle trap in the wrong direction: matching is by longest shared prefix, so everything before the edit remains perfectly reusable — a hash of the whole prompt would forfeit that, and providers don't. The forward-attention option breaks causality: generation-style attention is strictly backward-looking, which is precisely why state before the edit stays valid. This mechanism is also why Claude Code orders prompts stable-prefix-first — volatile content belongs at the end, where invalidation is cheap."}, {"id": "6.3-L3-05", "topic": "6.3", "topicName": "The write phase (decode)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague assumes extended thinking is nearly free because \"the thinking text isn't part of the answer we read.\" What is the actual cost profile of thinking tokens?", "options": ["They are billed at the cheaper input rate, since the model consumes its own reasoning as context — the way it consumes a prompt — before writing the visible answer", "They are decoded exactly like visible output — one serial pass each, billed at the output rate, and paid in wall-clock time before the answer proper begins", "They are produced during prefill, so they add to time-to-first-token but not to the bill", "They run on a smaller sidecar model, so they cost a fraction of normal output tokens"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Thinking is generation. Each thinking token comes from the same autoregressive loop as any visible token: a full serial forward pass reading the weights and the accumulated K/V state, produced one at a time in the memory-bandwidth-bound decode phase. That makes thinking tokens output tokens in every sense — priced at the ~5× output rate, and paid serially before the visible answer even starts, which is why heavy thinking noticeably delays and lengthens a response. The input-rate option gets the direction of the economics wrong: input is the cheap, parallel class, and thinking is the opposite. Prefill only reads what already exists — it cannot produce new text of any kind. And there is no sidecar model; the same weights that write your answer write the reasoning. Budget accordingly: on hard problems thinking is worth its price, but it is genuine output spend, not a discounted extra."}, {"id": "6.3-L3-06", "topic": "6.3", "topicName": "The write phase (decode)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "In a refactoring session you spend most of your wall-clock time watching Claude re-print entire files. Which lever attacks that waiting most directly?", "options": ["Constrain the output — ask for diffs instead of full files, since decode is serial and wall-clock time scales with every token generated", "Warm the prompt cache, which lets each decode step reuse the stored K/V state and raises the token-generation rate once the prefix is cached", "Trim CLAUDE.md so prefill finishes sooner and generation starts earlier", "Switch to a less-loaded region, since generation speed is set mainly by queue depth"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The waiting you feel while text streams is decode, and decode is a serial loop: each token is its own full forward pass, so a reply of half the length takes roughly half the time. Asking for diffs instead of full files cuts the token count at the source — the only lever that scales one-for-one with the wait. The cache option is the near-miss: prompt caching accelerates prefill, improving time-to-first-token, but it does nothing to the tokens-per-second of generation — decode is never cached. Trimming CLAUDE.md likewise shaves the pause before streaming starts, not the streaming itself. And a given model's decode rate is set by its size, precision, and memory bandwidth, not primarily by regional queues — true on Bedrock and Anthropic alike. The general rule: time-to-first-token is a prefill problem; everything after is an output-length problem."}, {"id": "6.3-L4-07", "topic": "6.3", "topicName": "The write phase (decode)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "During decode, a request leaves the GPU's compute units mostly idle while it waits on memory reads. How do serving stacks recover that wasted capacity?", "options": ["They precompute the rest of the reply from the prompt during prefill, so decode only has to verify it", "They store finished answers and replay them for other users whose prompts are similar", "They compress the KV cache between decode steps so each pass reads fewer bytes from memory and hands idle time back to the compute units", "Continuous batching — the idle capacity is filled with other users' requests, and the moment any sequence finishes, a waiting request takes its slot"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The serving answer to decode's idle compute is to multiplex: a rolling batch of many users' requests shares the same read of the weights, so the arithmetic units process dozens of sequences' next tokens while the memory system streams the parameters through once. Continuous batching keeps that batch full — the instant any sequence emits its last token, a waiting request refills the slot rather than waiting for the whole batch to drain. Your session's calls are anonymous entrants in that churn, which is another face of statelessness: there is no 'your' GPU to hold state on. The precompute option is impossible — the serial dependency is exactly what decode cannot escape; replies can't exist before they're generated. Answer replay would be a different (and wrong) layer: prompt caching reuses prefill state, never responses. KV-cache tricks target the bandwidth bottleneck itself, not the idle compute — batching and caching are orthogonal wins, stacked."}, {"id": "6.3-L4-08", "topic": "6.3", "topicName": "The write phase (decode)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "As a single long reply streams out, what is happening to that request's KV cache on the serving side?", "options": ["Nothing — it was fixed at prefill time, and decode only reads from it", "It shrinks as already-emitted tokens are evicted layer by layer, freeing GPU memory for the other requests sharing the batch", "It grows by one entry per layer per generated token, so each successive decode step reads slightly more state", "It is flushed continuously into the shared prefix cache so other requests can reuse it mid-reply"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Prefill builds the KV cache for the prompt, but decode keeps building it: every generated token gets its own Key and Value vectors at every layer, appended so the next token can attend over it — the reply must be able to reference itself. So the cache grows one entry per layer per token throughout generation, each step reads marginally more state than the last, and a long reply steadily raises the request's memory footprint on the GPU. The fixed-at-prefill option is the natural near-miss — it makes prefill's artifact sound final when it is only the starting balance. Eviction of emitted tokens would break generation outright: token 900 may need to attend to token 12 of the reply. And the prefix cache is a cross-call reuse mechanism keyed by exact token prefixes, persisted under a TTL — per-request working state isn't shared with strangers mid-reply. Weights are shared; KV is per-request."}, {"id": "6.4-L2-04", "topic": "6.4", "topicName": "Architecture basics", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Two files each contain about 100 \"words\" — one is plain English prose, the other minified JSON full of long identifiers. Why can their token counts differ substantially?", "options": ["They can't differ meaningfully — tokenizers split on whitespace and punctuation, so two files with equal word counts always land within a few tokens of each other, whatever the content", "The tokenizer splits text into chunks from a fixed vocabulary — common English words are often one token each, while rare identifiers and dense syntax fragment into many pieces", "JSON is pre-parsed into a structured format that is billed at a different token rate", "Prose is compressed by the API before tokenization, while code must be sent verbatim"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Tokenization maps text onto a fixed vocabulary of subword chunks, and how efficiently your text maps depends on how well it matches that vocabulary. Common English words usually land as single tokens (averaging roughly three-quarters of a word per token), but a name like `getUserAccountBalanceV2` or a run of brackets and quotes splinters into many fragments the vocabulary has no single entry for. So the JSON file can cost meaningfully more tokens than the prose despite equal 'word' counts — and since the context window, pricing, and rate limits are all denominated in tokens, 'how many tokens is this?' is a real question with a nonobvious answer, never a word count. Nothing is pre-parsed or compressed on the way in — the model receives token IDs either way, and every format is billed by the same per-token rules."}, {"id": "6.4-L3-05", "topic": "6.4", "topicName": "Architecture basics", "difficulty": 3, "question": "In attention, each token gets a Query, a Key, and a Value vector. Which mapping of roles is correct?", "options": ["Query: what this token is looking for; Key: what an earlier token offers as a match; Value: the content that gets blended in when the match scores high", "Key: what this token is looking for; Query: what each earlier token advertises to later ones; Value: the content that gets blended in when the match scores high", "Query: what this token is looking for; Key: what later tokens offer as a match; Value: content blended in from the tokens ahead", "Query and Key: how similar each token is to the user's question specifically; Value: the fragments of the eventual answer"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The retrieval metaphor runs one specific way: the current token broadcasts a Query ('what am I looking for?'), that Query is scored against the Keys of earlier tokens ('what do I offer?'), and the resulting weights decide how much of each earlier token's Value ('the content I carry') is blended into the current representation. That is, concretely, how a function defined 400 lines back influences the token being processed now. The swapped option inverts Query and Key — a genuinely easy slip, but the asymmetry matters: one Query probes many Keys, not the reverse. The forward-looking option breaks causality: in generation, tokens attend only backward, which is exactly why cached K/V for a prefix stays valid when new text is appended. And attention has nothing to do with the user's question specifically — it operates between all tokens at every layer. The Keys and Values here are precisely what the KV cache stores."}, {"id": "6.4-L4-06", "topic": "6.4", "topicName": "Architecture basics", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Per generated token, why does attention's workload grow as the context lengthens, while the feed-forward network's workload stays flat?", "options": ["Attention re-reads the raw prompt text at every step, while the FFN reads only its weights", "The FFN's per-token workload grows with context length as well, but its activations are cached and reused between decode steps, so the growth never shows up in wall-clock measurements", "Attention must score the new token against every prior token's Keys and blend their Values — more context, more to look at — while the FFN transforms each token's vector alone, independent of sequence length", "Attention runs once per layer but the FFN runs once per token, so their scaling reverses at long contexts"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The two halves of a transformer block have different relationships to the sequence. Attention is the cross-token mechanism: generating a token means computing its Query, scoring it against the Keys of everything before it, and blending the Values — work and memory reads that grow linearly with context length. That is why long contexts are genuinely more expensive per output token (not by pricing decree) and why the KV cache balloons as sessions grow. The FFN, by contrast, transforms each position's vector in isolation: same matrices, same cost, whether the context holds 500 tokens or 150,000. Nothing re-reads raw text — attention consults the numeric K/V state, which exists precisely to avoid that. The FFN has no per-token cross-context state to cache; the KV cache belongs to attention. And both sublayers run in every layer for every token — their difference is what they touch, not how often they run. This split is also why MoE targets the FFN."}, {"id": "6.5-L2-04", "topic": "6.5", "topicName": "Parameters & model size", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Within a model family, why does the top tier cost multiples — not just a few percent — more per token than the small tier?", "options": ["Larger models are trained on licensed data whose royalties are passed through per token", "Pricing follows context window size, which scales up with the tier", "Providers price by capability benchmark scores rather than by serving cost — the tier ladder is a market-segmentation choice, since running costs are roughly flat across model sizes", "Every output token must move the model's weights through memory — more parameters means more bytes per decode step, fewer tokens per second, and more hardware time bought per token"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Parameter count is directly coupled to serving economics. Producing each output token means reading the model's (active) weights out of accelerator memory, so a model with several times the parameters moves several times the bytes per decode step — decoding slower and tying up expensive hardware proportionally longer per token. Multiply that across every token served and the cost gap between tiers is structural multiples, not marketing margin. That is why family tiers differ in price by roughly an order of magnitude bottom to top. Royalty pass-through isn't how per-token pricing is constructed, context window is a separate spec that doesn't set the per-token rate, and while pricing certainly tracks capability commercially, the floor underneath it is serving cost — a bigger model is intrinsically more expensive to run. This coupling is also why matching the tier to the task, rather than defaulting to the largest, is the biggest cost lever available."}, {"id": "6.5-L3-05", "topic": "6.5", "topicName": "Parameters & model size", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague proposes: \"Always run the cheapest model, and if it fails, just rerun it — retries are still cheaper than the big model.\" What's the flaw?", "options": ["Retries are actually free — failed calls are not billed, so the policy costs nothing", "Below a capability threshold, some tasks fail on every retry — count the redone work (often finished on the bigger model anyway) and the cheap model becomes the expensive choice", "The direction is backwards — bigger models are cheaper per successful task because they stream faster", "The flaw is mechanical: rerunning an identical prompt returns the same cached failure from the prompt cache until its TTL expires, so retries must first wait out the cache window"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Retrying helps when failure is random; it does nothing when failure is structural. Capability isn't a dial that luck turns up — a task beyond a small model's threshold (a subtle architectural refactor, long-horizon agentic work) fails in similar ways on every attempt, so you pay for N failed runs, the time to review them, and then usually the frontier-model run you were avoiding. The right policy is matching capacity to difficulty per task: cheap tiers for genuinely cheap-tier-shaped work, bigger tiers where retries are costly. Every call is billed, successful or not — there's no free-failure clause on Bedrock or Anthropic. Bigger models generally stream slower, not faster, so the reversal option fails on mechanism. And the cache option misapplies caching: prompt caching stores prefill K/V state, never responses — a rerun regenerates fresh output every time (and at nonzero temperature, likely different output), which is exactly why identical failures point to capability, not caching."}, {"id": "6.5-L3-06", "topic": "6.5", "topicName": "Parameters & model size", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Why is one replica of a frontier-scale model served from many GPUs rather than a single one?", "options": ["At frontier scale the weights alone run to hundreds of gigabytes (params × bits ÷ 8) — far beyond one accelerator's memory — so they are sharded across many GPUs, loaded once, and shared read-only", "Each concurrent request needs its own working copy of the weights, and one GPU cannot hold copies for a full batch", "The weights would fit on one GPU, but sharding exists purely to parallelize decode, which is compute-bound at scale", "The KV caches of concurrent users' requests are what overflow a single GPU's memory — the weights themselves are comparatively small and would fit on one accelerator if served alone"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Run the sizing formula at frontier scale: a 200B-parameter model at 16 bits per weight is roughly 400 GB of weights (200 × 16 ÷ 8) — several times the memory of any single accelerator, before the KV caches of live requests are even counted. So one replica is split across many GPUs (within layers, across layers, and across experts for MoE), loaded once, and shared read-only by every request flowing through. The per-request-copy option inverts the design — sharing one copy is the whole point, and it's what makes serving economical. The 'would fit' option is wrong on both counts: the weights genuinely don't fit, and decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, not compute-bound. KV caches are a real and growing memory cost that shares the budget, but they sit on top of the weights — the weights set the floor, and at frontier scale the floor alone forces sharding."}, {"id": "6.6-L2-05", "topic": "6.6", "topicName": "Quantization", "difficulty": 2, "question": "As you store a model's weights with fewer and fewer bits, how does output quality typically respond?", "options": ["It degrades in direct proportion to the bits removed — each bit dropped costs about the same quality, linearly, all the way down the scale", "It is essentially unaffected at any level, since parameter count is what sets quality", "Nonlinearly: 16→8 bits is near-lossless, around 4 bits is a sweet spot with modest loss, and below that quality falls off a cliff", "It improves slightly at first, because rounding strips out noise left over from training"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The degradation curve is the key fact about quantization, and it is sharply nonlinear. Halving from 16 to 8 bits is typically near-lossless — the discarded precision was mostly headroom. Around 4 bits sits the industry's practical sweet spot: a 3–4× memory saving for modest, often barely measurable loss — the JPEG analogy's '80% quality' setting. Push further and errors from billions of coarsely rounded weights compound catastrophically: very low-bit models can be effectively broken. That cliff shape is why roughly-4-bit schemes became the default operating point rather than either extreme. The linear option misses the shape entirely, and 'unaffected at any level' overcorrects — parameters set capacity, but precision preserves it, and at aggressive levels the rounding error genuinely destroys behavior. Rounding is pure loss, never denoising. Knowing where the cliff sits is what turns quantization from a gamble into an engineering dial."}, {"id": "6.6-L2-06", "topic": "6.6", "topicName": "Quantization", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A 40B-parameter model is distributed as a roughly 25 GB file. About how many bits store each weight?", "options": ["About 16 bits — full precision, exactly as trained", "About 8 bits — a half-precision build", "About 2 bits — an aggressively compressed build tuned for edge hardware", "About 5 bits — a quantized build near the common 4–5-bit sweet spot"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Invert the sizing formula: size (GB) ≈ params (billions) × bits ÷ 8, so bits ≈ size × 8 ÷ params = 25 × 8 ÷ 40 = 5 bits per weight — a quantized release right in the widely used 4–5-bit sweet spot. Checking the distractors against the same formula: 16 bits would make the file about 80 GB (40 × 16 ÷ 8), 8 bits about 40 GB, and 2 bits only about 10 GB — each is off by the one arithmetic step people skip. Being able to run this in either direction is the practical skill: given a model's name and file size you can infer its precision, and given parameters and precision you can predict memory needs before anything is provisioned. Expect real files to land ~10–15% above the raw estimate from bundled overhead — close enough to answer 'full precision or quantized?' at a glance."}, {"id": "6.6-L3-07", "topic": "6.6", "topicName": "Quantization", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Two quantized builds of the same model both average ~4.5 bits per weight, yet one measurably degrades less. What most plausibly explains the gap?", "options": ["The better build pruned fewer parameters during the quantization process", "The better build allocates precision unevenly — extra bits for the weights that matter most, fewer for the rest — which beats uniform rounding at the same average", "The better build keeps a full-precision copy of the weights on disk and consults it whenever the model is uncertain, avoiding rounding error exactly where it matters", "At an identical average bit-width the rounding is necessarily identical, so the difference must be measurement noise"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "An average bit-width is a budget, not a recipe — schemes differ in how they spend it. Naive quantization rounds every weight onto the same coarse grid; smarter schemes measure which weights are most sensitive and buy them extra precision while economizing on the bulk, flattening the quality-loss curve at the same total size. That is why two builds with identical averages can behave measurably differently, and why quantization-scheme names encode more than a bit count. The pruning option confuses mechanisms: quantization never removes parameters — every weight survives, just stored more roughly. A disk-resident full-precision fallback isn't how inference works: decode is already memory-bandwidth-bound, and consulting disk per uncertain token would be ruinously slow — the quantized weights are what runs, full stop. And 'identical average implies identical rounding' is exactly the false premise: allocation within the budget is the differentiator. Precision, allocated unevenly, beats precision spread evenly."}, {"id": "6.6-L4-08", "topic": "6.6", "topicName": "Quantization", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A serving provider halves its precision from 16 to 8 bits per weight — a near-lossless step. Which chain of consequences is the right one?", "options": ["Each replica needs half the accelerator memory, so the same fleet runs more replicas, and bandwidth-bound decode speeds up because each token moves half the bytes — cost per token falls", "Prefill's arithmetic halves, so input-token prices can drop substantially, while decode is unaffected because output cost tracks the number of active parameters rather than their stored precision", "The freed memory doubles every request's usable context window, since window size is set by whatever memory the weights don't occupy", "The savings land on the next model version's training bill, which is where quantization's economics actually apply"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Follow the bytes. Weights at 8 bits occupy half the accelerator memory of 16-bit weights, so a replica needs fewer GPUs — the fleet fits more replicas, multiplying serving capacity. And because decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, halving bytes-per-weight roughly halves the data each token-generation step must move, raising tokens-per-second. Both effects cut cost per token; that economics is why providers quantize at all. The prefill option is a bound-and-phase trap: prefill is compute-bound, and quantization shrinks bytes stored and moved, not arithmetic performed — the big win lands on bandwidth-starved decode, the reverse of the claim. The context-window option attaches a real resource fact (freed memory does help KV capacity) to a wrong conclusion: the window is a model specification, not a leftover-memory allocation that doubles on its own. And quantization is a post-training serving choice — training runs at high precision; its bill is untouched."}, {"id": "6.7-L3-04", "topic": "6.7", "topicName": "MoE (mixture of experts)", "difficulty": 3, "question": "In an MoE model the router activates only a couple of experts per token. Why must every expert stay resident in memory anyway?", "options": ["Because experts share most of their weights, loading any one of them effectively loads them all", "They don't — modern serving stacks page idle experts out to NVMe and prefetch them back a few layers ahead of the router's choice, keeping only the hot experts resident", "Routing happens per token, per layer, at inference speed — any token may be sent to any expert, and swapping weights in from storage would be orders of magnitude too slow", "Because the router must read every expert's weights in full before it can make its choice"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The router's choice is made per token at every layer, in the microseconds of a forward pass — and the next token may route somewhere entirely different. Since any expert can be demanded at any moment, all of them must sit in fast accelerator memory; fetching gigabytes from storage on demand would stall generation by many orders of magnitude. This is exactly why MoE splits sizing into two numbers: total parameters set the memory footprint (every expert resident — use total for 'how many GPUs does a replica need?'), while active parameters set per-token compute and speed. The paging option describes a mechanism that the per-token timescale rules out. Experts don't share their core weights — separate FFNs holding distinct learned knowledge is the point of the design. And the router is a tiny network reading the token's internal state, not the experts' weights — its overhead is negligible next to the FFN work it saves."}, {"id": "6.7-L4-05", "topic": "6.7", "topicName": "MoE (mixture of experts)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "MoE famously leaves the prompt-caching story untouched. So what does it actually complicate on the serving side?", "options": ["Prefill loses its parallelism, because each token must wait to learn its expert assignment before processing can start", "The prefix cache must hold one K/V copy per expert combination, multiplying cache memory", "Requests must stay pinned to the serving node that holds \"their\" experts for the whole session, adding per-user affinity to an otherwise stateless API and complicating load balancing across the fleet", "Sharding and scheduling: experts are spread across GPUs (expert parallelism), tokens shuffle to whichever GPU holds their chosen expert at each layer, and batches are shaped to keep all experts evenly busy"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "MoE's costs are operational. Experts are distributed across GPUs — expert parallelism, on top of tensor and pipeline parallelism — so at every MoE layer, tokens must be shuffled to whichever GPU holds their routed expert and gathered back: an all-to-all communication step per layer. Schedulers also prefer batches that keep all experts evenly loaded, since an idle expert is stranded capacity. None of this is visible from the API; you see it only in price and speed. The distractors each attach a real concern to the wrong layer. Prefill stays fully parallel — routing is computed alongside everything else within the pass, not as a blocking pre-step. The prefix cache stores attention K/V, and MoE replaces the FFN, not attention — routing is deterministic, so one cached copy remains exactly valid. And per-user expert affinity would contradict the design: routing is per token, per layer, so no user 'owns' experts and statelessness survives untouched."}, {"id": "6.7-L4-06", "topic": "6.7", "topicName": "MoE (mixture of experts)", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague sizes GPU memory for a \"120B-total, 5B-active\" MoE served at ~4 bits per weight and computes 5 × 4 ÷ 8 ≈ 2.5 GB. What's the correct figure, and why?", "options": ["≈ 2.5 GB is right — only the active parameters occupy memory at any given moment", "≈ 60 GB — memory follows total parameters (120 × 4 ÷ 8), because every expert must stay resident; the 5B active figure governs per-token compute and speed, not footprint", "≈ 240 GB — MoE models cannot be quantized, so the full 16-bit figure applies", "≈ 10 GB — active parameters are what occupy GPU memory, but stable routing requires holding them at full 16-bit precision, which quadruples the colleague's 4-bit figure"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The colleague plugged the wrong parameter count into the right formula. Memory questions always use total parameters: the router picks experts per token at inference speed, so every expert must be resident — all 120B weights, giving 120 × 4 ÷ 8 ≈ 60 GB. Active parameters answer the other question: per-token compute, and therefore tokens-per-second and serving cost — the model stores like a 120B but runs like a 5B, and conflating the two directions is the classic MoE error. The 2.5 GB option applies 'active' to footprint, exactly backwards. The 240 GB option invents a rule: quantization rounds stored weights and is orthogonal to architecture — experts quantize like any FFN (240 GB is just the unquantized 16-bit figure). The 10 GB option compounds two mistakes: active-for-memory plus a fictional precision floor for routing, which is a tiny separate network, not a constraint on expert storage. Fit uses total; speed uses active."}, {"id": "6.8-L3-04", "topic": "6.8", "topicName": "Sampling & stop conditions", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A headless script asks Claude for a JSON array and occasionally receives output cut off mid-element. Which response field would have flagged the problem, and what does it indicate?", "options": ["stop_reason: \"max_tokens\" — the output ceiling ended the decode mid-thought; the reply is truncated, not complete", "stop_reason: \"end_turn\" — the model judged the array complete even though it wasn't", "stop_reason: \"stop_sequence\" — the closing bracket matched one of the API's built-in default stop strings for structured output", "stop_reason: \"tool_use\" — the model paused to call a tool and the script never resumed the loop"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Every response ends because some stop condition fired, and the API names it in stop_reason. Output cut off mid-element is the signature of max_tokens: the hard output ceiling halted decode wherever it happened to be — truncation, not completion. Treating every response as finished without checking this field is the classic scripting bug; the fix is to check stop_reason and either raise the limit or continue the generation. The distractors misread the other reasons. end_turn means the model emitted its trained end-of-turn token — a natural finish, which mid-token amputation is not. stop_sequence fires only on caller-supplied strings; the API has no default stop strings lying in wait for your brackets. And tool_use is a real mid-turn handoff in agentic flows, but it produces a structured tool-call block awaiting a result, not prose chopped mid-array. Reading stop_reason correctly is the difference between a robust pipeline and silent data corruption."}, {"id": "6.8-L3-05", "topic": "6.8", "topicName": "Sampling & stop conditions", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A bulk extraction pipeline produces differently-worded outputs for identical inputs between runs. Which request change most directly targets that variability?", "options": ["Raise top_p to 1.0 so the sampler weighs the identical full candidate pool on every run, removing the variation the nucleus cutoff introduces", "Raise max_tokens so the model isn't pressured into varying its phrasing", "Add a stop sequence so each response terminates at the same delimiter", "Lower temperature toward 0, which sharpens the distribution until the sampler essentially always takes the single most likely token"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Run-to-run variation comes from sampling: each decode step ends in a probability distribution, and a nonzero temperature lets the draw land on different tokens across runs, with the first divergence compounding through everything after. Lowering temperature toward 0 collapses the draw to the top token, making output essentially deterministic — the right dial for extraction, classification, and code, where you want repeatability over creativity. The distractors each pull a real lever in the wrong direction or at the wrong point. Raising top_p widens the candidate pool, increasing variety — the opposite of the goal; shrinking the nucleus would help, but temperature is the direct dial (and the guidance is to adjust one, not both). max_tokens caps length only — it never influences which token is chosen, and 'pressure' isn't a mechanism. A stop sequence standardizes where output ends, not the wording along the way. Note this is an API-scripting lever; interactive Claude Code uses the harness's own settings."}, {"id": "6.8-L4-06", "topic": "6.8", "topicName": "Sampling & stop conditions", "difficulty": 4, "question": "You run at temperature 0, yet across thousands of identical Bedrock calls a handful of outputs differ by a token or two. What's the accurate reading?", "options": ["The API silently enforces a minimum temperature above zero, so some sampling randomness always remains", "Temperature 0 pins only the first token; subsequent draws still sample from the full distribution", "Temperature 0 makes the sampling rule deterministic — always take the top token — but small serving-side numerical effects, such as batch composition, can occasionally flip which token is on top", "This is Bedrock-specific jitter — AWS inference profiles route calls across regional replicas with different kernel builds, and the same workload on Anthropic's own API would replay bit-identically"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Temperature 0 removes randomness from the selection rule: the sampler always takes the highest-probability token, no dice involved. But the probabilities themselves are computed in floating-point arithmetic on hardware where operation order can vary — with batch composition, kernel scheduling, and sharding details shifting run to run — so two near-tied candidates can occasionally swap ranks, and one flipped token then reconditions everything after it. Hence 'essentially deterministic': deterministic policy over not-perfectly-reproducible arithmetic. The minimum-temperature option invents an API behavior that doesn't exist. The first-token-only option invents a scope limit — temperature applies to every draw of the response identically. And the Bedrock-jitter option is a provider-delta trap: the same weights and the same serving realities sit behind both doors, so neither promises bit-identical replays at scale. Practical upshot: temperature 0 is the right call for repeatability, but pipelines should tolerate rare one-token variance rather than assume hashes will match."}, {"id": "6.9-L1-04", "topic": "6.9", "topicName": "The Claude model family", "difficulty": 1, "question": "You're handing Claude the hardest architectural reasoning problem you have, and cost is not a concern. Which tier is built for that end of the ladder?", "options": ["Haiku-class — its speed lets it attempt many more candidate approaches within the same wall-clock time, which wins on hard problems", "Opus-class — the frontier tier: maximum capability for the hardest reasoning, priced and paced accordingly", "Sonnet-class — the top tier, since the sonnet is the most demanding literary form", "Any of them — within a generation the tiers differ only in speed, not capability"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The family is a capability ladder, and Opus is its top rung: the largest, most capable tier in each generation, built for deep reasoning and the problems where failure is expensive — with the highest per-token price and slowest generation as the accepted trade. Haiku sits at the opposite end (fast, cheap, high-volume), and speed doesn't substitute for capability: a hard architectural problem isn't solved by many quick shallow attempts, because tasks beyond a tier's depth fail on every try. Sonnet is the balanced middle and daily workhorse, not the summit — and the names rank by the literary form's scale (haiku short, opus grand), which is how to remember the ordering. The 'only speed differs' option erases the ladder's whole point: the tiers differ in capability, cost, and speed together, which is precisely what makes matching tier to task the fundamental model-choice skill."}, {"id": "6.9-L2-05", "topic": "6.9", "topicName": "The Claude model family", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Your session's main loop runs a frontier-tier model, and you're about to fan out several search-and-summarize subagents. What's the cost-aware move Claude Code supports?", "options": ["Route the subagents to a cheaper, faster tier while the main loop stays on the frontier model — model choice is per-task, via /model, launch flags, or agent configuration", "Nothing — subagents automatically inherit a discounted rate on whatever model the parent uses", "Switch the entire session to the cheap tier before fanning out, since every agent spawned within a session must inherit and share the parent loop's model", "Run the subagents at a lower temperature, which is the setting that reduces their token pricing"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The tier ladder is only useful if you can act on it per task, and Claude Code exposes exactly that: /model switches the main loop mid-session, flags and env vars set it at launch, and subagent definitions can pin their own model — so delegated search-and-summarize work runs on a fast, cheap tier while the frontier model keeps the main thread. That matters doubly under fan-out: each subagent is its own loop with its own resend, so several agents multiply token volume, and the tier multiplier applies to all of it (on Bedrock, that's also your throughput quota being consumed). There is no automatic subagent discount — every call bills at its model's normal rates — and no one-model-per-session rule; mixed tiers are the intended pattern. Temperature shapes sampling randomness and has no effect on pricing. The habit to build: ask 'what's the cheapest tier that reliably does this?' per task, not per session."}, {"id": "6.9-L3-06", "topic": "6.9", "topicName": "The Claude model family", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague says: \"Bumping our long agentic session from a mid-tier to a frontier-tier model only makes the new replies more expensive.\" What are they missing?", "options": ["Nothing — input tokens are billed once when first sent, so earlier turns keep the original tier's price and only newly generated replies bill at frontier rates", "The opposite — frontier tiers include free cache reads, so long sessions get cheaper as they grow", "Only output pricing differs between tiers; input rates are identical across the ladder", "Every turn resends the entire growing conversation, and the new tier's rates apply to every token of every resend — the multiplier compounds across the whole session"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The stateless loop makes tier choice compound. There is no 'billed once': the API holds no conversation state, so every turn resends the full history — system prompt, tools, all prior turns — and every one of those tokens is billed again at the current model's input rates (uncached, cache-write, or cache-read, all of which scale with the tier). Switch a 100K-token session to a frontier tier and each subsequent turn re-bills that entire context at frontier prices, not just the fresh replies. That's why the tier spread — often an order of magnitude per token — multiplies into the biggest cost lever available, ahead of caching or compaction. The 'billed once' option imagines server-side memory that doesn't exist; free cache reads on frontier tiers aren't a thing (reads cost ~0.1× of the tier's own input rate); and both input and output rates differ by tier — the whole price sheet moves together, on Bedrock and Anthropic alike."}, {"id": "7.1-L2-01", "topic": "7.1", "topicName": "Setup & auth", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Your team is standing up Claude Code against AWS Bedrock. Which environment variable actually flips the provider?", "options": ["CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1", "AWS_BEDROCK_ENABLED=1", "ANTHROPIC_PROVIDER=bedrock", "CLAUDE_MODEL_BACKEND=aws"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1 is the single switch that reroutes the API layer to Bedrock's runtime endpoint. Everything above that layer — the CLI, prompt assembly, tools, hooks — is untouched; the harness just signs its requests with AWS credentials and uses Bedrock model IDs instead of hitting api.anthropic.com with an API key. The other three variables don't exist; they're plausible-sounding inventions, which is exactly why Bedrock setup trips people up — the real configuration is env-var heavy and the names must be exact. Alongside the switch you typically set a region (AWS_REGION, or let it resolve from your AWS profile) and ensure AWS credentials are available. Note the newer setup wizard (/setup-bedrock) can write this into the env block of ~/.claude/settings.json for you — but under the hood it's setting this same variable."}, {"id": "7.1-L2-02", "topic": "7.1", "topicName": "Setup & auth", "difficulty": 2, "question": "When Claude Code runs against Bedrock, where do its credentials come from?", "options": ["An Anthropic API key exported as ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, which Claude Code exchanges for temporary AWS credentials at session start", "The standard AWS SDK credential chain — environment keys, ~/.aws profiles, SSO, or an attached IAM role", "A token you paste during the /login flow, stored by the harness", "A Bedrock password saved in plain text in the project's settings.json"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "On Bedrock, Claude Code authenticates the way any AWS SDK client does: it walks the default credential provider chain — explicit env vars, shared config/credentials files, SSO-backed profiles, or instance/role credentials — and signs requests with what it finds (an AWS Bearer token for Bedrock API keys is the one alternative outside the chain). No Anthropic account is involved at all, which is why /logout isn't even available in a Bedrock session. ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is the Anthropic-direct mechanism and is ignored for Bedrock traffic, and /login pairs with Anthropic accounts and subscriptions, not AWS. This is the core setup delta: identity and billing move entirely to the AWS side, while the harness above the API layer behaves identically. No mechanism exchanges an Anthropic key for AWS credentials — the two identity systems never bridge."}, {"id": "7.1-L2-03", "topic": "7.1", "topicName": "Setup & auth", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Mid-afternoon, your Bedrock-backed Claude Code session starts failing with credential-expired errors — your AWS SSO token has timed out. What is the fix?", "options": ["Run /login again and choose Amazon Bedrock so the harness stores a refreshed session token", "Rotate your ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and restart", "Refresh your AWS credentials — e.g. run aws sso login, or let a configured awsAuthRefresh command do it", "Switch AWS_REGION to a region where your token is still valid"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Because Bedrock auth rides the AWS credential chain, an expired SSO token is an AWS-side problem with an AWS-side fix: re-run aws sso login for your profile. Claude Code even supports an awsAuthRefresh setting that runs a refresh command automatically when it detects expired credentials, so long sessions can heal without you leaving the CLI. /login and API keys belong to the Anthropic-direct world and do nothing here — there is no Anthropic account in the loop. And regions have nothing to do with credential lifetime; SSO tokens expire on a schedule regardless of where you send requests. The general rule: on Bedrock, every auth problem is debugged with AWS tooling (aws sts get-caller-identity, SSO login), never with Anthropic console actions."}, {"id": "7.1-L2-04", "topic": "7.1", "topicName": "Setup & auth", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Which AWS region does Claude Code send its Bedrock requests to?", "options": ["Whichever region AWS automatically routes to for lowest latency", "The region encoded inside your Bedrock API key — AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK credentials are minted per region and pin every request there", "Always us-east-1 — the region is fixed for Bedrock runtime calls", "The one resolved from AWS_REGION / AWS_DEFAULT_REGION or your active AWS profile's configured region"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Region selection follows standard AWS resolution: AWS_REGION wins, then AWS_DEFAULT_REGION, then the region configured on your active AWS profile, with us-east-1 as the last-resort fallback. This matters because Bedrock is regional — model access is granted per account and can differ per region, prompt caching support varies by region, and quotas are counted per model per region. Pointing at a region where the model isn't enabled produces access errors even with perfect credentials. There is no automatic latency-based routing at this layer (cross-region inference profiles pool capacity within a geography, but you still call one regional endpoint), keys don't embed regions, and nothing hard-codes us-east-1. You can confirm the resolved region with /status. Contrast with Anthropic direct: one global endpoint, no region decision to make at all."}, {"id": "7.2-L3-01", "topic": "7.2", "topicName": "Feature parity & lag", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A colleague warns you: \"If you move to Bedrock, hooks and skills will stop working — those are Anthropic API features.\" What's wrong with that claim?", "options": ["Hooks, skills, CLAUDE.md, MCP and the rest are harness features that run on your machine; switching provider only changes where the assembled prompt is sent", "They keep working, but each one must be re-enabled with a per-feature Bedrock flag in the env block of ~/.claude/settings.json — the /setup-bedrock wizard writes these for you", "Hooks keep working, but skills must be redeployed into your AWS account first", "Nothing — Bedrock only supports plain completions, so agentic features are unavailable"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Hooks fire in the harness process, skills are files injected into the prompt during assembly, CLAUDE.md is read from disk, and MCP servers are separate local or remote processes the harness talks to. None of that ever touches the provider — by the time the request leaves your machine, it's just a messages array with tool schemas, and Bedrock accepts the same Anthropic-shaped payload. So the entire CLI and harness machinery is provider-independent. What IS provider-dependent lives at the API layer: which models and betas are available, cache TTL options, quotas, and a few endpoint-coupled tools (e.g. the server-side WebSearch tool isn't available on Bedrock). The colleague's mistake is placing client-side machinery on the server. Mental model: Bedrock is a different door to the same model — the room you're standing in doesn't change."}, {"id": "7.2-L3-02", "topic": "7.2", "topicName": "Feature parity & lag", "difficulty": 3, "question": "In the four-layer stack — CLI → harness → API → model — which layers actually change when you set CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1?", "options": ["The harness — it swaps in a Bedrock-specific agentic loop", "Only the API layer: endpoint, request signing/auth, and the model identifiers used", "The model layer — AWS serves a Bedrock-adapted variant of the weights", "All four layers — the CLI ships a separate Bedrock build"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The switch is surgically narrow. Requests go to the Bedrock runtime endpoint in your region instead of api.anthropic.com, they're signed with AWS credentials (SigV4 or a Bedrock API key) instead of carrying an x-api-key header, and models are addressed by Bedrock IDs like us.anthropic.claude-...-v1:0 instead of Anthropic model names. The payload inside is still the Anthropic messages format — Bedrock's Invoke API wraps the same shape. The harness above runs the identical stateless loop: assemble the full conversation, send, execute tool calls, resend. And the model layer is literally the same Anthropic weights, hosted on AWS infrastructure — not a variant. Believing the harness or model changes leads to phantom debugging; the real deltas to watch are all API-layer facts: auth, regions, quotas, caching options, and model availability."}, {"id": "7.2-L3-03", "topic": "7.2", "topicName": "Feature parity & lag", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Anthropic announces a new Claude model today. A teammate on the Anthropic API is already using it, but it isn't selectable in your Bedrock setup. What's the most likely reason?", "options": ["Your Claude Code CLI is stale — alias defaults are compiled in at release time, so a brand-new model can't be requested until you update the binary", "Your AWS support plan tier is too low for new models", "New models reach Bedrock on AWS's rollout schedule and need access enabled in your account and region — direct-API users often get them sooner", "The harness deliberately blocks brand-new models on third-party providers until they're certified"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Model availability is an API-layer, provider-dependent fact. Anthropic ships new models and beta features to its own API first; Bedrock picks them up on AWS's schedule, sometimes same-day, sometimes with a lag, and even then the model must be enabled for your account and actually offered in your region. This is the classic feature-lag pattern Bedrock users live with: newest models, newest betas, and new API options arrive through AWS's pipeline. A stale CLI can matter for aliases and defaults, but the binding constraint here is availability on the provider — the harness will happily request any model ID you give it. AWS support plans have nothing to do with model catalogs, and the harness performs no certification gating; it's provider-agnostic by design. When this happens, check the Bedrock model catalog for your region, or pin an available model with the model env vars."}, {"id": "7.3-L3-01", "topic": "7.3", "topicName": "Caching differences", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Running Claude Code on Bedrock, you notice cache read/write token counts are always zero, while a colleague on the Anthropic API sees heavy cache traffic. What's the most plausible explanation?", "options": ["Prompt caching is negotiated with api.anthropic.com, so on Bedrock the harness strips cache_control breakpoints from requests and caching can never engage", "Caching only activates after your first /compact", "Bedrock caches silently and just doesn't report the token counts", "Prompt caching isn't available for that model/region combination on Bedrock — support varies by region — or it's been explicitly disabled"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Bedrock does support prompt caching for Claude models, with the same breakpoint/prefix mechanism the harness always uses — but support is per model and per region, and in a region without it your cache counters simply stay at zero while everything else works. The other Bedrock-specific possibility is that someone set DISABLE_PROMPT_CACHING=1 in the environment or settings. This is a textbook provider delta: the harness marks cache breakpoints identically either way; whether the server honors them is an API-layer fact. Caching is not tied to Anthropic keys, has nothing to do with /compact (which shrinks the conversation, and actually invalidates cached prefixes when it rewrites history), and Bedrock reports cache token usage when caching is active. The practical impact is cost: without cache reads, every turn's full resend bills at base input rates, so a long session gets substantially more expensive."}, {"id": "7.3-L3-02", "topic": "7.3", "topicName": "Caching differences", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A teammate on the Anthropic API uses the 1-hour cache TTL for long-running agents. What's the situation on your Bedrock setup?", "options": ["You can opt in with ENABLE_PROMPT_CACHING_1H=1 — the 1-hour TTL bills cache writes at a higher rate than the 5-minute default", "Bedrock only offers a 1-hour TTL, so you get it automatically", "TTL is a harness-side setting — the client stores and expires cache entries itself, so behavior is identical on any provider", "The TTL follows your Service Quotas tier — request a quota increase to unlock 1 hour"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Cache TTL is a property of the API layer, so it's exactly the kind of thing that can differ by provider. On Bedrock, Claude Code defaults to the 5-minute TTL and exposes an explicit opt-in (ENABLE_PROMPT_CACHING_1H=1) for the 1-hour lifetime; the longer TTL carries a higher cache-write rate on both providers, because the server holds your prefix's attention state 12× longer. The trade is worth it when gaps between turns routinely exceed 5 minutes — otherwise you're paying the premium for nothing. TTL is emphatically not a harness setting: the harness only marks cache breakpoints in the request; the provider decides which lifetimes exist and honors (or doesn't) the request. Service Quotas govern throughput (tokens and requests per minute), not cache behavior — a classic category confusion between the two Bedrock knobs."}, {"id": "7.3-L4-01", "topic": "7.3", "topicName": "Caching differences", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Which statement about prompt-cache pricing structure holds on BOTH Bedrock and the Anthropic API?", "options": ["Cache reads are free on both providers — reused prefix tokens are never billed; you pay only the one-time write premium (1.25× base for the 5-minute TTL) when the prefix is first stored", "Cache writes cost a premium over base input (1.25× for the 5-minute TTL) while cache reads cost a small fraction (~0.1×), so a cached prefix pays for itself after roughly one reuse", "Bedrock charges for cache reads but not writes; Anthropic does the reverse", "Caching discounts output tokens as well as input tokens"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The pricing structure is the same shape on both doors: writing a prefix into the cache costs more than plain input (1.25× base for the 5-minute TTL, higher for 1-hour), because the server must compute and then store the attention state; reading it back costs around a tenth of base input, because the server skips recomputing that prefix entirely. That asymmetry is why one reuse roughly breaks even and every subsequent turn is nearly free for the cached portion — and why Claude Code's stable-prefix-first prompt ordering is load-bearing. What differs between providers is who sets the absolute per-token prices (AWS on Bedrock, on your AWS bill), which TTLs are offered, and regional availability — not the write-premium/read-discount structure. Reads are never free, and caching never touches output pricing: the cache holds ingested prefix state, while output tokens are generated fresh every turn."}, {"id": "7.3-L4-02", "topic": "7.3", "topicName": "Caching differences", "difficulty": 4, "question": "You're mid-session on Bedrock with the default 5-minute cache TTL and step away for a 20-minute lunch. What happens on your next turn?", "options": ["The request errors with a cache-miss and you must resume the session", "The conversation history that was cached is lost, so the model forgets the early turns", "Nothing visible — the harness resends the full conversation as always, but the expired prefix is re-ingested and billed as a fresh cache write instead of cheap cache reads", "Claude Code detects the TTL expiry, runs an automatic /compact to shrink the transcript, and re-marks cache breakpoints so the rebuilt, smaller prefix starts a fresh 5-minute window"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "What the server caches is KV/attention state — the computed internal representation of your prefix — keyed to the exact token sequence, and it evaporates when the TTL lapses (each hit had been refreshing the clock). Nothing about your conversation is lost, because the conversation never lived server-side: the harness holds the transcript locally and resends the whole thing every turn regardless. So the only consequence of expiry is economic: the next request finds no matching prefix, the server re-ingests everything at cache-write rates, and subsequent turns are cheap again. No error, no amnesia, no automatic compaction. This is the stateless loop doing exactly its job. On Bedrock the lever for coffee-break-heavy workflows is the 1-hour TTL opt-in (ENABLE_PROMPT_CACHING_1H=1), trading a higher write rate for far fewer expiry-driven re-writes."}, {"id": "7.4-L3-01", "topic": "7.4", "topicName": "Throughput & limits", "difficulty": 3, "question": "On Bedrock on-demand, what governs how many tokens and requests per minute your Claude Code sessions can push through?", "options": ["Your Anthropic usage tier, which rises automatically with spend", "Your Claude subscription plan's weekly usage allowance", "A single Bedrock allowance per AWS account, pooled across all models and regions, so moving a workload between regions never changes your headroom", "Per-model, per-region AWS Service Quotas (tokens-per-minute and requests-per-minute), raisable via quota-increase requests"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Bedrock throughput is governed by AWS Service Quotas, and they're scoped per model per region for your account: Sonnet in us-east-1 has its own TPM/RPM budget separate from the same model in us-west-2. When you need more, you file a quota-increase request through the Service Quotas console — a deliberate, ticketed process. That's a structurally different regime from Anthropic direct, where rate limits come in usage tiers that scale up with your spend history, and subscription plans (Pro/Max) are a third regime that doesn't apply to API traffic at all. Getting the regime right matters operationally: on Bedrock, heavy parallel workloads (subagent fan-outs, CI fleets) burn against a fixed regional quota and start throttling, and the remedies are AWS-shaped — quota requests, cross-region inference profiles, or provisioned throughput — not spend-more-to-tier-up."}, {"id": "7.4-L3-02", "topic": "7.4", "topicName": "Throughput & limits", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Mid-session, your Bedrock-backed Claude Code starts surfacing ThrottlingException errors. What does that mean, and what's the right response?", "options": ["You've exceeded your account's Bedrock quota for that model/region; the harness backs off and retries — longer term, request a quota increase or lean on cross-region inference", "The conversation has outgrown the context window — Bedrock surfaces overflow as ThrottlingException rather than a validation error; run /compact to shrink the transcript below the limit", "Your IAM role lost its bedrock:InvokeModel permission mid-session", "Anthropic's API is rate-limiting your organization's key"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "ThrottlingException is Bedrock's 429: your request rate or token rate exceeded the account's quota for that model in that region, or the region is momentarily capacity-constrained. It's transient — the harness retries with exponential backoff, and light throttling often just looks like a slow session. Persistent throttling calls for AWS-side remedies: a Service Quotas increase, cross-region inference profiles that pool capacity across a geography, spreading load, or provisioned throughput. The distractors mix up failure layers. Context overflow is a harness/model concern with its own distinct behavior (auto-compaction), not a 429. A revoked IAM permission produces AccessDenied, a different error class entirely. And Anthropic's rate limiter is never in the picture on Bedrock — no Anthropic key is involved. Learning to read which door an error came through is half of Bedrock debugging."}, {"id": "7.4-L4-01", "topic": "7.4", "topicName": "Throughput & limits", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Your org runs a large Claude Code CI fleet on Bedrock and needs guaranteed capacity, not best-effort. Which mechanism actually reserves it?", "options": ["Raise your Anthropic usage tier by increasing monthly spend", "Purchase Bedrock provisioned throughput (dedicated model units) and point Claude Code at its ARN as the model ID", "Enable the 1-hour cache TTL so requests consume fewer quota tokens", "Set ANTHROPIC_BEDROCK_SERVICE_TIER=priority fleet-wide so every request runs on reserved capacity"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Provisioned throughput is Bedrock's reserved-capacity product: you commit to dedicated model units and get guaranteed throughput that on-demand traffic can't contend with; Claude Code consumes it by using the provisioned ARN as the model identifier. The tempting near-miss is the priority service tier — that buys preferential processing on shared on-demand capacity, improving latency and reducing throttle probability, but it reserves nothing; under regional pressure you can still be throttled. Caching cuts cost and helps token-quota consumption but provides zero capacity guarantee, and Anthropic usage tiers belong to the other provider entirely — on Bedrock your spend history with Anthropic is irrelevant. This is the throughput-regime contrast in its sharpest form: Anthropic direct scales limits with tiers and enterprise agreements; Bedrock offers an explicit capacity-reservation purchase, priced and governed like any other AWS resource."}, {"id": "7.4-L4-02", "topic": "7.4", "topicName": "Throughput & limits", "difficulty": 4, "question": "A colleague reads the model ID us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0 and says the \"us.\" prefix pins every request to us-east-1. What is that prefix actually?", "options": ["An availability-zone selector within your configured region", "A legacy naming artifact with no routing meaning", "A cross-region inference profile — AWS may route each request to any region in the US geography for capacity, and many bare foundation-model IDs can't even be invoked on-demand", "Shorthand for the US geography that resolves only when AWS_REGION is us-east-1 — in any other region you must invoke the bare foundation-model ID anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0 instead"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The prefix denotes a cross-region (system-defined) inference profile: you still call your one regional endpoint, but AWS is free to fulfill each request from any region within that geography (us., eu., apac., us-gov.), pooling capacity so bursts in one region don't immediately throttle you. It's the opposite of pinning — it deliberately un-pins fulfillment for availability and throughput. It's also frequently mandatory: invoking many Claude models by bare foundation-model ID fails with \"on-demand throughput isn't supported,\" because on-demand access is offered through the inference profile. This has governance implications too — data may be processed in any region of the geography, which matters for strict residency requirements (application inference profiles and Guardrails are the finer-grained tools there). Anthropic direct has no analog: one global endpoint, no region arithmetic. Nothing about the prefix constrains or requires AWS_REGION=us-east-1."}, {"id": "7.5-L2-01", "topic": "7.5", "topicName": "Billing & governance", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Where do the charges for your Bedrock-backed Claude Code usage actually show up?", "options": ["On an invoice in the Anthropic console", "Against your Claude Pro/Max subscription allowance", "In a dedicated Bedrock billing console separate from Cost Explorer, with its own per-model usage dashboards", "On your regular AWS bill — visible in Cost Explorer, sliceable by account, tag, region, and model like any other AWS service"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "On Bedrock, token spend is just AWS spend: it lands on the same consolidated bill as EC2 and S3, flows through Cost Explorer, respects cost-allocation tags and AWS Organizations account structure, and can be fenced with AWS Budgets alerts. That's a feature, not an accident — it's a major reason enterprises choose Bedrock, since procurement, budgeting, and chargeback machinery already exist on the AWS side (a dedicated account for Claude Code makes cost tracking trivially clean). The Anthropic console only bills Anthropic-direct API usage, and subscriptions (Pro/Max) are a separate consumer-plan world that never touches Bedrock traffic; there's no third-party Bedrock portal. Practical corollary: when someone asks \"what did Claude Code cost us last month?\", a Bedrock shop answers from Cost Explorer, while an Anthropic-direct shop answers from the Anthropic console — same harness, different ledger."}, {"id": "7.5-L2-02", "topic": "7.5", "topicName": "Billing & governance", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Which of these is a genuine reason enterprises route Claude Code through Bedrock rather than the Anthropic API?", "options": ["Traffic can stay on private networking via VPC endpoints, under existing AWS IAM, compliance, and data-residency controls, billed on the existing AWS account", "Bedrock responses are faster because AWS caches model outputs at the edge", "Bedrock receives new Claude models and betas before the Anthropic API does — AWS co-launches each Anthropic release across every commercial region on day one", "Bedrock's per-token prices are always lower than Anthropic's"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The enterprise case for Bedrock is governance, not the model: requests can traverse VPC endpoints (PrivateLink) instead of the public internet, access is controlled by the IAM roles and policies the org already operates, activity is auditable via CloudTrail, region choice supports data-residency commitments, AWS's compliance certifications apply, and spend rides the existing AWS bill under an existing AWS contract — no new vendor onboarding. The distractors invert real facts. Model outputs aren't edge-cached anywhere (each response is generated fresh; prompt caching stores ingested prefix state, not answers). New models typically arrive on the Anthropic API first, with Bedrock lagging — one of the genuine costs of choosing Bedrock. And pricing is broadly comparable, not systematically cheaper. Same weights, same harness behind either door; the door itself — networking, identity, audit, billing — is what enterprises are buying."}, {"id": "7.5-L3-01", "topic": "7.5", "topicName": "Billing & governance", "difficulty": 3, "question": "As a Bedrock admin, how do you enforce which Claude models your developers' Claude Code sessions can invoke?", "options": ["Set per-model limits on each team's workspace in the Anthropic console — Bedrock usage is metered against the workspace through your linked AWS account", "Scope IAM policies so bedrock:InvokeModel is allowed only on approved inference-profile/foundation-model ARNs, with CloudTrail for audit", "Add deny rules to each developer's project settings.json", "Maintain a model blocklist file inside ~/.claude/ on every machine"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "On Bedrock, the enforcement boundary is IAM. The invoke permissions name resources, so a policy that allows bedrock:InvokeModel and InvokeModelWithResponseStream only on specific inference-profile ARNs makes any other model physically uninvokable with those credentials — and CloudTrail records who invoked what, when. That's server-side enforcement no client can bypass. Client-side configuration (settings.json model settings, availableModels, files under ~/.claude/) can steer the /model picker and set defaults, and managed settings help standardize fleets, but a determined user with permissive credentials can point any client at any model — so client config is policy distribution, not enforcement. The Anthropic console governs Anthropic-direct keys and workspaces; it has no authority over Bedrock traffic. This is the governance delta in miniature: on Bedrock, model access control is just IAM, the same tool the org already uses for every other AWS resource."}, {"id": "7.6-L2-01", "topic": "7.6", "topicName": "What never changes", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A colleague claims: \"Bedrock serves AWS's own modified version of Claude, so you'll get different answers than on the Anthropic API.\" What's the accurate picture?", "options": ["They're right — AWS fine-tunes Claude on AWS-specific data before serving it", "Partially right — AWS quantizes the weights to save cost, so quality is slightly lower", "Wrong — Bedrock serves the same Anthropic model weights through AWS infrastructure; the real deltas are auth, billing, quotas, and rollout timing, not the model", "Wrong, but for a different reason — Bedrock is a thin proxy that forwards every request to Anthropic's own servers, which is why responses match the Anthropic API exactly"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Same weights, same model. Anthropic provides the model to AWS, and Bedrock hosts it — no fine-tuning, no quantized budget variant, no silent modification. Any response variation between providers is ordinary sampling variance, the same you'd see re-running one prompt twice on either provider. The proxy theory is wrong in the other direction: Bedrock genuinely runs inference on AWS infrastructure — that's precisely why VPC endpoints, data residency, and AWS compliance scope apply, which would be meaningless if requests bounced to Anthropic's servers. The durable deltas all sit at the API layer: how you authenticate (IAM vs API keys), who bills you (AWS vs Anthropic), how limits work (Service Quotas vs usage tiers), which TTLs and betas are offered, and when new models arrive. Anti-confusion invariant: Bedrock is a different door into the same room; never debug output quality by blaming the door."}, {"id": "7.6-L2-02", "topic": "7.6", "topicName": "What never changes", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Which of the following is identical whether your Claude Code talks to Bedrock or to the Anthropic API?", "options": ["Which prompt-cache TTL options are available by default", "When newly released Claude models become selectable", "How your throughput limits are calculated and raised — both providers meter per-minute token budgets that scale automatically with spend history", "The agentic loop itself — prompt assembly, the tool-use protocol, and the full-conversation resend every turn"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The entire loop is provider-invariant because it runs in the harness on your machine: layered prompt assembly (system prompt, tool schemas, CLAUDE.md, history), the tool_use/tool_result round-trip, permissions and hooks, compaction — and, keystone of all, the stateless resend of the whole conversation each turn. Bedrock even accepts the same Anthropic messages-format payload inside its Invoke API wrapper, so the protocol semantics carry over untouched. The other three are exactly the things that DO differ, all API-layer facts: cache TTL defaults and opt-ins vary by provider (Bedrock needs an explicit env var for the 1-hour TTL), new models hit the Anthropic API first and reach Bedrock on AWS's schedule per region, and limits are per-model-per-region Service Quotas on Bedrock versus spend-based usage tiers on Anthropic. Sorting every question into \"harness-side (never changes)\" versus \"API-side (check the provider)\" is the single most useful Bedrock habit."}, {"id": "7.1-L2-05", "topic": "7.1", "topicName": "Setup & auth", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You'd rather not export Bedrock env vars by hand. What does the /setup-bedrock wizard actually do?", "options": ["Opens the AWS console in a browser, walks you through creating a dedicated IAM user for Claude Code with bedrock:InvokeModel permissions, and downloads its access keys into ~/.aws/credentials", "Provisions a Bedrock API key in your AWS account and stores it in the system keychain", "Detects AWS profiles from ~/.aws, verifies which Claude models your account can actually invoke, and writes the resulting configuration into the env block of ~/.claude/settings.json", "Deploys a small proxy service into your AWS account that forwards Claude Code traffic to Bedrock"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The wizard (also offered at first login under \"3rd-party platform\") automates exactly the manual steps: it finds candidate AWS profiles in ~/.aws, checks which Claude models the account and region can really invoke, lets you pin them, and persists everything into the env block of ~/.claude/settings.json. That last part matters mechanically: the settings file's env block is just a durable way to place variables like CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK into the process environment — the wizard uses the same configuration-landing mechanism you'd use by hand, not a separate config system. It never creates IAM users, keys, or AWS-side resources; credentials remain whatever your AWS credential chain already provides, and there is no proxy — requests go straight to the Bedrock runtime endpoint. Keychain storage belongs to the Anthropic-direct OAuth world, not Bedrock setup."}, {"id": "7.1-L2-06", "topic": "7.1", "topicName": "Setup & auth", "difficulty": 2, "question": "You want the simplest \"paste one secret\" Bedrock setup — the closest analogue to an Anthropic API key. What is it?", "options": ["An Amazon Bedrock API key, exported as AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK", "Your Anthropic API key in ANTHROPIC_API_KEY — Claude Code exchanges it for AWS credentials automatically", "Running /login and choosing Amazon Bedrock, which stores a pasted token in the harness", "An AWS access key ID placed under a bedrockApiKey field in settings.json"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Amazon Bedrock API keys are the one credential mechanism that sits outside the standard AWS SDK chain: you export the key as AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK and requests carry it as a bearer token — no profiles, no SSO, no SigV4 key pair to manage. That makes it the closest Bedrock gets to the Anthropic key-in-an-env-var experience. The distractors each misplace the mechanism: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY identifies you to Anthropic's servers and is never exchanged for AWS credentials — there is no bridge between the two identity systems. /login is Anthropic OAuth for subscription plans; it isn't part of the Bedrock workflow at all, which is why /logout disappears in Bedrock sessions. And AWS access keys, when used, go through the normal credential chain (env vars or ~/.aws files), not a made-up settings.json field. On Bedrock, identity is always AWS-shaped."}, {"id": "7.1-L2-07", "topic": "7.1", "topicName": "Setup & auth", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A brand-new AWS account, admin credentials, correct region, CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1 — yet the very first request fails with an access error. What Bedrock-specific prerequisite is most likely missing?", "options": ["The AWS account hasn't been linked to an Anthropic organization in the Claude Console", "Anthropic models need a 24-hour activation period after the first invocation attempt", "Provisioned throughput must be purchased first — new AWS accounts can't invoke Claude on-demand until at least one Model Unit commitment exists", "Nobody has submitted the one-time use case details that an AWS account must file before it can invoke Anthropic models"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Before an AWS account can invoke Anthropic models on Bedrock, someone must submit use case details once — via the Bedrock console, or via the PutUseCaseForModelAccess API (which can cover a whole AWS Organization). Access is granted immediately after submission, but until then even an admin role gets access errors, because this is an account-level gate, not an IAM one. That's the trap in the scenario: admin credentials rule out missing bedrock:InvokeModel permissions, so the remaining prerequisite is the form. There is no Anthropic-side linking step — no Anthropic account exists anywhere in the Bedrock pipeline — no activation waiting period, and provisioned throughput is an optional capacity product, not a prerequisite; on-demand invocation works fine without it. This gate has no Anthropic-direct equivalent, which is why it surprises teams migrating over: with an API key, your first request just works."}, {"id": "7.1-L2-08", "topic": "7.1", "topicName": "Setup & auth", "difficulty": 2, "question": "On Bedrock, the aliases sonnet and opus resolve to defaults built into the CLI. How does a team deployment control which actual models those aliases hit?", "options": ["Aliases aren't supported on Bedrock — models are addressed by inference profile IDs like us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0, so every /model switch means typing the full profile ID", "Pin them with env vars: ANTHROPIC_MODEL for the session model, and ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL / ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_OPUS_MODEL / ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL for what each alias means", "Edit the alias mapping in the Anthropic console; the CLI syncs it at startup", "Register the aliases in the Bedrock model catalog for your region"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Aliases work fine on Bedrock, but they resolve to defaults compiled into the CLI — which can lag the newest release or point at a model your account hasn't enabled. The fix is pinning: ANTHROPIC_MODEL sets the session model outright, and the ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_*_MODEL variables redefine what each alias resolves to. Values can be cross-region inference profile IDs (us.anthropic.claude-...-v1:0) or even application inference profile ARNs, letting an organization route every request through a profile it administers. This is why the docs push pinning for team rollouts: users move to a new model when the administrator changes the pin, not when a CLI update silently changes a built-in default. The other options invent sync mechanisms that don't exist — the Anthropic console has no authority over Bedrock traffic, and Bedrock's catalog lists models, it doesn't define Claude Code's aliases."}, {"id": "7.2-L3-04", "topic": "7.2", "topicName": "Feature parity & lag", "difficulty": 3, "question": "On the Anthropic API your sessions use the WebSearch tool freely. After switching to Bedrock, the tool is simply gone. Why?", "options": ["Web access from Bedrock requires a NAT gateway in your VPC, and the harness disables the tool until networking is configured", "WebSearch executes on Anthropic's servers as a server-side tool; Bedrock doesn't provide that service, so the harness doesn't offer the tool there", "The tool exists but your IAM policy is missing the bedrock:InvokeWebSearch action", "WebSearch is a harness tool like Bash, so it still works — it was probably removed by a permissions deny rule"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "WebSearch is the clearest example of an API-dependent feature: unlike Bash or Read, which the harness executes locally, web search runs on Anthropic's infrastructure as a server-side tool. Bedrock hosts the model but doesn't operate that search service, so the capability simply doesn't exist through that door — and the harness, knowing which provider it's talking to, doesn't offer the tool. This is the parity rule's flip side: client-side machinery can't differ between providers, but anything executed server-side can. Distractor D is the tempting one because it applies the right rule to the wrong tool — WebSearch is precisely not harness-executed, which is the whole point. There's no bedrock:InvokeWebSearch IAM action, and VPC networking governs how your requests reach Bedrock, not what tools the model can call. Sorting each tool into client-side vs server-side predicts exactly which ones survive a provider switch."}, {"id": "7.2-L3-05", "topic": "7.2", "topicName": "Feature parity & lag", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Bedrock exposes two generic inference APIs: Converse and InvokeModel. Which one does Claude Code use, and why?", "options": ["Converse, because tool use on Bedrock requires the AWS-normalized toolConfig schema and cache breakpoints spelled cachePoint — the harness translates its requests into that shape on the way out", "Converse for ordinary turns, dropping to InvokeModel only when prompt caching is active", "InvokeModel, because Converse doesn't support streaming responses", "InvokeModel (streaming), because it's a passthrough that carries the native Anthropic Messages body — Claude Code keeps speaking its own protocol inside a Bedrock envelope"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Converse is AWS's normalized schema that papers over differences between vendors' models; InvokeModel is a passthrough that carries each vendor's native request body. Claude Code already speaks the Anthropic Messages protocol — system prompt, messages array, tool schemas, cache_control breakpoints — so it uses the InvokeModel streaming API and doesn't support Converse at all: the same request shape it sends to api.anthropic.com goes into Bedrock's envelope with an anthropic_version field marking the dialect. This is why tool use and caching behave identically across providers — the model sees the same conversation either way. The distractors are calibrated traps: Converse does support tool use and does support streaming (option C's load-bearing detail is wrong), and there's no per-feature API switching — one request shape, always. Converse spells cache breakpoints as cachePoint, but that's a fact about an API Claude Code never calls."}, {"id": "7.2-L3-06", "topic": "7.2", "topicName": "Feature parity & lag", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Yesterday your Bedrock sessions ran Opus. Today Claude Code starts with a notice that it's using Sonnet instead. A colleague says the CLI is corrupted and you should reinstall. What actually happened?", "options": ["At startup with Bedrock configured, Claude Code verifies its intended models are invokable; if the default isn't available in your account/region it falls back for the session — to an earlier version, or from Opus down to Sonnet — and the fallback isn't persisted", "AWS automatically downgrades requests to the next model tier down when your quota for the premium model is nearly exhausted — throttling pressure triggers the substitution, and the startup notice is Bedrock surfacing it for the session", "Auto-compaction switched to a smaller model so the summarized conversation would fit its context window", "Your IAM role's session policy expired mid-day, dropping your permissions to the default-tier model"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "This is the startup model check working as designed. Because model availability on Bedrock is per account and per region — and built-in alias defaults can point at models you haven't enabled — Claude Code verifies at session start that its intended models are actually invokable. When the check fails, it degrades gracefully for that session only: an earlier snapshot of the same tier, or Opus down to Sonnet, with a visible notice. Nothing is persisted, so tomorrow's session re-checks fresh. The durable fix is pinning explicit models via ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_*_MODEL rather than trusting aliases. The distractors each attach a real mechanism to the wrong layer: Bedrock never substitutes models on your behalf — quota exhaustion throttles, it doesn't downgrade; compaction shrinks the conversation and never touches model choice; and expired credentials produce auth errors, not silent tier changes. Model selection is always the harness's decision, made client-side."}, {"id": "7.3-L3-03", "topic": "7.3", "topicName": "Caching differences", "difficulty": 3, "question": "You've built a lean setup — the stable prefix of your prompt is only about 600 tokens — and on Bedrock you see no cache writes for it at all, though requests succeed. What's going on?", "options": ["Caching on Bedrock requires an explicit ENABLE_PROMPT_CACHING=1 opt-in in the env block of settings.json — unlike the Anthropic API, where the harness turns caching on by default", "Prefixes that small are cached client-side by the harness, so no server-side write is reported", "Your prefix is below the model's minimum cacheable length — 1,024 tokens for some Claude models, 4,096 for others — so the request succeeds but nothing is cached, silently", "The first session after enabling Bedrock never caches; writes begin with the next session"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Bedrock enforces a minimum cacheable prefix length that varies by model — 1,024 tokens for some Claude models, 4,096 for others — and the failure mode is deliberately quiet: a request whose marked prefix falls below the minimum succeeds normally, but the cache_control breakpoint is simply ignored and no cache entry is written. No error, no warning — just cache counters that stay at zero, which makes it easy to misdiagnose as a regional-support problem. The distractors invert real knobs or real layers: caching is on by default (the real env vars are DISABLE_PROMPT_CACHING to turn it off and ENABLE_PROMPT_CACHING_1H for the longer TTL — there's no basic opt-in), the harness never caches prompts client-side (the KV cache is computed attention state and can only live on the server that runs the model), and there's no first-session warm-up rule. Ironically, a too-lean prefix can cost more than a fatter one that clears the caching threshold."}, {"id": "7.3-L3-04", "topic": "7.3", "topicName": "Caching differences", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Your Bedrock usage metrics show inputTokens around 2,000 for a turn, but you know the harness resent a conversation of roughly 60,000 tokens. A colleague concludes Bedrock is under-metering you. What's really happening?", "options": ["Bedrock's inputTokens counts only non-cached tokens; the rest of the resend appears under cacheReadInputTokens and cacheWriteInputTokens, and total input is the sum of all three", "Bedrock compresses the request body before metering, so long conversations count fewer tokens", "The harness transmits only the newest turn each time — with Bedrock's InvokeModel session wrapper, the earlier conversation persists server-side, so metered input is just the new tail", "Cross-region routing split the request across several regions, each of which metered only its share"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Bedrock's usage accounting partitions input three ways: inputTokens is only the tokens processed at the normal uncached rate, while tokens served from cache land in cacheReadInputTokens and tokens newly written land in cacheWriteInputTokens. Total input = the sum of all three — so a 60k-token resend with a healthy cache legitimately shows ~2,000 uncached tokens plus ~58,000 in the cache-read counter. Far from under-metering, this is the caching win made visible, and it's the number to check when auditing session cost. The distractors each break a known invariant: no provider compresses requests before metering (tokens are the metering unit precisely because they're what the model ingests); the harness always resends the entire conversation — nothing persists server-side between turns, per the stateless loop; and a request is served by exactly one region even under cross-region inference — the profile routes whole requests, it never splits one."}, {"id": "7.3-L4-03", "topic": "7.3", "topicName": "Caching differences", "difficulty": 4, "question": "You run Claude Code on Bedrock through a cross-region inference profile. Turns are seconds apart, the prompt prefix is untouched, yet you occasionally see a full cache write instead of cheap reads. What explains it?", "options": ["The 5-minute TTL expired between turns, forcing re-ingestion of the prefix", "The KV cache lives in whichever region served the request; when routing sends a request to a different region, no cache exists there, so the prefix is written fresh — a cost blip, not a correctness problem", "Each appended tool_result changes the byte content of the messages array, and since cache keys require an exact match of the full request, every append invalidates the cached prefix from the top and forces a rewrite", "On Bedrock every cache hit re-bills the write rate to reset the TTL, which surfaces as periodic write charges"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The KV cache is physical server-side state — it exists in the region that computed it. A cross-region profile routes each request to the optimal region in its geography, so under shifting load a request can land in a region holding no cache for your prefix; the server re-ingests everything at write rates, and later requests to that region read cheaply. Nothing breaks: a missed cache is always just recomputed, so this is purely a cost blip. Each distractor is true in a different configuration: TTL expiry is exactly what happens after idle gaps, but the scenario stipulates turns seconds apart — every hit refreshes the clock. Appends at the end of the messages array extend the prefix without invalidating it (that's the stability gradient working); only edits to earlier content invalidate, and appends happen every turn, not occasionally. And hits refresh the TTL at no additional cost on both providers — re-billing writes per hit reverses the actual economics."}, {"id": "7.3-L4-04", "topic": "7.3", "topicName": "Caching differences", "difficulty": 4, "question": "You roll out ENABLE_PROMPT_CACHING_1H=1 across your Bedrock fleet. What does that actually buy you?", "options": ["Every Claude model in the fleet now caches with a 1-hour lifetime — TTL support is uniform across a provider's catalog, and the env var simply adds ttl: \"1h\" to each model's cache_control blocks", "The same write price as before — the TTL premium is charged on cache reads, which rise above 0.1× base", "Existing 5-minute cache entries are retroactively extended to one hour", "The 1-hour lifetime on models where Bedrock supports it — AWS lists TTL support per model ID, and some models offer only 5 minutes — with writes billed above the 5-minute tier's 1.25× rate"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "On Bedrock, the 1-hour TTL is a per-model capability: AWS's support table lists which TTLs each model ID offers, and while models like Sonnet 4.5 and Haiku 4.5 list both lifetimes, others list only 5 minutes — so a fleet-wide env var yields mixed results by model. Where it applies, the request adds ttl: \"1h\" to the cache_control block and writes bill at a premium above the 5-minute tier's 1.25× (Anthropic documents 2× base for 1-hour writes; Bedrock prices per model on the AWS pricing page). Each distractor is an expert trap: uniform TTL support is closer to true on the Anthropic API, not Bedrock; the premium sits on writes, not reads — reads stay at their small fraction, which is what preserves the payoff; and TTL is fixed per entry at write time — nothing retroactively extends live entries, and when both TTLs share one request, longer-TTL breakpoints must precede shorter ones."}, {"id": "7.4-L3-03", "topic": "7.4", "topicName": "Throughput & limits", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Your Bedrock session resends a 100k-token conversation every turn, yet your tokens-per-minute quota barely moves. How?", "options": ["The tokens-per-minute quota meters only output tokens; input is unmetered", "The harness deduplicates the resend, transmitting only the diff after the first turn", "Per AWS's guidance for Claude models, cache hits aren't deducted against your rate limit — a well-cached session burns quota mostly for the new tail of each turn", "Bedrock Service Quotas are averaged over a rolling hour rather than enforced per minute, so short bursts from one session rarely register"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Caching doesn't just cut cost — on Bedrock it directly relieves throughput pressure, because AWS's guidance for Claude models says cache read tokens aren't deducted against your rate limit. A session whose 100k-token prefix is served from cache consumes TPM quota mainly for the uncached tail: the newest turn, fresh tool results, and any re-written prefix. That coupling is why cache health and quota health rise and fall together — lose caching (region without support, prefix churn, TTL expiry) and the same session suddenly slams into TPM limits it never used to touch. The distractors each break a known mechanism: TPM quotas meter input and output — input dominates for a resending agent; the harness never sends diffs — the stateless loop resends the entire conversation every turn, which is precisely why the question is interesting; and Service Quotas are genuinely per-minute governors, not hourly averages — bursts absolutely count."}, {"id": "7.4-L3-04", "topic": "7.4", "topicName": "Throughput & limits", "difficulty": 3, "question": "A single interactive Claude Code session never throttles on your Bedrock account, but a workflow that fans out five parallel subagents starts hitting ThrottlingException almost immediately. Why?", "options": ["Each subagent runs its own loop with its own full-conversation resend, in parallel — five loops drawing simultaneously on the same per-model, per-region quota pool multiplies token consumption past what one session ever approaches", "Bedrock caps concurrent connections at four per credential, so the fifth agent trips the limiter", "Subagent traffic bills against a separate, smaller quota that AWS assigns for programmatic workloads", "The Task tool routes subagent calls through the Anthropic API rather than Bedrock — subagents are a server-side feature like WebSearch, so their traffic is throttled by Anthropic's organization rate-limit tiers, not your AWS quotas"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Subagents aren't cheap threads — each one is a complete agentic loop with its own context, resending its whole conversation every turn. Fan out five and you've multiplied per-minute token consumption several-fold, concentrated in a burst, and all of it lands on one quota pool: Bedrock Service Quotas are per model, per region, per account, with no per-session subdivision. An account sized for one interactive session throttles under parallel load it was never provisioned for — which is why quota planning is part of multi-agent design on Bedrock, and why the remedies are AWS-shaped: quota increases, cross-region inference profiles, or spreading models across pools. The distractors invent structure that doesn't exist: there's no per-credential connection cap of four, no separate \"programmatic\" quota class — all invocations draw from the same model/region pool — and subagents go through the same provider as the parent; nothing reroutes to Anthropic."}, {"id": "7.4-L4-03", "topic": "7.4", "topicName": "Throughput & limits", "difficulty": 4, "question": "You're choosing between a geographic (us.) and a global cross-region inference profile for a high-throughput Claude Code fleet with no data-residency constraints. Which statement is accurate?", "options": ["Geographic profiles are the cheaper option, since routing confined to one geography costs less than global routing", "Global profiles require each destination region to be enabled in your account before traffic can route there — model access and the use case form are granted per region, so an un-enabled region silently drops out of the routing pool until you submit access for it in the Bedrock console", "Inter-region routing traverses the public internet, so global profiles need VPC endpoints in every destination region", "A global profile can route to any supported commercial region for maximum throughput headroom, at roughly 10% cost savings — you pay your calling region's price with no routing surcharge, and CloudTrail records which region actually served each request"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Global profiles are the no-residency-constraint answer: routing across all supported commercial regions gives the deepest capacity pool, AWS prices them at roughly 10% below the geographic equivalent, you always pay your calling region's rate with no extra routing charge, and each invocation's actual serving region is auditable in CloudTrail's additionalEventData.inferenceRegion. Every distractor is true-sounding in a different configuration: the cost direction is inverted — global is the discounted option, not the premium one; destination regions don't need manual enablement, which is part of what makes profiles operationally light (contrast that with your calling region's model-access setup, which you do manage); and cross-region traffic stays on the AWS network, encrypted in transit — no public internet, no per-destination VPC endpoint requirement (your VPC endpoint concerns apply where you call from, not where AWS fulfills). Geographic profiles earn their keep only when residency rules require staying inside US, EU, or APAC. And the one-time use case form is per AWS account (or Organization), never per destination region."}, {"id": "7.4-L4-04", "topic": "7.4", "topicName": "Throughput & limits", "difficulty": 4, "question": "Your org is evaluating Bedrock Provisioned Throughput for its Claude Code CI fleet. Which statement about how it actually works is correct?", "options": ["Once purchased, the capacity is consumed automatically through your existing us. inference profile — no client change needed", "You buy Model Units, each delivering a defined input and output tokens-per-minute rate, billed hourly with commitment options of none, 1 month, or 6 months — and you invoke it via its ARN as the model ID, because inference profiles don't support provisioned throughput", "It's billed per token like on-demand, just at a discounted rate in exchange for the commitment", "The purchase raises your account's tokens-per-minute Service Quotas for that model in the region, so ordinary on-demand traffic benefits too — throttling stops once the higher quota takes effect, with no separate invocation path to manage"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Provisioned Throughput is a capacity purchase, not a pricing tier: Model Units each deliver a defined input/output tokens-per-minute rate, billing is hourly whether you use the capacity or not, and longer commitments (1 or 6 months versus none) buy lower hourly prices. Critically, it's a separate invocation path — you point Claude Code at the provisioned ARN as its model ID, because cross-region inference profiles don't support provisioned throughput. Each distractor is true of a different configuration: automatic consumption through your us. profile describes how on-demand works, and is exactly wrong here — keep the old model ID and you keep burning on-demand quota while paying for idle reserved capacity; per-token billing is the on-demand model, whereas provisioned bills for time; and the purchase doesn't touch Service Quotas at all — on-demand and provisioned are independent pools, so your regular traffic's throttling behavior is unchanged by the purchase."}, {"id": "7.5-L2-03", "topic": "7.5", "topicName": "Billing & governance", "difficulty": 2, "question": "Your company already pays for Claude Max seats. Can those subscriptions cover your team's Bedrock-backed Claude Code usage?", "options": ["Yes — link the seats through the AWS Marketplace subscription offer, and Bedrock usage draws down each seat's weekly usage allowance", "Yes — export the subscription token as AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK", "No — subscriptions attach to Anthropic accounts via /login; Bedrock has no subscription option at all, only pay-per-token charges on the AWS bill", "Only Max plans include Bedrock entitlement; Pro plans don't"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Subscriptions (Pro/Max) are an Anthropic-direct payment shape: /login attaches Claude Code usage to a flat monthly fee with usage limits, authenticated against an Anthropic account. Bedrock has no Anthropic account anywhere in its pipeline — identity is IAM, billing is the AWS invoice — so there is nothing for a subscription to attach to, and no subscription product exists on the Bedrock side at any plan level. Every Bedrock token is pay-per-token AWS spend, priced per model on the Bedrock pricing page. No Marketplace linkage exists, and AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK carries an Amazon Bedrock API key — an AWS credential — not anything issued by Anthropic; the two identity systems never bridge. Practical consequence for teams running both doors: the same developer's usage can land on two unrelated ledgers — subscription allowance when logged in to Anthropic, AWS bill when on Bedrock — and budgeting has to treat them separately."}, {"id": "7.5-L2-04", "topic": "7.5", "topicName": "Billing & governance", "difficulty": 2, "question": "The docs recommend running Claude Code in a dedicated AWS account. What's the actual rationale?", "options": ["Account boundaries are AWS's coarsest, most reliable cost-attribution line — with a dedicated account, \"what does Claude Code cost us\" becomes a one-filter Cost Explorer query", "Bedrock grants higher default quotas to accounts that run no other services", "Claude Code needs broad administrator-level permissions — bedrock:*, plus Service Quotas and CloudTrail access — that would be unsafe to grant inside a shared account, making isolation a security requirement", "The model-access use case form must be re-submitted whenever other AWS services run in the same account"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "It's a billing tactic. Tags and per-service line items work, but they depend on discipline — untagged resources leak, and Bedrock spend mingles with everything else in a shared account. An account boundary can't leak: every dollar inside it belongs to Claude Code by construction, so cost tracking, AWS Budgets alerts, and chargeback all reduce to filtering on one account ID. This slots into AWS Organizations exactly the way finance teams already handle other workloads. The distractors invent mechanics that don't exist: quota defaults are not a reward for single-purpose accounts — they follow model, region, and account history; Claude Code needs only scoped bedrock:InvokeModel-family permissions, and least-privilege works fine in shared accounts (a governance argument for IAM hygiene, not account isolation); and the use case form is one-time per account (or once per Organization via the API), never invalidated by other services' presence."}, {"id": "7.5-L3-02", "topic": "7.5", "topicName": "Billing & governance", "difficulty": 3, "question": "Several teams share one AWS account for Claude Code on Bedrock, and finance wants per-team spend. What's the AWS-native mechanism?", "options": ["Tag each developer's IAM role — Bedrock attributes token spend to the calling role's cost-allocation tags in Cost Explorer", "Create one Anthropic Console workspace per team and map their usage through workspace keys", "Enable CloudTrail invocation logging and export to Athena for token-count accounting per caller", "Create an application inference profile per team, carrying cost-allocation tags, and have each team's Claude Code invoke its profile ARN as the model ID"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Application inference profiles are Bedrock's below-account attribution unit: you create a profile ARN wrapping a model, attach cost-allocation tags to it, and spend flowing through it lands in standard AWS cost reporting under those tags. Claude Code cooperates through configuration — ANTHROPIC_MODEL accepts a profile ARN directly, and modelOverrides can map each entry in the /model picker to a team's ARN, so developers switch models freely while every request still flows through metered, administered profiles. The near-misses: IAM role tags govern access control and aren't how Bedrock inference charges reach Cost Explorer — the cost-bearing tagged resource is the profile, not the caller. CloudTrail-to-Athena genuinely reconstructs who invoked what, but that's audit machinery producing a spreadsheet, not cost allocation the billing system enforces and finance consumes natively. And Anthropic Console workspaces meter the other door entirely — they have no visibility into Bedrock traffic."}, {"id": "7.6-L2-03", "topic": "7.6", "topicName": "What never changes", "difficulty": 2, "question": "The Anthropic API calls a model claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929; Bedrock calls one us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0. What does the shared date stamp tell you?", "options": ["They share a training-data cutoff, but AWS builds and serves its own model from that data", "They name the same frozen model snapshot — Bedrock's ID wraps routing and versioning metadata (the us. prefix, the -v1:0 suffix) around the identical artifact", "The date records when AWS mirrored the snapshot into its catalog; behavior can drift afterward as each provider applies its own serving and safety patches independently", "It's coincidence — the two providers version their catalogs independently"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Anthropic trains a model, freezes it as a dated snapshot, and that same artifact is what both doors serve — the matching date stamp is readable proof, not coincidence. Everything Bedrock adds to the identifier is addressing metadata: us. names the cross-region inference profile geography, -v1:0 is AWS catalog versioning. Nothing inside the wrapper differs. The distractors describe worlds where the model layer would be provider-dependent, and none exist: AWS doesn't train or rebuild anything — Anthropic provides the model and Bedrock hosts it; and snapshots are immutable by design — neither provider \"patches\" a dated snapshot, which is precisely what makes pinning a dated model meaningful for reproducibility. Behavioral changes ship as new snapshots with new dates. This is the ID-level face of the keystone invariant: same weights, same context window, same tokenizer through either door — only the addressing scheme around the snapshot differs."}, {"id": "7.6-L2-04", "topic": "7.6", "topicName": "What never changes", "difficulty": 2, "question": "A colleague reports: \"Claude writes worse code for me on Bedrock than on the Anthropic API — the AWS door must degrade it somehow.\" Where should you actually look?", "options": ["Network latency through VPC endpoints, which can truncate long responses", "Bedrock's default Guardrails content filtering — it's enabled account-wide out of the box and post-processes model output, subtly rewriting code before the harness sees it unless an admin opts the account out in the Bedrock console settings", "Configuration — a lagging or differently pinned model version (Bedrock aliases resolve to built-in defaults that can trail the newest release), or a missing server-side feature like WebSearch — never the weights or the door itself", "Regional load — AWS serves a quantized replica of the model in busy regions"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Since both doors serve the same frozen snapshot, \"Bedrock made it dumber\" can never be the explanation — but the complaint often has a real cause in configuration. The usual suspects: the colleague's sessions are on different snapshots (Bedrock aliases resolve to CLI built-in defaults that can lag, and the startup check can silently fall back to an older version or a lower tier when the intended model isn't enabled — compare /status or /model output on both setups), or a task genuinely leaned on WebSearch, which doesn't exist on Bedrock. The distractors relocate degradation into layers where it can't occur: latency delays bytes but never truncates or alters them; Guardrails is opt-in via configured request headers, not a default filter; and AWS serves no quantized replica under load — capacity pressure produces throttling, never a quietly weaker model. Debug the configuration around the door; never blame the door."}];
